window.CHALKCAST_BANK={"course":"BIO 004 Human Anatomy","units":[{"week":1,"system":"Foundations","comps":[{"id":"w1-levels-organization","name":"Levels of structural organization","can":"List the six levels of structural organization in order from chemical to organismal and state what each level contains.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The tissue level of organization is built directly from which level?\nA) The cellular level  |  B) The organ system level\nC) The chemical level  |  D) The organ level\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The cellular level","why":"A tissue is a group of similar cells that work together to perform a specific function, so the tissue level is built directly from the cellular level. Stratified squamous epithelium, for example, is many layers of similar cells. Each level of organization is assembled from the level just below it."},{"t":"mc","q":"Organs are built from smaller units. Which level of organization sits one step below the organ level?\nA) The tissue level  |  B) The organismal level\nC) The cellular level  |  D) The chemical level\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The tissue level","why":"An organ is a structure made of two or more tissue types with a defined job, so the tissue level sits directly below it. The stomach, for example, combines epithelial, connective, muscle, and nervous tissue. Each level is assembled from the level just beneath it."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which order lists these levels from least to most complex: tissue, organ system, cellular, organ?\nA) Tissue, cellular, organ, organ system\nB) Cellular, organ, tissue, organ system\nC) Organ system, organ, tissue, cellular\nD) Cellular, tissue, organ, organ system\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Cellular, tissue, organ, organ system.","why":"Each level is built from the one before it. Cells group into tissues, two or more tissue types form an organ, and organs working together form an organ system. The chemical level sits below all of these and the organismal level above them."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which is the smallest of the six levels of structural organization?\nA) Chemical level  |  B) Tissue level\nC) Cellular level  |  D) Organ level\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Chemical level","why":"The chemical level, made of atoms and molecules such as oxygen, water, and proteins, is the smallest and simplest level of structural organization. Each level above it is built from the one below: chemicals form cells, cells form tissues, and tissues form organs. A problem at a low level, such as a faulty protein, can show up at every level above it."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which level of structural organization is the smallest living unit of the body?\nA) Organismal level  |  B) Chemical level\nC) Tissue level  |  D) Cellular level\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Cellular level","why":"The cellular level is the smallest level that is alive. A cell, such as a keratinocyte or a neuron, carries out the basic life processes on its own. The chemical level is smaller, but atoms and molecules by themselves are not alive."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: List the six levels of structural organization in order from chemical to organismal and state what each level contains. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to list the six levels of structural organization in order from chemical to organismal and state what each level contains."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to list the six levels of structural organization in order from chemical to organismal and state what each level contains. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: levels of structural organization. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, levels of structural organization, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w1-anatomical-position","name":"Anatomical position","can":"Place the body in anatomical position, define supine and prone, and explain why anatomical position is the fixed reference for every directional term.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient lies prone on the exam table, and a wound on the back of the thigh faces the ceiling. Which directional term correctly describes the surface of the thigh with the wound?\nA) Anterior  |  B) Posterior\nC) Inferior  |  D) Superior\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Posterior","why":"Directional terms are always given as if the body were in anatomical position, no matter how the patient is lying. In anatomical position the back of the thigh faces backward, so the wound is on the posterior surface. The fact that it faces the ceiling while the patient is prone does not change its name."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why does anatomical position serve as the reference for every directional term?\nA) Directional terms are defined from it, so descriptions hold in any posture\nB) It is the position most patients are in during an examination\nC) It is the position the body naturally takes when fully relaxed\nD) It is the only position in which the left and right sides match\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Directional terms are defined from it, so descriptions hold in any posture","why":"Every directional term is defined as if the body were in anatomical position: erect, facing forward, arms at the sides, palms forward. Because everyone uses that same reference, a phrase like anterior distal forearm points to the same spot whether the patient is standing, sitting, or lying down. Right and left are also always the patient's own right and left."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient is positioned lying on the back, face up, for an examination. Which term names this position?\nA) Prone  |  B) Supine\nC) Lateral recumbent  |  D) Anatomical position\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Supine","why":"Supine means lying face up, on the back. It is the most common position for examining the abdomen. Even with the patient supine, directional terms are still given as if the body were in anatomical position."},{"t":"mc","q":"A clinician facing a patient charts a mole on the left cheek. Whose left does the chart mean?\nA) The patient's own left\nB) The left side of a printed body diagram as you look at it\nC) The clinician's left while facing the patient\nD) Either side, as long as the chart says which view was used\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The patient's own left","why":"Anatomical right and left are always the patient's own right and left, never the observer's. When you face a patient, their left side is on your right, so charting from your own view would place the mole on the wrong cheek. Using the patient's sides is part of describing the body from anatomical position."},{"t":"mc","q":"In anatomical position, which way do the palms face?\nA) Toward the thighs  |  B) Forward\nC) Toward each other  |  D) Backward\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Forward","why":"In anatomical position the body stands erect with the feet flat, the head and eyes facing forward, and the arms at the sides with the palms facing forward. Turning the palms forward keeps the two forearm bones side by side instead of crossed, and it points the thumbs away from the body. Every directional term is defined from this one posture."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Place the body in anatomical position, define supine and prone, and explain why anatomical position is the fixed reference for every directional term. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to place the body in anatomical position, define supine and prone, and explain why anatomical position is the fixed reference for every directional term."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to place the body in anatomical position, define supine and prone, and explain why anatomical position is the fixed reference for every directional term. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: anatomical position. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, anatomical position, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w1-planes-sections","name":"Anatomical planes and sections","can":"Name the sagittal, midsagittal, parasagittal, frontal, transverse, and oblique planes and state the two parts each divides the body into.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A frontal section is cut through the head just in front of the ears. Which group of regions lies entirely on the anterior portion?\nA) Occipital and otic  |  B) Occipital, nasal, and mental\nC) Frontal, orbital, nasal, and oral  |  D) Orbital, otic, and occipital\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Frontal, orbital, nasal, and oral","why":"A frontal section divides the head into anterior and posterior portions. With the cut just in front of the ears, the forehead (frontal), eyes (orbital), nose (nasal), and mouth (oral) all fall on the anterior portion. The ears (otic) and the back of the head (occipital) fall on the posterior portion."},{"t":"mc","q":"A radiologist wants one image that shows both lungs side by side along their full height, from their tops down to the diaphragm. Which plane should the image be cut in?\nA) A transverse plane  |  B) A frontal (coronal) plane\nC) A midsagittal plane  |  D) A parasagittal plane\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A frontal (coronal) plane","why":"A frontal plane runs vertically from side to side, so a single frontal section can show the right and left lungs together along their full height, from the top of each lung down to the base resting on the diaphragm. A transverse slice also shows both lungs, but only at one horizontal level at a time. The view the radiologist needs decides the plane."},{"t":"mc","q":"What distinguishes a midsagittal plane from a parasagittal plane?\nA) It runs horizontally rather than vertically\nB) It lies exactly on the midline, producing equal halves\nC) It divides anterior from posterior parts\nD) It is set at an angle between horizontal and vertical\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It lies exactly on the midline, producing equal halves","why":"Both planes are sagittal, meaning vertical cuts that separate left from right. The midsagittal, or median, plane lies exactly on the midline and produces equal left and right halves. A parasagittal plane runs parallel to it but off the midline, so it produces unequal left and right parts."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which plane would separate the body into front and back portions?\nA) The transverse plane  |  B) The oblique plane\nC) The midsagittal plane  |  D) The frontal (coronal) plane\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The frontal (coronal) plane","why":"The frontal plane, also called the coronal plane, is a vertical plane that runs from side to side, dividing the body into anterior and posterior portions. Picture a cut running from one ear to the other straight down through the body. MRI images are often read in this plane."},{"t":"mc","q":"A surgeon makes a single vertical incision exactly on the midline of the abdomen. Which plane does the incision follow?\nA) The midsagittal plane  |  B) The transverse plane\nC) A parasagittal plane  |  D) The frontal plane\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The midsagittal plane","why":"A vertical cut placed exactly on the midline follows the midsagittal, or median, plane. The midline is the imaginary vertical line from the top of the head through the nose and down to the pelvis, so a cut along it would divide the body into equal left and right halves. A surgical incision down the middle of the abdomen lies in this plane."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Name the sagittal, midsagittal, parasagittal, frontal, transverse, and oblique planes and state the two parts each divides the body into. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to name the sagittal, midsagittal, parasagittal, frontal, transverse, and oblique planes and state the two parts each divides the body into."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to name the sagittal, midsagittal, parasagittal, frontal, transverse, and oblique planes and state the two parts each divides the body into. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: anatomical planes and sections. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, anatomical planes and sections, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w1-directional-terms","name":"Directional terms","can":"Use the paired directional terms precisely, including superior and inferior, anterior and posterior, medial and lateral, proximal and distal, superficial and deep, cranial and caudal, and ipsilateral and contralateral.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A structure in the forearm is described as both proximal and lateral. In anatomical position, where does it sit?\nA) Near the wrist end of the forearm, on the little-finger side\nB) Near the wrist end of the forearm, on the thumb side\nC) Near the elbow end of the forearm, on the thumb side\nD) Near the elbow end of the forearm, on the little-finger side\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Near the elbow end of the forearm, on the thumb side","why":"Proximal places the structure closer to where the limb attaches to the trunk, so toward the elbow end of the forearm. Lateral places it away from the midline, and with the palms facing forward in anatomical position, the thumb side of the forearm is the lateral side. Putting the two terms together gives the elbow end on the thumb side."},{"t":"mc","q":"A chart reads: wound on the posterior, distal forearm. Where on the body is the wound?\nA) On the front of the forearm, near the wrist\nB) On the back of the forearm, near the elbow\nC) On the front of the forearm, near the elbow\nD) On the back of the forearm, near the wrist\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) On the back of the forearm, near the wrist","why":"Posterior means the back surface, and distal means farther from the limb's attachment to the trunk, which on the forearm is the wrist end. Combining the two places the wound on the back of the forearm near the wrist. Reading one term at a time keeps the location from getting scrambled."},{"t":"mc","q":"A structure described as intermediate sits in what position?\nA) Between a medial and a lateral structure\nB) On the opposite side of the body from another structure\nC) Between a superior and an inferior structure\nD) Closer to the body surface than another structure\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Between a medial and a lateral structure","why":"Intermediate describes a structure lying between a more medial structure and a more lateral one. For example, the collarbone is intermediate between the breastbone, which is medial, and the shoulder, which is lateral. It is the in-between term for the medial and lateral axis only."},{"t":"mc","q":"Comparing the wrist with the elbow along the upper limb, which directional term applies to the wrist?\nA) Proximal  |  B) Distal\nC) Superficial  |  D) Medial\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Distal","why":"Along a limb, distal means farther from the point where the limb attaches to the trunk. The wrist is farther from the shoulder than the elbow is, so the wrist is distal to the elbow. The pair works in reverse too, so the elbow is proximal to the wrist."},{"t":"mc","q":"An anatomist needs to describe a structure as toward the back of the body. Which term and synonym are correct?\nA) Inferior, also called caudal  |  B) Anterior, also called ventral\nC) Posterior, also called dorsal  |  D) Superior, also called cranial\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Posterior, also called dorsal","why":"Posterior means toward the back of the body, and in humans its synonym is dorsal. Its opposite is anterior, also called ventral. Because humans stand upright, the back of the body and the dorsal surface are the same surface, so the two words can be used interchangeably."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Use the paired directional terms precisely, including superior and inferior, anterior and posterior, medial and lateral, proximal and distal, superficial and deep, cranial and caudal, and ipsilateral and contralateral. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to use the paired directional terms precisely, including superior and inferior, anterior and posterior, medial and lateral, proximal and distal, superficial and deep, cranial and caudal, and ipsilateral and contralateral."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to use the paired directional terms precisely, including superior and inferior, anterior and posterior, medial and lateral, proximal and distal, superficial and deep, cranial and caudal, and ipsilateral and contralateral. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: directional terms. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, directional terms, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w1-regional-terms","name":"Regional terms","can":"Identify the major anterior and posterior regional terms and sort each as axial or appendicular.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A chart lists three injuries: buccal, olecranal, and lumbar. How many are in the appendicular region, and which one?\nA) One, the buccal injury on the cheek\nB) Two, the olecranal and the lumbar injuries\nC) One, the olecranal injury at the back of the elbow\nD) None, because all three are on the trunk or head\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) One, the olecranal injury at the back of the elbow.","why":"Buccal is the cheek, olecranal is the back of the elbow, and lumbar is the lower back. The head, neck, and trunk are axial, and the limbs are appendicular. Only the elbow is on a limb, so only the olecranal injury is appendicular."},{"t":"mc","q":"The chest as a whole and the breastbone area within it have separate regional terms. Which pair matches them correctly?\nA) Sternal for the chest, thoracic for the breastbone\nB) Thoracic for the chest, mammary for the breastbone\nC) Thoracic for the chest, sternal for the breastbone\nD) Mammary for the chest, thoracic for the breastbone\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Thoracic for the chest, sternal for the breastbone","why":"Thoracic is the broad term for the whole chest area, and sternal is the narrower term for the area over the breastbone, the sternum. Many regional terms nest this way, with a small named region sitting inside a larger one. The umbilical region inside the abdominal region is another example."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which regional term names the lateral side of the leg, between the knee and the ankle?\nA) Tarsal  |  B) Crural\nC) Sural  |  D) Fibular\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Fibular","why":"Fibular, also called peroneal, names the lateral side of the leg, where the fibula runs between the knee and the ankle. The fibula is the thin lateral bone of the leg, so the region borrows its name. It appears on the posterior regional term list along with sural and calcaneal."},{"t":"mc","q":"The cheek and the chin each have a regional term. Which pairing is correct?\nA) Mental for the cheek, buccal for the chin\nB) Buccal for the cheek, mental for the chin\nC) Otic for the cheek, mental for the chin\nD) Buccal for the cheek, oral for the chin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Buccal for the cheek, mental for the chin","why":"Buccal names the cheek and mental names the chin. Mental comes from the Latin word for chin, not from the word for mind, which is a common source of confusion. Both are anterior regions of the face."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which regional term belongs to the appendicular region rather than the axial region?\nA) Lumbar  |  B) Sternal\nC) Cervical  |  D) Carpal\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Carpal","why":"The appendicular region is the limbs, and the axial region is the head, neck, and trunk, the central axis of the body. Carpal names the wrist, which is part of the upper limb, so it is appendicular. The other three terms all name parts of the neck or trunk."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the major anterior and posterior regional terms and sort each as axial or appendicular. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the major anterior and posterior regional terms and sort each as axial or appendicular."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the major anterior and posterior regional terms and sort each as axial or appendicular. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: regional terms. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, regional terms, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w1-body-cavities","name":"Body cavities","can":"Distinguish the dorsal and ventral body cavities and name every subdivision of each, including the cranial, vertebral, thoracic, pleural, pericardial, abdominal, and pelvic cavities.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A clinician needs to sample the fluid around the spinal cord. Which cavity holds that fluid, and which procedure reaches it?\nA) The pleural cavity, reached by thoracentesis\nB) The cranial cavity, reached by a lumbar puncture\nC) The peritoneal cavity, reached by paracentesis\nD) The vertebral cavity, reached by a lumbar puncture\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The vertebral cavity, reached by a lumbar puncture","why":"The spinal cord and the fluid around it lie in the vertebral cavity, part of the dorsal cavity. A lumbar puncture places a needle between vertebrae in the lower back to draw that fluid. The procedure name and the cavity have to match for the answer to be correct."},{"t":"mc","q":"A stab wound lets air into the right pleural cavity, and the right lung collapses. Why does the left lung keep working?\nA) The left lung has no pleura around it\nB) Each lung sits in its own separate pleural cavity\nC) The diaphragm separates the right lung from the left lung\nD) The pericardial cavity absorbs the air before it reaches the left side\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Each lung sits in its own separate pleural cavity","why":"Each lung sits in its own pleural cavity, and the two cavities are separated by the mediastinum. When air enters one pleural cavity, the seal that holds that lung expanded is lost and the lung collapses, but the other pleural cavity stays sealed. Keeping each lung in its own closed space limits the damage to one side."},{"t":"mc","q":"The cranial and vertebral cavities together make up which major body cavity?\nA) The ventral (anterior) cavity  |  B) The thoracic cavity\nC) The dorsal (posterior) cavity  |  D) The abdominopelvic cavity\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The dorsal (posterior) cavity","why":"The dorsal, or posterior, cavity has two subdivisions: the cranial cavity holding the brain and the vertebral cavity holding the spinal cord. The two are continuous through the large opening at the base of the skull, and both are lined by the meninges. The other major cavity, the ventral cavity, holds the organs of the chest and abdomen."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which two cavities are continuous, with no wall dividing them?\nA) The pleural and pericardial cavities  |  B) The abdominal and pelvic cavities\nC) The thoracic and abdominal cavities  |  D) The cranial and thoracic cavities\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The abdominal and pelvic cavities","why":"The abdominal and pelvic cavities are two named parts of one continuous space, the abdominopelvic cavity. No membrane or muscle separates them; the pelvic part is simply the lower portion cradled by the bony pelvis. That is why fluid or infection in the abdomen can settle into the pelvis."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which membranes line both the cranial cavity and the vertebral cavity?\nA) The pleura  |  B) The meninges\nC) The peritoneum  |  D) The pericardium\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The meninges","why":"The cranial and vertebral cavities form the dorsal cavity, one continuous space from the brain down the spinal cord. The meninges line this whole space, wrapping the brain and spinal cord together. Inflammation of these membranes is meningitis."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Distinguish the dorsal and ventral body cavities and name every subdivision of each, including the cranial, vertebral, thoracic, pleural, pericardial, abdominal, and pelvic cavities. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish the dorsal and ventral body cavities and name every subdivision of each, including the cranial, vertebral, thoracic, pleural, pericardial, abdominal, and pelvic cavities."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to distinguish the dorsal and ventral body cavities and name every subdivision of each, including the cranial, vertebral, thoracic, pleural, pericardial, abdominal, and pelvic cavities. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: body cavities. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, body cavities, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w1-serous-membranes","name":"Serous membranes","can":"Define a serous membrane and differentiate its parietal and visceral layers across the pleura, pericardium, and peritoneum.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has chest pain, and the clinician hears a scratchy rubbing sound timed with each heartbeat. Which membrane is most likely inflamed?\nA) The peritoneum  |  B) The pericardium\nC) The pleura  |  D) The meninges\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The pericardium","why":"Normally serous fluid lets the two layers of the serous pericardium glide past each other with every heartbeat. When the pericardium is inflamed, the layers rub instead of glide, producing a friction rub that keeps time with the heart. This is pericarditis, and the timing of the sound with the heartbeat points to the pericardium."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which layer of a serous membrane covers the outer surface of an organ?\nA) The visceral layer  |  B) The fibrous pericardium\nC) The parietal layer  |  D) The mucosa\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The visceral layer","why":"The visceral layer of a serous membrane lies directly on the surface of the organ. The word visceral refers to the viscera, the internal organs. The parietal layer is its partner, lining the body wall, and the serous fluid between them lets the organ move without friction."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which serous membrane surrounds each lung?\nA) Pleura  |  B) Pericardium\nC) Peritoneum  |  D) Meninges\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Pleura","why":"The pleura is the serous membrane of the lungs. Its visceral layer covers each lung, its parietal layer lines the chest wall, and the pleural cavity between them holds pleural fluid. That fluid lets the lungs slide against the chest wall during breathing."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the epithelium of a serous membrane called?\nA) Epicardium  |  B) Mucosa\nC) Endothelium  |  D) Mesothelium\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Mesothelium","why":"The epithelium of a serous membrane is called mesothelium. It is a thin simple squamous epithelium resting on a layer of loose connective tissue, and it secretes the serous fluid that lets organs glide against the body wall. The pleura, pericardium, and peritoneum are all built this way."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which serous membrane belongs to the abdominopelvic cavity?\nA) The peritoneum  |  B) The pleura\nC) The pericardium  |  D) The meninges\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The peritoneum","why":"The peritoneum is the serous membrane of the abdominopelvic cavity. Its parietal layer lines the abdominal wall, its visceral layer covers the abdominal organs, and the peritoneal cavity between them holds peritoneal fluid. It is the largest of the three serous membranes."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Define a serous membrane and differentiate its parietal and visceral layers across the pleura, pericardium, and peritoneum. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to define a serous membrane and differentiate its parietal and visceral layers across the pleura, pericardium, and peritoneum."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to define a serous membrane and differentiate its parietal and visceral layers across the pleura, pericardium, and peritoneum. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: serous membranes. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, serous membranes, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w1-mediastinum","name":"Mediastinum","can":"Map the boundaries and the four compartments of the mediastinum and identify a major structure contained in each.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"In the course's description of the mediastinum, which structure forms its posterior boundary?\nA) The sternum  |  B) The diaphragm\nC) The vertebral column  |  D) The lungs\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The vertebral column","why":"The mediastinum is the central compartment of the thoracic cavity, between the two lungs. Its boundaries are the thoracic inlet superiorly, the diaphragm inferiorly, the sternum anteriorly, the vertebral column posteriorly, and the lungs laterally. The vertebral column forms the back wall of this compartment."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which plane separates the superior mediastinum from the inferior mediastinum?\nA) The transverse thoracic plane  |  B) The transumbilical plane\nC) The median plane  |  D) The midclavicular plane\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The transverse thoracic plane","why":"The transverse thoracic plane is a horizontal plane passing from the sternal angle, the ridge where the manubrium meets the body of the sternum, back to the disc between the fourth and fifth thoracic vertebrae. Everything above it is the superior mediastinum and everything below it is the inferior mediastinum. Because the plane is horizontal, it divides the compartment into upper and lower parts."},{"t":"mc","q":"A mass is found in the inferior mediastinum, between the body of the sternum and the pericardium. Which subdivision contains it?\nA) The posterior mediastinum  |  B) The anterior mediastinum\nC) The middle mediastinum  |  D) The superior mediastinum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The anterior mediastinum","why":"The inferior mediastinum is divided by the pericardium into three parts. The anterior mediastinum is the narrow space in front of the pericardium, behind the body of the sternum. The middle mediastinum is the pericardium with the heart inside it, and the posterior mediastinum lies behind the pericardium."},{"t":"mc","q":"A radiologist reports a finding inside the pericardial sac, next to the heart. Which mediastinal subdivision is this?\nA) The superior mediastinum  |  B) The posterior mediastinum\nC) The middle mediastinum  |  D) The anterior mediastinum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The middle mediastinum","why":"The middle mediastinum is the pericardium and everything inside it, including the heart and the roots of the great vessels. It is the largest part of the inferior mediastinum. The anterior and posterior subdivisions lie in front of and behind it."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which structure passes through both the superior mediastinum and the posterior mediastinum?\nA) The ascending aorta  |  B) The pulmonary trunk\nC) The esophagus  |  D) The thymus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The esophagus","why":"The esophagus enters the thorax in the superior mediastinum and continues down behind the heart through the posterior mediastinum before passing through the diaphragm to reach the stomach. Because it runs the full length of the thorax, it crosses from one subdivision into the next. Long tubes like the esophagus and trachea do not stay within a single subdivision."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Map the boundaries and the four compartments of the mediastinum and identify a major structure contained in each. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to map the boundaries and the four compartments of the mediastinum and identify a major structure contained in each."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to map the boundaries and the four compartments of the mediastinum and identify a major structure contained in each. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: mediastinum. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, mediastinum, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w1-peritoneum-relationships","name":"Peritoneal relationships","can":"Classify abdominopelvic organs as intraperitoneal or retroperitoneal using SADPUCKER and define the mesentery, omenta, and mesocolon.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A surgeon notes that an organ is almost completely wrapped in visceral peritoneum and moves fairly freely within the peritoneal cavity. Which organ fits?\nA) An adrenal gland  |  B) The abdominal aorta\nC) The stomach  |  D) The right kidney\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The stomach","why":"An organ wrapped in visceral peritoneum and suspended within the peritoneal cavity is intraperitoneal. The stomach is intraperitoneal, held in place by folds of peritoneum but still able to move as it fills and empties. The other three choices all lie behind the peritoneum and are part of SAD PUCKER."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient with a leaking abdominal aorta reports flank and back pain rather than spreading abdominal pain. Where is the blood most likely collecting?\nA) In the retroperitoneum  |  B) In the pericardial cavity\nC) In a pleural cavity  |  D) In the peritoneal cavity\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) In the retroperitoneum","why":"The abdominal aorta is retroperitoneal, lying behind the peritoneum against the posterior wall, and it is part of the A in SAD PUCKER. Blood leaking from it collects in the retroperitoneum, near the back and flanks, instead of spreading through the peritoneal cavity. This is why knowing an organ's peritoneal position predicts how a bleed will present."},{"t":"mc","q":"In the course's version of SAD PUCKER, the letter D stands for which segments of the duodenum?\nA) The first and fourth segments  |  B) The second and third segments\nC) The first and second segments  |  D) The third and fourth segments\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The second and third segments","why":"The duodenum, the first part of the small intestine, curves around the head of the pancreas. In the course notes, D in SAD PUCKER stands for the second and third segments of the duodenum, which lie behind the peritoneum. The first segment, right after the stomach, is wrapped in peritoneum and stays more mobile."},{"t":"mc","q":"On a transverse section of the abdomen, the kidneys lie behind the parietal peritoneum, pressed against the posterior abdominal wall. How are they classified?\nA) Pelvic  |  B) Intraperitoneal\nC) Mediastinal  |  D) Retroperitoneal\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Retroperitoneal","why":"The retroperitoneum is the area behind the peritoneum but in front of the spine, and organs lying there are retroperitoneal. The kidneys sit in exactly this position, with peritoneum covering only their front surfaces. They are the K in SAD PUCKER."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which of these is a retroperitoneal structure?\nA) The stomach  |  B) The transverse colon\nC) The spleen  |  D) The ureters\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The ureters","why":"The ureters run from the kidneys down the posterior abdominal wall to the bladder, behind the peritoneum. They are the U in SAD PUCKER. The other three choices are intraperitoneal, wrapped in visceral peritoneum and suspended within the peritoneal cavity."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Classify abdominopelvic organs as intraperitoneal or retroperitoneal using SADPUCKER and define the mesentery, omenta, and mesocolon. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to classify abdominopelvic organs as intraperitoneal or retroperitoneal using SADPUCKER and define the mesentery, omenta, and mesocolon."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to classify abdominopelvic organs as intraperitoneal or retroperitoneal using SADPUCKER and define the mesentery, omenta, and mesocolon. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: peritoneal relationships. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, peritoneal relationships, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w1-abdominopelvic-map","name":"Abdominopelvic regions and quadrants","can":"Locate organs on the nine-region and four-quadrant grids and name the planes that draw each grid.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Why is the abdominopelvic area divided into four quadrants or nine regions rather than described simply as the belly?\nA) The zones mark where the abdominal muscles attach\nB) Each zone is lined by a different serous membrane\nC) Each zone contains exactly one organ\nD) It ties a symptom to the organs beneath that zone\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It ties a symptom to the organs beneath that zone","why":"Each quadrant or region has known organs beneath it, so placing pain in a specific zone points toward specific organs. Right lower quadrant pain, for example, raises suspicion of the appendix. Quadrants are the quick clinical shorthand, and the nine regions are the more precise version used when a location has to be described exactly."},{"t":"mc","q":"An older adult has pain in the left lower quadrant. Which condition does this location classically suggest?\nA) Sigmoid diverticulitis  |  B) Appendicitis\nC) Splenic rupture  |  D) Gallbladder inflammation\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Sigmoid diverticulitis","why":"The left lower quadrant holds the descending and sigmoid colon. Small pouches can form in the wall of the sigmoid colon, and when they become inflamed the condition is called diverticulitis, a classic cause of left lower quadrant pain in older adults. Knowing which organs lie in each quadrant narrows the likely cause of pain."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which abdominopelvic region lies directly below the umbilical region, in the lower center of the grid?\nA) The left lumbar region  |  B) The right iliac region\nC) The hypogastric (pubic) region  |  D) The epigastric region\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The hypogastric (pubic) region","why":"The hypogastric region, also called the pubic region, is the lower center box of the nine-region grid, directly below the umbilical region. The prefix hypo means below, so it is the region below the stomach area. Its key structure is the urinary bladder."},{"t":"mc","q":"In the four-quadrant scheme, which plane divides the abdomen into right and left?\nA) A transverse plane through the umbilicus  |  B) A frontal plane\nC) The median plane  |  D) A parasagittal plane\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The median plane","why":"Two planes draw the quadrants: the median, or midsagittal, plane and a transverse plane through the umbilicus. The median plane is vertical on the midline, so it splits the abdomen into right and left halves. The transverse plane through the navel then splits each half into upper and lower quadrants."},{"t":"mc","q":"A clinician places pain in the upper right region of the nine-region grid, over the gallbladder. Which region is this?\nA) The right hypochondriac region  |  B) The right iliac region\nC) The epigastric region  |  D) The right lumbar region\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The right hypochondriac region","why":"The right hypochondriac region is the upper right box of the grid, under the lower ribs on the right side. Hypochondriac means below the cartilage, referring to the costal cartilages of the ribs. Its key structures are the liver and the gallbladder."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Locate organs on the nine-region and four-quadrant grids and name the planes that draw each grid. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to locate organs on the nine-region and four-quadrant grids and name the planes that draw each grid."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to locate organs on the nine-region and four-quadrant grids and name the planes that draw each grid. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: abdominopelvic regions and quadrants. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, abdominopelvic regions and quadrants, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]}]},{"week":1,"system":"Cells & Tissues","comps":[{"id":"w1-generalized-cell","name":"Generalized cell regions","can":"Name the three basic regions of a generalized cell, the plasma membrane, cytoplasm, and nucleus, and state what each contains.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A cell has lost its nucleus and organelles entirely, freeing interior space. Which cell type shows this structural adaptation?\nA) The white blood cell  |  B) The red blood cell\nC) The skeletal muscle cell  |  D) The sperm cell\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The red blood cell","why":"A mature red blood cell pushes out its nucleus and loses its organelles as it develops, so it is described as anucleated. Losing these parts frees the space inside the cell for hemoglobin, the protein that carries oxygen. The trade-off is that the cell cannot repair or divide itself, so it lasts only a few months."},{"t":"mc","q":"A cell type changes its shape freely so it can squeeze between other cells to reach a site of infection. Which cell shows this structural adaptation?\nA) The red blood cell  |  B) The skeletal muscle cell\nC) The white blood cell  |  D) The nerve cell\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The white blood cell","why":"White blood cells must leave the bloodstream and travel through tissues to reach an injury or infection. They can change shape freely, which lets them squeeze through gaps between the cells of capillary walls and crawl through tissue. Most body cells stay in one place, so this ability to migrate is a special adaptation."},{"t":"mc","q":"Starting from the outside of a cell and moving inward, order these structures: glycocalyx, phospholipid bilayer, cytoplasm, nucleus.\nA) Glycocalyx, cytoplasm, phospholipid bilayer, nucleus\nB) Glycocalyx, phospholipid bilayer, cytoplasm, nucleus\nC) Cytoplasm, glycocalyx, phospholipid bilayer, nucleus\nD) Phospholipid bilayer, glycocalyx, cytoplasm, nucleus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Glycocalyx, phospholipid bilayer, cytoplasm, nucleus","why":"The glycocalyx is the sugar-rich coat on the outer surface of the cell, so it comes first. Next is the phospholipid bilayer of the plasma membrane, the boundary itself, then the cytoplasm, and finally the nucleus near the center. This order follows the three basic regions of the cell from the outside in."},{"t":"mc","q":"A red blood cell loses its nucleus and organelles as it matures. How does this structural change match its job?\nA) It allows the cell to contract forcefully\nB) It frees interior space for oxygen-carrying hemoglobin\nC) It increases the cell's surface area for absorption\nD) It lets the cell squeeze out between other cells\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It frees interior space for oxygen-carrying hemoglobin","why":"A red blood cell's job is to carry oxygen, and the protein that does this, hemoglobin, needs room. By losing its nucleus and organelles as it matures, the cell frees its interior to be filled with hemoglobin. This is a clear case of a cell's structure being shaped by its function."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which structure forms the outer boundary that encloses a cell and separates it from its surroundings?\nA) The glycocalyx  |  B) The cytoskeleton\nC) The plasma membrane  |  D) The nuclear envelope\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The plasma membrane","why":"The plasma membrane is a flexible, semipermeable boundary made mostly of lipids and proteins. It wraps the whole cell, separates the cell's contents from the fluid outside, and controls what passes in and out. Every other structure in the cell sits inside it, which is why it counts as one of the three basic regions of the cell."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Name the three basic regions of a generalized cell, the plasma membrane, cytoplasm, and nucleus, and state what each contains. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to name the three basic regions of a generalized cell, the plasma membrane, cytoplasm, and nucleus, and state what each contains."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to name the three basic regions of a generalized cell, the plasma membrane, cytoplasm, and nucleus, and state what each contains. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: generalized cell regions. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, generalized cell regions, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w1-plasma-membrane","name":"Plasma membrane structure","can":"Describe the plasma membrane components, distinguish integral from peripheral proteins, and identify the surface specializations microvilli, cilia, and flagellum.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A membrane protein is firmly embedded in the bilayer and spans it completely. How is this protein classified?\nA) As a glycolipid  |  B) As an integral protein\nC) As a peripheral protein  |  D) As cholesterol\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) As an integral protein","why":"Integral proteins are set firmly into the phospholipid bilayer, and most pass all the way through it from one side to the other. Because they cross the membrane, they can form channels, carriers, and receptors that connect the inside of the cell with the outside. Proteins that only rest on one surface are called peripheral proteins instead."},{"t":"mc","q":"A protein sits loosely attached to only the inner face of the membrane and is not embedded in the bilayer. Which type of membrane protein is it?\nA) A channel protein  |  B) An integral protein\nC) A transmembrane protein  |  D) A peripheral protein\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) A peripheral protein","why":"Peripheral proteins are attached loosely to the inner or outer face of the membrane, often bound to an integral protein, rather than being buried in the bilayer. Because they do not cross the membrane, they cannot form channels. Many act as enzymes or help connect the cytoskeleton to the membrane."},{"t":"mc","q":"A slide shows a cell surface covered with many short hair-like extensions. Which surface specialization is this?\nA) Cilia  |  B) Glycocalyx\nC) Microvilli  |  D) A flagellum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Cilia","why":"Cilia are short, hair-like projections, and a single cell can carry hundreds of them. Each has a core of microtubules in a 9 plus 2 arrangement, which lets cilia beat in coordinated waves. In the airways they sweep a layer of mucus and trapped particles up toward the throat."},{"t":"mc","q":"Where is cholesterol positioned in the plasma membrane?\nA) Lining the channels of the nuclear pores\nB) Studding the outer surface of the rough ER\nC) Forming the fuzzy coat on the outer surface\nD) Wedged among the phospholipids of the bilayer\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Wedged among the phospholipids of the bilayer","why":"Cholesterol molecules are tucked in between the phospholipids of the bilayer. From that position they stabilize the membrane and keep its fluidity steady as temperature changes, so the membrane does not become too stiff or too loose. They are part of the membrane fabric rather than a coat or a separate layer."},{"t":"mc","q":"The glycocalyx is built of which kinds of molecules?\nA) RNA and protein  |  B) Glycoproteins and glycolipids\nC) Actin and tubulin  |  D) Phospholipids only\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Glycoproteins and glycolipids","why":"The glycocalyx is the fuzzy coat on the outside of the plasma membrane, made of the sugar chains of glycoproteins and glycolipids. Its pattern of sugars differs between cell types, so it works like an identification tag. Cells use it to recognize one another, and the immune system uses it to tell self from non-self."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Describe the plasma membrane components, distinguish integral from peripheral proteins, and identify the surface specializations microvilli, cilia, and flagellum. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to describe the plasma membrane components, distinguish integral from peripheral proteins, and identify the surface specializations microvilli, cilia, and flagellum."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to describe the plasma membrane components, distinguish integral from peripheral proteins, and identify the surface specializations microvilli, cilia, and flagellum. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: plasma membrane structure. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, plasma membrane structure, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w1-nucleus","name":"Nucleus structure","can":"Identify the nuclear envelope, nuclear pores, nucleolus, nucleoplasm, chromatin, and chromosomes and state the role of each.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which structures are the protein-lined channels through the nuclear envelope that regulate traffic in both directions?\nA) Nuclear pores  |  B) Microvilli\nC) Vesicles  |  D) Gap junctions\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Nuclear pores","why":"Nuclear pores are openings through both layers of the nuclear envelope, each lined by a ring of proteins. They control traffic in both directions: messenger RNA and ribosomal subunits leave the nucleus, and proteins needed inside the nucleus enter. This lets the DNA stay protected inside while its instructions still reach the cytoplasm."},{"t":"mc","q":"A structure inside the nucleus appears tightly coiled and is visible only while the cell is dividing. What is it?\nA) A nuclear pore  |  B) The nucleolus\nC) A chromosome  |  D) Chromatin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A chromosome","why":"Before a cell divides, its loose chromatin coils tightly into compact, rod-shaped chromosomes. Coiling makes the DNA visible under the light microscope and lets it be divided cleanly between the two new cells. When the cell is not dividing, the same DNA relaxes back into chromatin."},{"t":"mc","q":"Messenger RNA must move out of the nucleus into the cytoplasm. Through which structure does it pass?\nA) The nucleolus  |  B) The nuclear pores\nC) Gap junctions  |  D) Transport vesicles\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The nuclear pores","why":"Messenger RNA is made in the nucleus but used by ribosomes in the cytoplasm, so it has to cross the nuclear envelope. It leaves through nuclear pores, the protein-lined channels that pass through both layers of the envelope. The pores control this traffic while keeping the DNA itself inside the nucleus."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which structure is the dense region inside the nucleus where ribosomal subunits are assembled?\nA) The nucleolus  |  B) The nucleoplasm\nC) Chromatin  |  D) The nuclear envelope\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The nucleolus","why":"The nucleolus is a dense, darkly staining region inside the nucleus with no membrane of its own. It is where ribosomal RNA is made and combined with proteins to assemble ribosomal subunits, which then leave through the nuclear pores. A cell that makes a lot of protein often has a large, obvious nucleolus."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the loose, threadlike form of DNA wound on proteins, present when the cell is not dividing, called?\nA) Nucleolus  |  B) Chromatin\nC) Chromosomes  |  D) Nucleoplasm\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Chromatin","why":"Chromatin is DNA wrapped around histone proteins and spread loosely through the nucleus as fine threads. This loose form lets the cell read its genes during normal activity. When the cell gets ready to divide, the chromatin coils tightly into compact chromosomes that are visible under the microscope."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the nuclear envelope, nuclear pores, nucleolus, nucleoplasm, chromatin, and chromosomes and state the role of each. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the nuclear envelope, nuclear pores, nucleolus, nucleoplasm, chromatin, and chromosomes and state the role of each."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the nuclear envelope, nuclear pores, nucleolus, nucleoplasm, chromatin, and chromosomes and state the role of each. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: nucleus structure. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, nucleus structure, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w1-organelles","name":"Organelle identification","can":"Identify the membranous and non-membranous organelles and state the structural job of each, including the endomembrane system path from rough ER to exocytosis.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Order these structures from where messenger RNA is made to where a finished protein leaves the cell: nuclear pore, rough ER, plasma membrane, nucleus.\nA) Rough ER, nuclear pore, nucleus, plasma membrane\nB) Nucleus, rough ER, nuclear pore, plasma membrane\nC) Nucleus, nuclear pore, rough ER, plasma membrane\nD) Nuclear pore, nucleus, rough ER, plasma membrane\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Nucleus, nuclear pore, rough ER, plasma membrane","why":"Messenger RNA is copied from DNA inside the nucleus. It leaves through a nuclear pore and reaches ribosomes on the rough ER, where the protein is built, and the finished protein is eventually released at the plasma membrane. The route runs from the center of the cell outward, which is a useful check for any sequence question like this."},{"t":"mc","q":"A pancreatic cell makes and exports large amounts of digestive enzyme protein. Which organelle would you expect to be especially abundant in it?\nA) Rough ER  |  B) Smooth ER\nC) Peroxisomes  |  D) Lysosomes\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Rough ER","why":"Proteins made for export are built by ribosomes on the rough ER, so the amount of rough ER sets how much protein a cell can produce. A pancreatic cell that ships out large amounts of digestive enzyme needs an extensive rough ER to keep up. The Golgi apparatus and secretory vesicles are also busy in such a cell, since they finish and release the product."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has a disorder in which mitochondria cannot make enough ATP. Which tissues are most likely to show problems first?\nA) Adipose and areolar tissue  |  B) Muscle and brain\nC) Epidermis and hair  |  D) Cartilage and tendon\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Muscle and brain","why":"Mitochondria supply most of the cell's ATP, so when they fail, the tissues that use the most energy are affected first. Skeletal and cardiac muscle, the brain, and the kidney have very high energy demands and are packed with mitochondria. That is why mitochondrial disorders often show up as muscle weakness and nervous system problems."},{"t":"mc","q":"A cell's Golgi apparatus stops working, but its rough ER still makes protein normally. Which problem would most directly follow?\nA) Ribosomes would stop building new proteins\nB) Proteins would not be sorted and packaged for delivery\nC) Messenger RNA would be trapped in the nucleus\nD) The cell could not make enough ATP\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Proteins would not be sorted and packaged for delivery","why":"The Golgi apparatus is the stop where proteins from the ER are modified, sorted, tagged, and packed into vesicles for their destinations. If it fails, proteins can still be made on the rough ER, but they cannot be finished and sent to the plasma membrane, to lysosomes, or out of the cell. The problem shows up at the shipping step, not the manufacturing step."},{"t":"mc","q":"A liver cell must detoxify drugs and make lipids. Which organelle would you expect to be especially well developed in it?\nA) Lysosomes  |  B) Smooth ER\nC) The Golgi apparatus  |  D) Rough ER\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Smooth ER","why":"Smooth ER is the organelle that makes lipids, detoxifies drugs, and stores calcium, and it has no ribosomes. A liver cell that clears drugs from the blood and makes lipids therefore carries a large amount of smooth ER. Matching the amount of each organelle to the cell's job is the same idea that explains why protein-exporting cells are full of rough ER."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the membranous and non-membranous organelles and state the structural job of each, including the endomembrane system path from rough ER to exocytosis. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the membranous and non-membranous organelles and state the structural job of each, including the endomembrane system path from rough ER to exocytosis."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the membranous and non-membranous organelles and state the structural job of each, including the endomembrane system path from rough ER to exocytosis. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: organelle identification. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, organelle identification, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w1-germ-layers","name":"Embryonic germ layers","can":"Name the three primary germ layers, ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm, and the tissues each one gives rise to.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Muscle, bone, cartilage, blood, and most connective tissue all develop from which germ layer?\nA) Endoderm  |  B) Mesoderm\nC) Ectoderm  |  D) Ectoderm and endoderm together\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Mesoderm","why":"The mesoderm is the middle germ layer. It forms muscle, bone, cartilage, blood, and most connective tissue, which together make up much of the body's bulk. Embryonic mesenchyme, the tissue that develops into all types of connective tissue, comes mainly from this layer."},{"t":"mc","q":"Nervous tissue and the epidermis of the skin both arise from which germ layer?\nA) Mesoderm  |  B) Both mesoderm and endoderm\nC) Ectoderm  |  D) Endoderm\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Ectoderm","why":"The ectoderm is the outermost of the three germ layers. It gives rise to the epidermis of the skin and to all nervous tissue, because the brain and spinal cord develop from a tube of ectoderm called the neural tube. This shared origin links the outer skin and the nervous system in development."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which chain correctly links a germ layer to a tissue it forms and then to a location of that tissue?\nA) Ectoderm forms nervous tissue, found in the brain\nB) Mesoderm forms the epidermis, found in the skin\nC) Endoderm forms nervous tissue, found in the brain\nD) Ectoderm forms bone, found in the skeleton\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Ectoderm forms nervous tissue, found in the brain","why":"The ectoderm, the outer germ layer, forms all nervous tissue, and the brain is made of nervous tissue, so this chain links correctly at every step. Checking each link separately is how to catch the errors in the other chains. The ectoderm also forms the epidermis, the mesoderm forms bone and most connective tissue, and the endoderm forms the linings of the gut and airways."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which germ layer forms the epithelial lining of the digestive and respiratory tracts?\nA) Mesoderm  |  B) Endoderm\nC) Mesenchyme  |  D) Ectoderm\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Endoderm","why":"The endoderm is the innermost of the three germ layers. It forms the epithelial lining of the digestive tract and the respiratory tract, which fits its position on the inside of the early embryo. The muscle and connective tissue around those linings come from the mesoderm."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which germ layer is the outer layer of the early embryo?\nA) The epidermis  |  B) Endoderm\nC) Mesoderm  |  D) Ectoderm\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Ectoderm","why":"The early embryo forms three germ layers, and the ectoderm is the outermost. It gives rise to the epidermis of the skin and to all nervous tissue, including the brain and spinal cord, which develop from the ectodermal neural tube. The mesoderm lies in the middle and the endoderm lies innermost."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Name the three primary germ layers, ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm, and the tissues each one gives rise to. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to name the three primary germ layers, ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm, and the tissues each one gives rise to."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to name the three primary germ layers, ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm, and the tissues each one gives rise to. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: embryonic germ layers. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, embryonic germ layers, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w1-epithelial-id","name":"Epithelial tissue identification","can":"Classify epithelial tissue by cell layers and cell shape and identify the basement membrane, apical specializations, and glandular epithelium on a slide.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A small cut that stays within the epidermis heals quickly and hurts noticeably. Which two structural features of epithelium explain this?\nA) Gap junctions and a rich blood supply\nB) Many dividing cells and a nerve supply\nC) An abundant matrix and a nerve supply\nD) A rich blood supply and many dividing cells\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Many dividing cells and a nerve supply","why":"Epithelium regenerates quickly because its cells divide often to replace those lost at the surface. It is also innervated, with nerve endings among its cells, so even a shallow cut is felt. It has no blood vessels of its own, which is why a cut that stays in the epidermis may not bleed."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a heavy smoker, part of the ciliated lining of the bronchi can be replaced by stratified squamous epithelium. What does the airway lose with this change?\nA) The cilia that sweep mucus out of the airway\nB) Its ability to stretch as the airway fills\nC) Its blood supply, since epithelium becomes vascular\nD) Its basement membrane anchoring the epithelium\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The cilia that sweep mucus out of the airway","why":"The normal bronchial lining is pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium, where goblet cells make mucus and cilia beat to carry trapped particles upward and out. Stratified squamous epithelium is tougher against irritation but has no cilia or goblet cells. So the airway gains protection at that spot but loses its cleaning action, and mucus and debris collect instead of being swept away."},{"t":"mc","q":"Epithelia are classified on two features. One is the shape of the surface cells. What is the other?\nA) The number of cell layers  |  B) The germ layer of origin\nC) The fiber type in the matrix  |  D) The presence of a duct\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The number of cell layers","why":"Epithelium is named using two features: the number of cell layers and the shape of the cells at the free surface. One layer is simple and more than one is stratified, and the surface cells are squamous (flat), cuboidal (cube-shaped), or columnar (tall). Putting the two together gives names such as simple squamous or stratified columnar."},{"t":"mc","q":"An epithelium classified as simple has how many cell layers?\nA) One  |  B) Many\nC) Three  |  D) Two\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) One","why":"A simple epithelium has a single layer of cells, with every cell resting on the basement membrane. A single layer keeps the path across the tissue short, so simple epithelia are built for diffusion, filtration, absorption, and secretion. Epithelia with more than one layer are called stratified and are built for protection."},{"t":"mc","q":"A gland releases its product through a duct onto a surface. Which gland is an example of this type?\nA) The thyroid gland  |  B) The pituitary gland\nC) A sweat gland  |  D) The adrenal gland\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A sweat gland","why":"Glands that release their product into a duct that carries it onto a surface or into a lumen are exocrine glands. Sweat glands are a clear example: sweat travels up a duct and out onto the skin surface. Salivary and sebaceous glands are other exocrine examples, while endocrine glands have no ducts and release hormones into the blood."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Classify epithelial tissue by cell layers and cell shape and identify the basement membrane, apical specializations, and glandular epithelium on a slide. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to classify epithelial tissue by cell layers and cell shape and identify the basement membrane, apical specializations, and glandular epithelium on a slide."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to classify epithelial tissue by cell layers and cell shape and identify the basement membrane, apical specializations, and glandular epithelium on a slide. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: epithelial tissue identification. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, epithelial tissue identification, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w1-cell-junctions","name":"Cell junctions","can":"Identify the five epithelial cell junctions, what each does, what it links to, and its major protein.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has a disorder in which the hemidesmosomes of the skin fail. Which separation is most likely?\nA) Cardiac muscle cells stop passing impulses to each other\nB) Fluid leaks between the cells of the bladder lining\nC) The epidermis lifts away from the basement membrane\nD) Neighboring epidermal cells pull apart from each other\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The epidermis lifts away from the basement membrane","why":"Hemidesmosomes rivet the basal cells of the epidermis to the basement membrane beneath them. When they fail, the whole epidermis can lift off the basement membrane, producing a deep blister. Loss of desmosomes causes a different blister, because there the cells separate from one another instead."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which junctions seal neighboring epithelial cells so substances cannot leak between them?\nA) Desmosomes  |  B) Tight junctions\nC) Basement membranes  |  D) Gap junctions\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Tight junctions","why":"Tight junctions are made of transmembrane proteins (claudins and occludins) that fuse the membranes of neighboring cells together, sealing the space between them. This stops substances from leaking between cells, so anything crossing the epithelium has to pass through the cells themselves. They are prominent in the linings of the stomach, intestines, and urinary bladder, and in the blood-brain barrier."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which junctions are channels that let neighboring cells pass ions and small molecules directly to each other?\nA) Gap junctions  |  B) Tight junctions\nC) Desmosomes  |  D) Microvilli\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Gap junctions","why":"Gap junctions are clusters of protein channels that connect the cytoplasm of neighboring cells. Ions and small molecules pass directly through them, which lets cells share nutrients and lets electrical signals spread from cell to cell. In cardiac muscle they help the heart contract in a coordinated way, and they are found in many other tissues too."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which cell junction uses cadherins linked to intermediate filaments to bind neighboring cells firmly, and is abundant in the outer epidermis and cardiac muscle?\nA) The gap junction  |  B) The hemidesmosome\nC) The desmosome  |  D) The tight junction\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The desmosome","why":"A desmosome is a spot of strong adhesion between two neighboring cells. Cadherin proteins hold the two cells together, and on the inside they connect to intermediate filaments, which resist pulling. That is why desmosomes are abundant in tissues under constant tension, such as the epidermis and cardiac muscle."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which cell junction is built from connexin proteins that form channels between neighboring cells?\nA) The gap junction  |  B) The tight junction\nC) The desmosome  |  D) The adherens junction\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The gap junction","why":"Gap junctions are clusters of channels made from connexin proteins that link the cytoplasm of two neighboring cells. Small ions and molecules pass directly through them, which lets cells share nutrients and lets electrical impulses spread from cell to cell. They are found in places such as cardiac muscle, the GI tract, the uterus, and some neurons."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the five epithelial cell junctions, what each does, what it links to, and its major protein. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the five epithelial cell junctions, what each does, what it links to, and its major protein."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the five epithelial cell junctions, what each does, what it links to, and its major protein. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: cell junctions. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, cell junctions, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]}]},{"week":2,"system":"Cells & Tissues","comps":[{"id":"w1-connective-id","name":"Connective tissue identification","can":"Describe connective tissue as cells, fibers, and ground substance and recognize the connective tissue proper subtypes, cartilage, bone, and blood on a slide.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A torn knee meniscus barely repairs itself. Which structural feature of cartilage explains this slow healing?\nA) Cartilage has a rich blood supply\nB) Cartilage is avascular, with no blood vessels\nC) Cartilage contains only elastic fibers\nD) Cartilage is made of many cell layers\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Cartilage is avascular, with no blood vessels","why":"Cartilage, including the fibrocartilage of the knee meniscus, has no blood vessels of its own. Repair depends on blood bringing oxygen, nutrients, and repair cells, so in cartilage these must slowly diffuse in through the matrix instead. That is why a torn meniscus heals poorly, while a skin wound closes in days."},{"t":"mc","q":"A tendon tears when it is twisted sideways rather than pulled along its length. Which structural property of dense regular connective tissue explains this?\nA) It is avascular like cartilage\nB) Its collagen runs in all directions\nC) It contains mostly elastic fibers\nD) Its collagen runs parallel in one direction\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Its collagen runs parallel in one direction","why":"In dense regular connective tissue, the collagen bundles all run parallel along the length of the tendon. That makes the tendon very strong when pulled in that one direction but much weaker against forces from other angles, such as twisting. Dense irregular tissue, with fibers in every direction, trades some of that one-way strength for resistance from all sides."},{"t":"mc","q":"After an ankle sprain, the area under the skin becomes puffy with excess fluid (edema). Which connective tissue's loose, fluid-holding matrix soaks up most of this fluid?\nA) Dense regular connective tissue  |  B) Compact bone\nC) Hyaline cartilage  |  D) Areolar connective tissue\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Areolar connective tissue","why":"Areolar connective tissue has loosely arranged fibers and a large amount of watery ground substance, so it acts as a reservoir for tissue fluid. When an area is injured or inflamed, extra fluid leaks into this loose tissue, and it swells like a sponge. That swelling is called edema."},{"t":"mc","q":"The extracellular matrix of a connective tissue is made of which two components together?\nA) Cells and fibers  |  B) Cells and ground substance\nC) Fibers and ground substance  |  D) Fibers and a basement membrane\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Fibers and ground substance","why":"The extracellular matrix is the nonliving material between connective tissue cells, made of protein fibers plus ground substance. The fibers give strength and stretch, and the ground substance fills the spaces and holds tissue fluid. In most connective tissues the matrix takes up far more of the volume than the cells do."},{"t":"mc","q":"Dense regular connective tissue is built of parallel collagen and forms which structures?\nA) The walls of large arteries  |  B) Tendons and ligaments\nC) Lymph nodes and the spleen  |  D) The dermis and organ capsules\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Tendons and ligaments","why":"Dense regular connective tissue is packed with collagen fibers that all run parallel, with rows of fibroblasts squeezed between them. This arrangement makes it extremely strong when pulled along the line of the fibers, which is exactly what tendons and ligaments need. Tendons connect muscle to bone, and ligaments connect bone to bone."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Describe connective tissue as cells, fibers, and ground substance and recognize the connective tissue proper subtypes, cartilage, bone, and blood on a slide. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to describe connective tissue as cells, fibers, and ground substance and recognize the connective tissue proper subtypes, cartilage, bone, and blood on a slide."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to describe connective tissue as cells, fibers, and ground substance and recognize the connective tissue proper subtypes, cartilage, bone, and blood on a slide. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: connective tissue identification. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, connective tissue identification, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]}]},{"week":8,"system":"Cells & Tissues","comps":[{"id":"w1-muscle-tissue-id","name":"Muscle tissue identification","can":"Recognize the three muscle tissue types, skeletal, cardiac, and smooth, by striations, cell shape, and number of nuclei.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A muscle slide shows branching striated cells with one nucleus each, joined end to end by dark crossbands. Which muscle type is it?\nA) Skeletal muscle  |  B) Smooth muscle\nC) Cardiac muscle  |  D) Dense regular connective tissue\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Cardiac muscle","why":"Branching, striated cells with a single central nucleus, joined end to end by dark lines called intercalated discs, identify cardiac muscle. The discs are where neighboring cells connect, and they contain desmosomes and gap junctions. Cardiac muscle is found only in the heart wall and contracts involuntarily."},{"t":"mc","q":"A muscle slide from the wall of a hollow organ shows spindle-shaped cells, each with a single nucleus and no striations. Which muscle type is it?\nA) Skeletal muscle  |  B) Smooth muscle\nC) Cardiac muscle  |  D) Dense regular connective tissue\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Smooth muscle","why":"Spindle-shaped cells, thick in the middle and tapered at both ends, each with one central nucleus and no striations, identify smooth muscle. It forms the walls of hollow organs such as the intestines, bladder, and blood vessels, where it contracts involuntarily to move contents along. The lack of striations is what gives it the name smooth."},{"t":"mc","q":"A cross section of muscle shows large, rounded-to-angular fiber profiles, each with several nuclei at its edge and none in its center. Which tissue is it?\nA) Skeletal muscle  |  B) Dense regular connective tissue\nC) Smooth muscle  |  D) Cardiac muscle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Skeletal muscle","why":"In a cross section you see each muscle fiber cut across like a slice of a cylinder. Skeletal muscle fibers are large and have many nuclei pushed to the periphery, so each profile shows nuclei around its edge and none in the middle. Cardiac and smooth muscle cells would show a single nucleus near the center when the cut passes through it."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which feature do skeletal and cardiac muscle share that smooth muscle does not?\nA) Spindle-shaped cells  |  B) Intercalated discs\nC) Striations  |  D) Voluntary control\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Striations","why":"Skeletal and cardiac muscle both show striations, the alternating light and dark bands across the cells, because their contractile proteins are lined up in regular repeating units. Smooth muscle has the same kinds of proteins but not in that orderly arrangement, so it looks smooth. That is how it got its name."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which tissue has long, cylindrical, striated cells with many nuclei?\nA) Smooth muscle  |  B) Skeletal muscle\nC) Cardiac muscle  |  D) Dense regular connective tissue\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Skeletal muscle","why":"Skeletal muscle cells are long, cylindrical, and contain many nuclei, and they show striations (stripes) under the microscope. Each cell forms when many embryonic cells fuse together, which is why it has so many nuclei. Skeletal muscle is attached to bones and produces voluntary movement."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Recognize the three muscle tissue types, skeletal, cardiac, and smooth, by striations, cell shape, and number of nuclei. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to recognize the three muscle tissue types, skeletal, cardiac, and smooth, by striations, cell shape, and number of nuclei."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to recognize the three muscle tissue types, skeletal, cardiac, and smooth, by striations, cell shape, and number of nuclei. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: muscle tissue identification. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, muscle tissue identification, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]}]},{"week":16,"system":"Cells & Tissues","comps":[{"id":"w1-nervous-tissue-id","name":"Nervous tissue identification","can":"Identify neurons and neuroglia in nervous tissue and the parts of a multipolar neuron.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A nervous tissue slide shows a large cell body with a prominent nucleus and several long processes branching from it, surrounded by many small nuclei. What is the large cell?\nA) A fibroblast  |  B) A neuroglial cell\nC) A multipolar neuron  |  D) A smooth muscle cell\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A multipolar neuron","why":"A large cell body with a prominent nucleus and several long processes extending from it is a multipolar neuron, the most common type of neuron. Its processes are many dendrites and a single axon. The many small nuclei scattered around it belong to neuroglia, the smaller supporting cells that far outnumber neurons."},{"t":"mc","q":"On a nervous tissue slide, one large neuron with long processes is surrounded by many small, dark nuclei that have no visible processes. What are the small nuclei?\nA) Synaptic bulbs  |  B) Dendrites\nC) Neuroglia  |  D) Smooth muscle cells\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Neuroglia","why":"Neuroglia are the supporting cells of nervous tissue. They are much smaller and far more numerous than neurons, so on a slide they appear as many small, dark nuclei scattered around the large neuron cell bodies. Telling neurons from neuroglia under the microscope is a listed lab skill."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which statement correctly compares neurons with neuroglia?\nA) Neuroglia carry impulses, while neurons only support and protect\nB) Neurons and neuroglia are the same size and both carry impulses\nC) Neuroglia are smaller and more numerous, and only neuroglia can divide\nD) Neurons are smaller and more numerous, and only neurons can divide\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Neuroglia are smaller and more numerous, and only neuroglia can divide","why":"Neurons are the excitable cells that receive and transmit nerve impulses, and mature neurons cannot divide. Neuroglia are the support cells that nourish, support, and protect neurons. They are smaller and far more numerous, and they keep the ability to divide."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which two cell types make up nervous tissue?\nA) Neuroglia and chondrocytes  |  B) Neurons and adipocytes\nC) Neurons and fibroblasts  |  D) Neurons and neuroglia\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Neurons and neuroglia","why":"Nervous tissue has two kinds of cells. Neurons are the signaling cells that carry electrical impulses, with a cell body and long processes. Neuroglia, or glial cells, are smaller supporting cells that protect, nourish, and insulate the neurons, and they far outnumber them."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which part of a neuron receives incoming signals from other cells?\nA) The axon  |  B) The axon hillock\nC) The synaptic bulb  |  D) The dendrites\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The dendrites","why":"Dendrites are the short, branched, tree-like processes that extend from the cell body. Their job is to receive incoming signals and carry them toward the cell body. A multipolar neuron has many dendrites but only one axon, which is how you tell them apart on a slide."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify neurons and neuroglia in nervous tissue and the parts of a multipolar neuron. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify neurons and neuroglia in nervous tissue and the parts of a multipolar neuron."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify neurons and neuroglia in nervous tissue and the parts of a multipolar neuron. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: nervous tissue identification. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, nervous tissue identification, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]}]},{"week":3,"system":"Cells & Tissues","comps":[{"id":"w1-body-membranes","name":"Body membranes","can":"Distinguish the cutaneous, mucous, serous, and synovial membranes by composition and location.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has an infection of the inner lining of the urinary bladder. Which type of body membrane is infected?\nA) A synovial membrane  |  B) The cutaneous membrane\nC) A serous membrane  |  D) A mucous membrane\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) A mucous membrane","why":"The urinary tract opens to the outside of the body through the urethra, so the inner lining of the bladder is a mucous membrane. Its epithelium is transitional epithelium, resting on a lamina propria of connective tissue. Infections that enter from the outside travel up along this mucous lining."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which body membrane is made of a keratinized epithelium over dense connective tissue and is a dry membrane?\nA) The cutaneous membrane  |  B) The mucous membrane\nC) The serous membrane  |  D) The synovial membrane\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The cutaneous membrane","why":"The cutaneous membrane is the skin. Its surface layer is the epidermis, a keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, and beneath it is the dermis, made of dense connective tissue. It is the only dry membrane in the body, because keratin makes its surface tough and water-resistant."},{"t":"mc","q":"A body membrane lines the airways, is kept moist, and is an epithelium over loose connective tissue. Which membrane is it?\nA) A serous membrane  |  B) A synovial membrane\nC) A mucous membrane  |  D) A cutaneous membrane\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A mucous membrane","why":"A mucous membrane lines body cavities and passages that open to the outside, such as the airways and the digestive, urinary, and reproductive tracts. It is made of an epithelium resting on loose connective tissue called the lamina propria, and it is kept moist by mucus. That moist surface traps particles and protects the lining."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which statement correctly compares serous and mucous membranes?\nA) Serous membranes line closed cavities, and mucous membranes line open tracts\nB) Mucous membranes line closed cavities, and serous membranes line open tracts\nC) Both line joint cavities and secrete synovial fluid\nD) Both are dry membranes covered by keratinized epithelium\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Serous membranes line closed cavities, and mucous membranes line open tracts","why":"The deciding feature is whether the space opens to the outside. Serous membranes, such as the pleura, pericardium, and peritoneum, line closed cavities and cover the organs inside them. Mucous membranes line the tracts that do open to the exterior, such as the digestive and respiratory tracts. Both are moist membranes."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which body membrane lines joint cavities and is made of connective tissue only, with no epithelium?\nA) The serous membrane  |  B) The cutaneous membrane\nC) The synovial membrane  |  D) The mucous membrane\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The synovial membrane","why":"The synovial membrane lines the cavities of synovial joints and is the only body membrane made entirely of connective tissue, with no epithelium. It secretes synovial fluid, which lubricates the joint. The other three membranes, mucous, serous, and cutaneous, each pair an epithelium with an underlying layer of connective tissue."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Distinguish the cutaneous, mucous, serous, and synovial membranes by composition and location. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish the cutaneous, mucous, serous, and synovial membranes by composition and location."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to distinguish the cutaneous, mucous, serous, and synovial membranes by composition and location. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: body membranes. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, body membranes, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]}]},{"week":3,"system":"Integumentary","comps":[{"id":"w1-skin-layers","name":"Skin layers and hypodermis","can":"Name the epidermis and dermis and the hypodermis beneath them and identify the tissue that composes each.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"An intradermal injection, such as a tuberculosis skin test, places a small amount of fluid in which layer?\nA) The dermis  |  B) The skeletal muscle beneath the hypodermis\nC) The stratum corneum  |  D) The hypodermis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The dermis","why":"Intradermal means within the dermis, so the needle tip sits just under the epidermis but above the hypodermis. Fluid placed there raises a small bump on the skin surface. The word itself tells you the layer: intra means within."},{"t":"mc","q":"A needle is pushed from the skin surface straight down to a muscle. In what order does it pass through the layers?\nA) Dermis, epidermis, hypodermis, then muscle\nB) Epidermis, hypodermis, dermis, then muscle\nC) Hypodermis, epidermis, dermis, then muscle\nD) Epidermis, dermis, hypodermis, then muscle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Epidermis, dermis, hypodermis, then muscle","why":"From the surface inward, the needle crosses the epidermis, then the dermis, then the hypodermis, and finally reaches the muscle beneath the deep fascia. The epidermis and dermis make up the skin, and the hypodermis is the fatty layer that anchors the skin to the muscle. This same order explains injection routes: intradermal stops in the dermis, subcutaneous in the hypodermis, and intramuscular in the muscle."},{"t":"mc","q":"A nurse must place an injection in the hypodermis. Which injection route reaches that layer?\nA) An intradermal injection  |  B) An intravenous injection\nC) An intramuscular injection  |  D) A subcutaneous injection\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) A subcutaneous injection","why":"Subcutaneous means beneath the skin, and the hypodermis is the subcutaneous layer, so a subcutaneous injection places medication in the fatty tissue under the dermis. Insulin is a common example. The route names follow the layers from shallow to deep: intradermal, subcutaneous, then intramuscular."},{"t":"mc","q":"A shallow paper cut stays entirely within the epidermis. What would you expect to happen?\nA) It does not bleed, because the epidermis has no blood vessels\nB) It bleeds slowly, because the stratum lucidum holds small veins\nC) It bleeds, because arteries run through the stratum basale\nD) It bleeds heavily, because capillary loops lie in the stratum corneum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It does not bleed, because the epidermis has no blood vessels","why":"The epidermis is avascular, meaning it has no blood vessels of its own. A cut that stays within it does not reach any vessels, so it does not bleed. Bleeding begins only when a cut reaches the dermis, where the capillary loops of the dermal papillae and deeper vessels are found."},{"t":"mc","q":"A burn blisters and reaches into the dermis but does not destroy its full thickness. How is this burn classified?\nA) A partial-thickness (second-degree) burn  |  B) A fourth-degree burn\nC) A full-thickness (third-degree) burn  |  D) A superficial (first-degree) burn\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A partial-thickness (second-degree) burn","why":"Burn classes follow the skin layers. A partial-thickness burn reaches into the dermis and blisters because the junction between dermis and epidermis fails. It can still heal on its own because surviving hair follicles and sweat glands, lined with epidermal cells, lie deep in the dermis."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Name the epidermis and dermis and the hypodermis beneath them and identify the tissue that composes each. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to name the epidermis and dermis and the hypodermis beneath them and identify the tissue that composes each."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to name the epidermis and dermis and the hypodermis beneath them and identify the tissue that composes each. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: skin layers and hypodermis. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, skin layers and hypodermis, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w1-epidermal-strata","name":"Epidermal strata and cells","can":"List the five epidermal strata in order deep to superficial and identify the keratinocytes, melanocytes, tactile cells, and dendritic cells.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A skin specimen lacks a stratum lucidum, has a thin stratum corneum, and contains many hair follicles. From which site was it most likely taken?\nA) The posterior forearm  |  B) The sole of the foot\nC) The palm of the hand  |  D) The pad of a fingertip\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The posterior forearm","why":"Skin with no stratum lucidum, a thin corneum, and hair follicles is thin skin, which covers every part of the body except the palms, soles, and palmar surfaces of the digits. The posterior forearm is ordinary hairy skin, so it fits. Thick skin has a lucidum and a thick corneum but no hair follicles."},{"t":"mc","q":"A keratinocyte is born in the deepest stratum and is pushed toward the surface. Which stratum does it reach last?\nA) The stratum granulosum  |  B) The stratum spinosum\nC) The stratum lucidum  |  D) The stratum corneum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The stratum corneum","why":"Keratinocytes are made in the stratum basale and are pushed upward as new cells form beneath them. As they rise they fill with keratin and die, and they end up in the stratum corneum at the surface, where they are eventually shed. This is true in both thick and thin skin, because the corneum is always the outermost stratum."},{"t":"mc","q":"Looking at a slide of the epidermis, you see a single row of cube-shaped, actively dividing cells resting on the basement membrane. Which stratum is this?\nA) The stratum spinosum  |  B) The stratum corneum\nC) The stratum granulosum  |  D) The stratum basale\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The stratum basale","why":"The stratum basale is a single row of cells sitting on the basement membrane, the thin layer that separates the epidermis from the dermis. These cells divide to make new keratinocytes, and the layer also holds melanocytes and tactile cells. On a slide, look for the one-cell-thick row right at the border with the dermis."},{"t":"mc","q":"In thick skin, in what order does a keratinocyte pass through the strata, from where it is born to the surface?\nA) Basale, granulosum, spinosum, lucidum, corneum\nB) Corneum, lucidum, granulosum, spinosum, basale\nC) Basale, spinosum, granulosum, lucidum, corneum\nD) Spinosum, basale, granulosum, corneum, lucidum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Basale, spinosum, granulosum, lucidum, corneum","why":"A keratinocyte is born in the stratum basale, then moves through the spinosum, the granulosum, the lucidum, and finally the corneum, where it is shed. The mnemonic Baby Screams Grandpa Loves Coffee gives this deep-to-superficial order. In thin skin the sequence is the same except that the lucidum is missing."},{"t":"mc","q":"In thin skin, which stratum lies directly beneath the stratum corneum?\nA) The stratum granulosum  |  B) The stratum basale\nC) The stratum lucidum  |  D) The stratum spinosum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The stratum granulosum","why":"Thin skin has four strata: basale, spinosum, granulosum, and corneum. Because the stratum lucidum is essentially missing in thin skin, the granulosum lies directly beneath the corneum. In thick skin the lucidum would slip in between those two layers."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: List the five epidermal strata in order deep to superficial and identify the keratinocytes, melanocytes, tactile cells, and dendritic cells. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to list the five epidermal strata in order deep to superficial and identify the keratinocytes, melanocytes, tactile cells, and dendritic cells."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to list the five epidermal strata in order deep to superficial and identify the keratinocytes, melanocytes, tactile cells, and dendritic cells. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: epidermal strata and cells. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, epidermal strata and cells, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w1-dermis-layers","name":"Dermis layers","can":"Distinguish the papillary and reticular layers of the dermis by tissue and identify the structures and sensory corpuscles each contains.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A full-thickness burn destroys the epidermis and dermis, and the center of the burn is often painless. Why?\nA) The skin regrows too quickly for pain to register\nB) The hypodermis blocks pain signals from the dermis\nC) The epidermis never had any sensory endings\nD) The nerve endings in the dermis have been destroyed\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The nerve endings in the dermis have been destroyed","why":"Most of the skin's sensory receptors, including free nerve endings that detect pain and temperature, lie in the dermis. A full-thickness burn destroys the dermis, so those receptors are destroyed too, and the center of the burn may feel numb. The less damaged edges, where nerve endings survive, are usually very painful."},{"t":"mc","q":"When a dehydrated patient's skin is pinched, it stays tented instead of springing back. Which explanation fits?\nA) The hypodermis has gained extra fat that holds the fold\nB) The dermis has lost tissue fluid, so it is less plump and recoils poorly\nC) The melanocytes have stopped making pigment\nD) The stratum corneum has dried out and locks the skin in place\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The dermis has lost tissue fluid, so it is less plump and recoils poorly","why":"The dermis is a connective tissue with collagen and elastic fibers that let skin stretch and recoil. When the body is well hydrated, the dermis is full of tissue fluid and pinched skin springs back quickly. In dehydration, the dermis loses fluid, so a pinched fold returns slowly or stays tented."},{"t":"mc","q":"On the fingertips, dermal papillae push the epidermis up into epidermal ridges that form fingerprints. Besides forming fingerprints, what do these ridges do?\nA) They anchor hairs that sense light touch\nB) They secrete sweat that cools the hand\nC) They increase grip and touch sensitivity\nD) They store fat that cushions the fingertip\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) They increase grip and touch sensitivity","why":"Epidermal ridges increase the surface area where dermis meets epidermis, which raises the number of touch corpuscles and makes the fingertips more sensitive. The ridges also increase friction, which improves grip. They form only where dermal papillae are well developed and lined up in rows, as on the palms, soles, and fingertips."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which order lists the superficial skin layer, the superficial dermal layer, and the deep dermal layer, from superficial to deep?\nA) Dermis, papillary layer, reticular layer\nB) Epidermis, reticular layer, hypodermis\nC) Epidermis, papillary layer, reticular layer\nD) Epidermis, reticular layer, papillary layer\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Epidermis, papillary layer, reticular layer","why":"The epidermis is the surface layer of the skin. Beneath it, the dermis has a thin superficial papillary layer of areolar connective tissue and a thick deep reticular layer of dense irregular connective tissue. The papilla in papillary refers to the dermal papillae that push up into the epidermis, a reminder that this layer touches the epidermis."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which dermal layer gives the skin most of its strength and its resistance to stretching?\nA) The hypodermis  |  B) The reticular layer\nC) The stratum spinosum  |  D) The papillary layer\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The reticular layer","why":"The reticular layer makes up about four fifths of the dermis and is built of thick bundles of collagen in a net-like arrangement. That collagen gives the skin its strength, while its elastic fibers allow stretch and recoil. When this layer is overstretched and torn, as in pregnancy, the result is visible stretch marks."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Distinguish the papillary and reticular layers of the dermis by tissue and identify the structures and sensory corpuscles each contains. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish the papillary and reticular layers of the dermis by tissue and identify the structures and sensory corpuscles each contains."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to distinguish the papillary and reticular layers of the dermis by tissue and identify the structures and sensory corpuscles each contains. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: dermis layers. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, dermis layers, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w1-skin-accessory","name":"Accessory structures of the skin","can":"Identify the accessory structures of the skin including the parts of a hair and follicle, the parts of a nail, and the five gland types and their secretion modes.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"An eccrine sweat gland and a sebaceous gland release their products in different ways. Which pairing is correct?\nA) Both glands are merocrine\nB) The eccrine gland is merocrine, and the sebaceous gland is holocrine\nC) Both glands are holocrine\nD) The eccrine gland is holocrine, and the sebaceous gland is merocrine\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The eccrine gland is merocrine, and the sebaceous gland is holocrine","why":"Eccrine sweat glands release watery sweat by exocytosis, leaving the secretory cells intact, which is merocrine secretion. Sebaceous glands fill whole cells with sebum and then rupture them, which is holocrine secretion. The two glands sit side by side in the dermis but use opposite strategies."},{"t":"mc","q":"Ceruminous glands and mammary glands are both described as modified versions of which gland type?\nA) Salivary glands  |  B) Sweat glands\nC) Sebaceous glands  |  D) Tarsal glands\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Sweat glands","why":"Ceruminous glands of the ear canal and mammary glands of the breast are both modified sweat glands. Each keeps the basic plan of a sweat gland, a secretory portion with a duct, but makes a specialized product: cerumen in the ear and milk in the breast. Recognizing them as sweat gland relatives helps you group the skin glands into oil glands and sweat glands."},{"t":"mc","q":"Hair follicles, nails, and skin glands all develop from which layer of the skin, even though most of them reach down into the dermis?\nA) The papillary layer  |  B) The hypodermis\nC) The epidermis  |  D) The dermis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The epidermis","why":"The accessory structures of the skin form as downgrowths of the epidermis. A hair follicle, for example, has an external root sheath that is a downward continuation of the epidermis, and the hair matrix arises from the stratum basale. Because follicles and glands are lined with epidermal cells living deep in the dermis, they can help regrow the epidermis after a partial-thickness burn."},{"t":"mc","q":"A section of axillary skin shows sweat glands whose ducts open into hair follicles rather than onto the skin surface. Which gland type is this?\nA) Ceruminous glands  |  B) Eccrine sweat glands\nC) Apocrine sweat glands  |  D) Sebaceous glands\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Apocrine sweat glands","why":"Apocrine sweat glands are found in the skin of the axillae, groin, areolae, and bearded regions of the face, and their ducts open into hair follicles. Their sweat contains the same components as eccrine sweat plus lipids and proteins. Eccrine glands, by contrast, open directly onto the skin surface."},{"t":"mc","q":"A specimen shows a plate of hard keratin over the dorsal tip of a finger, with dividing cells hidden under the skin fold at its proximal end. Which pair names the structure and its growth region?\nA) The nail and the hyponychium  |  B) The nail and the lunula\nC) The nail and the nail matrix  |  D) The hair and the hair bulb\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The nail and the nail matrix","why":"A plate of hard keratin over the dorsal fingertip is a nail. Its growth region is the nail matrix, epithelium proximal to the nail root, tucked under the skin fold, where dividing cells produce new nail. The new cells push the nail body forward toward the free edge."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the accessory structures of the skin including the parts of a hair and follicle, the parts of a nail, and the five gland types and their secretion modes. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the accessory structures of the skin including the parts of a hair and follicle, the parts of a nail, and the five gland types and their secretion modes."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the accessory structures of the skin including the parts of a hair and follicle, the parts of a nail, and the five gland types and their secretion modes. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: accessory structures of the skin. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, accessory structures of the skin, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]}]},{"week":4,"system":"Skeletal","comps":[{"id":"w2-cartilage-types","name":"Cartilage types and structure","can":"Identify the three cartilage types by fiber content and location and label chondrocytes, lacunae, matrix, and perichondrium.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A slide shows chondrocytes in lacunae and a tough matrix crowded with thick collagen bundles. No perichondrium wraps the edge of the tissue. Which structure could this sample have come from?\nA) A knee meniscus  |  B) Articular cartilage\nC) The epiglottis  |  D) A tracheal ring\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A knee meniscus.","why":"Work it in two steps. Thick bundles of collagen mark fibrocartilage, and fibrocartilage is the type with no perichondrium on your lab list. Fibrocartilage is found in the intervertebral discs, the knee menisci, and the pubic symphysis, so the meniscus is the only structure here that fits."},{"t":"mc","q":"A cartilage slide from an intervertebral disc shows a tough matrix dominated by thick collagen bundles. Which cartilage type is it?\nA) Fibrocartilage  |  B) Hyaline cartilage\nC) Dense regular connective tissue  |  D) Elastic cartilage\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Fibrocartilage","why":"A cartilage matrix dominated by thick bundles of collagen identifies fibrocartilage. Those collagen bundles let it resist compression and absorb shock, which is why it is found in intervertebral discs, the pubic symphysis, and the menisci of the knee. Its chondrocytes often sit in rows between the collagen bundles."},{"t":"mc","q":"A cartilage slide from the external ear shows a matrix packed with elastic fibers. Which tissue is it?\nA) Hyaline cartilage  |  B) Elastic cartilage\nC) Fibrocartilage  |  D) Elastic connective tissue\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Elastic cartilage","why":"Elastic cartilage has chondrocytes in lacunae like other cartilage, but its matrix is packed with dark-staining elastic fibers. These fibers let it bend and spring back into shape, which is why it forms the external ear (pinna) and the epiglottis. Hyaline cartilage would be too stiff for these structures."},{"t":"mc","q":"A tissue has cells in lacunae, a firm matrix with fine collagen, no blood vessels, and covers the ends of bones at a joint. Which tissue is it?\nA) Fibrocartilage  |  B) Hyaline cartilage\nC) Dense regular connective tissue  |  D) Compact bone\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Hyaline cartilage","why":"Chondrocytes in lacunae and an avascular matrix mark the tissue as cartilage. A firm matrix with fine collagen fibers points to hyaline cartilage, and its location covering the ends of bones, as articular cartilage, confirms it. Hyaline cartilage gives a smooth surface for joint movement."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which type of cartilage forms the costal cartilages that connect the ribs to the sternum?\nA) Elastic cartilage  |  B) Dense regular connective tissue\nC) Hyaline cartilage  |  D) Fibrocartilage\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Hyaline cartilage","why":"The costal cartilages are made of hyaline cartilage, the most common type, with fine collagen fibers in a glassy matrix. Hyaline cartilage gives firm but flexible support, which lets the rib cage move slightly during breathing. Hyaline cartilage also covers the ends of long bones and forms the nose, trachea, and larynx."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the three cartilage types by fiber content and location and label chondrocytes, lacunae, matrix, and perichondrium. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the three cartilage types by fiber content and location and label chondrocytes, lacunae, matrix, and perichondrium."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the three cartilage types by fiber content and location and label chondrocytes, lacunae, matrix, and perichondrium. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: cartilage types and structure. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, cartilage types and structure, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w2-cartilage-growth","name":"Cartilage growth modes","can":"Distinguish appositional from interstitial cartilage growth by where each begins and the direction of expansion.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Fibrocartilage has no perichondrium. Using that fact, which mode of growth would a knee meniscus be unable to use?\nA) Interstitial growth, which starts from the perichondrium\nB) Interstitial growth, since a meniscus has no chondrocytes\nC) Neither mode, since fibrocartilage cannot grow at all\nD) Appositional growth, which starts from the perichondrium\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Appositional growth, which starts from the perichondrium.","why":"First recall that the knee menisci are fibrocartilage, which has no perichondrium. Then recall where each growth mode begins. Appositional growth starts from the perichondrium, so a meniscus cannot use it, but it still has chondrocytes in its matrix, so interstitial growth remains possible."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which type of cartilage growth adds new cartilage at the outer surface, from the perichondrium?\nA) Interstitial growth  |  B) Endochondral ossification\nC) Appositional growth  |  D) Intramembranous ossification\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Appositional growth","why":"Cartilage grows in two directions. In appositional growth, cells from the perichondrium add new cartilage at the outer surface. In interstitial growth, chondrocytes divide inside the matrix and the cartilage expands from within."},{"t":"mc","q":"In interstitial growth of cartilage, where does the new cartilage come from?\nA) Osteoblasts beneath the periosteum\nB) Cells of the perichondrium at the surface\nC) Chondrocytes dividing inside the matrix\nD) Osteoclasts in the medullary cavity\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Chondrocytes dividing inside the matrix","why":"Cartilage grows in two ways. In interstitial growth, chondrocytes divide inside the matrix and the new cells secrete more matrix, so the cartilage expands from within. Interstitial means within the spaces, which describes growth from the inside out."},{"t":"mc","q":"What are the two ways cartilage grows?\nA) Intramembranous and endochondral growth  |  B) Proliferation and hypertrophy\nC) Appositional and interstitial growth  |  D) Compact and spongy growth\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Appositional and interstitial growth.","why":"Cartilage has two modes of growth. Appositional growth adds new cartilage at the outer surface, starting from the perichondrium. Interstitial growth expands the cartilage from within, as chondrocytes divide inside the matrix."},{"t":"mc","q":"Appositional growth of cartilage begins in which structure?\nA) The perichondrium  |  B) The lacunae\nC) The periosteum  |  D) The epiphyseal plate\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The perichondrium.","why":"The perichondrium is the connective tissue sheath wrapped around most cartilage. Appositional growth starts there, laying new cartilage onto the outer surface, so the cartilage gets thicker at its edge."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Distinguish appositional from interstitial cartilage growth by where each begins and the direction of expansion. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish appositional from interstitial cartilage growth by where each begins and the direction of expansion."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to distinguish appositional from interstitial cartilage growth by where each begins and the direction of expansion. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: cartilage growth modes. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, cartilage growth modes, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w2-bone-shapes","name":"Bone classification by shape","can":"Classify a bone as long, short, flat, irregular, or sesamoid and give an example of each.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A small bone forms within a tendon, as the patella does. Which shape class does it belong to?\nA) Short bone  |  B) Sesamoid bone\nC) Flat bone  |  D) Irregular bone\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Sesamoid bone","why":"Sesamoid bones are small bones that develop inside a tendon. The patella, or kneecap, is the classic example, sitting in the tendon of the quadriceps muscle as it crosses the knee. The name comes from their resemblance to sesame seeds."},{"t":"mc","q":"A bone is described as longer than it is wide, with a shaft and two ends. Which shape class and example fit this description?\nA) An irregular bone, such as a vertebra  |  B) A short bone, such as a carpal\nC) A flat bone, such as the sternum  |  D) A long bone, such as the humerus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) A long bone, such as the humerus","why":"Long bones are defined by being longer than they are wide, with a shaft (the diaphysis) and two expanded ends (the epiphyses). The humerus fits that description exactly. Size does not decide the class, since even the small phalanges of the fingers are long bones."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which list gives the correct shape class for a vertebra, a rib, and a carpal, in that order?\nA) Irregular, flat, and short  |  B) Short, long, and irregular\nC) Irregular, long, and sesamoid  |  D) Flat, flat, and short\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Irregular, flat, and short.","why":"Match each bone to its shape class. Vertebrae have complex shapes that fit no other group, so they are irregular. Ribs are thin and curved, which makes them flat bones even though they are long and narrow. Carpals are roughly cube-shaped, which makes them short bones."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which bone shape class describes the carpals and tarsals?\nA) Long bones  |  B) Short bones\nC) Flat bones  |  D) Irregular bones\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Short bones","why":"Short bones are roughly cube-shaped, about as long as they are wide. The carpals of the wrist and the tarsals of the ankle are the classic examples. Their blocky shape suits joints that need many small gliding movements rather than long levers."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which bone shape class includes the femur and the humerus?\nA) Irregular bones  |  B) Short bones\nC) Long bones  |  D) Flat bones\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Long bones","why":"Long bones are longer than they are wide, with a shaft and two ends. The femur of the thigh and the humerus of the arm are the textbook examples. Their shape makes them strong levers for movement."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Classify a bone as long, short, flat, irregular, or sesamoid and give an example of each. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to classify a bone as long, short, flat, irregular, or sesamoid and give an example of each."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to classify a bone as long, short, flat, irregular, or sesamoid and give an example of each. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: bone classification by shape. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, bone classification by shape, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w2-long-bone-gross","name":"Gross anatomy of a long bone","can":"Label the diaphysis, epiphysis, metaphysis, medullary cavity, articular cartilage, periosteum, endosteum, epiphyseal line, and marrow regions on a long bone.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which membrane covers the outer surface of a bone, and which lines its internal surfaces?\nA) Periosteum outside, endosteum inside\nB) Endosteum outside, periosteum inside\nC) Periosteum outside, perichondrium inside\nD) Perichondrium outside, endosteum inside\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Periosteum outside, endosteum inside","why":"The periosteum is the connective tissue membrane on the outer surface of a bone, and perforating (Sharpey) fibers anchor it firmly into the bone beneath. The endosteum is a thin membrane lining the internal surfaces, including the medullary cavity. The prefixes help: peri- means around, and endo- means within."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which structure caps the epiphysis where one bone meets another at a joint?\nA) The epiphyseal line  |  B) The endosteum\nC) Articular cartilage  |  D) The periosteum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Articular cartilage","why":"Articular cartilage is a layer of hyaline cartilage that caps the epiphysis where it meets another bone. Its smooth, slick surface reduces friction and cushions the bone ends as the joint moves. Because it has no blood supply, damage to it heals poorly."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which list orders the regions of a long bone along a straight line from the joint surface at one end into the shaft?\nA) Epiphysis, articular cartilage, diaphysis, metaphysis\nB) Articular cartilage, metaphysis, epiphysis, diaphysis\nC) Articular cartilage, epiphysis, metaphysis, diaphysis\nD) Diaphysis, metaphysis, epiphysis, articular cartilage\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Articular cartilage, epiphysis, metaphysis, diaphysis","why":"At the joint, articular cartilage caps the end of the bone. Beneath it is the epiphysis, the expanded end filled with spongy bone. Next comes the metaphysis, which holds the growth plate or epiphyseal line, and then the diaphysis, the shaft with the medullary cavity in its center."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which type of marrow fills the spaces between the trabeculae of spongy bone in the epiphyses?\nA) Yellow marrow  |  B) Hyaline cartilage\nC) Compact bone  |  D) Red marrow\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Red marrow","why":"Red marrow fills the spaces of spongy bone in the epiphyses and is the site of blood cell formation. Yellow marrow, which is mostly stored fat, fills the medullary cavity of the adult shaft. Remembering where each sits helps you place spongy bone at the ends and the marrow cavity in the shaft."},{"t":"mc","q":"An adult X-ray shows an epiphyseal line at the end of a long bone. What does this finding tell the clinician?\nA) The growth plate has closed\nB) The bone is being resorbed by osteoclasts\nC) The bone is still growing in length\nD) The bone has a recent fracture across its end\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The growth plate has closed","why":"The epiphyseal line is the bony remnant of the growth plate. It forms when the cartilage of the plate has been fully replaced by bone at the end of growth in length. Seeing a line instead of a cartilage gap tells the clinician the skeleton is mature."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Label the diaphysis, epiphysis, metaphysis, medullary cavity, articular cartilage, periosteum, endosteum, epiphyseal line, and marrow regions on a long bone. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to label the diaphysis, epiphysis, metaphysis, medullary cavity, articular cartilage, periosteum, endosteum, epiphyseal line, and marrow regions on a long bone."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to label the diaphysis, epiphysis, metaphysis, medullary cavity, articular cartilage, periosteum, endosteum, epiphyseal line, and marrow regions on a long bone. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: gross anatomy of a long bone. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, gross anatomy of a long bone, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w2-compact-spongy","name":"Compact and spongy bone microanatomy","can":"Label the parts of an osteon and contrast compact bone with the trabeculae of spongy bone by structure and location.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A long bone tends to break under a twisting force rather than a force along its length. Which structural feature explains this?\nA) Its osteons are aligned with the long axis\nB) It is made entirely of spongy bone\nC) Its osteons lie across the long axis\nD) It has no perforating canals\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Its osteons are aligned with the long axis","why":"The osteons of compact bone run parallel to the long axis of the bone, along the lines of stress it normally carries. That makes a long bone very strong against forces along its length, such as body weight. A twisting force acts off that axis, against the grain of the osteons, so it is the force most likely to cause a break."},{"t":"mc","q":"Compact bone is built from osteons, each with a central canal. Why does each osteon need a canal at its core?\nA) It stores yellow marrow for each individual osteon\nB) It brings blood vessels to the buried osteocytes\nC) It holds the single osteocyte that maintains the osteon\nD) It anchors the periosteum firmly to the bone surface\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It brings blood vessels to the buried osteocytes","why":"Mineralized bone matrix is too hard and dense for nutrients to diffuse through it. The central canal brings blood vessels (and nerves) right into the middle of each osteon, and canaliculi carry nutrients from there out to the osteocytes in their lacunae. Without that core blood supply, the cells deep in compact bone would die."},{"t":"mc","q":"Spongy bone fills the ends of long bones, while compact bone forms the walls of the shaft. Why does spongy bone suit the ends?\nA) It is denser than compact bone, so it adds the most strength\nB) The ends carry no load, so a weaker tissue is enough\nC) Its trabeculae give strength with less weight\nD) It contains the growth plate, so it lets the bone lengthen\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Its trabeculae give strength with less weight","why":"The ends of long bones receive forces from several directions at the joint. Spongy bone handles this with trabeculae, struts aligned along the lines of stress, so it is strong where force travels while staying light. Its spaces also hold red marrow, the site of blood cell formation."},{"t":"mc","q":"A slide shows an open lattice of thin bony struts with marrow in the spaces. Which tissue is it?\nA) Hyaline cartilage  |  B) Spongy bone\nC) Compact bone  |  D) Dense regular connective tissue\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Spongy bone","why":"Spongy bone is an open lattice of thin bony struts called trabeculae, with marrow filling the spaces between them. The lattice makes bone lighter while still supporting stress along the lines of the struts. It fills the ends of long bones and the inside of flat bones, beneath a shell of compact bone."},{"t":"mc","q":"A slide shows concentric rings of hard matrix around a central canal, with small cells in lacunae linked by tiny channels. Which tissue is it?\nA) Dense regular connective tissue  |  B) Compact bone\nC) Hyaline cartilage  |  D) Spongy bone\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Compact bone","why":"The concentric rings are lamellae arranged around a central (Haversian) canal, and together they form an osteon, the repeating unit of compact bone. The cells in lacunae are osteocytes, and the tiny channels connecting them are canaliculi. This ring pattern is what sets compact bone apart from every other connective tissue on a slide."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Label the parts of an osteon and contrast compact bone with the trabeculae of spongy bone by structure and location. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to label the parts of an osteon and contrast compact bone with the trabeculae of spongy bone by structure and location."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to label the parts of an osteon and contrast compact bone with the trabeculae of spongy bone by structure and location. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: compact and spongy bone microanatomy. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, compact and spongy bone microanatomy, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w2-bone-cells","name":"The four bone cells","can":"Identify the osteogenic cell, osteoblast, osteocyte, and osteoclast and state the role and origin of each.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A weight lifter's bones become denser over years of training. Why does repeated mechanical stress increase bone density?\nA) Loading reopens the epiphyseal plates so adult bones grow\nB) Loading turns the cartilage of the growth plate into new bone\nC) Osteoblasts add matrix where stress is high\nD) Loading makes osteoclasts switch to building new matrix\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Osteoblasts add matrix where stress is high","why":"Bone is living tissue that is constantly remodeled. When a bone carries more load, osteoblasts add matrix where stress is greatest and the trabeculae realign along the new stress lines, so the bone becomes denser and stronger. When load is removed, as in long bed rest, the balance shifts the other way and bone is lost."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which bone cell comes from a blood-cell line, unlike the other three bone cells?\nA) The osteoclast  |  B) The osteocyte\nC) The osteoblast  |  D) The osteogenic cell\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The osteoclast","why":"Three of the four bone cells form one lineage: the osteogenic cell divides to make osteoblasts, and osteoblasts become osteocytes. The osteoclast is the outsider. It comes from a blood-cell line, is large with many nuclei, and is the only bone cell that takes bone away."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which list orders the bone cells along a single developmental line, from the stem cell to the cell that maintains mature matrix?\nA) Osteogenic cell, osteoclast, osteocyte\nB) Osteocyte, osteoblast, osteogenic cell\nC) Osteoblast, osteogenic cell, osteocyte\nD) Osteogenic cell, osteoblast, osteocyte\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Osteogenic cell, osteoblast, osteocyte","why":"The osteogenic cell is the stem cell of bone tissue, and it divides to produce osteoblasts. An osteoblast builds matrix, and once it is surrounded by that matrix it becomes an osteocyte, which maintains the bone day to day. The osteoclast is not part of this line, since it comes from a blood-cell line."},{"t":"mc","q":"An osteoblast becomes surrounded by the matrix it has secreted. Which cell does it become?\nA) An osteogenic cell  |  B) A chondrocyte\nC) An osteoclast  |  D) An osteocyte\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) An osteocyte","why":"Osteoblasts build bone by secreting matrix around themselves. Once an osteoblast is fully surrounded, it sits in a lacuna and becomes an osteocyte, a mature bone cell that maintains the matrix day to day. This is the last step of the osteogenic cell to osteoblast to osteocyte lineage."},{"t":"mc","q":"A bone cell maintains the bone matrix day to day from within a lacuna. Which cell is this?\nA) The osteocyte  |  B) The osteogenic cell\nC) The osteoblast  |  D) The osteoclast\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The osteocyte","why":"Osteocytes are mature bone cells, former osteoblasts that became trapped in the matrix they made. Each sits in its own lacuna and maintains the surrounding matrix, staying connected to its neighbors through canaliculi. They are the most common cells in mature bone."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the osteogenic cell, osteoblast, osteocyte, and osteoclast and state the role and origin of each. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the osteogenic cell, osteoblast, osteocyte, and osteoclast and state the role and origin of each."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the osteogenic cell, osteoblast, osteocyte, and osteoclast and state the role and origin of each. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: the four bone cells. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, the four bone cells, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w2-ossification-growth","name":"Ossification and growth plate zones","can":"Contrast intramembranous and endochondral ossification and list the five growth plate zones in order from epiphysis to diaphysis.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The growth plate shows up as a gap on a child's X-ray. Why does it look like a gap?\nA) It is the medullary cavity filled with marrow\nB) It is cartilage, which is not mineralized like bone\nC) It is a fracture line that has not yet healed\nD) It is a space left where osteoclasts removed bone\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It is cartilage, which is not mineralized like bone","why":"X-rays show mineralized bone clearly, while unmineralized tissue such as cartilage lets the beam pass and looks dark. The epiphyseal plate is a disc of hyaline cartilage, so it appears as a gap between the shaft and the end of the bone. Once growth ends and bone replaces the plate, the gap is gone and a thin epiphyseal line remains."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which route of ossification builds the flat bones of the skull?\nA) Appositional growth  |  B) Endochondral ossification\nC) Interstitial growth  |  D) Intramembranous ossification\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Intramembranous ossification","why":"In intramembranous ossification, bone forms directly within a sheet of connective tissue membrane, with no cartilage model first. This is how the flat bones of the skull form. The name tells the story: intra- (within) a membrane."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which route of ossification forms most bones of the body by replacing a cartilage model?\nA) Intramembranous ossification  |  B) Endochondral ossification\nC) Interstitial growth  |  D) Appositional growth\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Endochondral ossification","why":"Endochondral ossification forms most bones of the body. A model of hyaline cartilage is laid down first, and bone gradually replaces it. The name means within (endo-) cartilage (chondr-), which describes where the bone forms."},{"t":"mc","q":"At which structure does a long bone grow longer?\nA) At the articular cartilage  |  B) At the periosteum\nC) At the epiphyseal plate  |  D) At the medullary cavity\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) At the epiphyseal plate","why":"The epiphyseal plate, or growth plate, is a disc of hyaline cartilage in the metaphysis between the shaft and the end. Cartilage there keeps growing and is replaced by bone on the shaft side, which pushes the epiphysis farther away and lengthens the bone. When growth ends, the plate closes and leaves the epiphyseal line."},{"t":"mc","q":"Appositional growth changes a long bone in which way?\nA) It loses its marrow  |  B) It grows wider\nC) It grows longer  |  D) It becomes shorter\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It grows wider","why":"Appositional growth adds new bone at the outer surface, beneath the periosteum, so the bone grows wider. Apposition means placing something next to something else, which describes laying new layers on the surface. At the same time, bone is removed from the inner surface so the medullary cavity widens too."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Contrast intramembranous and endochondral ossification and list the five growth plate zones in order from epiphysis to diaphysis. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to contrast intramembranous and endochondral ossification and list the five growth plate zones in order from epiphysis to diaphysis."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to contrast intramembranous and endochondral ossification and list the five growth plate zones in order from epiphysis to diaphysis. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: ossification and growth plate zones. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, ossification and growth plate zones, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]}]},{"week":5,"system":"Skeletal","comps":[{"id":"w2-skull-bones","name":"Cranial and facial bones","can":"Distinguish the eight cranial bones from the fourteen facial bones and name the region each bone forms.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which one cranial bone articulates with every other cranial bone?\nA) The occipital bone  |  B) The sphenoid bone\nC) The ethmoid bone  |  D) The frontal bone\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The sphenoid bone","why":"The sphenoid bone is the central wedge of the cranial base, shaped somewhat like a bat with its wings spread. Because it sits in the middle of the skull floor, it touches every other cranial bone. It contains the sella turcica, the optic canals, and the sphenoidal sinus."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which cranial bone is the deepest, lying between the orbits and forming part of the nasal cavity?\nA) The sphenoid bone  |  B) The temporal bone\nC) The frontal bone  |  D) The ethmoid bone\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The ethmoid bone","why":"The ethmoid bone is the deepest cranial bone, tucked between the orbits behind the nose. It forms the roof and part of the walls of the nasal cavity, the upper part of the nasal septum through its perpendicular plate, and it holds the ethmoidal air cells. Its cribriform plate lets the olfactory nerve filaments through."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which cranial bones come in pairs rather than as single bones?\nA) The parietal and temporal bones  |  B) The occipital and sphenoid bones\nC) The sphenoid and ethmoid bones  |  D) The frontal and occipital bones\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The parietal and temporal bones","why":"Of the eight cranial bones, only the parietal and temporal bones are paired, one on each side of the head. The frontal, occipital, sphenoid, and ethmoid bones are single bones that cross the midline. Two pairs plus four single bones gives the total of eight."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which two facial bones are single rather than paired?\nA) The vomer and the mandible  |  B) The zygomatic bones and the mandible\nC) The maxillae and the palatine bones  |  D) The nasal bones and the vomer\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The vomer and the mandible","why":"Of the fourteen facial bones, twelve come in six pairs and only two are single: the vomer and the mandible. Both cross the midline. The vomer forms the lower part of the nasal septum, and the mandible is the lower jaw."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which nasal conchae are separate facial bones rather than parts of the ethmoid bone?\nA) The lateral masses  |  B) The superior nasal conchae\nC) The middle nasal conchae  |  D) The inferior nasal conchae\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The inferior nasal conchae","why":"The nasal cavity has three pairs of scroll-shaped conchae on its lateral walls. The superior and middle conchae are projections of the ethmoid bone, but the inferior nasal conchae are separate facial bones. That is why the inferior nasal conchae are counted among the fourteen facial bones."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Distinguish the eight cranial bones from the fourteen facial bones and name the region each bone forms. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish the eight cranial bones from the fourteen facial bones and name the region each bone forms."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to distinguish the eight cranial bones from the fourteen facial bones and name the region each bone forms. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: cranial and facial bones. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, cranial and facial bones, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w2-skull-markings","name":"Significant skull bone markings","can":"Identify the key markings on each cranial and facial bone, including features of the sphenoid, ethmoid, temporal, occipital, maxillae, and mandible.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient loses the sense of smell after a head injury. Damage to which skull structure would best explain this?\nA) The mental foramen of the mandible\nB) The mastoid process of the temporal bone\nC) The cribriform plate of the ethmoid\nD) The sella turcica of the sphenoid\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The cribriform plate of the ethmoid","why":"The olfactory nerve filaments pass from the nasal lining up through the many small holes of the cribriform plate to reach the brain. The plate is thin and perforated, so a blow to the head can fracture it and tear these filaments. That cuts the pathway for smell."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which marking of the occipital bone articulates with the atlas, the first cervical vertebra?\nA) The external occipital protuberance  |  B) The occipital condyles\nC) The mastoid process  |  D) The condylar process\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The occipital condyles","why":"The occipital condyles are the paired knobs beside the foramen magnum. They rest on the superior articular facets of the atlas to form the atlanto-occipital joint. This is the joint that lets you nod your head yes."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which marking of the temporal bone forms the socket of the temporomandibular joint?\nA) The external acoustic meatus  |  B) The mastoid process\nC) The styloid process  |  D) The mandibular fossa\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The mandibular fossa","why":"The mandibular fossa is a shallow socket on the underside of the temporal bone, just in front of the ear canal. The condylar process of the mandible fits into it, forming the temporomandibular joint (TMJ), the jaw joint."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which marking of the ethmoid bone is the perforated plate that the olfactory nerve filaments pass through?\nA) The cribriform plate  |  B) The crista galli\nC) The perpendicular plate  |  D) The lateral masses\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The cribriform plate","why":"The cribriform plate is a horizontal plate of the ethmoid bone, full of small holes, that forms the roof of the nasal cavity. Filaments of the olfactory nerve, cranial nerve I, pass up through these holes from the nasal lining to the brain. Cribriform means sieve-like."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which process of the mandible is the anterior process where the temporalis chewing muscle attaches?\nA) The condylar process  |  B) The alveolar process\nC) The coronoid process  |  D) The styloid process\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The coronoid process","why":"The coronoid process is the thin, pointed anterior process at the top of the mandibular ramus. The temporalis, a chewing muscle that fans out over the side of the skull, attaches to it. The mandibular notch separates it from the condylar process behind."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the key markings on each cranial and facial bone, including features of the sphenoid, ethmoid, temporal, occipital, maxillae, and mandible. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the key markings on each cranial and facial bone, including features of the sphenoid, ethmoid, temporal, occipital, maxillae, and mandible."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the key markings on each cranial and facial bone, including features of the sphenoid, ethmoid, temporal, occipital, maxillae, and mandible. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: significant skull bone markings. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, significant skull bone markings, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w2-sutures-fontanelles","name":"Sutures and fontanelles","can":"Locate the four major sutures and the pterion by the bones they join and name the four fontanelles and where each lies.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which suture joins the frontal bone to the two parietal bones?\nA) The squamous suture  |  B) The sagittal suture\nC) The coronal suture  |  D) The lambdoid suture\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The coronal suture","why":"The coronal suture runs across the top of the skull from side to side, just behind the forehead. It joins the frontal bone in front to the two parietal bones behind. It runs in the same direction as the coronal (frontal) plane, which is how it got its name."},{"t":"mc","q":"The pterion is the junction of which four bones?\nA) Frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid\nB) Frontal, parietal, occipital, and sphenoid\nC) Parietal, temporal, occipital, and ethmoid\nD) Frontal, temporal, occipital, and sphenoid\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid","why":"The pterion is an H-shaped junction on the side of the skull, in the temple region, where the frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid (greater wing) bones meet. It is the thinnest part of the skull. The middle meningeal artery runs just beneath it, so a blow here can tear the artery."},{"t":"mc","q":"A student finds an immovable fibrous joint at the back of the skull joining the parietal bones to the occipital bone. Which suture is this?\nA) The sagittal suture  |  B) The squamous suture\nC) The coronal suture  |  D) The lambdoid suture\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The lambdoid suture","why":"The lambdoid suture is at the back of the skull, where the two parietal bones meet the occipital bone. Its shape resembles the Greek letter lambda, an upside-down V, which is how it got its name. Like all sutures, it is an immovable fibrous joint."},{"t":"mc","q":"On an infant's skull, a clinician feels a fibrous membrane gap where the frontal bone meets the two parietal bones. Which fontanelle is this?\nA) The posterolateral fontanelle  |  B) The posterior fontanelle\nC) The anterior fontanelle  |  D) The anterolateral fontanelle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The anterior fontanelle","why":"The anterior fontanelle is at the junction of the frontal bone and the two parietal bones, on the top of the head toward the front. It is the largest fontanelle and closes at about 18 to 24 months. In the adult, the same spot is where the coronal and sagittal sutures meet."},{"t":"mc","q":"A clinician studies the thin H-shaped junction on the side of the skull where four bones meet. Which region is this?\nA) The external occipital protuberance  |  B) The glabella\nC) The mastoid process  |  D) The pterion\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The pterion","why":"The pterion is the H-shaped junction in the temple region where the frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid bones meet. It is the thinnest part of the skull. Because the middle meningeal artery runs just beneath it, a blow here can cause dangerous bleeding inside the skull."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Locate the four major sutures and the pterion by the bones they join and name the four fontanelles and where each lies. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to locate the four major sutures and the pterion by the bones they join and name the four fontanelles and where each lies."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to locate the four major sutures and the pterion by the bones they join and name the four fontanelles and where each lies. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: sutures and fontanelles. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, sutures and fontanelles, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w2-skull-cavities","name":"Orbit, nasal cavity, palate, and sinuses","can":"Identify the bones forming the orbit, nasal septum, and hard palate and locate the four paranasal sinuses by the bone each occupies.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient fractures the back part of the bony roof of the mouth. Which bone is broken, and which group of skull bones does it belong to?\nA) A palatine bone, a facial bone  |  B) A maxilla, a facial bone\nC) The sphenoid, a cranial bone  |  D) The vomer, a facial bone\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A palatine bone, a facial bone.","why":"The hard palate is the bony roof of the mouth, with the maxillae in front and the palatine bones behind. A break at the back part therefore involves a palatine bone. The palatine bones are paired facial bones."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which marking of the maxilla forms the anterior part of the hard palate?\nA) The frontal process  |  B) The alveolar process\nC) The palatine process  |  D) The zygomatic process\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The palatine process","why":"The palatine process is a horizontal shelf on each maxilla. The right and left palatine processes meet in the midline to form the front part of the hard palate, the bony roof of the mouth. The palatine bones complete the hard palate behind them."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which bone forms part of the medial wall of the orbit and is grooved for the tear duct?\nA) The nasal bone  |  B) The zygomatic bone\nC) The palatine bone  |  D) The lacrimal bone\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The lacrimal bone","why":"The lacrimal bones are the smallest facial bones, one in the medial wall of each orbit. Each has a groove that holds the passage carrying tears from the eye toward the nasal cavity. Lacrimal means related to tears."},{"t":"mc","q":"A probe runs down the bony nasal septum from its upper part to its lower part. Which bones does it cross, in order?\nA) The nasal bone, then the vomer\nB) The vomer, then the perpendicular plate\nC) The perpendicular plate, then the vomer\nD) The cribriform plate, then the vomer\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The perpendicular plate, then the vomer","why":"The nasal septum is the partition between the right and left nasal cavities. Its upper bony part is the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid bone, and its lower bony part is the vomer. Septal cartilage completes the front of the septum."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which statement correctly describes the bones of the hard palate from front to back?\nA) The palatine bones in front, then the maxillae behind\nB) The maxillae in front, then the palatine bones behind\nC) The maxillae in front, then the vomer behind\nD) The vomer in front, then the maxillae behind\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The maxillae in front, then the palatine bones behind","why":"The hard palate is the bony roof of the mouth and the floor of the nasal cavity. The palatine processes of the two maxillae form its larger front part, and the horizontal plates of the two palatine bones form the back part. Despite their name, the palatine bones form only the posterior palate."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the bones forming the orbit, nasal septum, and hard palate and locate the four paranasal sinuses by the bone each occupies. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the bones forming the orbit, nasal septum, and hard palate and locate the four paranasal sinuses by the bone each occupies."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the bones forming the orbit, nasal septum, and hard palate and locate the four paranasal sinuses by the bone each occupies. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: orbit, nasal cavity, palate, and sinuses. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, orbit, nasal cavity, palate, and sinuses, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w2-skull-foramina","name":"Major skull foramina and contents","can":"Name the major skull foramina by the bone each pierces and state the nerve or vessel that passes through each.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The foramen magnum is a large opening in the occipital bone at the base of the skull. Why must an opening this large exist there?\nA) The esophagus passes through it on its way to the stomach\nB) The brainstem continues into the spinal cord through it\nC) It makes room for the atlas to rotate on the skull\nD) The internal jugular veins leave the skull through it\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The brainstem continues into the spinal cord through it","why":"The medulla oblongata, the lowest part of the brainstem, continues into the spinal cord at the foramen magnum. The cord must pass from the cranial cavity into the vertebral canal, so the skull needs a large opening directly above the spine. The vertebral arteries and spinal accessory nerve also pass through it."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which structure passes through the foramen magnum?\nA) The internal jugular vein  |  B) The spinal cord\nC) The optic nerve  |  D) The internal carotid artery\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The spinal cord","why":"The foramen magnum is the large opening in the occipital bone where the medulla oblongata of the brainstem continues into the spinal cord. The vertebral arteries and the spinal accessory nerve also pass through it. It is the one large opening between the brain and the spinal cord."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which opening transmits the optic nerve?\nA) The foramen ovale  |  B) The optic canal\nC) The jugular foramen  |  D) The superior orbital fissure\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The optic canal","why":"The optic canal is an opening in the sphenoid bone at the back of the orbit. The optic nerve, cranial nerve II, passes through it from the eye to the brain. It sits just medial to the superior orbital fissure."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which opening transmits the internal carotid artery into the skull?\nA) The carotid canal  |  B) The jugular foramen\nC) The stylomastoid foramen  |  D) The foramen magnum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The carotid canal","why":"The carotid canal is a passage through the temporal bone that carries the internal carotid artery into the cranial cavity. The internal carotid is one of the main arteries supplying the brain. Its name matches the vessel it carries."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which opening, located between the styloid and mastoid processes, is the exit of the facial nerve from the skull?\nA) The carotid canal  |  B) The internal acoustic meatus\nC) The jugular foramen  |  D) The stylomastoid foramen\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The stylomastoid foramen","why":"The stylomastoid foramen lies on the underside of the temporal bone, between the styloid process and the mastoid process. Its name simply joins the two landmarks it sits between. The facial nerve leaves the skull here on its way to the face."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Name the major skull foramina by the bone each pierces and state the nerve or vessel that passes through each. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to name the major skull foramina by the bone each pierces and state the nerve or vessel that passes through each."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to name the major skull foramina by the bone each pierces and state the nerve or vessel that passes through each. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: major skull foramina and contents. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, major skull foramina and contents, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w2-spine-regions","name":"Vertebral column regions and curvatures","can":"Name the five regions of the vertebral column with the vertebra count of each and identify the four curvatures as primary or secondary.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which two spinal curvatures are primary curvatures, present from birth?\nA) The cervical and thoracic curvatures  |  B) The lumbar and sacral curvatures\nC) The thoracic and sacral curvatures  |  D) The cervical and lumbar curvatures\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The thoracic and sacral curvatures","why":"A newborn's spine is curved in one long C shape, like its position in the uterus. The thoracic and sacral curvatures keep that original direction, so they are the primary curvatures. The cervical and lumbar curvatures form later as the baby lifts its head and then stands, so they are secondary."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which spinal curvature develops as an infant begins to hold up its head?\nA) The cervical curvature  |  B) The sacral curvature\nC) The thoracic curvature  |  D) The lumbar curvature\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The cervical curvature","why":"The cervical curvature is a secondary curve that forms in the neck when an infant starts lifting and holding up its head. Holding the head up against gravity bends the neck region the opposite way from the fetal C shape. The other secondary curve, the lumbar, comes later with standing and walking."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which spinal curvature is secondary and develops as a child stands and walks?\nA) The sacral curvature  |  B) The lumbar curvature\nC) The thoracic curvature  |  D) The cervical curvature\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The lumbar curvature","why":"The lumbar curvature forms in the lower back once a child begins standing and walking. Standing upright bends the lumbar spine opposite to the fetal C shape, which makes it a secondary curvature. It balances the trunk over the hips and legs."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which list gives the five regions of the vertebral column in order from superior to inferior?\nA) Thoracic, cervical, lumbar, sacrum, coccyx\nB) Cervical, lumbar, thoracic, sacrum, coccyx\nC) Cervical, thoracic, lumbar, coccyx, sacrum\nD) Cervical, thoracic, lumbar, sacrum, coccyx\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Cervical, thoracic, lumbar, sacrum, coccyx","why":"Moving down the body, the column begins in the neck (cervical), continues through the chest where the ribs attach (thoracic), then the lower back (lumbar). Below the lumbar region come the two fused bones, the sacrum between the hip bones and finally the coccyx, the tailbone. Matching each region to a body area makes the order easy to rebuild."},{"t":"mc","q":"During a school screening, a student bends forward and the nurse sees that the spine curves to one side. Which abnormal curvature is this?\nA) Kyphosis  |  B) Lordosis\nC) A herniated disc  |  D) Scoliosis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Scoliosis","why":"Scoliosis is an abnormal sideways curve of the spine. The normal curvatures all bend forward or backward, so any curve to the left or right is abnormal. The other named abnormal curvatures, kyphosis and lordosis, are exaggerations of the normal front-to-back curves."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Name the five regions of the vertebral column with the vertebra count of each and identify the four curvatures as primary or secondary. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to name the five regions of the vertebral column with the vertebra count of each and identify the four curvatures as primary or secondary."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to name the five regions of the vertebral column with the vertebra count of each and identify the four curvatures as primary or secondary. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: vertebral column regions and curvatures. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, vertebral column regions and curvatures, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w2-typical-vertebra","name":"Typical vertebra and intervertebral disc","can":"Label the parts of a typical vertebra and the anulus fibrosus and nucleus pulposus of an intervertebral disc.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which two parts of a vertebra connect the vertebral arch to the body?\nA) The transverse processes  |  B) The pedicles\nC) The articular processes  |  D) The laminae\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The pedicles","why":"The pedicles are two short, thick bars that project backward from the vertebral body and form the sides of the arch. Pedicle means little foot, since they are the feet the arch stands on. Notches above and below each pedicle help form the intervertebral foramina where spinal nerves exit."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which two flat plates complete the vertebral arch behind?\nA) The pedicles  |  B) The bodies\nC) The laminae  |  D) The spinous processes\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The laminae","why":"The laminae are two flat plates of bone that extend from the pedicles toward the midline, where they meet to close the back of the vertebral arch. The spinous process projects from the spot where they join. A laminectomy, removal of the laminae, is a surgery that opens the arch to relieve pressure on the spinal cord."},{"t":"mc","q":"Through which openings do the spinal nerves exit the vertebral column?\nA) The intervertebral foramina  |  B) The transverse foramina\nC) The sacral hiatus  |  D) The vertebral foramina\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The intervertebral foramina","why":"The intervertebral foramina are openings on each side between two stacked vertebrae, formed by notches above and below the pedicles. A spinal nerve leaves the vertebral canal through each one. That is why a bulging disc or bone spur near this opening can press on a nerve and send pain down a limb."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has pain that travels down one leg after the soft core of a lumbar disc pushes through its torn outer ring. What is this condition called?\nA) Scoliosis  |  B) A herniated disc\nC) Kyphosis  |  D) A fractured dens\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A herniated disc","why":"A herniated disc happens when the nucleus pulposus bulges through a tear in the anulus fibrosus. The bulge can press on a spinal nerve as it exits through an intervertebral foramen. Because that nerve carries signals from the leg, the pain is felt along the limb rather than only in the back."},{"t":"mc","q":"A broken vertebra is treated as an emergency mainly because of a delicate structure that runs through the column. Which structure is at risk?\nA) The esophagus  |  B) The spinal cord\nC) The red bone marrow  |  D) The nucleus pulposus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The spinal cord","why":"The stacked vertebral foramina form the vertebral canal, which carries the spinal cord. A fractured vertebra can shift or splinter into the canal and injure the cord, which can cause paralysis below the level of injury. That is why a vertebral fracture is dangerous for what runs inside the column, not only for the bone."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Label the parts of a typical vertebra and the anulus fibrosus and nucleus pulposus of an intervertebral disc. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to label the parts of a typical vertebra and the anulus fibrosus and nucleus pulposus of an intervertebral disc."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to label the parts of a typical vertebra and the anulus fibrosus and nucleus pulposus of an intervertebral disc. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: typical vertebra and intervertebral disc. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, typical vertebra and intervertebral disc, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w2-regional-vertebrae","name":"Regional and specialized vertebrae","can":"Distinguish cervical, thoracic, and lumbar vertebrae by an identifying feature and describe the atlas, axis, dens, sacrum, and coccyx.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Lumbar vertebrae have much larger bodies than cervical vertebrae. What best explains this size difference?\nA) Lower vertebrae carry more of the body's weight above them\nB) Lumbar vertebrae must anchor the twelve pairs of ribs\nC) Lumbar vertebrae need room for transverse foramina\nD) Lumbar vertebrae surround a much thicker spinal cord\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Lower vertebrae carry more of the body's weight above them","why":"Weight adds up as you move down the spine. Cervical vertebrae carry only the head, but lumbar vertebrae carry the head, arms, and entire trunk. Larger, thicker bodies spread that heavier load, so vertebral body size increases steadily from the neck to the lower back."},{"t":"mc","q":"The sacrum forms from five vertebrae that fuse into one solid bone. Why is a fused bone an advantage at this location?\nA) It lets the lower back twist more freely\nB) It protects the spinal cord, which ends inside the sacrum\nC) It forms a stable base that passes body weight into the pelvis and legs\nD) It gives the floating ribs a place to attach\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It forms a stable base that passes body weight into the pelvis and legs","why":"The sacrum sits between the hip bones and transfers the weight of the entire upper body into the pelvic girdle at the sacroiliac joints. Movement here would make the base unstable, so five separate vertebrae fusing into one rigid bone gives the strength and stability this load transfer needs. Mobility is provided higher up in the column instead."},{"t":"mc","q":"The atlanto-occipital joint, where the occipital condyles meet the atlas, mainly allows which head movement?\nA) Opening and closing the jaw  |  B) Tilting the skull bones apart\nC) Nodding the head yes  |  D) Shaking the head no\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Nodding the head yes","why":"The atlanto-occipital joint forms where the occipital condyles rest on the superior articular facets of the atlas. Its shape allows the head to rock forward and back, which is the nodding yes motion. Rotation of the head, the no motion, happens at the joint between the atlas and the axis instead."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which vertebral region has transverse foramina in its transverse processes?\nA) The thoracic region  |  B) The cervical region\nC) The lumbar region  |  D) The sacral region\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The cervical region","why":"Only cervical vertebrae have transverse foramina, small holes through each transverse process. The vertebral arteries travel up through these holes on their way to the brain. Because no other region has them, a transverse foramen is enough by itself to identify a cervical vertebra."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which vertebral region has costal facets where the ribs articulate?\nA) The coccygeal region  |  B) The cervical region\nC) The thoracic region  |  D) The lumbar region\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The thoracic region","why":"Costal facets are small smooth joint surfaces on thoracic vertebrae, on the bodies and on the transverse processes, where the ribs meet the spine. Costal means rib. Since ribs attach only in the chest, costal facets identify a thoracic vertebra."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Distinguish cervical, thoracic, and lumbar vertebrae by an identifying feature and describe the atlas, axis, dens, sacrum, and coccyx. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish cervical, thoracic, and lumbar vertebrae by an identifying feature and describe the atlas, axis, dens, sacrum, and coccyx."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to distinguish cervical, thoracic, and lumbar vertebrae by an identifying feature and describe the atlas, axis, dens, sacrum, and coccyx. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: regional and specialized vertebrae. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, regional and specialized vertebrae, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w2-thoracic-cage","name":"Sternum and ribs","can":"Identify the parts of the sternum and rib markings and classify the twelve rib pairs as true, false, or floating by anterior attachment.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient fractures rib 11. A student worries the broken rib will pull on the sternum. Why is that not a concern?\nA) Rib 11 is a true rib whose own cartilage absorbs the pull\nB) Rib 11 is a floating rib with no anterior attachment\nC) Rib 11 joins the costal cartilage of rib 10 instead\nD) Rib 11 attaches to the xiphoid process, which is cartilage\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Rib 11 is a floating rib with no anterior attachment.","why":"Place the rib in its group, then recall that group's attachment. Ribs 11 and 12 are the floating ribs, a subset of the false ribs, and they have no anterior attachment at all. With no link to the sternum, a break in rib 11 cannot pull on it."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which ridge marks where the manubrium meets the body of the sternum?\nA) The xiphoid process  |  B) The sternal angle\nC) The costal cartilage  |  D) The jugular notch\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The sternal angle","why":"The sternal angle is a slight horizontal ridge where the manubrium joins the body of the sternum. It lies at the level of the second rib, so it is a reliable landmark you can feel through the skin. Clinicians find it first and then count ribs downward from there."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which rib pairs are the true ribs?\nA) Pairs 8 to 10  |  B) Pairs 1 to 7\nC) Pairs 11 and 12  |  D) Pairs 8 to 12\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Pairs 1 to 7","why":"The true ribs are pairs 1 through 7, and each one attaches directly to the sternum by its own costal cartilage. They are called true because they have a direct line to the sternum. The ribs below them are false ribs, which do not reach the sternum on their own."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which rib pairs are the floating ribs, with no anterior attachment at all?\nA) Pairs 8 to 10  |  B) Pairs 1 and 2\nC) Pairs 11 and 12  |  D) Pairs 1 to 7\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Pairs 11 and 12","why":"Pairs 11 and 12 are the floating ribs. They attach to the vertebrae in back but end freely in the muscles of the abdominal wall, with no cartilage connection to the sternum. They are counted as a subset of the false ribs, pairs 8 to 12."},{"t":"mc","q":"The tubercle of a rib articulates with which part of a thoracic vertebra?\nA) The spinous process  |  B) A transverse process\nC) The vertebral body  |  D) A pedicle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A transverse process","why":"Each rib makes two joints with the spine. The head of the rib meets the vertebral bodies, and the tubercle, a knob a little farther along the rib, meets the transverse process. The transverse processes of thoracic vertebrae carry costal facets for this purpose."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the parts of the sternum and rib markings and classify the twelve rib pairs as true, false, or floating by anterior attachment. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the parts of the sternum and rib markings and classify the twelve rib pairs as true, false, or floating by anterior attachment."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the parts of the sternum and rib markings and classify the twelve rib pairs as true, false, or floating by anterior attachment. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: sternum and ribs. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, sternum and ribs, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]}]},{"week":6,"system":"Skeletal","comps":[{"id":"w2-pectoral-girdle","name":"Pectoral girdle and its markings","can":"Identify the markings of the clavicle and scapula and explain how the clavicle links the upper limb to the axial skeleton.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Why is the clavicle so prone to fracture in a fall onto an outstretched arm?\nA) It is a slender strut that carries force from the arm to the trunk\nB) It is made mostly of cartilage, which snaps easily\nC) It bears the full weight of the trunk when standing\nD) It is buried deep under thick muscle that pins it in place\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It is a slender strut that carries force from the arm to the trunk","why":"The clavicle holds the shoulder out from the body and is the only bony link between the arm and the axial skeleton. When a person lands on an outstretched hand, the force travels up the arm and through the clavicle toward the sternum. Because the bone is slender and lies just under the skin, it often snaps, usually in its middle third."},{"t":"mc","q":"The clavicle is the only bony link in which connection?\nA) The radius to the ulna  |  B) The first rib to the sternum\nC) The scapula to the humerus  |  D) The upper limb to the axial skeleton\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The upper limb to the axial skeleton","why":"The clavicle is the sole bony strut connecting the upper limb to the axial skeleton. Its medial end joins the manubrium of the sternum, and its lateral end joins the acromion of the scapula. The scapula itself is held to the trunk only by muscles, which is why the shoulder is so mobile."},{"t":"mc","q":"The sternal end of the clavicle articulates with which structure?\nA) The body of the sternum  |  B) The manubrium of the sternum\nC) The coracoid process of the scapula  |  D) The acromion of the scapula\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The manubrium of the sternum","why":"The sternal end is the medial end of the clavicle. It meets the manubrium, the top part of the sternum, at the sternoclavicular joint. This is the only joint that connects the upper limb to the axial skeleton."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which scapular process forms the point of the shoulder at the lateral tip of the spine?\nA) The acromion  |  B) The superior angle\nC) The coracoid process  |  D) The glenoid cavity\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The acromion","why":"The acromion is the flattened lateral end of the scapular spine. It forms the bony point at the top of the shoulder that you can feel, and it articulates with the lateral end of the clavicle at the acromioclavicular joint. Acromion means the highest point of the shoulder."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which hollow of the scapula lies on its anterior surface, facing the ribs?\nA) The subscapular fossa  |  B) The supraspinous fossa\nC) The glenoid cavity  |  D) The infraspinous fossa\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The subscapular fossa","why":"The subscapular fossa is the broad, shallow hollow covering most of the anterior surface of the scapula, the side that faces the ribs. Sub means under, and this surface lies underneath the scapula against the thoracic wall. The subscapularis muscle fills it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the markings of the clavicle and scapula and explain how the clavicle links the upper limb to the axial skeleton. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the markings of the clavicle and scapula and explain how the clavicle links the upper limb to the axial skeleton."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the markings of the clavicle and scapula and explain how the clavicle links the upper limb to the axial skeleton. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: pectoral girdle and its markings. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, pectoral girdle and its markings, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w2-arm-forearm","name":"Humerus, radius, and ulna markings","can":"Identify the key markings of the humerus, radius, and ulna and state which forearm bone is lateral and which forms the elbow hinge.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The forearm has two parallel bones, the radius and ulna, instead of one. What movement does this two-bone design make possible?\nA) Tilting the wrist toward the thumb or little finger\nB) Bending and straightening the elbow\nC) Bringing the thumb across the palm to the fingertips\nD) Turning the palm up and down as the radius rotates around the ulna\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Turning the palm up and down as the radius rotates around the ulna","why":"Turning the palm down (pronation) and up (supination) happens when the radius rotates around the ulna at the proximal and distal radioulnar joints. When the palm turns down, the radius crosses over the ulna. A single forearm bone could not cross over itself, so this rotation depends on having two bones."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which marking on the distal humerus articulates with the radius?\nA) The olecranon fossa  |  B) The medial epicondyle\nC) The trochlea  |  D) The capitulum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The capitulum","why":"The capitulum is a small rounded knob on the lateral side of the distal humerus. Capitulum means little head, and the disc-shaped head of the radius rests against it. In anatomical position the radius is lateral, so it pairs with the lateral articular surface."},{"t":"mc","q":"In anatomical position, on which side of the forearm does the radius lie?\nA) The medial side, the little-finger side\nB) The anterior side, in front of the ulna\nC) The lateral side, the thumb side\nD) The posterior side, behind the ulna\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The lateral side, the thumb side","why":"In anatomical position, with the palms facing forward, the radius lies on the lateral side of the forearm, in line with the thumb. The ulna lies on the medial side, in line with the little finger. The two bones sit side by side, so they are described as lateral and medial rather than front and back."},{"t":"mc","q":"The trochlear notch of the ulna grips which marking of the humerus?\nA) The medial epicondyle  |  B) The olecranon fossa\nC) The capitulum  |  D) The trochlea\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The trochlea","why":"The trochlear notch is a deep C-shaped curve on the proximal ulna, between the olecranon and the coronoid process. It wraps around the spool-shaped trochlea of the humerus like a wrench around a bolt. This tight fit forms the hinge of the elbow."},{"t":"mc","q":"The head of the radius articulates with which two structures at the elbow?\nA) The olecranon fossa and the coronoid process\nB) The trochlea of the humerus and the trochlear notch of the ulna\nC) The capitulum of the humerus and the ulnar notch of the radius\nD) The capitulum of the humerus and the radial notch of the ulna\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The capitulum of the humerus and the radial notch of the ulna","why":"The disc-shaped head of the radius touches two bones. Its top surface rests against the capitulum of the humerus, and its rim spins against the radial notch on the side of the ulna. The joint with the radial notch is the proximal radioulnar joint, which lets the radius rotate when the palm turns."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the key markings of the humerus, radius, and ulna and state which forearm bone is lateral and which forms the elbow hinge. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the key markings of the humerus, radius, and ulna and state which forearm bone is lateral and which forms the elbow hinge."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the key markings of the humerus, radius, and ulna and state which forearm bone is lateral and which forms the elbow hinge. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: humerus, radius, and ulna markings. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, humerus, radius, and ulna markings, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w2-hand-bones","name":"Bones of the hand","can":"Name the eight carpals in their two rows and the metacarpals and phalanges and locate the carpal tunnel and its contents.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A scaphoid fracture that is missed on the first X-ray may never heal properly. Which feature of the scaphoid explains this?\nA) It is a sesamoid bone inside a tendon\nB) It sits in the distal row, away from the radius\nC) It is the largest carpal, so it heals slowly\nD) Its blood supply is poor\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Its blood supply is poor","why":"Bone heals by bringing in blood, cells, and nutrients to the break. The scaphoid has a poor blood supply, and in many people the vessels enter only at one end, so a break can cut off part of the bone. Without treatment, that part may not heal and can even die, which is why scaphoid injuries are taken seriously."},{"t":"mc","q":"On an articulated wrist, a student puts a finger on the carpal most often dislocated, then moves one bone toward the little finger within the same row. Which bone is the student touching now?\nA) The triquetrum  |  B) The scaphoid\nC) The pisiform  |  D) The capitate\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The triquetrum.","why":"Two steps get you there. The carpal most often dislocated is the lunate, the second bone of the proximal row. The proximal row runs scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform from the thumb side (So Long To Pinky), so one bone toward the little finger is the triquetrum."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which carpal bone articulates with the first metacarpal at the base of the thumb?\nA) The trapezoid  |  B) The scaphoid\nC) The trapezium  |  D) The capitate\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The trapezium","why":"The trapezium is the distal-row carpal on the thumb side. It meets the base of the first metacarpal at the carpometacarpal joint of the thumb, a saddle joint that lets the thumb swing across the palm. A helpful memory link is that the trapeziUM sits under the thUMb."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which list names the phalanges of the index finger in order from the palm to the fingertip?\nA) Proximal, distal, middle  |  B) Proximal, middle, distal\nC) Distal, middle, proximal  |  D) Proximal and distal only\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Proximal, middle, distal","why":"Each finger other than the thumb has three phalanges named by their position. The proximal phalanx is closest to the palm, the middle phalanx is next, and the distal phalanx forms the fingertip. Proximal means nearer to the trunk and distal means farther from it."},{"t":"mc","q":"The metacarpals are numbered 1 to 5. Where does the numbering begin?\nA) At the little-finger side  |  B) At the thumb side\nC) At the index finger  |  D) At the middle finger\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) At the thumb side","why":"Metacarpals are numbered from lateral to medial in anatomical position, starting with the thumb as number 1 and ending with the little finger as number 5. The digits follow the same numbering, so the thumb is digit 1. This matches the lab list, which numbers metacarpals lateral to medial."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Name the eight carpals in their two rows and the metacarpals and phalanges and locate the carpal tunnel and its contents. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to name the eight carpals in their two rows and the metacarpals and phalanges and locate the carpal tunnel and its contents."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to name the eight carpals in their two rows and the metacarpals and phalanges and locate the carpal tunnel and its contents. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: bones of the hand. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, bones of the hand, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w2-bone-markings-vocab","name":"Bone markings vocabulary","can":"Classify a named bone marking as a projection, depression, or opening and match each term such as process, tuberosity, fossa, or sulcus to its description.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The deltoid tuberosity, the mandibular fossa, and the mental foramen are three bone markings. Which list classifies them in that order?\nA) Projection, depression, opening  |  B) Projection, opening, depression\nC) Depression, depression, opening  |  D) Projection, depression, depression\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Projection, depression, opening.","why":"Classify each by its term. A tuberosity is a large rough projection. A fossa is a shallow basin, which makes it a depression. A foramen is a hole for vessels or nerves, which makes it an opening."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which of these bone markings is a depression?\nA) The greater tubercle  |  B) The mastoid process\nC) The supraorbital foramen  |  D) The intertubercular sulcus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The intertubercular sulcus.","why":"Depressions dip into the bone without passing through it, and the two depression terms are fossa and sulcus. The intertubercular sulcus is a groove between the tubercles of the humerus, so it is a depression."},{"t":"mc","q":"Where one vertebra meets the next, the joining surfaces are smooth and nearly flat. Which kind of marking is this?\nA) A fossa, a depression  |  B) A facet, an articular surface\nC) A tubercle, a projection  |  D) A sulcus, a depression\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A facet, an articular surface.","why":"A facet is a smooth, nearly flat articular surface where one bone meets another. The articular facets of the vertebrae, on their articular processes, are the standard example."},{"t":"mc","q":"In bone-marking vocabulary, what is a tuberosity?\nA) A shallow basin  |  B) A groove\nC) A hole for vessels or nerves  |  D) A large, rough projection\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) A large, rough projection","why":"Every bone marking is a projection, a depression, or an opening. A tuberosity is a projection: a large, rough area where a strong tendon attaches, such as the deltoid tuberosity of the humerus or the radial tuberosity. A tubercle is the smaller, rounded version."},{"t":"mc","q":"The olecranon fossa is an example of which kind of bone marking?\nA) A tubercle, a small rounded bump\nB) A foramen, a hole for vessels or nerves\nC) An epicondyle, a raised area above a condyle\nD) A fossa, a shallow basin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) A fossa, a shallow basin","why":"A fossa is a depression, a basin-like hollow in a bone surface. The olecranon fossa on the back of the distal humerus is a deep one that holds the olecranon of the ulna when the elbow straightens. Other examples include the supraspinous and infraspinous fossae of the scapula."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Classify a named bone marking as a projection, depression, or opening and match each term such as process, tuberosity, fossa, or sulcus to its description. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to classify a named bone marking as a projection, depression, or opening and match each term such as process, tuberosity, fossa, or sulcus to its description."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to classify a named bone marking as a projection, depression, or opening and match each term such as process, tuberosity, fossa, or sulcus to its description. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: bone markings vocabulary. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, bone markings vocabulary, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w2-pelvic-girdle","name":"Hip bone and pelvis","can":"Name the three bones that fuse at the acetabulum, identify the markings of the ilium, ischium, and pubis, and describe the pelvis and its sex differences.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The auricular surface of the ilium joins which bone at the sacroiliac joint?\nA) The coccyx  |  B) The fifth lumbar vertebra\nC) The pubis  |  D) The sacrum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The sacrum","why":"The auricular surface is the ear-shaped area on the posterior, medial side of the ilium. It meets a matching auricular surface on the side of the sacrum, forming the sacroiliac joint. This joint is where the weight of the upper body passes from the vertebral column into the pelvic girdle and on to the legs."},{"t":"mc","q":"The pubic symphysis is the joint where which structures meet at the front of the pelvis?\nA) The ischium and the ilium  |  B) The pubis and the sacrum\nC) The two ilia  |  D) The two pubic bones\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The two pubic bones","why":"The pubic symphysis is the midline joint at the front of the pelvis where the left and right pubic bones meet. A disc of fibrocartilage joins them, making it a cartilaginous joint that is slightly movable. It closes the pelvic ring in front, while the sacroiliac joints close it in back."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which bones, together with the sacrum and coccyx, form the bony pelvis?\nA) The two hip bones  |  B) The two ilia only\nC) The lumbar vertebrae  |  D) The two femurs\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The two hip bones","why":"The bony pelvis is a ring made of the two hip bones in front and on the sides, closed in back by the sacrum and coccyx. Each hip bone includes the fused ilium, ischium, and pubis, so it takes the whole hip bone, not just the ilium, to complete the ring. The femurs attach to the pelvis but are not part of it."},{"t":"mc","q":"The strong, roughened knob of the ischium that you sit on is which marking?\nA) The iliac crest  |  B) The ischial spine\nC) The ischial tuberosity  |  D) The pubic crest\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The ischial tuberosity","why":"The ischial tuberosity is the thick, rough knob at the bottom of the ischium. When you sit upright, your weight rests on the left and right ischial tuberosities, which is why they are often called the sitting bones. Their rough surface also anchors the hamstring muscles."},{"t":"mc","q":"A clinician feels for the prominent bony point at the front end of the iliac crest to locate an injection site. Which marking is it?\nA) The ischial spine  |  B) The anterior superior iliac spine\nC) The anterior inferior iliac spine  |  D) The posterior superior iliac spine\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The anterior superior iliac spine","why":"The anterior superior iliac spine, often shortened to ASIS, is the point where the iliac crest ends at the front. It sits just under the skin, so clinicians can feel it to find injection sites and check whether the pelvis is level. The anterior inferior iliac spine lies a little below it and is much harder to feel."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Name the three bones that fuse at the acetabulum, identify the markings of the ilium, ischium, and pubis, and describe the pelvis and its sex differences. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to name the three bones that fuse at the acetabulum, identify the markings of the ilium, ischium, and pubis, and describe the pelvis and its sex differences."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to name the three bones that fuse at the acetabulum, identify the markings of the ilium, ischium, and pubis, and describe the pelvis and its sex differences. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: hip bone and pelvis. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, hip bone and pelvis, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w2-thigh-knee","name":"Femur and patella markings","can":"Identify the key markings of the femur and the base and apex of the patella.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which markings of the femur articulate with the tibia at the knee?\nA) The greater and lesser trochanters  |  B) The medial and lateral epicondyles\nC) The head and the neck  |  D) The medial and lateral condyles\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The medial and lateral condyles","why":"The medial and lateral condyles are the large, rounded knobs at the distal femur. Their smooth surfaces rest on the medial and lateral condyles of the tibia, forming the two tibiofemoral joints of the knee. The epicondyles sit just above them and serve as attachment points rather than joint surfaces."},{"t":"mc","q":"The fovea capitis is a small pit found on which part of the femur?\nA) The neck  |  B) The lateral condyle\nC) The head  |  D) The greater trochanter\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The head","why":"The fovea capitis is a small pit near the center of the femoral head. A ligament runs from this pit to the acetabulum, helping anchor the head in the socket, and a small artery travels with it. Its name means pit of the head, which is a quick way to remember where it is."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the broad superior border of the patella called?\nA) The apex  |  B) The base\nC) The crest  |  D) The fovea\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The base","why":"The patella is shaped like a rounded triangle pointing down. Its broad top edge is called the base, and its pointed bottom tip is called the apex. The quadriceps tendon attaches along the base, and the patellar ligament leaves from the apex."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which marking is the rounded ball at the proximal end of the femur that seats into the hip socket?\nA) The neck of the femur  |  B) The lateral condyle\nC) The head of the femur  |  D) The greater trochanter\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The head of the femur","why":"The head of the femur is the smooth, rounded ball at the top of the bone. It fits into the acetabulum of the hip bone to form the hip joint, a ball-and-socket joint. The narrower neck connects the head to the shaft, and the small pit on the head is the fovea capitis."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which femoral marking is the notch between the distal condyles on the posterior surface?\nA) The linea aspera  |  B) The intercondylar fossa\nC) The greater trochanter  |  D) The fovea capitis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The intercondylar fossa","why":"The intercondylar fossa is the deep notch between the medial and lateral condyles on the back of the distal femur. The cruciate ligaments of the knee attach inside this notch. On the front of the distal femur, the condyles are joined instead by the smooth patellar surface where the kneecap glides."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the key markings of the femur and the base and apex of the patella. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the key markings of the femur and the base and apex of the patella."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the key markings of the femur and the base and apex of the patella. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: femur and patella markings. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, femur and patella markings, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w2-leg-bones","name":"Tibia and fibula markings","can":"Identify the markings of the tibia and fibula and state which bone bears weight and how to tell them apart.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which leg bone is the weight-bearing bone, and on which side of the leg does it lie?\nA) The tibia, on the medial side  |  B) The tibia, on the lateral side\nC) The fibula, on the lateral side  |  D) The fibula, on the medial side\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The tibia, on the medial side","why":"The tibia is the thick shin bone on the medial side of the leg, and it carries the body's weight from the femur down to the talus. The fibula lies lateral to it and is slender because it does not bear body weight. Remembering that the tibia is medial also tells you that the medial malleolus at the ankle belongs to the tibia."},{"t":"mc","q":"The medial malleolus is the bump on the inner side of the ankle. Which bone bears it?\nA) The calcaneus  |  B) The fibula\nC) The talus  |  D) The tibia\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The tibia","why":"The medial malleolus is the distal end of the tibia, which projects downward on the medial side of the ankle. Because the tibia is the medial bone of the leg, its malleolus is the medial one. Together with the lateral malleolus of the fibula, it forms the sides of the socket that holds the talus at the ankle joint."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which bone bears the lateral malleolus at the ankle?\nA) The talus  |  B) The tibia\nC) The femur  |  D) The fibula\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The fibula","why":"The lateral malleolus is the distal end of the fibula, the bump on the outer side of the ankle. The fibula is the lateral bone of the leg, so its malleolus is the lateral one. It sits a little lower than the medial malleolus and is the common site of an ankle fracture after a rolled ankle."},{"t":"mc","q":"The tibial tuberosity on the front of the tibia is the attachment site for which structure?\nA) The calcaneal tendon  |  B) The quadriceps tendon\nC) The patellar ligament  |  D) The anterior cruciate ligament\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The patellar ligament","why":"The tibial tuberosity is the roughened bump on the anterior tibia just below the knee. The patellar ligament runs from the patella down to this tuberosity. Because the patellar ligament is really the continuation of the quadriceps tendon, this bump is where the pull of the quadriceps finally reaches the tibia to extend the knee."},{"t":"mc","q":"Moving distally from the knee along the medial side of the leg, which bone do you follow down to the ankle?\nA) The femur  |  B) The talus\nC) The fibula  |  D) The tibia\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The tibia","why":"The tibia is the medial bone of the leg, so tracing the inner side of the leg from knee to ankle follows the tibia the whole way. It begins with its medial and lateral condyles at the knee and ends in the medial malleolus, the inner ankle bump. Its sharp anterior border is the shin you can feel under the skin."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the markings of the tibia and fibula and state which bone bears weight and how to tell them apart. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the markings of the tibia and fibula and state which bone bears weight and how to tell them apart."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the markings of the tibia and fibula and state which bone bears weight and how to tell them apart. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: tibia and fibula markings. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, tibia and fibula markings, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w2-foot-bones","name":"Bones and arches of the foot","can":"Name the seven tarsals, the metatarsals, and the phalanges and identify the three arches of the foot.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The calcaneus is large and projects backward behind the ankle. Why does its size and shape suit its job?\nA) It articulates with all five metatarsals, so it must be wide\nB) It absorbs heel strike and gives the calcaneal tendon leverage\nC) It receives the body's weight directly from the tibia\nD) It forms the ankle joint between the two malleoli\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It absorbs heel strike and gives the calcaneal tendon leverage","why":"The calcaneus is the heel bone, the largest tarsal. When you walk, the heel hits the ground first, so this bone must withstand that impact. Its backward projection also anchors the calcaneal (Achilles) tendon far behind the ankle, giving the calf muscles leverage to lift the heel and push off."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which tarsal bone articulates with the tibia and fibula and receives the body's weight from the leg?\nA) The calcaneus  |  B) The navicular\nC) The talus  |  D) The cuboid\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The talus","why":"The talus is the highest tarsal bone. It fits between the medial and lateral malleoli and articulates with the tibia above, forming the ankle (talocrural) joint. Weight passes from the tibia to the talus, and from there it spreads back to the calcaneus and forward through the other tarsals to the metatarsals."},{"t":"mc","q":"How many phalanges does each toe other than the great toe have?\nA) Two  |  B) One\nC) Four  |  D) Three\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Three","why":"Toes 2 through 5 each have three phalanges: a proximal, a middle, and a distal phalanx. The great toe, the hallux, is the exception, with only a proximal and a distal phalanx. This is the same pattern as the fingers and the thumb."},{"t":"mc","q":"How many metatarsals form the sole of the foot, and how are they numbered?\nA) Five, numbered I to V starting at the great toe\nB) Fourteen, numbered so each matches a phalanx\nC) Seven, numbered I to VII starting at the great toe\nD) Five, numbered I to V starting at the little toe\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Five, numbered I to V starting at the great toe","why":"Each foot has five metatarsals, one behind each toe. They are numbered I to V from medial to lateral, so metatarsal I sits behind the great toe (the hallux) and metatarsal V sits behind the little toe. The same medial-to-lateral numbering is used for the toes themselves."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which three arches spread body weight across the foot?\nA) The anterior and posterior longitudinal arches and the transverse arch\nB) The medial and lateral transverse arches and the longitudinal arch\nC) The medial and lateral longitudinal arches and the transverse arch\nD) The medial, intermediate, and lateral longitudinal arches\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The medial and lateral longitudinal arches and the transverse arch","why":"The foot has two longitudinal arches running front to back, a higher medial one and a lower lateral one, plus one transverse arch running side to side across the foot. Together they spread weight between the heel and the balls of the foot and spring back with each step. When the medial longitudinal arch drops, the result is flat feet."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Name the seven tarsals, the metatarsals, and the phalanges and identify the three arches of the foot. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to name the seven tarsals, the metatarsals, and the phalanges and identify the three arches of the foot."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to name the seven tarsals, the metatarsals, and the phalanges and identify the three arches of the foot. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: bones and arches of the foot. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, bones and arches of the foot, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]}]},{"week":6,"system":"Joints","comps":[{"id":"w2-joint-classification","name":"Structural and functional joint classification","can":"Classify a joint by structure as fibrous, cartilaginous, or synovial and by function as synarthrosis, amphiarthrosis, or diarthrosis and state the criteria for each.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A joint is described as freely movable. Which functional class does it belong to?\nA) An amphiarthrosis  |  B) A synarthrosis\nC) A synchondrosis  |  D) A diarthrosis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) A diarthrosis","why":"A diarthrosis is a freely movable joint. All synovial joints, such as the shoulder, elbow, hip, and knee, are diarthroses because their fluid-filled cavity lets the bone ends move over each other. The other functional classes are the immovable synarthrosis and the slightly movable amphiarthrosis."},{"t":"mc","q":"A joint has no synovial cavity, its bones are held by dense connective tissue rich in collagen, and it permits little or no movement. Which structural class is it?\nA) A fibrous joint  |  B) A synovial joint\nC) A diarthrosis  |  D) A cartilaginous joint\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A fibrous joint","why":"Fibrous joints have no cavity, and dense connective tissue rich in collagen fibers holds the bones closely together. That tight binding allows little or no movement. Sutures, syndesmoses (including the interosseous membranes), and gomphoses are the fibrous joints."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which description correctly gives the structural class, the functional class, and the synovial joint type of the elbow?\nA) A cartilaginous joint, an amphiarthrosis, and a symphysis\nB) A fibrous joint, a synarthrosis, and a suture\nC) A synovial joint, a diarthrosis, and a hinge joint\nD) A synovial joint, an amphiarthrosis, and a ball-and-socket joint\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A synovial joint, a diarthrosis, and a hinge joint","why":"The elbow has a synovial cavity and capsule, so structurally it is a synovial joint, and like all synovial joints it is a diarthrosis, freely movable. Its convex and concave surfaces allow only flexion and extension, which makes it a hinge joint. Naming a joint all three ways is exactly what lab practicals ask for."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is a joint, or articulation?\nA) The shaft of a long bone\nB) The lining of a body cavity\nC) The place where two or more bones meet\nD) A cord of tissue that joins muscle to bone\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The place where two or more bones meet","why":"A joint, also called an articulation, is any place where two or more bones meet. Some joints are fused or bound so tightly that they do not move, like the sutures of the skull, while others, like the knee, move freely. Joints are classified both by their structure and by how much movement they allow."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which two criteria define the structural classification of joints?\nA) The blood supply and the nerve supply of the joint\nB) Whether a synovial cavity is present and what tissue binds the bones\nC) The degree of movement and the number of axes\nD) The shape of the bone surfaces and the strength of the ligaments\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Whether a synovial cavity is present and what tissue binds the bones","why":"The structural classification looks at how a joint is built. It asks whether there is a synovial cavity between the bones and what kind of connective tissue holds them together, dense fibrous tissue, cartilage, or an articular capsule. That gives the three structural classes: fibrous, cartilaginous, and synovial."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Classify a joint by structure as fibrous, cartilaginous, or synovial and by function as synarthrosis, amphiarthrosis, or diarthrosis and state the criteria for each. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to classify a joint by structure as fibrous, cartilaginous, or synovial and by function as synarthrosis, amphiarthrosis, or diarthrosis and state the criteria for each."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to classify a joint by structure as fibrous, cartilaginous, or synovial and by function as synarthrosis, amphiarthrosis, or diarthrosis and state the criteria for each. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: structural and functional joint classification. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, structural and functional joint classification, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w2-fibrous-cartilaginous","name":"Fibrous and cartilaginous joints","can":"Describe the three fibrous joint types and the two cartilaginous joint types by structure, mobility, and example.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The pubic bones are joined by a broad disc of fibrocartilage. Which structural type of joint is the pubic symphysis?\nA) A synchondrosis  |  B) A syndesmosis\nC) A symphysis  |  D) A suture\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A symphysis","why":"A symphysis is a cartilaginous joint in which the bones are joined by a broad, flat disc of fibrocartilage. It is slightly movable, an amphiarthrosis. Examples include the pubic symphysis, the manubriosternal joint, and the joints between vertebral bodies, and all symphyses lie in the midline."},{"t":"mc","q":"The shafts of the radius and ulna are bound by a broad sheet of dense connective tissue that allows slight movement. Which type of fibrous joint is this?\nA) A syndesmosis  |  B) A gomphosis\nC) A synostosis  |  D) A suture\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A syndesmosis","why":"A syndesmosis is a fibrous joint in which bones are joined by a ligament or a broad sheet of dense connective tissue, called an interosseous membrane, so slight movement is possible. That makes it an amphiarthrosis. The interosseous membranes between the radius and ulna and between the tibia and fibula are both syndesmoses."},{"t":"mc","q":"The epiphyseal growth plate joins bone to bone with hyaline cartilage and permits no movement. Which joint type is it?\nA) A synchondrosis  |  B) A syndesmosis\nC) A symphysis  |  D) A gomphosis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A synchondrosis","why":"A synchondrosis is a cartilaginous joint in which the connecting material is hyaline cartilage. It is immovable, a synarthrosis. The epiphyseal growth plate is the classic example, and when bone replaces its cartilage after growth ends, the joint becomes a synostosis."},{"t":"mc","q":"A fibrous joint has more space between the bones than a suture, and the bones are united by a ligament, so it is slightly movable. Which fibrous joint type fits?\nA) A synchondrosis  |  B) A suture\nC) A gomphosis  |  D) A syndesmosis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) A syndesmosis","why":"A syndesmosis is a fibrous joint with more distance between the bones and more dense connective tissue than a suture, arranged as a ligament. This looser binding allows slight movement, so it is an amphiarthrosis. The distal tibiofibular joint, held by the anterior tibiofibular ligament, is the standard example."},{"t":"mc","q":"A synchondrosis at a growth plate is gradually replaced by bone, fusing two parts into one. What is the resulting joint called?\nA) A symphysis  |  B) A synostosis\nC) A suture  |  D) A syndesmosis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A synostosis","why":"A synostosis is a joint where two separate bones, or parts of a bone, have fused completely into one by bone. When growth ends, the hyaline cartilage of the epiphyseal plate is replaced by bone, and the synchondrosis becomes a synostosis. Skull sutures that ossify in adulthood also become synostoses."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Describe the three fibrous joint types and the two cartilaginous joint types by structure, mobility, and example. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to describe the three fibrous joint types and the two cartilaginous joint types by structure, mobility, and example."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to describe the three fibrous joint types and the two cartilaginous joint types by structure, mobility, and example. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: fibrous and cartilaginous joints. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, fibrous and cartilaginous joints, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w2-synovial-structure","name":"Synovial joint structure and accessory parts","can":"Identify the structures of a synovial joint, its accessory structures such as menisci, labra, bursae, and tendon sheaths, and its nerve and blood supply.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The fibular collateral ligament of the knee lies outside the articular capsule. Which category of ligament is it?\nA) A retinaculum  |  B) An intracapsular ligament\nC) An articular disc  |  D) An extracapsular ligament\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) An extracapsular ligament","why":"Ligaments that lie outside the articular capsule are called extracapsular ligaments. The fibular (lateral) and tibial (medial) collateral ligaments of the knee are examples. They reinforce the sides of the joint and resist sideways bending."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which connective tissue forms the fibrous membrane, the outer layer of an articular capsule?\nA) Dense regular connective tissue  |  B) Hyaline cartilage\nC) Areolar connective tissue  |  D) Dense irregular connective tissue\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Dense irregular connective tissue","why":"The fibrous membrane is made of dense irregular connective tissue, in which collagen fibers run in many directions. That arrangement lets the capsule resist pulling from many angles while staying flexible. It attaches to the periosteum of the bones, and its thickened bundles form many of the joint's ligaments."},{"t":"mc","q":"A tube-shaped bursa wraps a tendon where it passes through a tight space at the wrist. What is this structure called?\nA) An articular fat pad  |  B) A tendon sheath\nC) An articular disc  |  D) A labrum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A tendon sheath","why":"A tendon sheath is a tube-shaped bursa that wraps around a tendon where it runs through a tight space, such as at the wrist and ankle. The fluid inside lets the tendon slide with little friction as the muscle contracts. Ordinary bursae are flat sacs, while a tendon sheath is rolled into a tube."},{"t":"mc","q":"A rim of fibrocartilage deepens the socket of a ball-and-socket joint and is found at the shoulder and hip. What is this structure?\nA) A bursa  |  B) A labrum\nC) An articular fat pad  |  D) A meniscus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A labrum","why":"A labrum is a ring of fibrocartilage attached around the edge of a socket, which makes the socket deeper and slightly larger. The glenoid labrum surrounds the glenoid cavity of the shoulder, and the acetabular labrum surrounds the acetabulum of the hip. A deeper socket holds the ball more securely."},{"t":"mc","q":"The fibrous membrane of a synovial joint's articular capsule attaches to which covering of the bones?\nA) The articular cartilage  |  B) The synovial membrane\nC) The endosteum  |  D) The periosteum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The periosteum","why":"The fibrous membrane is the strong outer layer of the articular capsule, made of dense irregular connective tissue. It attaches to the periosteum, the connective tissue membrane covering the outer surface of each bone, so the capsule is continuous with the bones it unites. Thickened bundles of this membrane form the joint's ligaments."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the structures of a synovial joint, its accessory structures such as menisci, labra, bursae, and tendon sheaths, and its nerve and blood supply. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the structures of a synovial joint, its accessory structures such as menisci, labra, bursae, and tendon sheaths, and its nerve and blood supply."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the structures of a synovial joint, its accessory structures such as menisci, labra, bursae, and tendon sheaths, and its nerve and blood supply. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: synovial joint structure and accessory parts. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, synovial joint structure and accessory parts, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w2-joint-movements","name":"Movements at synovial joints","can":"Demonstrate and name the gliding, angular, rotational, and special movements permitted at synovial joints.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"From anatomical position, a person turns the forearm so the palm faces posteriorly. Which movement is this, and what happens to the forearm bones?\nA) Supination, with the radius crossing over the ulna\nB) Pronation, with the ulna crossing over the radius\nC) Pronation, with the radius crossing over the ulna\nD) Medial rotation, with neither forearm bone moving\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Pronation, with the radius crossing over the ulna.","why":"Start in anatomical position with the palm facing anteriorly, which is supination. Turning the palm to face posteriorly is pronation. In pronation the radius crosses over the ulna, using the proximal and distal radioulnar joints."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which movement is the direct opposite of abduction?\nA) Circumduction  |  B) Adduction\nC) Rotation  |  D) Extension\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Adduction","why":"Abduction moves a body part away from the midline, as when you raise your arm out to the side. Adduction brings it back toward the midline, so the two are opposites. A handy memory aid is that adduction adds the limb back to the body."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient rolls the ankle so the sole turns inward, and then a therapist turns it outward. Name the two movements in order.\nA) Eversion, then inversion  |  B) Dorsiflexion, then plantar flexion\nC) Pronation, then supination  |  D) Inversion, then eversion\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Inversion, then eversion","why":"Turning the sole inward, toward the other foot, is inversion. Turning it outward is eversion. Rolled ankles usually happen in inversion, which is why the lateral side of the ankle, including the lateral malleolus and its ligaments, is so often injured."},{"t":"mc","q":"Starting in anatomical position, you rotate the forearm so the palm faces backward, then return it. Which sequence of movements is this?\nA) Pronation, then supination  |  B) Supination, then pronation\nC) Protraction, then retraction  |  D) Inversion, then eversion\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Pronation, then supination","why":"In anatomical position the palm faces anteriorly, which is the supinated position. Turning the palm to face posteriorly is pronation, and returning it to face forward is supination. The radius crosses over the ulna in pronation and lies parallel to it in supination."},{"t":"mc","q":"Reaching the arms forward, then squaring the shoulders back, moves the scapulae through which pair of movements?\nA) Rotation, then circumduction  |  B) Protraction, then retraction\nC) Retraction, then protraction  |  D) Elevation, then depression\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Protraction, then retraction","why":"Reaching forward slides the scapulae forward around the chest wall, which is protraction. Squaring the shoulders back pulls the scapulae toward the spine, which is retraction. Both happen in the horizontal plane."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Demonstrate and name the gliding, angular, rotational, and special movements permitted at synovial joints. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to demonstrate and name the gliding, angular, rotational, and special movements permitted at synovial joints."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to demonstrate and name the gliding, angular, rotational, and special movements permitted at synovial joints. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: movements at synovial joints. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, movements at synovial joints, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w2-synovial-types","name":"Six synovial joint types","can":"Name the six synovial joint types with their axes of movement and give a body example of each.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A joint lets a limb move away from the midline, back toward it, forward and back, and trace a circle with its distal end, but it does not rotate. How many axes of motion are needed for these movements?\nA) One axis  |  B) Two axes\nC) Three axes  |  D) Four axes\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Two axes","why":"Abduction and adduction happen around one axis, and flexion and extension happen around a second axis. Circumduction is not a separate axis; it is a blend of those four movements in sequence that makes the distal end trace a circle. Since the joint does not rotate, two axes are enough, which is the pattern of biaxial joints like the condyloid radiocarpal joint."},{"t":"mc","q":"The joint between the trapezium and the first metacarpal is a saddle joint. What does this joint shape let the thumb do?\nA) Move only like a hinge in one plane\nB) Swing across the palm to touch the fingertips (opposition)\nC) Bend at a middle phalanx like the other fingers\nD) Rotate the radius to turn the palm over\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Swing across the palm to touch the fingertips (opposition)","why":"In a saddle joint, both surfaces are curved like a horse saddle, one fitting crosswise into the other. This allows movement in two planes, so the thumb can move toward and away from the palm and across it. Combining those motions lets the thumb meet the fingertips, which makes a precision grip possible."},{"t":"mc","q":"A synovial joint has flat or slightly curved surfaces that glide across one another. Which type is it?\nA) A hinge joint  |  B) A saddle joint\nC) A plane joint  |  D) A pivot joint\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A plane joint","why":"A plane joint has flat or slightly curved articulating surfaces that slide back and forth and side to side over each other, a movement called gliding. Examples are the intercarpal joints in the wrist and the intertarsal joints in the foot. The angle between the bones does not change during gliding."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which type of synovial joint moves around three axes, allowing triaxial movement?\nA) A condyloid joint  |  B) A pivot joint\nC) A ball-and-socket joint  |  D) A hinge joint\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A ball-and-socket joint","why":"A ball-and-socket joint has a rounded ball fitting into a cup, so the bone can move around three axes: flexion and extension, abduction and adduction, and rotation. The shoulder and hip are the two examples. This makes them the most freely movable synovial joints."},{"t":"mc","q":"The carpometacarpal joint of the thumb has surfaces that are each both concave and convex. Which synovial joint type is it?\nA) A plane joint  |  B) A pivot joint\nC) A saddle joint  |  D) A condyloid joint\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A saddle joint","why":"In a saddle joint, each articulating surface curves up in one direction and down in the other, like a rider sitting in a saddle. The two surfaces fit together and allow biaxial movement. The carpometacarpal joint at the base of the thumb is the main example, and it allows the thumb to move across the palm in opposition."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Name the six synovial joint types with their axes of movement and give a body example of each. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to name the six synovial joint types with their axes of movement and give a body example of each."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to name the six synovial joint types with their axes of movement and give a body example of each. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: six synovial joint types. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, six synovial joint types, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w2-shoulder-knee","name":"Shoulder and knee joint structure","can":"Describe the articulating bones and supporting ligaments of the glenohumeral and tibiofemoral joints and identify the rotator cuff, menisci, and cruciate and collateral ligaments.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which knee structure is the C-shaped fibrocartilage disc on the medial side of the joint?\nA) The medial meniscus  |  B) The transverse ligament\nC) The lateral meniscus  |  D) The tibial collateral ligament\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The medial meniscus","why":"The knee has two menisci, fibrocartilage pads that sit on top of the tibial condyles. The medial meniscus is C-shaped, while the lateral meniscus is more circular, almost O-shaped. The medial meniscus is one of the three structures torn in the unhappy triad."},{"t":"mc","q":"An athlete takes a lateral blow to a planted knee and tears the tibial collateral ligament, the medial meniscus, and the anterior cruciate ligament together. What is this injury pattern called?\nA) A dislocated knee  |  B) Bursitis\nC) A rotator cuff tear  |  D) The unhappy triad\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The unhappy triad","why":"The unhappy triad is a knee injury from a blow to the lateral side of a planted leg. The force bends the knee inward and tears three structures at once: the tibial collateral ligament, the medial meniscus, and the anterior cruciate ligament. It is common in contact sports such as football."},{"t":"mc","q":"A surgeon notes a thin, loose articular capsule and a shallow socket holding a large bone head, making the joint highly mobile but unstable. Which joint is being described?\nA) The coxal (hip) joint  |  B) The glenohumeral (shoulder) joint\nC) The tibiofemoral (knee) joint  |  D) The humeroulnar (elbow) joint\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The glenohumeral (shoulder) joint","why":"The glenohumeral joint pairs the large head of the humerus with the small, shallow glenoid cavity of the scapula, inside a thin, loose capsule. That design gives the shoulder the greatest range of motion of any joint, but the head is held in place mostly by the rotator cuff muscles rather than by bone or ligaments. The result is a joint that dislocates easily."},{"t":"mc","q":"A ligament crosses within the knee joint and stops the tibia from sliding forward relative to the femur. Which ligament is described?\nA) The anterior cruciate ligament  |  B) The tibial collateral ligament\nC) The fibular collateral ligament  |  D) The posterior cruciate ligament\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The anterior cruciate ligament","why":"The anterior cruciate ligament, or ACL, is an intracapsular ligament that crosses inside the knee from the front of the tibia to the femur. Its job is to stop the tibia from sliding forward under the femur. It is torn in roughly 70 percent of serious knee injuries."},{"t":"mc","q":"A clinician examines a joint that contains three articulations in one joint cavity: a medial and a lateral articulation between the femur and tibia, plus one between the patella and the femur. Which joint is this?\nA) The knee (tibiofemoral) joint  |  B) The shoulder joint\nC) The hip joint  |  D) The ankle joint\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The knee (tibiofemoral) joint","why":"The knee is the largest and most complex joint in the body. Inside one joint cavity it has three articulations: the medial and lateral tibiofemoral joints between the femoral and tibial condyles, and the patellofemoral joint between the patella and the patellar surface of the femur. It works as a modified hinge joint."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Describe the articulating bones and supporting ligaments of the glenohumeral and tibiofemoral joints and identify the rotator cuff, menisci, and cruciate and collateral ligaments. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to describe the articulating bones and supporting ligaments of the glenohumeral and tibiofemoral joints and identify the rotator cuff, menisci, and cruciate and collateral ligaments."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to describe the articulating bones and supporting ligaments of the glenohumeral and tibiofemoral joints and identify the rotator cuff, menisci, and cruciate and collateral ligaments. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: shoulder and knee joint structure. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, shoulder and knee joint structure, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]}]},{"week":7,"system":"Cardiovascular","comps":[{"id":"cv-surfaces","name":"Heart wall layers, pericardium, and internal features","can":"Name the three heart wall layers and the pericardial membranes, and locate internal features such as auricles, pectinate muscles, fossa ovalis, and trabeculae carneae.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Fluid builds up in the pericardial cavity and presses on the heart from outside. Which heart function is most directly limited?\nA) Oxygen pickup in the lungs  |  B) Closing of the valves\nC) Spread of the electrical signal  |  D) Filling of the chambers\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Filling of the chambers","why":"The fibrous pericardium does not stretch much, so extra fluid in the pericardial cavity has nowhere to go but inward. That pressure squeezes the heart from outside and keeps the chambers from expanding to fill with blood. With less filling, less blood is pumped out with each beat."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which list gives the three layers of the heart wall in order from outermost to innermost?\nA) Epicardium, then myocardium, then endocardium\nB) Endocardium, then myocardium, then epicardium\nC) Epicardium, then endocardium, then myocardium\nD) Myocardium, then epicardium, then endocardium\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Epicardium, then myocardium, then endocardium","why":"The heart wall is built from outside in as epicardium, myocardium, and endocardium. The prefixes give the order away: epi means upon, myo means muscle, and endo means within. So the thin membrane on the surface is outside, the thick muscle is in the middle, and the smooth lining is inside."},{"t":"mc","q":"The term myocardial infarction names the heart wall layer that dies in a heart attack. Which layer is it?\nA) The endocardium  |  B) The myocardium\nC) The epicardium  |  D) The fibrous pericardium\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The myocardium","why":"Myocardial means relating to the myocardium, the thick middle layer of cardiac muscle that contracts. An infarction is an area of tissue that dies when its blood supply is cut off. In a heart attack a blocked coronary artery starves part of the myocardium of oxygen, so the pumping muscle itself is damaged."},{"t":"mc","q":"A thin film of serous fluid fills the pericardial cavity. What does this fluid do during every heartbeat?\nA) It stores extra blood for the next beat\nB) It supplies oxygen to the outer layers of the heart wall\nC) It reduces friction as the serous layers slide\nD) It keeps the heart from overfilling with blood\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It reduces friction as the serous layers slide","why":"The heart moves with every beat, roughly a hundred thousand times a day, and its surface would rub against the sac around it. The serous fluid in the pericardial cavity lets the visceral and parietal layers slide smoothly over each other with very little friction. The same idea applies to the pleural fluid around the lungs and the peritoneal fluid in the abdomen."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the main job of the fluid-filled pericardial cavity?\nA) It stores extra blood for the ventricles\nB) It supplies oxygen to the heart wall\nC) It lets the heart beat with little friction\nD) It anchors the heart to the diaphragm\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It lets the heart beat with little friction","why":"The pericardial cavity holds a thin film of serous fluid between the parietal and visceral layers of the serous pericardium. As the heart contracts and relaxes, the two layers slide past each other on this fluid with very little friction. Without it, the beating heart would rub against its sac."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Name the three heart wall layers and the pericardial membranes, and locate internal features such as auricles, pectinate muscles, fossa ovalis, and trabeculae carneae. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to name the three heart wall layers and the pericardial membranes, and locate internal features such as auricles, pectinate muscles, fossa ovalis, and trabeculae carneae."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to name the three heart wall layers and the pericardial membranes, and locate internal features such as auricles, pectinate muscles, fossa ovalis, and trabeculae carneae. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: heart wall layers, pericardium, and internal features. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, heart wall layers, pericardium, and internal features, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"cv-chambers","name":"Heart chambers and septa","can":"Identify the four heart chambers and the interatrial and interventricular septa, and state what blood each chamber receives and where it sends it.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which chamber forms most of the anterior surface of the heart, lying just behind the sternum and ribs?\nA) The left ventricle  |  B) The right atrium\nC) The left atrium  |  D) The right ventricle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The right ventricle","why":"The heart is rotated in the chest so that its right side faces forward. As a result, the right ventricle forms most of the anterior, or sternocostal, surface, lying right behind the sternum and ribs. The left ventricle sits more to the left and underneath, forming the apex and resting on the diaphragm."},{"t":"mc","q":"In the adult heart, the septum completely separates the right side from the left side. Why is this full separation important?\nA) It anchors the four heart valves in place\nB) It keeps oxygen-poor and oxygen-rich blood from mixing\nC) It mixes the two bloods so oxygen levels even out\nD) It lets the right and left sides beat at different times\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It keeps oxygen-poor and oxygen-rich blood from mixing","why":"The right side of the heart handles oxygen-poor blood on its way to the lungs, and the left side handles oxygen-rich blood on its way to the body. A complete septum keeps these two streams apart. When there is a hole in the septum, called a septal defect, the bloods can mix and the blood sent to the body carries less oxygen."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which statement correctly compares the atria and the ventricles?\nA) Atria and ventricles both pump blood out of the heart\nB) Atria pump blood out, and ventricles receive returning blood\nC) Atria have thicker walls because they hold more blood\nD) Atria receive returning blood, and ventricles pump it out\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Atria receive returning blood, and ventricles pump it out","why":"The atria are the receiving chambers: blood returning from the body or lungs enters them first. The ventricles are the pumping chambers that push blood out into the pulmonary trunk and aorta. This difference in job explains why the ventricles have much thicker walls than the atria."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why is the wall of the left ventricle thicker than the wall of the right ventricle?\nA) It receives more blood than the right ventricle\nB) It pumps blood through the systemic circuit to the whole body\nC) It must push blood up into the left atrium\nD) It holds the coronary sinus within its wall\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It pumps blood through the systemic circuit to the whole body","why":"The left ventricle pumps blood into the aorta and through the systemic circuit, which reaches every tissue of the body. Pushing blood through such a long, branching circuit takes much more force than pushing it to the nearby lungs. Thicker myocardium gives the left ventricle that extra force."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why can the right ventricle have a thinner wall than the left ventricle?\nA) It receives oxygen-rich blood that is easier to move\nB) It pumps less blood with each beat\nC) Its valves do most of the pumping work\nD) It only pumps blood to the nearby lungs\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It only pumps blood to the nearby lungs","why":"The right ventricle drives the pulmonary circuit, a short loop to the lungs right beside the heart. Moving blood through this short circuit takes less force than pushing it through the whole body. So the right ventricle needs a thinner wall, and in cross section it wraps around the thicker left ventricle in a crescent shape."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the four heart chambers and the interatrial and interventricular septa, and state what blood each chamber receives and where it sends it. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the four heart chambers and the interatrial and interventricular septa, and state what blood each chamber receives and where it sends it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the four heart chambers and the interatrial and interventricular septa, and state what blood each chamber receives and where it sends it. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: heart chambers and septa. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, heart chambers and septa, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"cv-valves","name":"Heart valves and what each separates","can":"Identify the four heart valves by type and location, and state which two chambers or vessels each one separates and where it prevents backflow.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"If the tricuspid valve could not close at all, blood pushed backward by the right ventricle would enter the right atrium and back up toward which vessels?\nA) The pulmonary veins  |  B) The aorta\nC) The coronary arteries  |  D) The venae cavae\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The venae cavae","why":"With no working tricuspid valve, each right ventricular contraction sends blood back into the right atrium. The right atrium is fed by the superior and inferior venae cavae, so blood backs up into those veins and the veins of the body. Tracing backward from a failed valve tells you where the congestion builds."},{"t":"mc","q":"If the mitral valve could not close at all, blood pushed backward by the left ventricle would enter the left atrium and back up toward which organs?\nA) The body tissues, through the venae cavae\nB) The heart wall, through the coronary sinus\nC) The lungs, through the pulmonary veins\nD) The body tissues, through the aorta\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The lungs, through the pulmonary veins","why":"With no working mitral valve, each left ventricular contraction sends blood back into the left atrium. The left atrium is fed by the pulmonary veins from the lungs, so blood backs up into those veins and the lungs. This is why severe mitral valve failure can cause fluid to build up in the lungs."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which two valves are the atrioventricular valves?\nA) The pulmonary and aortic valves  |  B) The tricuspid and mitral valves\nC) The tricuspid and pulmonary valves  |  D) The mitral and aortic valves\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The tricuspid and mitral valves","why":"Atrioventricular valves sit between an atrium and a ventricle, which is what the name means. The tricuspid valve is on the right side and the mitral valve is on the left side. Both have chordae tendineae and papillary muscles that hold them shut when the ventricles contract."},{"t":"mc","q":"When the left ventricle contracts, which valve closes to keep blood from flowing back into the left atrium?\nA) The tricuspid valve  |  B) The pulmonary valve\nC) The aortic valve  |  D) The mitral valve\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The mitral valve","why":"Each valve prevents backflow into the chamber behind it. The mitral valve sits between the left atrium and left ventricle, so when the left ventricle squeezes, the mitral valve snaps shut and blood cannot return to the left atrium. Blood is then forced out through the open aortic valve instead."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which two valves have pocket-shaped cusps and no chordae tendineae?\nA) The mitral and aortic valves  |  B) The tricuspid and mitral valves\nC) The tricuspid and pulmonary valves  |  D) The pulmonary and aortic valves\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The pulmonary and aortic valves","why":"The pulmonary and aortic valves are the semilunar valves, named for their half-moon shaped cusps. Each cusp is a small pocket that fills with blood when flow tries to reverse, and the filled pockets press together to seal the opening. Because they close themselves this way, they need no chordae tendineae or papillary muscles."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the four heart valves by type and location, and state which two chambers or vessels each one separates and where it prevents backflow. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the four heart valves by type and location, and state which two chambers or vessels each one separates and where it prevents backflow."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the four heart valves by type and location, and state which two chambers or vessels each one separates and where it prevents backflow. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: heart valves and what each separates. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, heart valves and what each separates, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"cv-valve-support","name":"Chordae tendineae and papillary muscles","can":"Identify the chordae tendineae and papillary muscles and describe how they anchor and hold the atrioventricular valves shut.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A heart attack kills a papillary muscle in the left ventricle. Which valve is most at risk of failing?\nA) The mitral valve  |  B) The aortic valve\nC) The tricuspid valve  |  D) The pulmonary valve\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The mitral valve","why":"The papillary muscles of the left ventricle anchor the chordae tendineae of the mitral valve. If one dies, its chordae go slack and the mitral cusps can be pushed back into the left atrium. The aortic valve also opens from the left ventricle, but it has no papillary muscles."},{"t":"mc","q":"After a heart attack, a papillary muscle in the left ventricle is damaged, and its chordae tendineae can no longer hold the valve cusps shut. When the left ventricle contracts, into which chamber will some blood leak backward?\nA) Left atrium  |  B) Left ventricle\nC) Right ventricle  |  D) Right atrium\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Left atrium","why":"The papillary muscles of the left ventricle anchor the chordae tendineae of the mitral valve, the valve between the left atrium and the left ventricle. When the ventricle contracts, these structures normally hold the mitral cusps closed so blood can only go out into the aorta. If a papillary muscle fails, the cusps can be pushed back and some blood leaks backward into the left atrium."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which heart valves have chordae tendineae attached to their cusps?\nA) All four heart valves  |  B) Only the pulmonary and aortic valves\nC) Only the mitral valve  |  D) Only the tricuspid and mitral valves\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Only the tricuspid and mitral valves","why":"Chordae tendineae belong only to the atrioventricular valves, the tricuspid and mitral. These valves face the full force of ventricular contraction and need cords and papillary muscles to keep their cusps from flipping into the atria. The semilunar valves have pocket-shaped cusps that fill with blood and close themselves, so they have no chordae."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has ruptured chordae tendineae. Which valves are most directly affected?\nA) All four valves equally  |  B) The semilunar valves\nC) The AV valves  |  D) The pulmonary valve only\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The AV valves","why":"Chordae tendineae attach only to the cusps of the AV valves, the tricuspid and mitral. When they rupture, the cusps lose their anchor and can be pushed back into the atrium when the ventricle contracts. The semilunar valves have no chordae and are not directly affected."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which sequence traces the anchoring chain that holds an AV valve shut, from the ventricle wall to the valve?\nA) Papillary muscle, chordae tendineae, valve cusp\nB) Chordae tendineae, papillary muscle, valve cusp\nC) Trabeculae carneae, pectinate muscle, valve cusp\nD) Papillary muscle, valve cusp, chordae tendineae\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Papillary muscle, chordae tendineae, valve cusp","why":"The papillary muscles rise from the ventricle wall. Chordae tendineae run from their tips up to the edges of the AV valve cusps. When the ventricle contracts, this chain stays taut and keeps the cusps from being pushed back into the atrium, which is why damage anywhere along it can make the valve fail."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the chordae tendineae and papillary muscles and describe how they anchor and hold the atrioventricular valves shut. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the chordae tendineae and papillary muscles and describe how they anchor and hold the atrioventricular valves shut."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the chordae tendineae and papillary muscles and describe how they anchor and hold the atrioventricular valves shut. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: chordae tendineae and papillary muscles. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, chordae tendineae and papillary muscles, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"cv-cardiac-muscle","name":"Cardiac muscle tissue and the intercalated disc","can":"Describe cardiac muscle tissue and identify the cardiomyocyte, striations, and intercalated disc with its desmosomes and gap junctions.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Cardiac muscle cells are joined end to end by which structures?\nA) Synapses  |  B) Tight junctions\nC) Hemidesmosomes  |  D) Intercalated discs\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Intercalated discs","why":"Cardiac muscle cells are joined end to end by intercalated discs, which show up as dark lines across the fibers under the microscope. Each disc contains desmosomes, which hold the cells together as the heart contracts, and gap junctions, which let the contraction signal pass directly from cell to cell. Together they let the heart muscle contract as a coordinated unit."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which two cell junctions are found in the intercalated discs of cardiac muscle?\nA) Tight junctions and desmosomes  |  B) Tight junctions and hemidesmosomes\nC) Hemidesmosomes and gap junctions  |  D) Desmosomes and gap junctions\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Desmosomes and gap junctions","why":"Intercalated discs, where cardiac muscle cells meet end to end, contain desmosomes and gap junctions. The desmosomes keep the cells from pulling apart as the heart contracts, and the gap junctions let the contraction signal pass directly from one cell to the next. Together they let the heart wall work as one coordinated unit."},{"t":"mc","q":"Two cell junctions are found in the intercalated discs of cardiac muscle. Which one holds neighboring cells together so they do not pull apart as the heart contracts?\nA) Tight junctions  |  B) Hemidesmosomes\nC) Gap junctions  |  D) Desmosomes\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Desmosomes","why":"Intercalated discs contain desmosomes and gap junctions, and each has a different job. Desmosomes strongly bind one cell to the next and keep them from separating under tension, which matters in a muscle that contracts with every heartbeat. Gap junctions are channels that let the impulse pass from cell to cell so the heart contracts as a unit."},{"t":"mc","q":"Within the intercalated discs, which junctions let the contraction signal pass directly from one cardiomyocyte to the next?\nA) Gap junctions  |  B) Tight junctions\nC) Desmosomes  |  D) Sarcomeres\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Gap junctions","why":"Gap junctions are tiny protein channels that connect the cytoplasm of neighboring cells. In the intercalated discs they let the electrical signal for contraction pass straight from one cardiomyocyte to the next. This is why the heart muscle contracts as one coordinated unit rather than cell by cell."},{"t":"mc","q":"Under the microscope, a tissue shows short, branching, striated cells, each with one central nucleus, joined end to end by dark crossing lines. Which tissue is it?\nA) Dense regular connective tissue  |  B) Cardiac muscle\nC) Smooth muscle  |  D) Skeletal muscle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Cardiac muscle","why":"Cardiac muscle cells are short and branched, with striations and usually one central nucleus. The dark lines crossing the fibers are intercalated discs, the junctions where cells meet end to end, and they are found only in cardiac muscle. Branching plus intercalated discs is the quickest way to identify this tissue on a slide."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Describe cardiac muscle tissue and identify the cardiomyocyte, striations, and intercalated disc with its desmosomes and gap junctions. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to describe cardiac muscle tissue and identify the cardiomyocyte, striations, and intercalated disc with its desmosomes and gap junctions."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to describe cardiac muscle tissue and identify the cardiomyocyte, striations, and intercalated disc with its desmosomes and gap junctions. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: cardiac muscle tissue and the intercalated disc. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, cardiac muscle tissue and the intercalated disc, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"cv-blood-pathway","name":"Pathway of blood through the heart","can":"Trace one drop of blood through the four chambers and four valves of the heart in correct order.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A red blood cell leaves a vein in the foot and travels to the left ventricle. In what order does it pass through the other three chambers?\nA) Right ventricle, right atrium, left atrium\nB) Right atrium, left atrium\nC) Left atrium, right atrium, right ventricle\nD) Right atrium, right ventricle, left atrium\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Right atrium, right ventricle, left atrium","why":"Blood from the foot returns through the leg veins and the inferior vena cava to the right atrium. It crosses the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle, which pumps it through the pulmonary circuit to the lungs. The pulmonary veins return it to the left atrium, and the mitral valve lets it into the left ventricle."},{"t":"mc","q":"A drop of blood is in the pulmonary trunk. After it goes to the lungs and comes back to the heart, which valve will it cross second?\nA) The mitral valve  |  B) The pulmonary valve\nC) The aortic valve  |  D) The tricuspid valve\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The aortic valve","why":"From the lungs, blood returns through the pulmonary veins into the left atrium. The first valve it crosses is the mitral valve into the left ventricle, and the second is the aortic valve as the left ventricle pumps it into the aorta."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which sequence correctly traces blood from the right ventricle to the left atrium?\nA) Aortic valve, aorta, lungs, pulmonary veins, left atrium\nB) Pulmonary valve, pulmonary trunk, lungs, venae cavae, left atrium\nC) Pulmonary valve, pulmonary trunk, lungs, pulmonary veins, left atrium\nD) Tricuspid valve, pulmonary trunk, lungs, pulmonary veins, left atrium\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Pulmonary valve, pulmonary trunk, lungs, pulmonary veins, left atrium","why":"This route is the pulmonary circuit. Blood leaves the right ventricle through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk, travels in the pulmonary arteries to the lungs, picks up oxygen, and returns through the pulmonary veins to the left atrium. Everything in this loop carries the word pulmonary, which makes it easier to trace."},{"t":"mc","q":"A drop of blood is being pumped out of the right ventricle. Which structure does it pass through next?\nA) The tricuspid valve  |  B) The pulmonary valve\nC) The aortic valve  |  D) The mitral valve\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The pulmonary valve","why":"When the right ventricle contracts, blood leaves through the pulmonary valve, the semilunar valve at the ventricle's exit, and enters the pulmonary trunk. From there it travels to the lungs. The pulmonary valve then closes so the blood cannot fall back into the right ventricle."},{"t":"mc","q":"A drop of blood has just returned from the lungs through the pulmonary veins. Which chamber does it enter?\nA) The right atrium  |  B) The right ventricle\nC) The left atrium  |  D) The left ventricle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The left atrium","why":"The four pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood from the lungs into the left atrium. This is the end of the pulmonary circuit and the start of the left side of the heart. From the left atrium, the blood passes the mitral valve into the left ventricle."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Trace one drop of blood through the four chambers and four valves of the heart in correct order. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace one drop of blood through the four chambers and four valves of the heart in correct order."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to trace one drop of blood through the four chambers and four valves of the heart in correct order. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: pathway of blood through the heart. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, pathway of blood through the heart, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"bvn-great-vessels","name":"Great vessels and aortic arch","can":"Identify the great vessels attached to the base of the heart and state which circuit each one serves.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A catheter is pushed up the descending thoracic aorta and around the aortic arch toward the ascending aorta. Which branch of the arch does it pass first?\nA) The left subclavian artery  |  B) The brachiocephalic trunk\nC) The left common carotid artery  |  D) The right subclavian artery\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The left subclavian artery","why":"Moving from the right side of the body to the left, the arch gives off the brachiocephalic trunk, the left common carotid artery, and the left subclavian artery. A catheter coming up from the descending aorta travels the arch from left to right, so it meets the branches in reverse order. The left subclavian is the first one it passes."},{"t":"mc","q":"A clot blocks the brachiocephalic trunk near its origin. Which regions lose much of their arterial supply?\nA) The left upper limb and the left side of the head and neck\nB) Both upper limbs, but not the head and neck\nC) The left side of the head and neck only\nD) The right upper limb and the right side of the head and neck\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The right upper limb and the right side of the head and neck","why":"The brachiocephalic trunk is the shared stem for the right side only, dividing into the right common carotid artery to the head and neck and the right subclavian artery to the upper limb. A block at its origin cuts off both of those branches. The left side is unaffected because the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries leave the arch on their own."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why does the aorta arise from the left ventricle rather than the right?\nA) The left ventricle receives blood straight from the venae cavae\nB) The left ventricle is the chamber nearest the lungs\nC) The left ventricle holds oxygen-poor blood bound for the lungs\nD) The left ventricle holds oxygen-rich blood bound for the body\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The left ventricle holds oxygen-rich blood bound for the body","why":"Blood reaching the left ventricle has just returned from the lungs through the left atrium, so it is rich in O2. The aorta carries blood to the tissues of the body, which need that oxygen. Connecting the aorta to the left ventricle sends freshly oxygenated blood into the systemic circuit."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why does the pulmonary trunk arise from the right ventricle?\nA) The right ventricle receives blood from the pulmonary veins\nB) The right ventricle has the thickest wall of the four chambers\nC) The right ventricle holds oxygen-poor blood that must go to the lungs\nD) The right ventricle holds oxygen-rich blood bound for the body\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The right ventricle holds oxygen-poor blood that must go to the lungs","why":"The right ventricle receives oxygen-poor blood that has just returned from the body. That blood needs to pick up O2, so the right ventricle pumps it into the pulmonary trunk and on to the lungs. Linking the right ventricle to the pulmonary trunk sends used blood straight to be refreshed."},{"t":"mc","q":"In the pulmonary circuit, blood that has picked up oxygen in the lung capillaries leaves the lungs through which vessels?\nA) Pulmonary veins  |  B) Pulmonary arteries\nC) Pulmonary trunk  |  D) Venae cavae\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Pulmonary veins","why":"After gas exchange in the lung capillaries, oxygen-rich blood collects into the pulmonary veins. There are four of them, two from each lung, and they carry the blood back to the left atrium. They are veins because they carry blood toward the heart, even though their blood is rich in oxygen."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the great vessels attached to the base of the heart and state which circuit each one serves. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the great vessels attached to the base of the heart and state which circuit each one serves."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the great vessels attached to the base of the heart and state which circuit each one serves. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: great vessels and aortic arch. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, great vessels and aortic arch, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"cv-coronary","name":"Coronary circulation","can":"Trace coronary circulation from the aorta through the coronary arteries, myocardial capillaries, cardiac veins, and coronary sinus to the right atrium, and identify the major coronary vessels.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient with a blocked right coronary artery develops an abnormal heart rhythm. Which structures, usually supplied by the RCA, best explain this?\nA) SA and AV nodes  |  B) Pulmonary and aortic valves\nC) Anterior septum and apex  |  D) Left atrium and left auricle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) SA and AV nodes","why":"In most people the right coronary artery supplies the SA node, the pacemaker that starts each beat, and the AV node, which passes the signal on to the ventricles. If the RCA is blocked, these nodal cells are starved of oxygen and may not fire or conduct normally. That is why an RCA blockage can cause a rhythm problem in addition to damaged muscle."},{"t":"mc","q":"Coronary artery disease narrows the coronary arteries. Why can this lead to pumping failure?\nA) The narrowed arteries let blood leak backward through the valves\nB) The narrowed arteries block blood from entering the right atrium\nC) The pericardium thickens and stops the heart from beating\nD) The myocardium receives too little oxygen and contracts more weakly\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The myocardium receives too little oxygen and contracts more weakly","why":"Coronary artery disease is a narrowing of the coronary arteries, usually by fatty plaques in the vessel wall (atherosclerosis). Less blood reaches the myocardium, so the muscle cells run short of oxygen and cannot contract with full force. If the supply drops far enough, muscle dies, and the weakened wall cannot pump enough blood for the body."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why is damage to the apex especially serious in a left coronary blockage?\nA) The apex is where the pulmonary veins enter the heart\nB) The apex holds the aortic valve, which would stop closing\nC) The apex contains the SA node that starts every heartbeat\nD) It is formed by the left ventricle, the chamber that pumps blood to the body\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) It is formed by the left ventricle, the chamber that pumps blood to the body","why":"The apex is the blunt inferior tip of the heart, and it is formed by the left ventricle. Its muscle is supplied by the left coronary artery through the LAD. If that muscle dies, the left ventricle, which pumps blood into the aorta and out to the whole body, loses part of its pumping wall."},{"t":"mc","q":"A blockage in the right coronary artery causes a rhythm problem as well as damaged muscle. What anatomy explains the rhythm problem?\nA) It usually supplies the left atrium\nB) It usually supplies the apex of the heart\nC) It usually supplies the SA and AV nodes\nD) It usually supplies the anterior septum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It usually supplies the SA and AV nodes","why":"The SA node and AV node are part of the conduction system, the specialized cells that start and relay each heartbeat. In most people both nodes get their blood from the right coronary artery. When the RCA is blocked, the nodes can be starved of oxygen along with the muscle, so the beat may slow, become irregular, or be blocked at the AV node."},{"t":"mc","q":"A blockage in which coronary artery is most likely to cause a rhythm problem by cutting off blood to the SA node, in most people?\nA) The coronary sinus  |  B) The left marginal artery\nC) The anterior interventricular artery  |  D) The right coronary artery\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The right coronary artery","why":"In most people, the SA node receives its blood supply from the right coronary artery, which also usually supplies the AV node. If the right coronary artery is blocked, the SA node can be starved of oxygen along with the heart wall it supplies. That is why a right coronary artery occlusion can cause a rhythm problem as well as damage to the heart muscle."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Trace coronary circulation from the aorta through the coronary arteries, myocardial capillaries, cardiac veins, and coronary sinus to the right atrium, and identify the major coronary vessels. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace coronary circulation from the aorta through the coronary arteries, myocardial capillaries, cardiac veins, and coronary sinus to the right atrium, and identify the major coronary vessels."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to trace coronary circulation from the aorta through the coronary arteries, myocardial capillaries, cardiac veins, and coronary sinus to the right atrium, and identify the major coronary vessels. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: coronary circulation. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, coronary circulation, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"cv-conduction-anat","name":"Conduction system components and pathway","can":"Locate each component of the conduction system and trace the pathway in order from the SA node to the Purkinje fibers.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"If the AV node passed the signal on with no delay at all, what would most likely go wrong?\nA) The SA node would stop firing\nB) The Purkinje fibers would send the signal back to the atria\nC) The atria would contract after the ventricles\nD) The ventricles would contract before they finished filling\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The ventricles would contract before they finished filling","why":"Normally the AV node holds the signal for a fraction of a second while the atria finish squeezing their blood into the ventricles. Without that delay, the ventricles would be signaled almost at the same moment as the atria. They would start contracting before they were fully loaded, so less blood would be pumped out with each beat."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient's AV node is damaged, so the signal is delayed or stopped there. What timing problem can result?\nA) The SA node is forced to fire faster\nB) The atria stop receiving the signal\nC) The atria and ventricles beat out of step\nD) The atrioventricular valves stay open\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The atria and ventricles beat out of step","why":"A block at the AV node is called heart block. The SA node still fires and the atria still contract, but the signal is delayed or stopped before it reaches the ventricles. The ventricles may then beat late, skip beats, or beat at a slower rate of their own, so the atria and ventricles fall out of step."},{"t":"mc","q":"If the brief delay at the AV node disappeared, how would ventricular filling be affected?\nA) The ventricles would contract before the atria finished emptying\nB) Filling would not change, because the delay has no purpose\nC) The ventricles would fill more completely than usual\nD) The atria would contract after the ventricles\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The ventricles would contract before the atria finished emptying","why":"The pause at the AV node holds the signal just long enough for the atria to finish pushing blood into the ventricles. Without it, the signal would race into the ventricles while the atria were still contracting, and the ventricles would squeeze down before they were completely full. Each beat would then pump out less blood."},{"t":"mc","q":"Suppose the ventricles contracted from the base downward instead of from the apex upward. Why would this be inefficient?\nA) The atrioventricular valves would never open\nB) The coronary arteries would carry blood backward\nC) Blood would be pushed away from the outflow valves\nD) The atria would contract after the ventricles\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Blood would be pushed away from the outflow valves","why":"The pulmonary and aortic valves sit at the base of the ventricles, at the top of the heart. Contracting from the apex upward squeezes blood toward those exits. If contraction began at the base, it would push blood down toward the closed apex, away from the valves, so less blood would leave with each beat."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient's atria contract normally, but the signal never reaches the ventricles. Where is the block most likely to be?\nA) At the AV node or AV bundle  |  B) At the SA node\nC) In the Purkinje fibers  |  D) In the walls of the atria\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) At the AV node or AV bundle","why":"If the atria contract normally, the SA node fired and the signal crossed the atrial walls. The ventricles receive that signal only through the AV node and then the AV bundle, the single bridge through the fibrous skeleton. A signal that stops between the atria and the ventricles must therefore be blocked at the AV node or AV bundle, which is what heart block means."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Locate each component of the conduction system and trace the pathway in order from the SA node to the Purkinje fibers. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to locate each component of the conduction system and trace the pathway in order from the SA node to the Purkinje fibers."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to locate each component of the conduction system and trace the pathway in order from the SA node to the Purkinje fibers. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: conduction system components and pathway. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, conduction system components and pathway, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"cv-cardiac-nerves","name":"Nerve supply to the heart","can":"Describe the autonomic nerve supply reaching the heart through the cardiac plexus and name the sympathetic and vagal parasympathetic sources.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"During exercise, a person's heart rate climbs from 70 to 140 beats per minute. Which statement best explains the change?\nA) The AV node took over as the pacemaker\nB) The nervous system began generating each heartbeat directly\nC) Sympathetic nerves made the SA node fire faster\nD) The vagus nerve made the SA node fire faster\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Sympathetic nerves made the SA node fire faster","why":"The SA node keeps setting the rhythm during exercise, but sympathetic nerves reaching it through the cardiac plexus speed up its firing. The nerves adjust the rate to match the body's needs; they do not create the beats. This split lets the heart keep a steady beat on its own while the nervous system fine-tunes the pace."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which nerves reach the heart through the cardiac plexus and speed up the heart rate?\nA) The parasympathetic nerves  |  B) The somatic motor nerves\nC) The phrenic nerves  |  D) The sympathetic nerves\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The sympathetic nerves","why":"Sympathetic nerves belong to the part of the autonomic nervous system that prepares the body for activity. Their fibers reach the heart through the cardiac plexus and end on the SA and AV nodes, where they raise the heart rate. They speed up the beat that the SA node already makes; they do not create it."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which statement correctly describes the role of the autonomic nerves that reach the heart?\nA) They generate each heartbeat, and the SA node relays it\nB) They start the heartbeat only during exercise\nC) They adjust the rate that the SA node already sets\nD) They supply the heart's blood vessels but not its nodes\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) They adjust the rate that the SA node already sets","why":"The heartbeat is generated by the conduction system itself, starting at the SA node, a property called autorhythmicity. Sympathetic and parasympathetic nerves reach the SA and AV nodes through the cardiac plexus and only speed up or slow down that existing rhythm. A transplanted heart, whose nerves have been cut, still beats for this reason."},{"t":"mc","q":"A student says that nerves from the brain start each heartbeat. Which statement corrects this?\nA) The AV node starts each beat, and the SA node delays it\nB) The SA node starts it, and nerves only adjust the rate\nC) The Purkinje fibers start each beat, and the atria follow\nD) The vagus nerve starts each beat, and the SA node relays it\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The SA node starts it, and nerves only adjust the rate","why":"The heart has autorhythmicity, the built-in ability to generate its own rhythm. The SA node, in the wall of the right atrium, fires first and sets the pace of the whole heart without any signal from the nervous system. Autonomic nerves reaching the heart through the cardiac plexus only adjust that pace, with sympathetic nerves speeding it and the vagus nerve slowing it. A transplanted heart, whose nerves have been cut, still beats."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which region of the brain contains the cardiac centers that adjust heart rate?\nA) The medulla oblongata  |  B) The midbrain\nC) The cerebellum  |  D) The thalamus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The medulla oblongata","why":"The cardiac centers are regions of the medulla oblongata, the lowest part of the brainstem, just above the spinal cord. From there, autonomic nerves travel to the heart and reach the SA and AV nodes through the cardiac plexus, where they speed up or slow down the rate the SA node sets. The medulla also holds centers for breathing, which is one reason damage to it is so dangerous."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Describe the autonomic nerve supply reaching the heart through the cardiac plexus and name the sympathetic and vagal parasympathetic sources. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to describe the autonomic nerve supply reaching the heart through the cardiac plexus and name the sympathetic and vagal parasympathetic sources."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to describe the autonomic nerve supply reaching the heart through the cardiac plexus and name the sympathetic and vagal parasympathetic sources. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: nerve supply to the heart. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, nerve supply to the heart, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]}]},{"week":8,"system":"Cardiovascular","comps":[{"id":"bvn-vessel-tunics","name":"Three tunics of a vessel wall","can":"Name the three tunics of a vessel wall, state the tissue each contains, and explain which layer forms a capillary wall.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which tunic makes up the entire wall of a capillary?\nA) The tunica intima  |  B) The tunica media\nC) The tunica externa  |  D) All three tunics, each very thin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The tunica intima","why":"A capillary wall is only the tunica intima: a single layer of endothelium on a basement membrane. Capillaries lack a tunica media and a tunica externa entirely. This one-cell-thick wall is what makes exchange possible between the blood and the tissues."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which vessel type has a wall made of a single tunic, the tunica intima only?\nA) Venules  |  B) Muscular arteries\nC) Capillaries  |  D) Arterioles\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Capillaries","why":"Capillaries are the only vessels whose wall is just the tunica intima, an endothelium on a basement membrane. They lack both the tunica media and the tunica externa. A wall this thin is what allows gases, nutrients and wastes to cross between the blood and the tissues."},{"t":"mc","q":"On a slide, a vessel wall is a single layer of endothelium on a basement membrane, with no tunica media and no tunica externa. Which vessel type is it?\nA) A vein  |  B) A muscular artery\nC) A capillary  |  D) An arteriole\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A capillary","why":"A wall made of only endothelium on a basement membrane is the tunica intima alone, and the capillary is the only vessel built this way. Lacking the other two tunics keeps the wall as thin as possible. That thinness is what lets gases, nutrients and wastes pass between the blood and the tissue."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which tunic is the middle layer whose smooth muscle sets the diameter of the vessel?\nA) The tunica media  |  B) The tunica externa\nC) The tunica intima  |  D) The basement membrane\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The tunica media","why":"The tunica media is the middle layer, made of smooth muscle and elastic fibers. When its smooth muscle contracts, the vessel narrows (vasoconstriction), and when it relaxes, the vessel widens (vasodilation). This is how vessels adjust how much blood flows through them."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the order of the three tunics of a vessel wall, from the lumen outward?\nA) Tunica intima, tunica media, tunica externa\nB) Tunica intima, tunica externa, tunica media\nC) Tunica media, tunica intima, tunica externa\nD) Tunica externa, tunica media, tunica intima\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Tunica intima, tunica media, tunica externa","why":"Starting at the lumen, the tunica intima comes first as the endothelial lining. The tunica media of smooth muscle and elastic fibers surrounds it, and the collagen-rich tunica externa forms the outer covering. The names match the order: intima for innermost, media for middle, externa for external."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Name the three tunics of a vessel wall, state the tissue each contains, and explain which layer forms a capillary wall. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to name the three tunics of a vessel wall, state the tissue each contains, and explain which layer forms a capillary wall."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to name the three tunics of a vessel wall, state the tissue each contains, and explain which layer forms a capillary wall. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: three tunics of a vessel wall. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, three tunics of a vessel wall, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"bvn-vessel-types","name":"The five vessel types","can":"Compare arteries, arterioles, capillaries, venules, and veins by wall structure, direction of flow, and role.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"On a slide, a thin, very porous vessel sits just downstream of a capillary bed, and white blood cells are squeezing through its wall into the tissue. Which vessel type is it?\nA) A postcapillary venule  |  B) A metarteriole\nC) A muscular artery  |  D) A continuous capillary\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A postcapillary venule","why":"Blood leaving a capillary bed enters the venules, and the first one is the postcapillary venule, which is loosely joined and very porous. Venules are where white blood cells leave the bloodstream to enter the tissues. The vessel's place downstream of the bed and the cells crossing its wall both point to it."},{"t":"mc","q":"The pulmonary veins carry oxygen-rich blood, yet they are still classified as veins. Why?\nA) They have thicker walls than arteries\nB) They carry blood toward the heart\nC) They carry oxygen-poor blood\nD) They carry blood away from the heart\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) They carry blood toward the heart","why":"Arteries and veins are named for the direction of flow, not for how much oxygen the blood carries. Any vessel that carries blood away from the heart is an artery, and any vessel that carries blood toward the heart is a vein. The pulmonary veins bring blood from the lungs back to the left atrium, so they are veins even though that blood is rich in oxygen."},{"t":"mc","q":"A student says arteries always carry oxygen-rich blood. Which vessels prove this statement wrong?\nA) Coronary arteries  |  B) Pulmonary arteries\nC) Aorta  |  D) Pulmonary veins\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Pulmonary arteries","why":"An artery is any vessel that carries blood away from the heart, whatever its oxygen content. The pulmonary arteries, branches of the pulmonary trunk, carry oxygen-poor blood away from the right ventricle to the lungs. They are true arteries carrying oxygen-poor blood, so the student's rule does not hold."},{"t":"mc","q":"A student says veins always carry oxygen-poor blood. Which vessels prove this statement wrong?\nA) Venae cavae  |  B) Pulmonary arteries\nC) Pulmonary veins  |  D) Cardiac veins\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Pulmonary veins","why":"A vein is any vessel that carries blood toward the heart. The pulmonary veins carry blood from the lungs, where it has just picked up oxygen, back to the left atrium, so they are veins that carry oxygen-rich blood. Direction, not oxygen content, decides whether a vessel is an artery or a vein."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why are the pulmonary arteries unusual among arteries?\nA) They carry blood toward the heart instead of away from it\nB) They branch directly from the left ventricle\nC) They have no tunica media in their walls\nD) They carry oxygen-poor blood away from the heart\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) They carry oxygen-poor blood away from the heart","why":"Most arteries carry oxygen-rich blood, but the pulmonary arteries carry oxygen-poor blood from the right ventricle to the lungs. They are still arteries because an artery is defined by direction: it carries blood away from the heart. In the lungs this blood picks up oxygen before returning in the pulmonary veins."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Compare arteries, arterioles, capillaries, venules, and veins by wall structure, direction of flow, and role. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to compare arteries, arterioles, capillaries, venules, and veins by wall structure, direction of flow, and role."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to compare arteries, arterioles, capillaries, venules, and veins by wall structure, direction of flow, and role. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: the five vessel types. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, the five vessel types, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"bvn-artery-cap-vein-kinds","name":"Kinds of artery, capillary, and vein","can":"Distinguish elastic and muscular arteries, continuous, fenestrated, and sinusoid capillaries, and identify venous valves and their supporting features.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"One valve in a leg vein no longer closes properly and lets blood flow backward. What happens locally?\nA) Blood leaks out into the tissue above the valve\nB) The vein wall thickens and narrows its lumen\nC) Blood pools in the vein segment below the failed valve\nD) Blood pools in the artery that supplies the leg\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Blood pools in the vein segment below the failed valve","why":"In the leg, venous blood is moving upward toward the heart. A working valve catches any blood that slides back. When a valve fails, blood falls back through it and collects in the segment of vein below (farther from the heart), raising pressure there and stretching that part of the vein."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which vessels have valves in their walls?\nA) Arterioles  |  B) Arteries\nC) Veins  |  D) Capillaries\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Veins","why":"Veins, especially those in the limbs, have valves formed from flaps of the tunica intima. Blood in veins moves at low pressure and often has to travel uphill against gravity, so the valves keep it from sliding backward. Arteries do not need valves because the high pressure from the heart keeps their blood moving forward."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which capillary type has an unbroken endothelial lining and is the most common type?\nA) The fenestrated capillary  |  B) The continuous capillary\nC) The sinusoid  |  D) The postcapillary venule\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The continuous capillary","why":"Continuous capillaries have an unbroken endothelial lining, interrupted only by narrow clefts between the cells. They are the most common type, found in muscle, skin, the lungs and the central nervous system. Their tight wall keeps most substances from leaking out, which is especially important in the brain."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which capillary type has an endothelium with small pores called fenestrations?\nA) The metarteriole  |  B) The sinusoid\nC) The continuous capillary  |  D) The fenestrated capillary\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The fenestrated capillary","why":"Fenestrated capillaries have small pores, called fenestrations (from the Latin word for windows), through their endothelial cells. These pores let fluid and small molecules move quickly, so these capillaries are found where rapid filtration or absorption happens. Examples include the kidney, the small intestine, the choroid plexus and most endocrine glands."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which capillary type is a wide, leaky vessel with large gaps between its cells?\nA) The metarteriole  |  B) The sinusoid\nC) The fenestrated capillary  |  D) The continuous capillary\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The sinusoid","why":"Sinusoids are wide, winding capillaries with large fenestrations, wide clefts between cells, and an incomplete basement membrane. They are the leakiest capillary type, built for tissues where whole cells or large proteins must cross. They are found in the liver, spleen, red bone marrow, anterior pituitary and adrenal glands."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Distinguish elastic and muscular arteries, continuous, fenestrated, and sinusoid capillaries, and identify venous valves and their supporting features. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish elastic and muscular arteries, continuous, fenestrated, and sinusoid capillaries, and identify venous valves and their supporting features."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to distinguish elastic and muscular arteries, continuous, fenestrated, and sinusoid capillaries, and identify venous valves and their supporting features. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: kinds of artery, capillary, and vein. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, kinds of artery, capillary, and vein, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"bvn-circulatory-routes","name":"Circulatory routes, portal systems, and anastomoses","can":"Describe the pulmonary and systemic circuits and identify portal systems and anastomoses as alternate circulatory arrangements.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"An artery whose region has no anastomoses is suddenly blocked. What is most likely to happen?\nA) Blood reroutes through a collateral vessel with no harm\nB) The tissue begins drawing its blood from lymphatic vessels\nC) Blood flows backward through veins to reach the tissue\nD) The tissue it supplies loses blood flow and may die\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The tissue it supplies loses blood flow and may die","why":"Without an anastomosis, the blocked artery is the only path to the tissue it supplies. Blood cannot detour around the block, so the downstream tissue is starved of oxygen. If the block lasts, the tissue dies, which is what happens to heart muscle in a heart attack or brain tissue in a stroke."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why is the heart described as a double pump?\nA) It beats twice for every breath\nB) It pumps blood and lymph through separate chambers\nC) The atria and the ventricles each pump blood to the body\nD) The right side pumps to the lungs, and the left side pumps to the body\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The right side pumps to the lungs, and the left side pumps to the body","why":"The heart is two pumps working side by side. The right side receives blood from the body and pumps it to the lungs in the pulmonary circuit, and the left side receives blood from the lungs and pumps it to the body in the systemic circuit. Both sides contract at the same time with each heartbeat."},{"t":"mc","q":"Blood that passes through the pulmonary valve enters which circuit?\nA) The pulmonary circuit  |  B) The hepatic portal system\nC) The coronary circulation  |  D) The systemic circuit\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The pulmonary circuit","why":"The pulmonary valve opens into the pulmonary trunk, which divides into the pulmonary arteries to the lungs. Blood that passes through it has entered the pulmonary circuit, where it will pick up O2 before returning to the left atrium. A problem with this valve therefore affects flow to the lungs first."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which chamber pumps blood into the pulmonary circuit, and which chamber receives it when it returns?\nA) Leaves from the left ventricle, returns to the right atrium\nB) Leaves from the right ventricle, returns to the left atrium\nC) Leaves from the right atrium, returns to the left ventricle\nD) Leaves from the left atrium, returns to the right ventricle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Leaves from the right ventricle, returns to the left atrium","why":"The pulmonary circuit is the loop to the lungs and back. The right ventricle pumps oxygen-poor blood through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk and pulmonary arteries. After gas exchange in the lungs, the pulmonary veins return oxygen-rich blood to the left atrium, which ends the circuit."},{"t":"mc","q":"In the systemic circuit, which vessel carries blood out of the heart, and which vessels bring it back?\nA) Pulmonary trunk carries it out, venae cavae bring it back\nB) Aorta carries it out, pulmonary veins bring it back\nC) Aorta carries it out, venae cavae bring it back\nD) Pulmonary trunk carries it out, pulmonary veins bring it back\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Aorta carries it out, venae cavae bring it back","why":"The systemic circuit is the loop from the left side of the heart out to the body and back to the right side. The left ventricle pumps oxygen-rich blood into the aorta, which branches into arteries, arterioles, and capillaries throughout the body. The blood then returns through venules and veins to the superior and inferior venae cavae, which empty into the right atrium."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Describe the pulmonary and systemic circuits and identify portal systems and anastomoses as alternate circulatory arrangements. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to describe the pulmonary and systemic circuits and identify portal systems and anastomoses as alternate circulatory arrangements."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to describe the pulmonary and systemic circuits and identify portal systems and anastomoses as alternate circulatory arrangements. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: circulatory routes, portal systems, and anastomoses. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, circulatory routes, portal systems, and anastomoses, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"bvn-vessel-disorders","name":"Vessel wall disorders, arterial and venous","can":"Describe atherosclerosis and how it changes an artery wall, and name common arterial and venous disorders by the structure each affects.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Chronic hypertension makes the heart pump against higher pressure in the systemic arteries. Which chamber's wall is most likely to thicken over time?\nA) The right atrium  |  B) The left ventricle\nC) The left atrium  |  D) The right ventricle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The left ventricle","why":"Hypertension is chronically high pressure in the arteries, and the systemic arteries are fed by the left ventricle through the aorta. Like any muscle that is made to work harder, the myocardium of the left ventricle thickens to generate the extra force. The left ventricle already has the thickest wall of the four chambers because it pumps blood to the whole body."},{"t":"mc","q":"Varicose veins form when a structure inside superficial leg veins fails. Which structure is it, and which tunic of the vein wall forms it?\nA) The venous valves, formed by the tunica media\nB) The internal elastic lamina, in the tunica media\nC) The vasa vasorum, found in the tunica externa\nD) The venous valves, formed by the tunica intima\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The venous valves, formed by the tunica intima","why":"Varicose veins are superficial veins that become twisted and swollen when their valves fail, so blood pools in them. Venous valves are flaps of the tunica intima, common in the limb veins, that keep blood from flowing backward. When the flaps no longer close, blood slips back and the vein swells."},{"t":"mc","q":"Atherosclerosis narrowing the coronary arteries produces which named disorder?\nA) Peripheral artery disease  |  B) Stroke\nC) Hypertension  |  D) Coronary artery disease\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Coronary artery disease","why":"The coronary arteries supply the myocardium, the muscle of the heart wall. When atherosclerotic plaque narrows them, the disorder is called coronary artery disease. It can starve the heart muscle of oxygen and lead to a heart attack. The same plaque in other arteries gets a different name based on location."},{"t":"mc","q":"Hemorrhoids are best classified as which kind of vessel disorder?\nA) Atherosclerosis of the rectal arteries\nB) Varicose veins of the rectal and anal region\nC) An aneurysm of a rectal artery\nD) Inflammation of a rectal vein\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Varicose veins of the rectal and anal region","why":"Hemorrhoids are varicose veins in the rectal and anal region. Just as in the legs, the veins there stretch, swell, and bulge when blood pools in them. They are a venous disorder, which fits the general pattern that vein problems involve pooled blood under low pressure."},{"t":"mc","q":"A clot from a leg vein breaks free and lodges in an artery of the lung. What is this disorder called?\nA) Phlebitis  |  B) Pulmonary embolism\nC) Chronic venous insufficiency  |  D) Deep vein thrombosis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Pulmonary embolism","why":"An embolus is a clot or fragment that travels through the bloodstream and lodges somewhere else. When that clot ends up in an artery of the lung, the disorder is a pulmonary embolism. It is a life-threatening emergency because it blocks blood flow to part of the lung, and the clot most often comes from a deep vein of the leg."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Describe atherosclerosis and how it changes an artery wall, and name common arterial and venous disorders by the structure each affects. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to describe atherosclerosis and how it changes an artery wall, and name common arterial and venous disorders by the structure each affects."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to describe atherosclerosis and how it changes an artery wall, and name common arterial and venous disorders by the structure each affects. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: vessel wall disorders, arterial and venous. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, vessel wall disorders, arterial and venous, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"bvn-fetal-remnants","name":"Fetal circulation, shunts, and adult remnants","can":"Identify the fetal vessels and three shunts, state how each reroutes blood, and name the adult remnant each becomes.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"An adult liver shows a fibrous cord on its inferior surface that ends at the inferior vena cava. Which fetal shunt did this cord once carry blood as?\nA) An umbilical artery  |  B) The ductus arteriosus\nC) The umbilical vein  |  D) The ductus venosus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The ductus venosus","why":"The ductus venosus carried blood through the fetal liver toward the inferior vena cava. After birth it closes and becomes the ligamentum venosum, a fibrous cord on the inferior surface of the liver that still reaches the inferior vena cava. Its position on the liver and its connection to the vena cava both point back to the ductus venosus."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a cadaver, paired fibrous cords run up the inner surface of the anterior abdominal wall toward the umbilicus. Which fetal vessels are these the remnants of?\nA) The ductus venosus  |  B) The umbilical vein\nC) The umbilical arteries  |  D) The ductus arteriosus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The umbilical arteries","why":"The two umbilical arteries carried oxygen-poor blood from the fetus out through the umbilicus to the placenta. After birth they close into the medial umbilical ligaments, which run up the inner surface of the anterior abdominal wall toward the umbilicus. The fact that the cords are paired matches the two arteries."},{"t":"mc","q":"In an adult heart, the interatrial septum shows a shallow oval depression where an opening once sat. Which fetal structure left this depression?\nA) The umbilical vein  |  B) The ductus venosus\nC) The ductus arteriosus  |  D) The foramen ovale\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The foramen ovale","why":"The shallow depression is the fossa ovalis, and it marks where the foramen ovale used to be. In the fetus the foramen ovale was an opening in the interatrial septum that let blood pass from the right atrium to the left. Because it was a hole rather than a vessel, it closes into a depression instead of a fibrous cord."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a cadaver, a round fibrous cord runs in the free edge of the falciform ligament from the umbilicus to the liver. Which fetal vessel was this cord?\nA) The umbilical vein  |  B) The ductus venosus\nC) The ductus arteriosus  |  D) An umbilical artery\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The umbilical vein","why":"The cord is the ligamentum teres, the round ligament of the liver. Before birth it was the umbilical vein, which carried oxygen-rich blood from the placenta through the umbilicus to the liver. Its path in the free edge of the falciform ligament traces exactly where the vein once ran."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why is the fossa ovalis found in the interatrial septum?\nA) It marks where the fetal foramen ovale joined the atria\nB) It is where the superior vena cava enters\nC) It marks where the ductus arteriosus once attached\nD) It is the opening where the coronary sinus drains\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It marks where the fetal foramen ovale joined the atria","why":"Before birth, the foramen ovale was an opening in the interatrial septum that let blood pass from the right atrium to the left atrium, bypassing the lungs. After birth, a flap seals it shut, leaving a shallow oval depression called the fossa ovalis. Its location simply marks where that fetal opening used to be."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the fetal vessels and three shunts, state how each reroutes blood, and name the adult remnant each becomes. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the fetal vessels and three shunts, state how each reroutes blood, and name the adult remnant each becomes."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the fetal vessels and three shunts, state how each reroutes blood, and name the adult remnant each becomes. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: fetal circulation, shunts, and adult remnants. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, fetal circulation, shunts, and adult remnants, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]}]},{"week":11,"system":"Respiratory","comps":[{"id":"resp-zones","name":"Upper and lower tracts, conducting and respiratory zones","can":"Distinguish the upper and lower respiratory tracts and the conducting and respiratory zones, and assign the major airway structures to each.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A segment of airway carries air but has no alveoli in its walls. Which of these could it be?\nA) An alveolar sac  |  B) An alveolar duct\nC) A terminal bronchiole  |  D) A respiratory bronchiole\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A terminal bronchiole","why":"An airway with no alveoli belongs to the conducting zone, which only carries air from the nose down to the terminal bronchioles. The terminal bronchiole is the last airway of that zone. Past it, every structure has alveoli in or along its walls: respiratory bronchioles have a few budding from their walls, alveolar ducts are lined entirely with them, and alveolar sacs are clusters of them."},{"t":"mc","q":"The nose, nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and pharynx together belong to which division of the respiratory system?\nA) The upper respiratory tract  |  B) The respiratory zone\nC) The lower respiratory tract  |  D) The bronchial tree\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The upper respiratory tract","why":"The respiratory system is grouped by location into upper and lower tracts. The upper respiratory tract is the nose, nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and pharynx, which take in air and condition it. The lower tract begins at the larynx and includes the trachea, bronchi, and lungs."},{"t":"mc","q":"The respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli together make up which functional zone?\nA) The upper respiratory tract  |  B) The pleural cavity\nC) The respiratory zone  |  D) The conducting zone\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The respiratory zone","why":"The respiratory system is also grouped by function into two zones. The respiratory zone is made of the structures where gas exchange happens: the respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, and alveoli. Everything from the nose to the terminal bronchioles only carries air and forms the conducting zone."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which division contains the passages that only carry air, from the nose to the terminal bronchioles?\nA) The lower respiratory tract  |  B) The respiratory zone\nC) The bronchial tree  |  D) The conducting zone\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The conducting zone","why":"The conducting zone includes every passage that carries air without exchanging gases, from the nose to the terminal bronchioles. Along the way it warms, moistens, and filters the incoming air. Gas exchange only begins in the respiratory zone, starting with the respiratory bronchioles."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which of these structures belongs to the upper respiratory tract?\nA) The larynx  |  B) The bronchi\nC) The trachea  |  D) The pharynx\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The pharynx","why":"The upper respiratory tract is the nose, nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and pharynx. The lower tract begins at the larynx and continues with the trachea, bronchi, and lungs. The pharynx is the last structure of the upper tract, and the larynx is the first of the lower tract."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Distinguish the upper and lower respiratory tracts and the conducting and respiratory zones, and assign the major airway structures to each. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish the upper and lower respiratory tracts and the conducting and respiratory zones, and assign the major airway structures to each."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to distinguish the upper and lower respiratory tracts and the conducting and respiratory zones, and assign the major airway structures to each. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: upper and lower tracts, conducting and respiratory zones. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, upper and lower tracts, conducting and respiratory zones, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"resp-upper-tract","name":"Nose, nasal cavity, and pharynx","can":"Identify the parts of the nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and the three regions of the pharynx.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Food and air travel together through one region of the pharynx until it opens into both the larynx and the esophagus. Which region is it?\nA) The oropharynx  |  B) The laryngopharynx\nC) The nasal vestibule  |  D) The nasopharynx\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The laryngopharynx","why":"The laryngopharynx runs from the epiglottis down to the esophagus. At its lower end, the air path continues forward into the larynx and the food path continues back into the esophagus. It is where the two paths divide."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which region of the pharynx carries air only, and never food?\nA) The esophagus  |  B) The oropharynx\nC) The nasopharynx  |  D) The laryngopharynx\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The nasopharynx","why":"The nasopharynx lies behind the nasal cavity and above the soft palate. During swallowing the soft palate lifts to close it off, so food does not enter, and it carries air only. Because it never meets food, it is lined with pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium rather than the tougher stratified squamous of the lower regions."},{"t":"mc","q":"Air passes from the back of the nasal cavity into the nasopharynx. Through which openings does it pass?\nA) The glottis  |  B) The choanae\nC) The nares  |  D) The auditory tubes\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The choanae","why":"The choanae are the paired posterior openings of the nasal cavity. Air that entered through the nostrils crosses the nasal cavity and leaves through the choanae into the nasopharynx. From there it continues down through the oropharynx and laryngopharynx to the larynx."},{"t":"mc","q":"How do the nasal conchae and meatuses help prepare incoming air?\nA) They divide the airflow into right and left streams\nB) They close the nasopharynx during each swallow\nC) They swirl the air over the warm, moist lining\nD) They are the surface where gas exchange takes place\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) They swirl the air over the warm, moist lining","why":"The scroll-shaped conchae and the passages beneath them break the airflow into swirling currents. That turbulence pushes more of the air against the warm, moist mucosa, which warms it, adds moisture, and lets mucus trap particles. This is part of how the conducting zone conditions air before it reaches the delicate alveoli."},{"t":"mc","q":"Air breathed in through the nose passes through the three regions of the pharynx in which order?\nA) Nasopharynx, oropharynx, laryngopharynx\nB) Oropharynx, nasopharynx, laryngopharynx\nC) Nasopharynx, laryngopharynx, oropharynx\nD) Laryngopharynx, oropharynx, nasopharynx\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Nasopharynx, oropharynx, laryngopharynx","why":"The pharynx is the throat, and its three regions are stacked from top to bottom. The nasopharynx lies behind the nasal cavity above the soft palate, the oropharynx lies behind the oral cavity, and the laryngopharynx runs from the epiglottis down to where the airway and esophagus divide. Air from the nose meets them in that top-to-bottom order."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the parts of the nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and the three regions of the pharynx. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the parts of the nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and the three regions of the pharynx."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the parts of the nasal cavity, paranasal sinuses, and the three regions of the pharynx. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: nose, nasal cavity, and pharynx. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, nose, nasal cavity, and pharynx, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"resp-larynx","name":"Larynx","can":"Identify the cartilages and folds of the larynx, including the thyroid and cricoid cartilages, epiglottis, arytenoids, vocal cords, and glottis.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"An emergency airway is placed just above a single, ring-shaped cartilage that lies below the thyroid cartilage. Which cartilage is this ring?\nA) A tracheal cartilage  |  B) The cricoid cartilage\nC) The epiglottis  |  D) The arytenoid cartilage\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The cricoid cartilage","why":"The cricoid cartilage is a single ring of hyaline cartilage at the bottom of the larynx, directly below the thyroid cartilage. It is the only complete ring of cartilage in the airway. The cricothyroid ligament fills the gap between the thyroid and cricoid cartilages, and it is the landmark for an emergency airway."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person laughs while swallowing, food slips into the larynx, and a coughing fit follows. Which structure normally folds over the laryngeal inlet to stop this?\nA) The trachealis muscle  |  B) The epiglottis\nC) The cricoid cartilage  |  D) The soft palate\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The epiglottis","why":"During a normal swallow, the larynx rises and the epiglottis folds down over the laryngeal inlet, so food is directed into the esophagus. If the timing fails, as when someone laughs or talks while swallowing, food can slip into the larynx. The coughing fit is the airway trying to clear it."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient coughs violently after a sip of water goes down the wrong way and enters the laryngeal inlet. Which structure failed to seal the airway during that swallow?\nA) The epiglottis  |  B) The soft palate\nC) The thyroid cartilage  |  D) The trachealis muscle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The epiglottis","why":"During a normal swallow, the epiglottis folds back over the laryngeal inlet so food and liquid slide past into the esophagus. When something slips past it into the larynx, the airway responds with a coughing fit to clear it. So liquid entering the laryngeal inlet means the epiglottis did not seal the opening in time."},{"t":"mc","q":"During a swallow, a bit of food slips into the laryngeal opening and triggers coughing. Which structure normally folds over the opening to prevent this?\nA) The epiglottis  |  B) The thyroid cartilage\nC) The soft palate  |  D) The cricoid cartilage\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The epiglottis","why":"The epiglottis is a flexible, spoon-shaped flap of elastic cartilage at the top of the larynx. During swallowing it folds back over the laryngeal inlet, so food and liquid slide past into the esophagus instead of into the airway. When food slips past it, coughing is the airway's attempt to clear it."},{"t":"mc","q":"A student laughs while eating, and a bit of food slips into the larynx instead of the esophagus, setting off a coughing fit. Which structure normally covers the laryngeal inlet during a swallow to prevent this?\nA) Soft palate  |  B) Epiglottis\nC) Thyroid cartilage  |  D) Trachealis muscle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Epiglottis","why":"During a swallow the larynx rises and the epiglottis folds down over the laryngeal inlet like a lid, steering food back toward the esophagus. If someone laughs or talks while swallowing, the airway may be open at the wrong moment and food can slip past the epiglottis. The coughing fit is the airway's attempt to push the food back out."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the cartilages and folds of the larynx, including the thyroid and cricoid cartilages, epiglottis, arytenoids, vocal cords, and glottis. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the cartilages and folds of the larynx, including the thyroid and cricoid cartilages, epiglottis, arytenoids, vocal cords, and glottis."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the cartilages and folds of the larynx, including the thyroid and cricoid cartilages, epiglottis, arytenoids, vocal cords, and glottis. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: larynx. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, larynx, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"resp-tree","name":"Trachea and bronchial tree","can":"Trace air through the trachea and bronchial tree in order from the trachea and carina down to the terminal bronchioles.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"On a slide, a student finds a small conducting airway with a wall of smooth muscle, no cartilage, and no alveoli. Which structure is it?\nA) An alveolar duct  |  B) A segmental bronchus\nC) A bronchiole  |  D) A respiratory bronchiole\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A bronchiole","why":"Bronchioles are the smallest conducting airways. As the bronchial tree branches, cartilage disappears and smooth muscle takes over, so a bronchiole wall has smooth muscle and no cartilage. Because no alveoli open from its wall, it still only carries air."},{"t":"mc","q":"A child inhales a small bead, and an X-ray shows it lodged in the right lung. Which feature of the airway best explains why it went right?\nA) The left main bronchus has no cartilage\nB) The right lung has three lobes and the left has two\nC) The left lung has a cardiac notch for the heart\nD) The right main bronchus is wider and more vertical\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The right main bronchus is wider and more vertical","why":"At the carina the trachea splits into the two main bronchi, and they are not mirror images. The right main bronchus is wider, shorter, and more vertical, nearly in line with the trachea, while the left is narrower and angles off more sharply. An inhaled object falling down the trachea follows the straighter, wider path, so it most often lands in the right lung."},{"t":"mc","q":"An inhaled peanut reaches the carina. Which airways could it enter next?\nA) The right or left lobar bronchus  |  B) The right or left segmental bronchus\nC) The right or left main bronchus  |  D) The larynx or the trachea\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The right or left main bronchus","why":"The carina is the internal ridge at the bottom of the trachea where it forks into the two main bronchi, also called the primary bronchi. Anything that reaches the carina must go into either the right or the left main bronchus. Each main bronchus then enters a lung and divides into lobar bronchi."},{"t":"mc","q":"A tumor blocks one segmental (tertiary) bronchus. Which part of the lung loses its air supply?\nA) One bronchopulmonary segment  |  B) One entire lung\nC) One single alveolus  |  D) One entire lobe\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) One bronchopulmonary segment","why":"Each segmental (tertiary) bronchus supplies one bronchopulmonary segment, a distinct section of a lobe with its own airway. Blocking that bronchus cuts off air to that one segment while the neighboring segments keep working. This is why each segment is treated as a separate functional unit of the lung."},{"t":"mc","q":"Air leaving the trachea passes the carina. Which airways does it enter next?\nA) The alveolar ducts  |  B) The lobar bronchi\nC) The main bronchi  |  D) The terminal bronchioles\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The main bronchi","why":"The carina is the internal ridge at the bottom of the trachea where it forks in two. Each fork is a main (primary) bronchus, one entering each lung at the hilum. From there the main bronchi divide into lobar, then segmental, bronchi."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Trace air through the trachea and bronchial tree in order from the trachea and carina down to the terminal bronchioles. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace air through the trachea and bronchial tree in order from the trachea and carina down to the terminal bronchioles."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to trace air through the trachea and bronchial tree in order from the trachea and carina down to the terminal bronchioles. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: trachea and bronchial tree. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, trachea and bronchial tree, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"resp-lungs-pleura","name":"Lungs and pleurae","can":"Identify the lobes, fissures, and surfaces of each lung and the parietal and visceral pleurae with the pleural cavity.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The left lung has a cardiac notch along its anterior border. What is the structural reason for it?\nA) It anchors the visceral pleura  |  B) It marks where the apex begins\nC) It makes room for the heart  |  D) It allows the trachea to fork\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It makes room for the heart","why":"The heart sits in the mediastinum and tilts toward the left side of the thorax. The left lung gives up room for it, and the indentation along its anterior border is called the cardiac notch. This is also why the left lung is smaller and has only two lobes."},{"t":"mc","q":"A surgeon cuts inward through the thoracic wall toward a lung. Which pleura is reached first?\nA) The visceral pleura  |  B) The pleural fluid\nC) The parietal pleura  |  D) The pericardium\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The parietal pleura","why":"The parietal pleura lines the inner surface of the thoracic wall, so it is the first serous layer reached from outside. Beneath it is the pleural cavity with its thin film of fluid, and then the visceral pleura on the surface of the lung. Parietal means wall and visceral means organ."},{"t":"mc","q":"The right lung has both a horizontal fissure and an oblique fissure. Which fissure pattern does the left lung have?\nA) An oblique fissure only  |  B) A horizontal fissure only\nC) No fissures at all  |  D) A horizontal fissure and an oblique fissure\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) An oblique fissure only","why":"The left lung has two lobes, superior and inferior, separated by a single oblique fissure. The right lung needs two fissures to separate three lobes, and its horizontal fissure sets off the middle lobe. Since the left lung has no middle lobe, it has no horizontal fissure."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which part of the lung is the broad inferior surface that rests on the diaphragm?\nA) The cardiac notch  |  B) The base of the lung\nC) The hilum of the lung  |  D) The apex of the lung\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The base of the lung","why":"The base of each lung is its broad, concave lower surface, shaped to sit on the dome of the diaphragm. The opposite end is the narrow apex, which rises above the clavicle. The heart uses the same two words the other way around, with its apex pointing down and its base at the top."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which thin, sealed, fluid-filled space between two membranes lets the lung glide as it expands?\nA) The pleural cavity  |  B) The mediastinum\nC) The alveolar sac  |  D) The hilum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The pleural cavity","why":"The pleural cavity is the thin space between the parietal pleura on the thoracic wall and the visceral pleura on the lung. It holds only a film of serous fluid, which lets the two layers slide smoothly as the lung expands and recoils. Because it is sealed, letting air in causes the lung to collapse."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the lobes, fissures, and surfaces of each lung and the parietal and visceral pleurae with the pleural cavity. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the lobes, fissures, and surfaces of each lung and the parietal and visceral pleurae with the pleural cavity."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the lobes, fissures, and surfaces of each lung and the parietal and visceral pleurae with the pleural cavity. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: lungs and pleurae. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, lungs and pleurae, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"resp-histo","name":"Respiratory zone and alveolar structure","can":"Describe the structure of an alveolus and identify the type I and type II alveolar cells, alveolar macrophages, respiratory membrane, and pulmonary capillaries.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"On a slide, an airway has a few alveoli budding from its walls. It is the first airway of the respiratory zone. Which structure is it?\nA) A respiratory bronchiole  |  B) An alveolar sac\nC) A terminal bronchiole  |  D) An alveolar duct\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A respiratory bronchiole","why":"The respiratory bronchiole comes right after the terminal bronchiole. A few alveoli bud from its walls, so some gas exchange can happen there, which makes it the first part of the respiratory zone. Farther down, the alveolar ducts are lined entirely with alveoli."},{"t":"mc","q":"Inhaling a toxic gas damages a patient's Type I alveolar cells. Which process is most directly impaired?\nA) Mucus clearance by cilia in the trachea\nB) Removal of debris from the alveolar surface\nC) Surfactant secretion by the alveolar wall\nD) Gas diffusion across the alveolar wall\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Gas diffusion across the alveolar wall","why":"Type I alveolar cells are the thin, flat cells that form most of the alveolar wall, and they are the cells that gases cross. If they are damaged, the barrier between air and blood is disrupted, and gas diffusion (movement of gases from high to low concentration across the wall) is impaired. The other jobs listed belong to other cells."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which description fits an alveolar sac?\nA) The thin barrier between alveolar air and blood\nB) A cluster of alveoli that share a common space\nC) A single tiny air sac where gases cross\nD) A passage whose walls are lined entirely with alveoli\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A cluster of alveoli that share a common space","why":"An alveolar sac sits at the end of an alveolar duct and looks like a bunch of grapes. Several alveoli open into one shared central space, the sac. Air reaches the sac through the alveolar duct, and gas exchange happens across the walls of the individual alveoli."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which structures form the dense net that wraps each alveolus?\nA) The pulmonary capillaries  |  B) The alveolar ducts\nC) The terminal bronchioles  |  D) The bronchial arteries\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The pulmonary capillaries","why":"Each alveolus is wrapped in a dense net of pulmonary capillaries. The walls of the alveolus and the capillary lie so close together that they form the respiratory membrane, where gases cross between air and blood. These capillaries come from branches of the pulmonary arteries and drain into the pulmonary veins."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which respiratory passage has walls lined entirely with alveoli?\nA) A respiratory bronchiole  |  B) A terminal bronchiole\nC) A segmental bronchus  |  D) An alveolar duct\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) An alveolar duct","why":"Alveolar ducts come after the respiratory bronchioles in the respiratory zone. Their walls are made entirely of alveoli opening one after another, and they lead into the alveolar sacs. Moving from respiratory bronchiole to alveolar duct, the number of alveoli increases from a few to complete coverage."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Describe the structure of an alveolus and identify the type I and type II alveolar cells, alveolar macrophages, respiratory membrane, and pulmonary capillaries. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to describe the structure of an alveolus and identify the type I and type II alveolar cells, alveolar macrophages, respiratory membrane, and pulmonary capillaries."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to describe the structure of an alveolus and identify the type I and type II alveolar cells, alveolar macrophages, respiratory membrane, and pulmonary capillaries. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: respiratory zone and alveolar structure. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, respiratory zone and alveolar structure, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"resp-thoracic-diaphragm","name":"Thoracic wall and diaphragm","can":"Identify the diaphragm, the intercostal muscles, and the three structures passing through the diaphragm at T8, T10, and T12.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A mass presses on the opening of the diaphragm at vertebral level T10. Which structure passing through that opening is most directly compressed, causing trouble swallowing?\nA) The thoracic duct  |  B) The inferior vena cava\nC) The esophagus  |  D) The aorta\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The esophagus","why":"The opening at T10 is the esophageal hiatus, which transmits the esophagus and the vagus nerves. A mass narrowing this opening squeezes the esophagus as food passes from the thorax to the stomach, which makes swallowing difficult. Linking the vertebral level to the opening, and the opening to its contents, solves this kind of question."},{"t":"mc","q":"Moving from the highest diaphragm opening to the lowest, which list gives where each opening sits?\nA) Muscle, then central tendon, then between the crura\nB) Between the crura, then muscle, then central tendon\nC) Central tendon, then between the crura, then muscle\nD) Central tendon, then muscle, then between the crura\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Central tendon, then muscle, then between the crura","why":"The highest opening is the caval opening for the inferior vena cava, in the central tendon at T8. Next is the esophageal hiatus, in the muscle at T10. Lowest is the aortic hiatus at T12, which lies behind the diaphragm between its two crura."},{"t":"mc","q":"During a forceful exhalation, such as blowing out birthday candles, which muscle layer pulls the ribs down?\nA) Diaphragm  |  B) Internal intercostals\nC) Serratus posterior superior  |  D) External intercostals\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Internal intercostals","why":"Quiet exhalation is mostly passive, but a forceful exhalation needs muscles that pull the ribs down and shrink the chest. The internal intercostals depress the ribs, and their downward and backward fibers suit that job. The innermost intercostals and the transversus thoracis act with them."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which sequence lists the muscle layers of an intercostal space from superficial to deep?\nA) External, internal, innermost intercostals\nB) Innermost, internal, external intercostals\nC) Internal, external, innermost intercostals\nD) External, innermost, internal intercostals\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) External, internal, innermost intercostals","why":"The intercostal space between two ribs is filled by three sheets of muscle. The external intercostals lie outermost, the internal intercostals lie just deep to them with fibers at right angles, and the innermost intercostals form the deepest layer, separated from the internal layer by the neurovascular bundle. Their names match their order from the outside in."},{"t":"mc","q":"As you take a deep breath in, which intercostal layer helps lift the ribs, and which way do its fibers run?\nA) Internal intercostals, fibers downward and backward\nB) External intercostals, fibers downward and backward\nC) External intercostals, fibers downward and forward\nD) Internal intercostals, fibers downward and forward\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) External intercostals, fibers downward and forward","why":"The external intercostals elevate the ribs, which widens the chest and helps draw air in. Their fibers run downward and forward, the hands-in-pockets direction. The internal intercostals, whose fibers run at right angles downward and backward, do the opposite and depress the ribs in forced exhalation."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the diaphragm, the intercostal muscles, and the three structures passing through the diaphragm at T8, T10, and T12. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the diaphragm, the intercostal muscles, and the three structures passing through the diaphragm at T8, T10, and T12."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the diaphragm, the intercostal muscles, and the three structures passing through the diaphragm at T8, T10, and T12. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: thoracic wall and diaphragm. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, thoracic wall and diaphragm, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"resp-disorders","name":"Respiratory disorders by structure affected","can":"Name common respiratory disorders and state which airway, alveolar, or pleural structure each one affects.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Why does laryngitis make the voice hoarse?\nA) The nasal mucosa swells and makes extra mucus that muffles the voice\nB) The epiglottis stays open and lets air slip past the vocal folds\nC) The vocal folds are inflamed and swollen, so they cannot vibrate normally\nD) The cricoid cartilage softens, so the larynx cannot hold its shape\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The vocal folds are inflamed and swollen, so they cannot vibrate normally","why":"Laryngitis affects the larynx and its vocal folds. The folds become inflamed and swollen, so they cannot vibrate normally, and the voice turns hoarse. The problem is in the folds themselves, not in the cartilage frame of the larynx or in the nasal cavity."},{"t":"mc","q":"A stab wound punctures the chest wall, and the lung on that side collapses. Into which space has air entered?\nA) The alveoli  |  B) The mediastinum\nC) The pericardial cavity  |  D) The pleural cavity\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The pleural cavity","why":"The pleural cavity is a sealed space between the parietal and visceral pleurae that normally holds only a thin film of fluid. A wound through the chest wall lets air into this space, breaking the seal, and the lung collapses. This is called a pneumothorax."},{"t":"mc","q":"An infection inflames the air sacs of the lung and fills them with fluid. Which structure is affected, and which disorder is this?\nA) The trachea, in COPD  |  B) The bronchioles, in asthma\nC) The pleural cavity, in pneumothorax  |  D) The alveoli, in pneumonia\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The alveoli, in pneumonia","why":"The air sacs of the lung are the alveoli. In pneumonia, an infection inflames the alveoli and fills them with fluid, so they cannot hold air for gas exchange. Naming both the structure and the disorder links the problem to the exact place it happens."},{"t":"mc","q":"In emphysema, many small alveoli merge into a few large ones. Which structural feature of the lung is lost?\nA) The seal of the pleural cavity  |  B) The C-shaped tracheal rings\nC) The large surface area for gas exchange  |  D) The cartilage of the bronchi\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The large surface area for gas exchange","why":"Many tiny alveoli together have far more wall surface than a few large sacs holding the same volume. When emphysema destroys the walls between alveoli, the small sacs merge and much of that surface disappears. Less surface means less area where gases can cross, and the change is permanent."},{"t":"mc","q":"A premature newborn's Type II alveolar cells are not yet making enough surfactant. What is most likely to happen to the baby's alveoli?\nA) Their walls are permanently destroyed\nB) They leak air out into the pleural cavity\nC) They fill with fluid from an infection\nD) They collapse because surface tension stays high\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) They collapse because surface tension stays high","why":"Surfactant lowers the surface tension of the thin layer of water lining each alveolus. Without enough of it, that surface tension stays high and tends to pull the tiny sacs closed, especially at the end of each breath out. So a newborn without enough surfactant has alveoli that collapse easily, which makes breathing hard work."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Name common respiratory disorders and state which airway, alveolar, or pleural structure each one affects. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to name common respiratory disorders and state which airway, alveolar, or pleural structure each one affects."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to name common respiratory disorders and state which airway, alveolar, or pleural structure each one affects. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: respiratory disorders by structure affected. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, respiratory disorders by structure affected, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]}]},{"week":8,"system":"Muscular","comps":[{"id":"w4-ct-coverings","name":"Connective tissue coverings","can":"Name the epimysium, perimysium, and endomysium and trace how the three sheaths merge into the tendon that anchors muscle to bone.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A muscle strain tears fibers within the belly of a muscle, while a different injury separates the muscle from the bone entirely. Which structure is torn in that second injury?\nA) The endomysium  |  B) The tendon\nC) The perimysium  |  D) The sarcolemma\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The tendon","why":"The three connective tissue sheaths are continuous with one another and with the tendon, so a fiber's pull is carried all the way to the bone. A strain tears fibers and their endomysium inside the belly, but the muscle stays attached. A tendon rupture breaks the cord that joins muscle to bone, separating them entirely, and the two injuries heal on very different timelines."},{"t":"mc","q":"Moving from the outside of a whole muscle inward, which sheath is reached immediately after the epimysium?\nA) The sarcolemma  |  B) The endomysium\nC) The deep fascia  |  D) The perimysium\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The perimysium","why":"The epimysium wraps the whole muscle, and just inside it the muscle is divided into fascicles, each wrapped in perimysium. The perimysium is therefore the next sheath reached after the epimysium. The endomysium comes after that, around each individual fiber."},{"t":"mc","q":"When all three connective tissue sheaths merge and continue beyond the muscle as a cord that attaches to bone, what do they form?\nA) A tendon  |  B) The deep fascia\nC) The periosteum  |  D) A ligament\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A tendon","why":"The epimysium, perimysium, and endomysium are continuous with each other, and at the ends of the muscle they merge and extend beyond the fibers. When this merged tissue forms a cord, it is a tendon, which anchors the muscle to bone. Because the sheaths are continuous with the tendon, the pull of every fiber is carried all the way to the bone."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which statement correctly compares a tendon and a ligament?\nA) A tendon connects muscle to bone, and a ligament connects bone to bone\nB) A ligament connects muscle to bone, and a tendon connects bone to bone\nC) A tendon connects muscle to muscle, and a ligament connects muscle to bone\nD) Both connect muscle to bone, but a ligament is a flat sheet\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A tendon connects muscle to bone, and a ligament connects bone to bone","why":"Tendons and ligaments are both made of dense regular connective tissue, with parallel collagen fibers that resist pulling. The difference is what they connect. A tendon attaches a muscle to a bone and carries the muscle's pull, while a ligament attaches one bone to another and holds a joint together."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which structure is a sheet of dense connective tissue that wraps muscles, separates them, and binds muscles with a common action into functional groups?\nA) The epimysium  |  B) An aponeurosis\nC) The superficial fascia  |  D) The deep fascia\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The deep fascia","why":"Deep fascia is a sheet of dense connective tissue that lies outside the epimysium. It wraps muscles, separates neighboring muscles from each other, and binds muscles that share a common action into groups called compartments. That is why the flexors and extensors of the forearm sit in separate fascial compartments."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Name the epimysium, perimysium, and endomysium and trace how the three sheaths merge into the tendon that anchors muscle to bone. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to name the epimysium, perimysium, and endomysium and trace how the three sheaths merge into the tendon that anchors muscle to bone."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to name the epimysium, perimysium, and endomysium and trace how the three sheaths merge into the tendon that anchors muscle to bone. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: connective tissue coverings. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, connective tissue coverings, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w4-muscle-organization","name":"Levels of organization","can":"Order the nested levels of skeletal muscle from whole muscle through fascicle, fiber, and myofibril down to the myofilament.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"In Duchenne muscular dystrophy, dystrophin fails to link the thin filaments to the sarcolemma. Which two levels of muscle organization does this broken link connect?\nA) The myofilament and the muscle fiber  |  B) The myofibril and the fascicle\nC) The fascicle and the whole muscle  |  D) The muscle fiber and the fascicle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The myofilament and the muscle fiber","why":"Thin filaments are myofilaments, the smallest level, packed inside the myofibrils. The sarcolemma is the plasma membrane of the muscle fiber, a single muscle cell. Dystrophin bridges these two levels so the force of the sarcomere can reach the surface of the fiber and pass on to the connective tissue."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which sequence orders the levels of skeletal muscle organization from largest to smallest?\nA) Muscle, fascicle, fiber, myofibril, myofilament\nB) Myofilament, myofibril, fiber, fascicle, muscle\nC) Muscle, fascicle, fiber, myofilament, myofibril\nD) Muscle, fiber, fascicle, myofibril, myofilament\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Muscle, fascicle, fiber, myofibril, myofilament","why":"A skeletal muscle is a set of nested structures. The whole muscle is wrapped in epimysium and divided into fascicles wrapped in perimysium. Each fascicle holds muscle fibers, single cells wrapped in endomysium. Each fiber is packed with myofibrils, and each myofibril is built of thick and thin myofilaments."},{"t":"mc","q":"Tracing a skeletal muscle from the outside inward, past the whole muscle and its fascicles, which single cell is reached next?\nA) The muscle fiber  |  B) The myofibril\nC) The myofilament  |  D) The sarcomere\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The muscle fiber","why":"A fascicle is a bundle of muscle fibers wrapped in perimysium. Inside it, each muscle fiber is a single long cell wrapped in its own endomysium. Going further in, each fiber is filled with myofibrils, which are built of sarcomeres made from thick and thin myofilaments."},{"t":"mc","q":"Moving inward from the whole muscle, which structure is the first one you reach that is a single cell?\nA) The myofilament  |  B) The myofibril\nC) The muscle fiber  |  D) The fascicle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The muscle fiber","why":"The whole muscle and each fascicle are groups of many cells, so neither is a single cell. The next level in, the muscle fiber, is one long multinucleated muscle cell. Everything below it, the myofibrils and myofilaments, are structures inside that cell rather than cells of their own."},{"t":"mc","q":"A structure is described as a single long, cylindrical cell with many peripheral nuclei, wrapped in endomysium. Which level of muscle organization is it?\nA) The myofibril  |  B) The fascicle\nC) The muscle fiber  |  D) The sarcomere\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The muscle fiber","why":"A muscle fiber is one skeletal muscle cell. It is long and cylindrical, carries many nuclei just under the sarcolemma, and is wrapped in its own endomysium. It sits between the fascicle (a bundle of fibers) above it and the myofibril (a contractile rod inside it) below it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Order the nested levels of skeletal muscle from whole muscle through fascicle, fiber, and myofibril down to the myofilament. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to order the nested levels of skeletal muscle from whole muscle through fascicle, fiber, and myofibril down to the myofilament."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to order the nested levels of skeletal muscle from whole muscle through fascicle, fiber, and myofibril down to the myofilament. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: levels of organization. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, levels of organization, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w4-muscle-fiber-parts","name":"Muscle fiber internal structure","can":"Identify the sarcolemma, sarcoplasm, myonuclei, myofibrils, sarcoplasmic reticulum, terminal cisternae, T tubules, and triad of a muscle fiber.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A signal arriving at the surface of a muscle fiber must reach the calcium store throughout the fiber almost at once. Which repeating structure makes this possible?\nA) The triad  |  B) The myofibril\nC) The Z disc  |  D) The sarcomere\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The triad","why":"The triad is one transverse (T) tubule flanked by two terminal cisternae of the sarcoplasmic reticulum. The T tubule brings the surface signal deep into the fiber, and right beside it the cisternae hold calcium. Because triads repeat along every myofibril, the signal reaches the calcium store throughout the fiber almost at the same moment."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which statement correctly compares myoglobin and glycosomes in a muscle fiber?\nA) Myoglobin stores oxygen, and glycosomes store glycogen as a source of glucose\nB) Myoglobin stores calcium ions, and glycosomes store oxygen\nC) Both store oxygen, but only myoglobin is red\nD) Myoglobin stores glycogen, and glycosomes store oxygen\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Myoglobin stores oxygen, and glycosomes store glycogen as a source of glucose","why":"A working muscle fiber needs both oxygen and fuel to make ATP. Myoglobin, a red pigment in the sarcoplasm, stores oxygen. Glycosomes are granules of glycogen that are broken down to glucose when the fiber is active, supplying the fuel side of energy production."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which structure carries the signal that arrives at the sarcolemma deep into the interior of the muscle fiber?\nA) The endomysium  |  B) The T tubule\nC) The terminal cistern  |  D) The myofibril\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The T tubule","why":"T tubules are narrow tunnels of the sarcolemma that dive into the fiber at right angles to its length. Because they are continuous with the surface membrane, they carry the surface signal deep into the cell. This lets the signal reach the sarcoplasmic reticulum around every myofibril at almost the same moment."},{"t":"mc","q":"Most tubules of the sarcoplasmic reticulum run lengthwise along the myofibril. At which region of the sarcomere do they connect with one another?\nA) At the H zone  |  B) At each A band and I band junction\nC) At the Z disc  |  D) Within the I band\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) At the H zone","why":"The longitudinal tubules of the sarcoplasmic reticulum run along the myofibril and connect with each other in the region of the H zone, the lighter center of the A band. At the A band and I band junctions, the network widens into terminal cisterns that sit beside the T tubules. Knowing these two landmarks helps you picture the sleeve of sarcoplasmic reticulum around each sarcomere."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which pair of structures is found in large numbers around the myofibrils and sarcoplasmic reticulum, supplying energy for contraction?\nA) Ribosomes and lysosomes  |  B) Mitochondria and glycogen granules\nC) Nuclei and centrioles  |  D) T tubules and terminal cisterns\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Mitochondria and glycogen granules","why":"Contraction uses a great deal of ATP, so the fiber packs energy-producing structures close to where they are needed. Mitochondria, which make most of the ATP, sit in rows between the myofibrils, and glycogen granules supply the glucose fuel. Both cluster around the myofibrils and sarcoplasmic reticulum."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the sarcolemma, sarcoplasm, myonuclei, myofibrils, sarcoplasmic reticulum, terminal cisternae, T tubules, and triad of a muscle fiber. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the sarcolemma, sarcoplasm, myonuclei, myofibrils, sarcoplasmic reticulum, terminal cisternae, T tubules, and triad of a muscle fiber."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the sarcolemma, sarcoplasm, myonuclei, myofibrils, sarcoplasmic reticulum, terminal cisternae, T tubules, and triad of a muscle fiber. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: muscle fiber internal structure. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, muscle fiber internal structure, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w4-sarcomere","name":"Sarcomere bands and filaments","can":"Diagram a sarcomere and name the Z disc, A band, I band, H zone, M line, and zone of overlap, stating which filaments occupy each region.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A cross section cut through one region of a sarcomere shows only thin filaments. Which band was cut, and which structure runs through the center of that band?\nA) The H zone, with the M line at its center\nB) The A band, with the H zone at its center\nC) The I band, with a Z disc at its center\nD) The I band, with the M line at its center\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The I band, with a Z disc at its center","why":"The I band is the light band and holds thin filaments only. A Z disc runs through its center, which is why one I band is shared by two neighboring sarcomeres. The H zone, by contrast, holds thick filaments only."},{"t":"mc","q":"A cross section through a sarcomere shows each thick filament surrounded by thin filaments. Which region was cut, and which band is it part of?\nA) The H zone, at the center of the A band\nB) The zone of overlap, at the edge of the A band\nC) The zone of overlap, in the middle of the I band\nD) The I band, right beside the Z disc\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The zone of overlap, at the edge of the A band","why":"Thick and thin filaments interleave only in the zone of overlap. That region lies at each end of the A band, where thin filaments reaching in from the Z discs overlap the thick filaments. The H zone at the center has thick filaments only, and the I band has thin filaments only."},{"t":"mc","q":"A cut through the exact middle of a sarcomere hits a protein line that binds the thick filaments together. Which line is it, and which region surrounds it?\nA) The M line, surrounded by the H zone\nB) The Z disc, surrounded by the I band\nC) The M line, surrounded by the I band\nD) The Z disc, surrounded by the H zone\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The M line, surrounded by the H zone","why":"The M line is the protein line in the center of the H zone, and it anchors the thick filaments to one another. The H zone is the lighter region in the center of the A band, where only thick filaments lie. The Z discs sit at the two ends of the sarcomere, not its middle."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which sarco term names the contractile unit of a myofibril, running from one Z disc to the next?\nA) The sarcolemma  |  B) The sarcomere\nC) The sarcoplasmic reticulum  |  D) The sarcoplasm\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The sarcomere","why":"The sarcomere is the contractile unit of muscle, the segment of a myofibril from one Z disc to the next. Thousands of sarcomeres line up end to end along each myofibril, and their repeating light and dark bands create the striations of skeletal muscle. The name combines sarco, flesh, with mere, meaning part."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which region of the sarcomere contains only thin filaments?\nA) The zone of overlap  |  B) The H zone\nC) The I band  |  D) The A band\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The I band","why":"The I band is the region at each end of a sarcomere where thin filaments extend from the Z disc but have not yet reached the thick filaments. Because it contains only thin filaments, it is less dense and appears light. Its mirror image is the H zone in the center, which contains only thick filaments."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Diagram a sarcomere and name the Z disc, A band, I band, H zone, M line, and zone of overlap, stating which filaments occupy each region. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to diagram a sarcomere and name the Z disc, A band, I band, H zone, M line, and zone of overlap, stating which filaments occupy each region."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to diagram a sarcomere and name the Z disc, A band, I band, H zone, M line, and zone of overlap, stating which filaments occupy each region. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: sarcomere bands and filaments. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, sarcomere bands and filaments, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w4-myofilament-proteins","name":"Myofilament and structural proteins","can":"Distinguish thick from thin filaments by protein and anchor point and identify the roles of titin, nebulin, alpha-actinin, myomesin, and dystrophin.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A mutation removes the protein that fastens titin to the Z disc. Which protein is missing, and which filament loses the anchor that titin gives it?\nA) Myomesin; the thick filament loses its anchor\nB) Nebulin; the thin filament loses its anchor\nC) Alpha-actinin; the thick filament loses its anchor\nD) Alpha-actinin; the thin filament loses its anchor\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Alpha-actinin; the thick filament loses its anchor","why":"Alpha-actinin sits at the Z disc and binds both the thin filaments and titin there. Titin spans from the Z disc to the M line and anchors the thick filament. If alpha-actinin is missing, titin comes loose from the Z disc, so the thick filament loses the anchor that titin gives it."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which structural protein links the thin filaments to the sarcolemma and is faulty in Duchenne muscular dystrophy?\nA) Titin  |  B) Dystrophin\nC) Myomesin  |  D) Nebulin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Dystrophin","why":"Dystrophin links the thin filaments of the sarcomere to the sarcolemma, so the force of contraction is passed from the sarcomere out to the connective tissue. In Duchenne muscular dystrophy the gene for dystrophin is faulty, so this link fails. The fiber then damages itself with ordinary use, which causes progressive weakness."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which structural protein spans from the Z disc to the M line, anchors the thick filament, and gives the sarcomere elastic recoil?\nA) Nebulin  |  B) Titin\nC) Dystrophin  |  D) Alpha-actinin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Titin","why":"Titin is a giant structural protein that stretches from the Z disc to the M line. It anchors each thick filament in place, and its spring-like structure gives the sarcomere elastic recoil and keeps it from being overstretched. Titin helps explain the property of elasticity at the level of the sarcomere."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which intermediate filament protein surrounds the Z discs and ties neighboring myofibrils together side by side?\nA) Titin  |  B) Myomesin\nC) Nebulin  |  D) Desmin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Desmin","why":"Desmin is an intermediate filament protein that wraps around the Z discs and connects each myofibril to its neighbors. By tying the myofibrils together at their Z discs, it keeps the sarcomeres of neighboring myofibrils lined up, which is part of why the bands look continuous across the whole fiber. Desmin is not the elastic filament of the sarcomere; that role belongs to titin."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which protein anchors the thin filaments and titin to the Z disc?\nA) Titin  |  B) Myomesin\nC) Desmin  |  D) Alpha-actinin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Alpha-actinin","why":"Alpha-actinin is a structural protein of the Z disc. It binds the ends of the thin filaments and the ends of titin, holding them firmly at the border of the sarcomere. Its name hints at its partner, actin, which is the main protein of the thin filaments it anchors."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Distinguish thick from thin filaments by protein and anchor point and identify the roles of titin, nebulin, alpha-actinin, myomesin, and dystrophin. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish thick from thin filaments by protein and anchor point and identify the roles of titin, nebulin, alpha-actinin, myomesin, and dystrophin."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to distinguish thick from thin filaments by protein and anchor point and identify the roles of titin, nebulin, alpha-actinin, myomesin, and dystrophin. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: myofilament and structural proteins. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, myofilament and structural proteins, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w4-muscle-tissue-types","name":"Three muscle tissue types","can":"Compare skeletal, cardiac, and smooth muscle by location, striations, control, and cell features including intercalated discs.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Cardiac muscle is striated, involuntary, and made of branching cells joined by intercalated discs. Which of these features is unique to cardiac muscle among the three muscle types?\nA) Involuntary control  |  B) Intercalated discs joining cells\nC) A spindle-shaped cell body  |  D) Striations across the cells\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Intercalated discs joining cells","why":"Intercalated discs joining branching cells are found only in cardiac muscle. Its striations are shared with skeletal muscle, and its involuntary control is shared with smooth muscle, so neither feature identifies cardiac muscle alone. Looking for the one feature no other tissue has is the fastest way to identify it on a slide."},{"t":"mc","q":"You need a sample of smooth muscle tissue to make a slide. Which location would give you one?\nA) The wall of the small intestine  |  B) The wall of the left ventricle\nC) The biceps brachii  |  D) The tongue\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The wall of the small intestine","why":"Smooth muscle lives in the walls of hollow organs, and the small intestine has layers of it that squeeze food along. Each of the other locations contains striated muscle instead. Matching each muscle tissue to its typical location is part of identifying it in lab."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which feature do cardiac and smooth muscle share that skeletal muscle does not?\nA) Involuntary control  |  B) Attachment to bones\nC) Many peripheral nuclei  |  D) Striations\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Involuntary control","why":"Cardiac muscle pumps blood and smooth muscle moves material through hollow organs, and neither one is under conscious control. Skeletal muscle is the only voluntary muscle tissue. Grouping features this way, which are shared and which are unique, makes the three tissues easier to tell apart."},{"t":"mc","q":"A slide labeled tongue shows striated, multinucleated fibers running in several directions. Which tissue is it, and how is it controlled?\nA) Skeletal muscle, under voluntary control\nB) Smooth muscle, under involuntary control\nC) Cardiac muscle, under involuntary control\nD) Smooth muscle, under voluntary control\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Skeletal muscle, under voluntary control","why":"Striations together with many nuclei per fiber mark skeletal muscle. The tongue is skeletal muscle, which is why you can move it at will to speak, chew, and swallow. Its fibers run in several directions, which lets the tongue change shape as well as position."},{"t":"mc","q":"A tissue is striated, contracts without conscious control, and has cells joined by intercalated discs. Which tissue is it?\nA) Cardiac muscle  |  B) Smooth muscle\nC) Nervous tissue  |  D) Skeletal muscle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Cardiac muscle","why":"Cardiac muscle is the only muscle tissue that is striated but involuntary, and it is the only one with intercalated discs, the junctions that join its branching cells end to end. Any one of those intercalated discs on a slide is enough to identify the tissue as cardiac muscle. It is found only in the heart wall."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Compare skeletal, cardiac, and smooth muscle by location, striations, control, and cell features including intercalated discs. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to compare skeletal, cardiac, and smooth muscle by location, striations, control, and cell features including intercalated discs."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to compare skeletal, cardiac, and smooth muscle by location, striations, control, and cell features including intercalated discs. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: three muscle tissue types. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, three muscle tissue types, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w4-fascicle-patterns","name":"Fascicle arrangement patterns","can":"Identify parallel, fusiform, circular, convergent, and pennate fascicle patterns and give an example muscle for each, relating architecture to power versus range of motion.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A surgeon planning a tendon transfer needs a muscle built for power to take over a demanding job. Which fascicle architecture should the surgeon look for?\nA) A pennate muscle  |  B) A circular muscle\nC) A parallel muscle  |  D) A fusiform muscle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A pennate muscle","why":"In a tendon transfer, a working muscle is rerouted to take over the job of a muscle that no longer works, so its architecture should match that job. A pennate muscle packs many short fascicles at an angle around a tendon, so more fibers pull and it produces more power. Parallel and fusiform muscles favor range of motion instead."},{"t":"mc","q":"A muscle fiber can shorten to about two thirds of its resting length. Why does a muscle with longer fascicles move its insertion farther?\nA) They contract with more force  |  B) They contain more nuclei\nC) They shorten by a greater distance  |  D) They attach at a steeper angle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) They shorten by a greater distance","why":"If every fiber shortens by about one third of its length, a long fiber shortens by more centimeters than a short fiber. A muscle with long fascicles therefore pulls its insertion a greater distance toward its origin. That is why parallel muscles, with long fascicles, have a larger range of motion than pennate muscles."},{"t":"mc","q":"A circular muscle, such as a sphincter, has fascicles arranged in rings around an opening. Why is this ring arrangement suited to controlling a passage?\nA) Contracting the ring widens the opening\nB) Contracting the ring pulls a bone toward the origin\nC) Contracting the ring narrows or closes the opening\nD) Ring fibers pack at an angle around a central tendon\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Contracting the ring narrows or closes the opening","why":"When the concentric fibers of a circular muscle shorten, the ring gets smaller and the opening in the center narrows or closes. That lets the muscle control what passes through, as the orbicularis oris does at the mouth. Relaxing the ring lets the opening widen again."},{"t":"mc","q":"The pectoralis major has a broad origin, and its fascicles narrow onto a single tendon, giving the muscle a fan shape. Which fascicle arrangement is this?\nA) Convergent  |  B) Circular\nC) Pennate  |  D) Fusiform\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Convergent","why":"A convergent muscle has a broad origin whose fascicles come together onto a single tendon of insertion. This gives the muscle a triangular or fan shape. The pectoralis major spreads across the chest from the clavicle and sternum and converges on a tendon that inserts on the humerus."},{"t":"mc","q":"A muscle is built for power, with many short fascicles packed at an angle around a central tendon. Which fascicle pattern is this?\nA) Circular  |  B) Convergent\nC) Parallel  |  D) Pennate\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Pennate","why":"A pennate muscle packs many short fascicles at an angle around a tendon, like the barbs of a feather. Because so many fibers fit into the muscle and all pull on the tendon, the muscle produces more power. The trade-off is a smaller range of motion, because short fascicles cannot shorten very far."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify parallel, fusiform, circular, convergent, and pennate fascicle patterns and give an example muscle for each, relating architecture to power versus range of motion. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify parallel, fusiform, circular, convergent, and pennate fascicle patterns and give an example muscle for each, relating architecture to power versus range of motion."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify parallel, fusiform, circular, convergent, and pennate fascicle patterns and give an example muscle for each, relating architecture to power versus range of motion. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: fascicle arrangement patterns. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, fascicle arrangement patterns, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w4-muscle-roles-naming","name":"Muscle roles and naming","can":"Classify agonist, antagonist, synergist, and fixator roles in a movement and decode a muscle name from its direction, size, shape, location, action, origins, or attachments.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The biceps brachii is the prime mover during elbow flexion but acts as an antagonist during elbow extension. What does this show about muscle roles?\nA) Each muscle has one fixed role for life\nB) Only flexor muscles can be prime movers\nC) Its role depends on which movement is happening\nD) A muscle changes roles only after an injury\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Its role depends on which movement is happening","why":"Agonist, antagonist, synergist, and fixator are job titles for a specific movement, not permanent labels. The biceps brachii is the prime mover when the elbow bends, but when the triceps brachii straightens the elbow, the biceps relaxes and stretches as the antagonist. The same muscle can even act as a synergist or fixator in other movements."},{"t":"mc","q":"The brachialis attaches to the distal humerus and to the ulna, and it flexes the elbow. During elbow flexion the forearm moves while the arm stays steady. Which attachment is its origin?\nA) Its attachment on the scapula  |  B) Its attachment on the radius\nC) Its attachment on the ulna  |  D) Its attachment on the humerus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Its attachment on the humerus","why":"The origin is the attachment on the bone that stays relatively still, and in the limbs it is usually the proximal one. When the brachialis flexes the elbow, the humerus stays steady and the ulna moves up toward it. That makes the humerus attachment the origin and the ulna attachment the insertion."},{"t":"mc","q":"When a muscle contracts, which statement describes the usual movement of its attachments?\nA) The insertion is pulled toward the origin\nB) Both attachments are pushed apart\nC) The origin is pulled toward the insertion\nD) The belly lengthens while both attachments stay still\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The insertion is pulled toward the origin","why":"A contracting muscle shortens and pulls on both of its attachments, but the bone carrying the origin is held steady. As a result, the insertion on the more movable bone is pulled toward the origin. This is why the insertion is defined as the attachment that moves."},{"t":"mc","q":"The biceps brachii runs from the scapula to the radius. When it flexes the elbow, the forearm rises while the scapula stays steady. Which bone carries its insertion?\nA) The humerus  |  B) The scapula\nC) The radius  |  D) The ulna\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The radius","why":"The insertion is the attachment on the bone that moves, which is pulled toward the origin. During elbow flexion the forearm rises while the scapula stays steady, so the attachment on the radius (at the radial tuberosity) is the insertion. The scapula attachments are the origins of its two heads."},{"t":"mc","q":"In the name gluteus maximus, which naming criterion does the word maximus use?\nA) Action  |  B) Shape\nC) Relative size  |  D) Direction of fibers\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Relative size","why":"Maximus means largest, so it describes the muscle's relative size compared with its neighbors. The gluteus maximus is the largest of the three gluteal muscles, with the gluteus medius and gluteus minimus being smaller. The other word, gluteus, names the location in the buttock region."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Classify agonist, antagonist, synergist, and fixator roles in a movement and decode a muscle name from its direction, size, shape, location, action, origins, or attachments. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to classify agonist, antagonist, synergist, and fixator roles in a movement and decode a muscle name from its direction, size, shape, location, action, origins, or attachments."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to classify agonist, antagonist, synergist, and fixator roles in a movement and decode a muscle name from its direction, size, shape, location, action, origins, or attachments. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: muscle roles and naming. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, muscle roles and naming, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w4-lever-systems","name":"Lever systems","can":"Identify the fulcrum, effort, and load of a lever and classify first-, second-, and third-class levers with a body example of each.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Most body movements run on third-class levers, which work at a mechanical disadvantage. What does the body gain in return?\nA) A smaller force needed from the muscle\nB) A greater load the limb can lift\nC) Speed and a wide range of motion\nD) A more stable joint\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Speed and a wide range of motion","why":"In a third-class lever the muscle inserts close to the joint, so the effort acts closer to the fulcrum than the load. That is a mechanical disadvantage, meaning the muscle must pull much harder than the weight it lifts. In exchange, a small shortening of the muscle swings the far end of the bone through a large arc quickly, so the body gains speed and range of motion."},{"t":"mc","q":"When you stand on tiptoe, the ball of the foot is the fulcrum, the body's weight is the load, and the calf muscles pull up on the heel as the effort. Which lever class is this?\nA) A third-class lever  |  B) A second-class lever\nC) A fourth-class lever  |  D) A first-class lever\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A second-class lever","why":"To classify a lever, find which part is in the middle. On tiptoe, the fulcrum is at the ball of the foot in front, the effort is at the heel in back, and the body's weight passes down through the ankle in between. With the load in the middle, this is a second-class lever, like a wheelbarrow."},{"t":"mc","q":"Nodding the head uses the atlanto-occipital joint, the posterior neck muscles, and the weight of the face. Which lever class is this?\nA) A first-class lever  |  B) A third-class lever\nC) It is not a lever system  |  D) A second-class lever\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A first-class lever","why":"In head nodding, the atlanto-occipital joint between the skull and the atlas is the fulcrum. The weight of the face sits in front of the joint as the load, and the posterior neck muscles pull from behind as the effort. With the fulcrum in the middle, this is a first-class lever, like a seesaw."},{"t":"mc","q":"When the biceps brachii flexes the elbow to lift a weight in the hand, which lever class is at work?\nA) A fourth-class lever  |  B) A second-class lever\nC) A first-class lever  |  D) A third-class lever\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) A third-class lever","why":"In elbow flexion, the elbow joint is the fulcrum, the weight in the hand is the load at the far end, and the biceps brachii pulls on the radius just below the elbow as the effort. With the effort in the middle, this is a third-class lever. Third-class levers are the most common in the body."},{"t":"mc","q":"Whether a lever works at a mechanical advantage or a mechanical disadvantage depends on what?\nA) The length of the muscle's tendon\nB) The size of the muscle supplying the effort\nC) How far the effort and the load each act from the fulcrum\nD) The number of origins the muscle has\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) How far the effort and the load each act from the fulcrum","why":"Mechanical advantage comes from comparing distances. When the effort acts farther from the fulcrum than the load, a small effort can move a large load, which is a mechanical advantage. When the effort acts closer to the fulcrum than the load, a larger effort is needed, which is a mechanical disadvantage, but the load moves faster and farther."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the fulcrum, effort, and load of a lever and classify first-, second-, and third-class levers with a body example of each. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the fulcrum, effort, and load of a lever and classify first-, second-, and third-class levers with a body example of each."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the fulcrum, effort, and load of a lever and classify first-, second-, and third-class levers with a body example of each. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: lever systems. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, lever systems, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w7-lab-facial-expression","name":"Muscles of facial expression","can":"Identify the muscles of facial expression including occipitofrontalis, orbicularis oculi, orbicularis oris, buccinator, zygomaticus, and platysma on a specimen or model.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"How do most muscles of facial expression differ from typical skeletal muscles of the limbs?\nA) They contract without any nerve signal reaching them\nB) They insert into the skin instead of onto a bone\nC) They lack an origin and attach only at an insertion\nD) They are made of smooth muscle, not skeletal muscle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) They insert into the skin instead of onto a bone","why":"Most muscles of facial expression arise from bones of the skull but insert into the skin of the face or into other facial muscles rather than onto a bone. When they contract, they pull on the skin, which is how they produce smiles, frowns, and raised eyebrows. Limb muscles, in contrast, usually cross a joint and insert on a bone to move it."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which muscle closes the eyelids, as in blinking?\nA) Orbicularis oculi  |  B) Levator palpebrae superioris\nC) Frontalis  |  D) Orbicularis oris\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Orbicularis oculi","why":"The orbicularis oculi is a ring of muscle around the eye. Its fascicles are arranged in a circle, so contracting it squeezes the eyelids closed like a sphincter, for blinking or squinting tightly. Its name tells you this: orbicularis means small circle, and oculi means of the eye."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which muscle closes and purses the lips?\nA) Buccinator  |  B) Orbicularis oculi\nC) Orbicularis oris  |  D) Zygomaticus major\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Orbicularis oris","why":"The orbicularis oris encircles the mouth, with fascicles arranged in rings that act like a sphincter. Contracting it closes the lips and pushes them forward, as when whistling or kissing. Its name means small circle of the mouth."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which muscle raises the eyebrows and wrinkles the forehead?\nA) Orbicularis oculi  |  B) Occipital belly of the occipitofrontalis\nC) Procerus  |  D) Frontal belly of the occipitofrontalis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Frontal belly of the occipitofrontalis","why":"The occipitofrontalis has two bellies joined by a flat sheet of tendon over the top of the skull, the galea aponeurotica (epicranial aponeurosis). The frontal belly, over the forehead, inserts into the skin of the eyebrows; when it contracts it raises the eyebrows and wrinkles the forehead. The occipital belly at the back anchors the aponeurosis and pulls the scalp backward."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the muscles of facial expression including occipitofrontalis, orbicularis oculi, orbicularis oris, buccinator, zygomaticus, and platysma on a specimen or model. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the muscles of facial expression including occipitofrontalis, orbicularis oculi, orbicularis oris, buccinator, zygomaticus, and platysma on a specimen or model."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the muscles of facial expression including occipitofrontalis, orbicularis oculi, orbicularis oris, buccinator, zygomaticus, and platysma on a specimen or model. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: muscles of facial expression. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, muscles of facial expression, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w7-lab-mastication","name":"Muscles of mastication","can":"Identify the muscles of mastication masseter, temporalis, and the medial and lateral pterygoids on a specimen or model.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"When you clench your teeth, you can feel one muscle harden over the back of your cheek and another over your temple. Which two muscles are these?\nA) Buccinator and frontal belly  |  B) Medial and lateral pterygoids\nC) Masseter and temporalis  |  D) Masseter and sternocleidomastoid\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Masseter and temporalis","why":"The masseter and the temporalis are the two superficial muscles of mastication, and both elevate the mandible to close the jaw. The masseter lies over the back of the cheek near the angle of the jaw, and the temporalis fans over the side of the skull at the temple. Because both are close to the surface, clenching makes both easy to feel."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which two muscles of mastication sandwich the ramus of the mandible, one attached to its lateral surface and one to its medial surface?\nA) Masseter and medial pterygoid  |  B) Temporalis and lateral pterygoid\nC) Masseter and temporalis  |  D) Medial and lateral pterygoids\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Masseter and medial pterygoid","why":"The masseter runs from the zygomatic arch to the lateral ramus and angle of the mandible. The medial pterygoid runs from the medial surface of the lateral pterygoid plate to the medial surface of the ramus and angle. They hold the ramus between them from outside and inside, and both elevate the mandible."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which two muscles of mastication both protrude the mandible, pushing the lower jaw forward?\nA) Masseter and temporalis  |  B) Temporalis and lateral pterygoid\nC) Medial and lateral pterygoids  |  D) Masseter and medial pterygoid\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Medial and lateral pterygoids","why":"The medial pterygoid elevates and protrudes the mandible, and the lateral pterygoid protrudes it and opens the jaw. Both start on the lateral pterygoid plate of the sphenoid and both help with side to side grinding. The temporalis does the opposite of protrusion, retracting the mandible."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which chewing muscle elevates the mandible and can be felt bulging at the angle of the jaw when you clench your teeth?\nA) Lateral pterygoid  |  B) Temporalis\nC) Buccinator  |  D) Masseter\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Masseter","why":"The masseter runs from the zygomatic arch down to the angle and lateral surface of the ramus of the mandible. It is the prime mover for closing the jaw and one of the strongest muscles for its size. Because it lies just under the skin at the back of the cheek, you can feel it harden at the angle of the jaw when you clench your teeth."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which fan-shaped muscle over the temporal bone elevates and retracts the mandible?\nA) Masseter  |  B) Zygomaticus major\nC) Temporalis  |  D) Medial pterygoid\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Temporalis","why":"The temporalis has a broad, fan-shaped origin on the temporal fossa on the side of the skull, and its fascicles converge onto the coronoid process of the mandible. It elevates the mandible to close the jaw, and its posterior fibers retract it (pull it backward). Its name comes from its location over the temporal bone."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the muscles of mastication masseter, temporalis, and the medial and lateral pterygoids on a specimen or model. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the muscles of mastication masseter, temporalis, and the medial and lateral pterygoids on a specimen or model."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the muscles of mastication masseter, temporalis, and the medial and lateral pterygoids on a specimen or model. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: muscles of mastication. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, muscles of mastication, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w7-lab-neck","name":"Muscles of the neck","can":"Identify the sternocleidomastoid, the scalenes, and the suprahyoid and infrahyoid muscles of the neck on a specimen or model.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"When the right sternocleidomastoid contracts alone, which way does the face turn?\nA) Down toward the chest  |  B) Up toward the ceiling\nC) Toward the left side  |  D) Toward the right side\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Toward the left side","why":"The sternocleidomastoid runs from the sternum and clavicle in front up to the mastoid process behind the ear. When the right muscle shortens, it pulls the right mastoid process down and forward, which rotates the head so the face turns toward the left, the opposite side. You can see and feel the right muscle stand out when you turn your face to the left."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which list contains only suprahyoid muscles?\nA) Sternohyoid, omohyoid, thyrohyoid, sternothyroid\nB) Digastric, sternohyoid, mylohyoid, omohyoid\nC) Sternocleidomastoid, scalenes, stylohyoid, geniohyoid\nD) Digastric, mylohyoid, stylohyoid, geniohyoid\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Digastric, mylohyoid, stylohyoid, geniohyoid","why":"The hyoid bone sorts the anterior neck muscles. Suprahyoid muscles sit above it and elevate it: the digastric, mylohyoid, stylohyoid, and geniohyoid. Infrahyoid muscles sit below it and depress it: the sternohyoid, sternothyroid, omohyoid, and thyrohyoid."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which muscle runs from the sternum and clavicle to the mastoid process, flexing the neck and turning the head?\nA) Platysma  |  B) Sternohyoid\nC) Trapezius  |  D) Sternocleidomastoid\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Sternocleidomastoid","why":"The sternocleidomastoid is named for its attachments: the sternum, the clavicle (cleido), and the mastoid process of the temporal bone. When both sides contract together, they flex the neck, bringing the chin toward the chest. When one side contracts alone, it turns the face toward the opposite side."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which suprahyoid muscle forms the floor of the mouth?\nA) Geniohyoid  |  B) Stylohyoid\nC) Mylohyoid  |  D) Thyrohyoid\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Mylohyoid","why":"The mylohyoid runs from the mylohyoid line on the inside of the mandible to the body of the hyoid. It elevates the hyoid and forms the floor of the mouth. Like the other suprahyoid muscles, it sits above the hyoid bone."},{"t":"mc","q":"The scalene muscles run from the cervical vertebrae down to which ribs?\nA) Ribs 3 to 5  |  B) Ribs 7 to 12\nC) Ribs 1 and 2  |  D) Rib 12 only\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Ribs 1 and 2","why":"The scalenes start on the transverse processes of the cervical vertebrae. The anterior and middle scalenes insert on rib 1, and the posterior scalene inserts on rib 2. That is why the scalenes elevate ribs 1 and 2."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the sternocleidomastoid, the scalenes, and the suprahyoid and infrahyoid muscles of the neck on a specimen or model. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the sternocleidomastoid, the scalenes, and the suprahyoid and infrahyoid muscles of the neck on a specimen or model."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the sternocleidomastoid, the scalenes, and the suprahyoid and infrahyoid muscles of the neck on a specimen or model. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: muscles of the neck. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, muscles of the neck, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w7-lab-extraocular","name":"Extraocular muscles","can":"Identify the four rectus and two oblique extraocular muscles and the levator palpebrae superioris on a specimen or model.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient's upper eyelid droops, but the eye itself moves normally up, down, toward the nose, and away from it. Which muscle is most likely affected?\nA) Superior rectus  |  B) Levator palpebrae superioris\nC) Orbicularis oculi  |  D) Superior oblique\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Levator palpebrae superioris","why":"Normal movement in every direction means the four recti and the two obliques are working. The muscle in the orbit that raises the upper eyelid is the levator palpebrae superioris, so a drooping lid with normal eye movement points to it."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which four extraocular muscles share an origin at the common tendinous ring at the back of the orbit?\nA) Superior and inferior recti, plus both obliques\nB) Superior, inferior, medial, and lateral recti\nC) Medial and lateral recti, plus both obliques\nD) Both obliques, plus the medial and superior recti\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Superior, inferior, medial, and lateral recti","why":"All four rectus muscles start on the common tendinous ring at the back of the orbit and run forward to attach to the eyeball. The obliques start elsewhere. The superior oblique starts on the body of the sphenoid above and medial to the optic canal, and the inferior oblique starts on the floor of the orbit at the front, on the maxilla."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which pair of extraocular muscles moves the eye in opposite directions, one toward the nose and one away from it?\nA) Superior rectus and inferior rectus\nB) Superior oblique and inferior oblique\nC) Medial rectus and lateral rectus\nD) Lateral rectus and superior oblique\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Medial rectus and lateral rectus","why":"The medial rectus attaches to the medial surface of the eyeball and adducts the eye, moving it toward the nose. The lateral rectus attaches to the lateral surface and abducts the eye, moving it away from the nose. They form an opposing pair."},{"t":"mc","q":"How many extraocular muscles move each eyeball?\nA) Seven: four recti, two obliques, and a levator\nB) Eight: four recti and four obliques\nC) Four: the four recti only\nD) Six: four recti and two obliques\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Six: four recti and two obliques","why":"Six extraocular muscles attach to each eyeball and move it: the superior, inferior, medial, and lateral recti, and the superior and inferior obliques. The recti pull the eye up, down, in, and out, while the obliques help with rotation and diagonal movements. The levator palpebrae superioris sits in the orbit too, but it raises the upper eyelid rather than moving the eyeball."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which extraocular muscle turns the eye laterally, away from the nose?\nA) Inferior rectus  |  B) Superior rectus\nC) Medial rectus  |  D) Lateral rectus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Lateral rectus","why":"The lateral rectus attaches to the lateral side of the eyeball, so when it contracts it pulls the front of the eye outward, away from the nose. This movement is called abduction of the eye. Its partner on the opposite side of the eyeball, the medial rectus, pulls the eye toward the nose."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the four rectus and two oblique extraocular muscles and the levator palpebrae superioris on a specimen or model. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the four rectus and two oblique extraocular muscles and the levator palpebrae superioris on a specimen or model."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the four rectus and two oblique extraocular muscles and the levator palpebrae superioris on a specimen or model. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: extraocular muscles. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, extraocular muscles, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]}]},{"week":11,"system":"Lymphatic & Immune","comps":[{"id":"w4-lymph-pathway","name":"Lymph pathway","can":"Define lymph and trace its one-way route from lymphatic capillaries through collecting vessels, nodes, trunks, and ducts back to the subclavian veins.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has an infected cut on the right hand. Lymph from the right upper limb reaches the blood through which duct?\nA) The right lumbar trunk  |  B) The right lymphatic duct\nC) The cisterna chyli  |  D) The thoracic duct\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The right lymphatic duct","why":"Lymph from the right upper limb travels in the right subclavian trunk. That trunk empties into the right lymphatic duct, which drains the upper right quarter of the body. The duct then returns the lymph to the blood at the junction of the right subclavian and right internal jugular veins."},{"t":"mc","q":"Lymph from the left leg reaches the bloodstream through which duct?\nA) The intestinal trunk  |  B) The right lymphatic duct\nC) The left lumbar trunk  |  D) The thoracic duct\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The thoracic duct","why":"Lymph from the left leg travels in the left lumbar trunk to the cisterna chyli, the start of the thoracic duct. The thoracic duct carries it up through the thorax and empties at the junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins. Both legs drain this way, since everything below the diaphragm belongs to the thoracic duct."},{"t":"mc","q":"After a meal, lymph draining from the stomach and small intestine travels through which trunk?\nA) The intestinal trunk  |  B) The lumbar trunk\nC) The bronchomediastinal trunk  |  D) The subclavian trunk\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The intestinal trunk","why":"The intestinal trunk drains the digestive organs of the abdomen, so lymph from the stomach and small intestine passes through it. After a meal that lymph includes chyle from the lacteals. The intestinal trunk empties into the cisterna chyli at the start of the thoracic duct."},{"t":"mc","q":"A tumor blocks the thoracic duct at its end, where it joins the veins in the left side of the neck. Lymph from which region would still return to the blood normally?\nA) The left upper limb  |  B) The left leg\nC) The abdominal organs  |  D) The right side of the head and neck\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The right side of the head and neck","why":"Lymph from the right side of the head and neck travels in the right jugular trunk to the right lymphatic duct, which is a separate vessel that empties on the right side of the neck. A block at the end of the thoracic duct does not touch that route. Every other region listed sends its lymph through the thoracic duct, so it would back up behind the tumor."},{"t":"mc","q":"Lymph from the right side of the scalp enters which trunk before reaching its duct?\nA) The left jugular trunk  |  B) The right jugular trunk\nC) The right subclavian trunk  |  D) The right bronchomediastinal trunk\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The right jugular trunk","why":"The scalp is part of the head, and the head and neck are drained by the jugular trunks. Because the scalp region is on the right, its lymph enters the right jugular trunk. That trunk then empties into the right lymphatic duct."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Define lymph and trace its one-way route from lymphatic capillaries through collecting vessels, nodes, trunks, and ducts back to the subclavian veins. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to define lymph and trace its one-way route from lymphatic capillaries through collecting vessels, nodes, trunks, and ducts back to the subclavian veins."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to define lymph and trace its one-way route from lymphatic capillaries through collecting vessels, nodes, trunks, and ducts back to the subclavian veins. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: lymph pathway. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, lymph pathway, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w4-lymph-vessels-node","name":"Vessel and node structure","can":"Describe the structure of lymphatic capillaries, valved collecting vessels, and a lymph node including capsule, cortex, medulla, hilum, and afferent and efferent vessels.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A vessel is described as a blind-ended tube in the tissue spaces that is more permeable than a blood capillary. Which vessel is it?\nA) A lymphatic collecting vessel  |  B) A lymphatic trunk\nC) A blood capillary  |  D) A lymphatic capillary\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) A lymphatic capillary","why":"Lymphatic capillaries are the only vessels in this list that begin as dead-end tubes in the tissue spaces. Their loosely overlapping endothelial cells make them more permeable than blood capillaries, so they can take up fluid along with proteins and cells. Every other lymphatic vessel receives lymph from somewhere upstream."},{"t":"mc","q":"After a fatty meal, a vessel in the center of an intestinal villus fills with milky fluid. What is the vessel, and what is the fluid?\nA) A lymph node holding lymph  |  B) A collecting vessel carrying bile\nC) A lacteal carrying chyle  |  D) A blood capillary carrying plasma\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A lacteal carrying chyle","why":"The lymphatic capillary in the center of each villus is a lacteal. Dietary fats are too large to enter the blood capillaries of the villus, so the lacteal absorbs them, and the fat turns the lymph milky. That milky, fat-rich lymph is called chyle."},{"t":"mc","q":"Lymphatic capillaries have overlapping cells that act as one-way flaps. What does this one-way design accomplish?\nA) Only water and small ions are allowed in\nB) Lymph can flow back and forth between nodes\nC) Fluid is actively pumped into the capillary\nD) Tissue fluid can enter but cannot leak back out\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Tissue fluid can enter but cannot leak back out","why":"The overlapping cell edges open inward when fluid builds up in the tissue spaces and press closed when pressure inside the capillary rises. That arrangement lets interstitial fluid, proteins, and even cells enter, while stopping them from flowing back out. Once inside, the fluid is lymph and can only move onward toward the heart."},{"t":"mc","q":"A histology slide shows a fine mesh of reticular fibers packed with small round cells that have large nuclei and no visible granules. What tissue is this?\nA) Lymphatic tissue  |  B) Dense regular connective tissue\nC) Hyaline cartilage  |  D) Adipose tissue\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Lymphatic tissue","why":"The cells described are lymphocytes, agranular white blood cells with a large round nucleus. A reticular fiber mesh packed with lymphocytes is lymphatic tissue, the tissue found in lymph nodes, the spleen, tonsils, and MALT. The reticular fibers provide the framework and the lymphocytes fill the spaces."},{"t":"mc","q":"A slide of a lymph node's outer cortex shows a round cluster of B cells with a pale center where cells are multiplying. What is the pale center called?\nA) A thymic corpuscle  |  B) A medullary sinus\nC) The subcapsular sinus  |  D) A germinal center\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) A germinal center","why":"The pale center of a lymphatic follicle is called the germinal center. It forms after an antigen arrives, when B cells in the follicle begin multiplying rapidly. The dividing cells there become plasma cells and memory B cells, and a follicle with a germinal center is a secondary nodule."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Describe the structure of lymphatic capillaries, valved collecting vessels, and a lymph node including capsule, cortex, medulla, hilum, and afferent and efferent vessels. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to describe the structure of lymphatic capillaries, valved collecting vessels, and a lymph node including capsule, cortex, medulla, hilum, and afferent and efferent vessels."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to describe the structure of lymphatic capillaries, valved collecting vessels, and a lymph node including capsule, cortex, medulla, hilum, and afferent and efferent vessels. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: vessel and node structure. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, vessel and node structure, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w4-lymphatic-organs","name":"Primary and secondary organs","can":"Sort red bone marrow, thymus, lymph nodes, spleen, tonsils, and MALT into primary and secondary lymphoid organs with location and role.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A lymphatic organ in the mediastinum, above the heart and behind the sternum, is large in a child but shrinks after puberty. Which organ is it?\nA) The spleen  |  B) Red bone marrow\nC) The pharyngeal tonsil  |  D) The thymus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The thymus","why":"The thymus sits in the mediastinum, above the heart and behind the sternum. It is large in infants and children, when large numbers of T cells are being produced, and then shrinks after puberty. In older adults it is mostly replaced by fat."},{"t":"mc","q":"A slide of the thymic medulla shows rounded whorls of flattened epithelial cells in concentric layers. What are these whorls?\nA) Billroth's cords  |  B) Hassall's corpuscles\nC) Germinal centers  |  D) Lymphatic follicles\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Hassall's corpuscles","why":"Concentric whorls of epithelial cells in the thymic medulla are Hassall's corpuscles, also called thymic corpuscles. They are found nowhere else in the lymphatic system, so seeing them confirms both that the tissue is thymus and that you are looking at the medulla. The surrounding cells are mature T cells, macrophages, and dendritic cells."},{"t":"mc","q":"A baby is born without a thymus. Which cells would fail to mature normally?\nA) B cells  |  B) Erythrocytes\nC) Platelets  |  D) T cells\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) T cells","why":"The thymus is the primary lymphatic organ where T cells mature. Pre-T cells are still made in the red bone marrow, but without a thymus they have nowhere to finish developing, so the child would have very few functional T cells. B cells, red cells, and platelets all form and mature in the red bone marrow, so they would not depend on the thymus."},{"t":"mc","q":"Unlike a lymph node, the thymus has no afferent lymphatic vessels and no follicles. What does this tell you about the thymus?\nA) It is not a lymphatic organ  |  B) It does not contain any lymphocytes\nC) It does not filter lymph  |  D) It filters blood like the spleen\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It does not filter lymph","why":"Afferent vessels are the vessels that carry lymph into a lymph node so it can be filtered. Because the thymus has none, lymph never flows through it, and the thymus is not a filter. Its job is to mature T cells, which makes it a primary lymphatic organ rather than a filtering organ like a lymph node."},{"t":"mc","q":"A child snores and breathes through the mouth because enlarged lymphatic tissue blocks the back of the nasal passage. Which tonsil is most likely enlarged?\nA) The pharyngeal tonsil  |  B) A tubal tonsil\nC) A palatine tonsil  |  D) A lingual tonsil\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The pharyngeal tonsil","why":"The pharyngeal tonsil, or adenoid, sits on the posterior wall of the nasopharynx, right where air from the nose passes into the throat. When it swells, which is common in children, it blocks airflow through the nose and forces mouth breathing. The other tonsils sit lower or off to the side and do not block the nasal airway this way."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Sort red bone marrow, thymus, lymph nodes, spleen, tonsils, and MALT into primary and secondary lymphoid organs with location and role. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to sort red bone marrow, thymus, lymph nodes, spleen, tonsils, and MALT into primary and secondary lymphoid organs with location and role."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to sort red bone marrow, thymus, lymph nodes, spleen, tonsils, and MALT into primary and secondary lymphoid organs with location and role. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: primary and secondary organs. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, primary and secondary organs, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w4-lymphatic-disorders","name":"Lymphatic disorders","can":"Name edema, lymphedema, lymphadenopathy, lymphoma, splenomegaly, and tonsillitis and identify the structure each one affects.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Months after surgery that removed many lymph nodes from her armpit, a patient has long-lasting swelling of that arm. Which disorder is most likely?\nA) Varicose veins  |  B) Lymphedema\nC) Lymphoma  |  D) Lymphadenitis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Lymphedema","why":"Lymphedema is long-term swelling caused by blocked or missing lymphatic vessels, so fluid builds up in the tissues instead of draining away. Removing many axillary lymph nodes also removes the lymphatic vessels that pass through them. The arm then cannot drain its interstitial fluid well, and it stays swollen."},{"t":"mc","q":"After surgery that removes many lymph nodes from the armpit, a patient develops long-term swelling of that arm. Which disorder is this?\nA) Lymphedema  |  B) Tonsillitis\nC) Splenomegaly  |  D) Lymphadenopathy\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Lymphedema","why":"Lymphedema is long-term swelling caused by blocked or missing lymphatic vessels. When many nodes and the vessels running through them are removed, fluid from that limb can no longer drain well, so interstitial fluid builds up. The swelling lasts because the drainage pathway itself has been lost."},{"t":"mc","q":"A breast tumor sheds cancer cells into nearby lymphatic capillaries. Where are those cells most likely to lodge first?\nA) In the first lymph node the region drains into  |  B) In the thoracic duct\nC) In the right atrium of the heart  |  D) In the spleen\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) In the first lymph node the region drains into","why":"Lymphatic capillaries are permeable enough to take in cells, including cancer cells. Lymph then flows through collecting vessels to the lymph nodes, which filter it before it moves on. The first node a region drains into, called the sentinel node, is where cancer cells usually lodge first, which is why surgeons check it to see whether a cancer has begun to spread."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient takes a hard blow to the left upper abdomen and develops heavy internal bleeding from a torn lymphatic organ. Which organ is it?\nA) The spleen  |  B) An inguinal lymph node\nC) A palatine tonsil  |  D) The thymus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The spleen","why":"The spleen sits in the left upper abdomen behind the stomach, protected by the lower left ribs. Its soft tissue tears easily and it holds a large volume of blood, so a tear causes heavy internal bleeding, called a ruptured spleen. Patients with mononucleosis are told to avoid contact sports because their enlarged spleen ruptures more easily."},{"t":"mc","q":"During a physical exam, a clinician feels tender, enlarged lumps in a patient's neck and armpit. Which structures are enlarged?\nA) The lymph nodes  |  B) The lymphatic capillaries\nC) The thoracic duct  |  D) The lacteals\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The lymph nodes","why":"Swollen glands are enlarged lymph nodes. The cervical and axillary regions hold large clusters of nodes, and when the lymphocytes inside meet an infection they multiply, making the nodes swell and often become tender. That is why clinicians feel the neck, armpits, and groin during an exam."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Name edema, lymphedema, lymphadenopathy, lymphoma, splenomegaly, and tonsillitis and identify the structure each one affects. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to name edema, lymphedema, lymphadenopathy, lymphoma, splenomegaly, and tonsillitis and identify the structure each one affects."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to name edema, lymphedema, lymphadenopathy, lymphoma, splenomegaly, and tonsillitis and identify the structure each one affects. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: lymphatic disorders. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, lymphatic disorders, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]}]},{"week":9,"system":"Blood","comps":[{"id":"w4-blood-composition","name":"Blood composition and plasma","can":"Describe blood as a fluid connective tissue and identify plasma, formed elements, hematocrit, buffy coat, and the plasma proteins albumin, globulins, and fibrinogen.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has severe liver failure, so the liver can no longer make proteins normally. Which group of plasma proteins is least directly affected?\nA) The alpha and beta globulins  |  B) Albumin\nC) Fibrinogen  |  D) The gamma globulins\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The gamma globulins","why":"The liver makes albumin, fibrinogen, and the alpha and beta globulins, so all of these fall when the liver fails. The gamma globulins, the antibodies, are made by plasma cells of the immune system instead. Because their source is outside the liver, they are the group least directly affected."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person moves to a high altitude, where each breath brings in less oxygen, and gradually makes more red blood cells. Which change would show up on a blood test?\nA) A higher hematocrit  |  B) A larger buffy coat\nC) A lower hematocrit  |  D) A lower plasma albumin level\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A higher hematocrit","why":"When tissues are short of oxygen, the kidney releases erythropoietin, which signals the red bone marrow to make more erythrocytes. More red cells means a larger share of the blood volume is erythrocytes, so the hematocrit rises. The extra red cells carry more oxygen, which helps make up for the thinner air."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which statement correctly describes the two major fractions of whole blood?\nA) Plasma about 92 percent, formed elements about 8 percent\nB) Plasma about 55 percent, buffy coat about 45 percent\nC) Plasma about 45 percent, formed elements about 55 percent\nD) Plasma about 55 percent, formed elements about 45 percent\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Plasma about 55 percent, formed elements about 45 percent","why":"Whole blood separates into two major fractions. Plasma, the fluid matrix, is about 55 percent of the volume, and the formed elements, the erythrocytes, leukocytes, and platelets, are about 45 percent. A thin buffy coat of leukocytes and platelets lies between them in a spun sample, but it is part of the formed elements."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which list puts the three major classes of plasma proteins in order from most to least abundant?\nA) Albumin, fibrinogen, globulins  |  B) Globulins, albumin, fibrinogen\nC) Fibrinogen, albumin, globulins  |  D) Albumin, globulins, fibrinogen\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Albumin, globulins, fibrinogen","why":"Albumin makes up about 60 percent of the plasma proteins, the globulins about 35 percent, and fibrinogen about 4 percent. So the order from most to least abundant is albumin, then globulins, then fibrinogen. Fibrinogen is the largest single molecule but the smallest share."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which plasma proteins are not made in the liver, and which cells make them?\nA) Gamma globulins, made by plasma cells  |  B) Fibrinogen, made by platelets\nC) Albumin, made by plasma cells  |  D) Alpha globulins, made by macrophages\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Gamma globulins, made by plasma cells","why":"The gamma globulins are the antibodies, the proteins of immune defense. They are made by plasma cells, which develop from activated B lymphocytes. All the other major plasma proteins, albumin, fibrinogen, and the alpha and beta globulins, come from the liver."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Describe blood as a fluid connective tissue and identify plasma, formed elements, hematocrit, buffy coat, and the plasma proteins albumin, globulins, and fibrinogen. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to describe blood as a fluid connective tissue and identify plasma, formed elements, hematocrit, buffy coat, and the plasma proteins albumin, globulins, and fibrinogen."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to describe blood as a fluid connective tissue and identify plasma, formed elements, hematocrit, buffy coat, and the plasma proteins albumin, globulins, and fibrinogen. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: blood composition and plasma. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, blood composition and plasma, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w4-formed-elements","name":"Formed elements and leukocytes","can":"Identify erythrocytes, platelets, and the five leukocytes on a smear and describe erythrocyte structure and the granulocyte and agranulocyte groups.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Mature red blood cells have no mitochondria. Why is this helpful for a cell whose job is carrying oxygen?\nA) It lets the cell make more hemoglobin as it travels\nB) It lets the cell store oxygen in its membrane\nC) It means the cell does not use up the oxygen it carries\nD) It lets the cell divide faster in the bloodstream\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It means the cell does not use up the oxygen it carries","why":"Mitochondria are the organelles that use oxygen to make most of a cell's energy. A mature red cell has ejected its mitochondria, along with its nucleus, so it makes its small energy supply without oxygen. As a result, nearly all the oxygen it picks up in the lungs is delivered to the tissues instead of being consumed on the way."},{"t":"mc","q":"What advantage does the biconcave disc shape give an erythrocyte?\nA) Extra room inside for organelles\nB) A rigid shape that holds its form in narrow vessels\nC) A smaller surface that slows the loss of gases\nD) More surface area and flexibility\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) More surface area and flexibility","why":"The biconcave shape gives an erythrocyte about 30 percent more surface area than a sphere of the same volume, so gases move in and out more quickly. The thin, flexible disc can also bend and fold to squeeze single file through capillaries narrower than itself. Both features serve the job of carrying oxygen."},{"t":"mc","q":"Because it lacks a nucleus and organelles, a mature erythrocyte cannot do which of the following?\nA) Divide or make new proteins  |  B) Carry oxygen\nC) Hold hemoglobin  |  D) Bend through capillaries\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Divide or make new proteins","why":"Cell division and protein synthesis both depend on a nucleus, which holds the DNA instructions, and on organelles such as ribosomes. A mature erythrocyte has ejected its nucleus and most organelles, so it cannot divide or replace its proteins. That is why red cells wear out after about 120 days and must be replaced by the bone marrow."},{"t":"mc","q":"A blood cell is an anucleate biconcave disc about 7.5 um across, with no organelles and a lifespan of about 120 days. What is it?\nA) A platelet  |  B) An erythrocyte\nC) A neutrophil  |  D) A lymphocyte\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) An erythrocyte","why":"Each clue points to the erythrocyte. It has no nucleus and no organelles, it is a biconcave disc about 7.5 um across, and it lives about 120 days before the spleen and liver remove it. No other formed element has this combination."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why is the nucleus of a basophil often difficult to see on a stained smear?\nA) It has been ejected from the cell  |  B) It is very small and stains pale\nC) Large dark granules cover it  |  D) It is hidden by hemoglobin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Large dark granules cover it","why":"A basophil has a lobed nucleus, but its cytoplasm is packed with large granules that stain dark purple to blue-black. These granules sit over the nucleus and hide much of it. The dark, crowded look of the cell is what identifies it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify erythrocytes, platelets, and the five leukocytes on a smear and describe erythrocyte structure and the granulocyte and agranulocyte groups. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify erythrocytes, platelets, and the five leukocytes on a smear and describe erythrocyte structure and the granulocyte and agranulocyte groups."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify erythrocytes, platelets, and the five leukocytes on a smear and describe erythrocyte structure and the granulocyte and agranulocyte groups. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: formed elements and leukocytes. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, formed elements and leukocytes, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w4-hematopoiesis","name":"Hematopoiesis","can":"Explain where blood cells form and identify the hemocytoblast, megakaryocyte, and the myeloid and lymphoid lines of hematopoiesis.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A bone marrow transplant is meant to restore a patient's ability to make every type of blood cell. Which cell must the donated marrow contain for this to work?\nA) The reticulocyte  |  B) The proerythroblast\nC) The myeloid stem cell  |  D) The hemocytoblast\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The hemocytoblast","why":"Only the hemocytoblast, the pluripotent hematopoietic stem cell, can give rise to every formed element. If donated marrow contains these stem cells, they settle in the patient's marrow and rebuild both the myeloid and lymphoid lines. A committed cell further down the tree could restore only one branch."},{"t":"mc","q":"The myeloid stem cell gives rise to every formed element except which one?\nA) The monocyte  |  B) The platelet\nC) The lymphocyte  |  D) The erythrocyte\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The lymphocyte","why":"The myeloid line produces erythrocytes, platelets, and the leukocytes, with one exception. Lymphocytes come from the separate lymphoid line. So neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils, and monocytes are myeloid, while lymphocytes are lymphoid."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which lineage branches from the myeloid stem cell?\nA) The T lymphocyte lineage  |  B) The megakaryocyte lineage\nC) The natural killer cell lineage  |  D) The B lymphocyte lineage\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The megakaryocyte lineage","why":"The myeloid stem cell gives rise to several lineages: the red cell line, the granulocyte line, the monocyte line, and the megakaryocyte line that produces platelets. The lymphocytes, including B cells, T cells, and natural killer cells, all come from the separate lymphoid line."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which is the first stage of erythropoiesis that has no nucleus?\nA) The erythroblast  |  B) The proerythroblast\nC) The reticulocyte  |  D) The normoblast\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The reticulocyte","why":"The nucleus is ejected at the end of the normoblast stage. The cell that results, the reticulocyte, is the first stage with no nucleus. It still carries ribosome remnants until it matures into an erythrocyte."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which stage of erythropoiesis comes directly between the proerythroblast and the normoblast?\nA) The reticulocyte  |  B) The megakaryoblast\nC) The hemocytoblast  |  D) The erythroblast\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The erythroblast","why":"After the proerythroblast commits to the red cell line, it develops through the erythroblast stages, during which hemoglobin builds up in the cytoplasm. Next comes the normoblast, which ejects the nucleus. So the order is proerythroblast, erythroblast, normoblast."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Explain where blood cells form and identify the hemocytoblast, megakaryocyte, and the myeloid and lymphoid lines of hematopoiesis. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to explain where blood cells form and identify the hemocytoblast, megakaryocyte, and the myeloid and lymphoid lines of hematopoiesis."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to explain where blood cells form and identify the hemocytoblast, megakaryocyte, and the myeloid and lymphoid lines of hematopoiesis. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: hematopoiesis. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, hematopoiesis, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w4-blood-disorders","name":"Blood disorders","can":"Name anemia, sickle cell disease, polycythemia, leukemia, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, and hemophilia and identify the component each one affects.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A child has painful episodes in which small blood vessels in the hands and feet become blocked. A blood smear shows many stiff, crescent-shaped red cells. Which disorder fits best?\nA) Polycythemia  |  B) Iron-deficiency anemia\nC) Beta-thalassemia  |  D) Sickle cell disease\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Sickle cell disease","why":"Crescent-shaped red cells are the signature of sickle cell disease. The abnormal hemoglobin S makes the cells rigid and curved, so they cannot fold through narrow capillaries and instead jam them. Blocked small vessels starve the tissue downstream of oxygen, which causes the painful episodes."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient's hematocrit is well above normal, and the blood is thick and slow-moving. Which disorder fits, and which layer of a spun tube of this blood is larger than normal?\nA) Polycythemia; the buffy coat  |  B) Polycythemia; the erythrocyte layer\nC) Leukemia; the buffy coat  |  D) Anemia; the plasma layer\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Polycythemia; the erythrocyte layer","why":"The hematocrit is the percentage of blood volume made up of erythrocytes, so a high hematocrit means extra red cells. An excess of erythrocytes that thickens the blood and slows its flow is polycythemia. In a spun tube the erythrocytes form the layer below the buffy coat, so that layer is larger."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person with anemia often feels tired and short of breath. Why do these symptoms follow from anemia?\nA) Too few leukocytes leave the body open to infection\nB) Too many red cells thicken the blood and slow flow\nC) Too few platelets allow constant slow bleeding\nD) Fewer red cells carry less oxygen to the tissues\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Fewer red cells carry less oxygen to the tissues","why":"Anemia means too few red cells or too little hemoglobin, so the blood carries less oxygen. Muscles and other tissues then receive less oxygen than they need, which causes fatigue, and the body responds by breathing harder, which feels like shortness of breath. The symptoms follow directly from the red cell's job of carrying oxygen."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which description fits anemia?\nA) The blood clots too slowly because a clotting factor is missing\nB) The blood carries less oxygen because there are too few red cells or too little hemoglobin\nC) The blood has too few platelets, so the risk of bleeding rises\nD) The blood has too many red cells, so it thickens and flows slowly\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The blood carries less oxygen because there are too few red cells or too little hemoglobin","why":"Anemia is a problem of the erythrocytes. Either there are too few red cells, or the red cells hold too little hemoglobin, and in both cases the blood carries less oxygen to the tissues. Because the cause can be a low count or low hemoglobin, anemia is described as a reduced oxygen-carrying capacity rather than simply a low red cell number."},{"t":"mc","q":"In sickle cell disease, which part of the hemoglobin molecule carries the inherited change?\nA) The beta-globin chain  |  B) The heme group\nC) The alpha-globin chain  |  D) The red cell plasma membrane\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The beta-globin chain","why":"Hemoglobin is built from four protein chains, two alpha-globin and two beta-globin, each holding a heme group with one iron. Sickle cell disease is caused by a change in the gene for the beta-globin chain. The altered beta chains make the hemoglobin stick together when oxygen is low, which bends the red cell into a sickle shape."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Name anemia, sickle cell disease, polycythemia, leukemia, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, and hemophilia and identify the component each one affects. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to name anemia, sickle cell disease, polycythemia, leukemia, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, and hemophilia and identify the component each one affects."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to name anemia, sickle cell disease, polycythemia, leukemia, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, and hemophilia and identify the component each one affects. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: blood disorders. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, blood disorders, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]}]},{"week":9,"system":"Muscular","comps":[{"id":"w4-lab-chest-anterior-arm","name":"Chest and anterior arm muscles","can":"Identify on the cadaver or model the pectoralis major, pectoralis minor, serratus anterior, deltoid, biceps brachii, brachialis, and coracobrachialis with their actions.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"When a patient pushes against a wall, the medial border of one scapula lifts away from the ribs like a wing. Which muscle is not doing its job?\nA) Pectoralis major  |  B) Serratus anterior\nC) Latissimus dorsi  |  D) Pectoralis minor\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Serratus anterior","why":"The serratus anterior runs from the outer surfaces of the upper eight or nine ribs to the anterior surface of the medial border of the scapula. That position lets it pull the scapula forward and hold it flat against the chest wall. When it fails, pushing forward makes the medial border lift off the back, a sign called a winged scapula."},{"t":"mc","q":"On a model, a tag sits on deltoid fibers that arise from the lateral clavicle and pass in front of the shoulder joint. What movement do these fibers produce?\nA) Flex and medially rotate the arm  |  B) Extend and laterally rotate the arm\nC) Adduct and extend the arm  |  D) Abduct the arm\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Flex and medially rotate the arm","why":"Fibers arising from the lateral clavicle are the anterior fibers of the deltoid. Because they pass in front of the shoulder joint to the deltoid tuberosity, they swing the arm forward (flexion) and turn it inward (medial rotation). Working out where fibers start and which side of the joint they cross lets you predict the action."},{"t":"mc","q":"A weightlifter ruptures the distal biceps tendon at the radial tuberosity, yet can still bend the elbow fairly well. Which muscle, lying deep to the biceps, is mainly responsible?\nA) Coracobrachialis  |  B) Triceps brachii, medial head\nC) Brachialis  |  D) Anconeus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Brachialis","why":"The brachialis lies directly beneath the biceps and inserts separately on the ulna. When the biceps tendon tears away from the radius, the brachialis is untouched and still flexes the elbow. What the patient loses most is the biceps' contribution, especially strong supination."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which actions does the pectoralis minor produce?\nA) Depresses and protracts the scapula\nB) Depresses and steadies the clavicle\nC) Retracts the scapula and rotates the glenoid down\nD) Flexes, adducts, and medially rotates the arm\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Depresses and protracts the scapula","why":"The pectoralis minor runs from ribs 3 to 5 up to the coracoid process. Pulling the coracoid down and forward, it depresses the scapula and draws it forward around the chest wall, which is protraction. When the scapula is held still, the same pull works in reverse and lifts the ribs, which is why it can assist forced inspiration."},{"t":"mc","q":"After reflecting the pectoralis major on a cadaver, you find a thin triangular muscle running from ribs 3 to 5 up to a hook-like process on the front of the scapula. Which muscle is it?\nA) Serratus anterior  |  B) Subclavius\nC) Pectoralis minor  |  D) Coracobrachialis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Pectoralis minor","why":"The pectoralis minor lies immediately deep to the pectoralis major. It arises from ribs 3 to 5 and its fibers converge upward on the coracoid process, the hook-like projection on the front of the scapula. Its triangular shape and coracoid insertion identify it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify on the cadaver or model the pectoralis major, pectoralis minor, serratus anterior, deltoid, biceps brachii, brachialis, and coracobrachialis with their actions. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify on the cadaver or model the pectoralis major, pectoralis minor, serratus anterior, deltoid, biceps brachii, brachialis, and coracobrachialis with their actions."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify on the cadaver or model the pectoralis major, pectoralis minor, serratus anterior, deltoid, biceps brachii, brachialis, and coracobrachialis with their actions. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: chest and anterior arm muscles. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, chest and anterior arm muscles, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w4-lab-forearm-compartments","name":"Forearm compartments","can":"Identify the anterior flexor and posterior extensor forearm muscles including flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus, pronator teres, and extensor digitorum and state each compartment action.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"After a knife cut across the palm side of a finger, a patient can bend the middle knuckle of that finger but cannot bend the fingertip, even when the other fingers are held straight. Which tendon was most likely cut?\nA) Flexor digitorum superficialis  |  B) Flexor digitorum profundus\nC) Extensor digitorum  |  D) Flexor pollicis longus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Flexor digitorum profundus","why":"Bending the fingertip happens at the distal interphalangeal joint, and the only muscle that reaches the distal phalanx of digits 2 to 5 is flexor digitorum profundus. The patient can still bend the middle knuckle because flexor digitorum superficialis, which inserts on the middle phalanx, is intact. Losing only the fingertip movement points to a cut profundus tendon beyond the point where superficialis attaches."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient can bend the thumb at its knuckle but cannot bend the tip of the thumb. Which muscle's tendon is most likely damaged?\nA) Extensor pollicis longus  |  B) Flexor pollicis longus\nC) Abductor pollicis longus  |  D) Flexor pollicis brevis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Flexor pollicis longus","why":"The tip of the thumb bends at the interphalangeal joint, and the only muscle that inserts on the palmar side of the distal phalanx of the thumb is flexor pollicis longus. The knuckle (metacarpophalangeal joint) can still bend because flexor pollicis brevis, a short thenar muscle in the hand, inserts on the proximal phalanx and crosses that joint. Losing tip flexion alone points to the long flexor."},{"t":"mc","q":"A golfer has pain at the medial epicondyle that gets worse when he flexes his wrist against resistance. Which structure is most likely irritated?\nA) The triceps brachii tendon at the olecranon\nB) The biceps brachii tendon at the radial tuberosity\nC) The common extensor tendon at the lateral epicondyle\nD) The common flexor tendon at the medial epicondyle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The common flexor tendon at the medial epicondyle","why":"Golfer's elbow (medial epicondylitis) is irritation of the shared tendon where the superficial anterior forearm muscles attach to the medial epicondyle. Pronator teres, flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus and flexor carpi ulnaris all pull on that one spot, so resisted wrist flexion loads the sore tendon and reproduces the pain. Knowing the common flexor origin lets you predict which movements will hurt."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person pours water from a pitcher by turning the palm from facing up to facing down while keeping the elbow straight. Which two muscles produce this movement?\nA) Brachialis and brachioradialis\nB) Supinator and biceps brachii\nC) Flexor carpi radialis and flexor carpi ulnaris\nD) Pronator teres and pronator quadratus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Pronator teres and pronator quadratus","why":"Turning the palm from facing up to facing down is pronation, in which the radius rolls across the front of the ulna. Pronator teres and pronator quadratus both pull the radius toward the ulna to make that happen, with pronator quadratus as the prime mover. Keeping the elbow straight rules out the elbow flexors and leaves rotation of the forearm as the only movement."},{"t":"mc","q":"When a patient flexes the wrist, the hand drifts toward the thumb side instead of bending straight forward. Weakness of which muscle best explains this?\nA) Palmaris longus  |  B) Flexor carpi radialis\nC) Flexor carpi ulnaris  |  D) Extensor carpi ulnaris\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Flexor carpi ulnaris","why":"Straight wrist flexion depends on balanced pull from flexor carpi radialis on the thumb side and flexor carpi ulnaris on the little finger side. If flexor carpi ulnaris is weak, the radial flexor's pull is unopposed, so the hand both flexes and abducts toward the thumb. Thinking of each muscle as a pull on one corner of the wrist lets you predict the drift."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the anterior flexor and posterior extensor forearm muscles including flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus, pronator teres, and extensor digitorum and state each compartment action. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the anterior flexor and posterior extensor forearm muscles including flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus, pronator teres, and extensor digitorum and state each compartment action."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the anterior flexor and posterior extensor forearm muscles including flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus, pronator teres, and extensor digitorum and state each compartment action. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: forearm compartments. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, forearm compartments, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w4-lab-posterior-shoulder-cuff","name":"Posterior shoulder and rotator cuff","can":"Identify the trapezius, latissimus dorsi, rhomboids, levator scapulae, and the four rotator cuff muscles supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis with their actions.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A house painter who works overhead every day develops shoulder pain from a cuff tendon pinched between the head of the humerus and the acromion. Which tendon is most likely affected?\nA) The infraspinatus tendon  |  B) The subscapularis tendon\nC) The teres minor tendon  |  D) The supraspinatus tendon\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The supraspinatus tendon","why":"The supraspinatus tendon runs from the supraspinous fossa across the top of the shoulder, passing under the acromion to reach the greater tubercle. The acromion is a bony roof with no give, so repeated overhead arm raising pinches the tendon against it. This is why impingement and rotator cuff tears are concentrated in the supraspinatus."},{"t":"mc","q":"During a chin-up, the arms are pulled down and back from an overhead position to lift the body. Which back muscle is the prime mover of that arm movement?\nA) Trapezius  |  B) Serratus anterior\nC) Latissimus dorsi  |  D) Levator scapulae\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Latissimus dorsi","why":"A chin-up brings the humerus from overhead down toward the side of the body, which is adduction and extension of the arm. The latissimus dorsi is the large back muscle that runs from the lower back to the humerus and produces exactly those movements. Because the hands are fixed on the bar, its pull lifts the trunk up toward the arms instead of moving the arms."},{"t":"mc","q":"To reach high overhead, the scapula must rotate so the glenoid cavity turns upward. Which pair of muscles produces this upward rotation?\nA) Rhomboid major and levator scapulae  |  B) Trapezius and serratus anterior\nC) Latissimus dorsi and teres major  |  D) Pectoralis minor and subclavius\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Trapezius and serratus anterior","why":"Raising the arm fully overhead needs the scapula to swing so the glenoid cavity faces upward. The upper and lower fibers of the trapezius pull the scapula from above and from the spine, while the serratus anterior pulls its inferior angle forward around the chest wall. Working together, they rotate the glenoid upward."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has weak lateral rotation of the arm, and imaging shows wasting of the muscle that fills the fossa below the scapular spine. Which muscle is affected?\nA) Supraspinatus  |  B) Subscapularis\nC) Teres minor  |  D) Infraspinatus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Infraspinatus","why":"Two clues point to the same muscle. The fossa below the scapular spine is the infraspinous fossa, which is filled by the infraspinatus. The infraspinatus is also a lateral rotator of the arm, so its wasting would weaken that movement."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient cannot turn the arm inward against resistance. Imaging shows a tear in the one rotator cuff tendon that crosses in front of the shoulder joint. Which muscle is torn?\nA) Subscapularis  |  B) Supraspinatus\nC) Infraspinatus  |  D) Teres minor\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Subscapularis","why":"Turning the arm inward is medial rotation. The subscapularis is the only cuff muscle on the anterior surface of the scapula, and its tendon crosses the front of the shoulder joint to reach the lesser tubercle. A tear here weakens medial rotation while the other cuff muscles still work."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the trapezius, latissimus dorsi, rhomboids, levator scapulae, and the four rotator cuff muscles supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis with their actions. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the trapezius, latissimus dorsi, rhomboids, levator scapulae, and the four rotator cuff muscles supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis with their actions."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the trapezius, latissimus dorsi, rhomboids, levator scapulae, and the four rotator cuff muscles supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis with their actions. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: posterior shoulder and rotator cuff. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, posterior shoulder and rotator cuff, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]}]},{"week":9,"system":"Regional Vessels & Nerves","comps":[{"id":"w4-lab-ue-arteries-veins","name":"Upper-extremity arteries and veins","can":"Trace and identify the subclavian, axillary, brachial, radial, and ulnar arteries and the cephalic, basilic, and median cubital veins on the upper limb.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"While taking a blood pressure, a nurse places the stethoscope in the cubital fossa just medial to the biceps brachii tendon, below the cuff. Which artery is the nurse listening over?\nA) Axillary artery  |  B) Ulnar artery\nC) Radial artery  |  D) Brachial artery\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Brachial artery","why":"The brachial artery runs down the medial side of the arm and enters the cubital fossa just medial to the biceps tendon, before it divides into the radial and ulnar arteries. That is where the stethoscope is placed, below the cuff that compresses the same artery higher up the arm. Knowing that the brachial artery has not yet divided at this point is what makes it the answer."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person cuts the anterior wrist on the thumb side with broken glass, and bright red blood spurts in rhythm with the heartbeat. Which artery has most likely been cut?\nA) Radial artery  |  B) Median cubital vein\nC) Ulnar artery  |  D) Cephalic vein\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Radial artery","why":"Bright red blood that spurts with each heartbeat comes from an artery, not a vein. On the anterior wrist, the artery on the thumb side is the radial artery, the same vessel used to take the pulse. It lies close to the surface there, which is why it can be felt easily and also cut easily."},{"t":"mc","q":"A fall fractures the surgical neck of the humerus. Which artery, wrapping around the back of that neck with the axillary nerve, is most at risk?\nA) Deep brachial artery  |  B) Subscapular artery\nC) Posterior circumflex humeral artery  |  D) Anterior circumflex humeral artery\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Posterior circumflex humeral artery","why":"The anterior and posterior circumflex humeral arteries are branches of the axillary artery that circle the surgical neck, the narrowing just below the tubercles of the humerus. The posterior circumflex humeral artery travels around the back of the neck together with the axillary nerve. A fracture at this site can injure both the artery and the nerve that run with it."},{"t":"mc","q":"A fracture through the middle of the humeral shaft damages the radial nerve in the radial groove. Which artery, running in the same groove, could be torn as well?\nA) Posterior circumflex humeral artery  |  B) Deep brachial artery\nC) Axillary artery  |  D) Radial artery\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Deep brachial artery","why":"The deep brachial artery branches from the brachial artery and spirals around the back of the humerus in the radial groove, right beside the radial nerve. Both lie pressed against the bone at mid-shaft, so a fracture line there can injure the artery and the nerve together. Structures that share a groove share the same risk."},{"t":"mc","q":"During a blood draw from the median cubital vein, the needle goes too deep. Which structures lie just beneath the vein and are at risk?\nA) The radial artery and the radial nerve\nB) The brachial artery and the median nerve\nC) The deep brachial artery and the axillary nerve\nD) The ulnar artery and the ulnar nerve\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The brachial artery and the median nerve","why":"In the cubital fossa, the brachial artery and the median nerve lie immediately deep to the median cubital vein. The only thing separating them is the bicipital aponeurosis, a flat sheet of tendon from the biceps. That is why a blood draw is taken at this site, and why it is taken carefully."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Trace and identify the subclavian, axillary, brachial, radial, and ulnar arteries and the cephalic, basilic, and median cubital veins on the upper limb. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace and identify the subclavian, axillary, brachial, radial, and ulnar arteries and the cephalic, basilic, and median cubital veins on the upper limb."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to trace and identify the subclavian, axillary, brachial, radial, and ulnar arteries and the cephalic, basilic, and median cubital veins on the upper limb. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: upper-extremity arteries and veins. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, upper-extremity arteries and veins, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w4-lab-ue-nerves","name":"Upper-extremity nerves","can":"Identify the major nerves of the upper limb including the musculocutaneous, median, ulnar, radial, and axillary nerves and the brachial plexus they arise from.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A fracture through the middle of the humeral shaft damages the structures that lie in a groove on its posterior surface. Which structures are at risk?\nA) The ulnar nerve and the basilic vein\nB) The median nerve and the brachial artery\nC) The axillary nerve and the posterior circumflex humeral artery\nD) The radial nerve and the deep brachial artery\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The radial nerve and the deep brachial artery","why":"The groove on the posterior surface of the mid humeral shaft is the radial groove. The radial nerve and the deep brachial artery travel in it, pressed against the bone between the lateral and medial heads of the triceps. A mid-shaft fracture can stretch or cut them because they lie right on the bone at this level."},{"t":"mc","q":"Bumping the funny bone sends a tingling jolt into the little finger. Which nerve is being struck, and where does it run?\nA) The median nerve, through the carpal tunnel\nB) The ulnar nerve, behind the medial epicondyle\nC) The axillary nerve, beside the surgical neck\nD) The radial nerve, in the radial groove\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The ulnar nerve, behind the medial epicondyle","why":"The ulnar nerve passes just behind the medial epicondyle of the humerus, where it lies under the skin with little padding. A knock there compresses the nerve, and because it carries sensation from the little-finger side of the hand, the tingle is felt in the little finger. The funny bone is really this nerve, not a bone."},{"t":"mc","q":"An older adult falls and fractures the surgical neck of the humerus. Afterward, a patch of skin over the deltoid is numb. Which nerve was most likely injured?\nA) Ulnar nerve  |  B) Axillary nerve\nC) Musculocutaneous nerve  |  D) Radial nerve\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Axillary nerve","why":"The axillary nerve leaves the posterior cord and wraps around the back of the surgical neck of the humerus, so a fracture there can stretch or tear it. The nerve carries sensation from the skin over the deltoid, which explains the numb patch. The location of the fracture and the location of the numbness both point to the same nerve."},{"t":"mc","q":"During a difficult birth, a baby's head is pulled sharply away from the shoulder, stretching the C5 and C6 roots. Which part of the brachial plexus is most likely injured?\nA) The lower trunk  |  B) The medial cord\nC) The upper trunk  |  D) The middle trunk\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The upper trunk","why":"Pulling the head away from the shoulder widens the angle between the neck and shoulder and stretches the highest part of the plexus. The C5 and C6 roots join to form the upper trunk, so the upper trunk takes the injury. The affected baby has weakness around the shoulder and upper arm, the regions served by those roots."},{"t":"mc","q":"A climber slips and grabs a ledge overhead, and the arm is yanked sharply upward, stretching the C8 and T1 roots. Which part of the brachial plexus is most likely injured?\nA) The middle trunk  |  B) The upper trunk\nC) The lower trunk  |  D) The lateral cord\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The lower trunk","why":"When the arm is pulled forcefully upward, the lowest part of the plexus is stretched. The C8 and T1 roots join to form the lower trunk, so the lower trunk is injured. The deficit falls mainly on the hand and the medial side of the forearm, which these roots serve."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the major nerves of the upper limb including the musculocutaneous, median, ulnar, radial, and axillary nerves and the brachial plexus they arise from. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the major nerves of the upper limb including the musculocutaneous, median, ulnar, radial, and axillary nerves and the brachial plexus they arise from."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the major nerves of the upper limb including the musculocutaneous, median, ulnar, radial, and axillary nerves and the brachial plexus they arise from. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: upper-extremity nerves. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, upper-extremity nerves, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]}]},{"week":12,"system":"Digestive","comps":[{"id":"w5-canal-wall","name":"Four-layer gut wall","can":"Name the four layers of the alimentary canal wall from lumen outward and state the dominant tissue of each.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The mucosa is the innermost tunic of the alimentary canal wall. What tissue forms the part of the mucosa that directly touches food in the lumen?\nA) A serous membrane  |  B) Areolar connective tissue\nC) An epithelium  |  D) A thin layer of smooth muscle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) An epithelium","why":"The canal wall has four tunics (layers), running mucosa, submucosa, muscularis, and serosa from the lumen outward. The mucosa itself has three parts: an epithelium facing the lumen, the lamina propria of areolar connective tissue beneath it, and a thin muscularis mucosae at its outer edge. Only the epithelium is in direct contact with food, so it is the tissue that lines the lumen."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which part of the mucosa is a layer of areolar connective tissue lying just beneath the epithelium?\nA) The lamina propria  |  B) The submucosa\nC) The muscularis mucosae  |  D) The serosa\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The lamina propria","why":"The mucosa has three parts from the lumen outward: the epithelium that touches food, the lamina propria beneath it, and the thin muscularis mucosae. The lamina propria is areolar connective tissue, a loose tissue that supports the epithelium and carries small vessels close to the surface. Because it belongs to the mucosa itself, it is separate from the submucosa, the next tunic out."},{"t":"mc","q":"On most of the esophagus, the outermost layer of the wall is a fibrous adventitia. Which tunic does the adventitia replace?\nA) The serosa  |  B) The mucosa\nC) The submucosa  |  D) The muscularis externa\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The serosa","why":"In the abdomen, the outermost tunic of the canal is the serosa, a serous membrane that is the visceral peritoneum. The esophagus runs mostly through the thorax, outside the peritoneum, so it has no serous covering there. Instead a fibrous adventitia, a layer of connective tissue, forms its outer layer and blends with the surrounding tissues."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which tunic of the canal wall contains the muscularis mucosae, a thin layer of smooth muscle?\nA) The mucosa  |  B) The submucosa\nC) The serosa  |  D) The muscularis externa\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The mucosa","why":"The muscularis mucosae is part of the mucosa, as its name says. The mucosa has three parts from the lumen outward: the epithelium, the lamina propria of areolar connective tissue, and the thin muscularis mucosae. It is easy to confuse with the muscularis externa, but that is a separate, much thicker tunic farther out in the wall."},{"t":"mc","q":"A tunic of the canal wall is areolar connective tissue carrying blood and lymphatic vessels and a nerve plexus, lying just outside the mucosa. Which tunic is it?\nA) The submucosa  |  B) The serosa\nC) The mucosa  |  D) The muscularis externa\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The submucosa","why":"The submucosa is the second tunic from the lumen, sitting just outside the mucosa. It is areolar connective tissue that carries the larger blood and lymphatic vessels of the wall and the submucosal plexus, the nerve network that controls secretion. Its vessels supply the mucosa just inside it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Name the four layers of the alimentary canal wall from lumen outward and state the dominant tissue of each. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to name the four layers of the alimentary canal wall from lumen outward and state the dominant tissue of each."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to name the four layers of the alimentary canal wall from lumen outward and state the dominant tissue of each. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: four-layer gut wall. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, four-layer gut wall, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w5-enteric-plexuses","name":"Enteric nerve plexuses","can":"Locate the submucosal and myenteric plexuses in the gut wall and match each to the wall layer it sits within.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A biopsy of the colon removes the mucosa and the submucosa but none of the muscularis. Which enteric plexus could appear in the sample?\nA) Neither plexus, since both lie in the serosa  |  B) Only the myenteric plexus\nC) Only the submucosal plexus  |  D) Both the submucosal and myenteric plexuses\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Only the submucosal plexus.","why":"Each enteric plexus sits in a different layer of the wall. The submucosal (Meissner) plexus lies in the submucosa, just deep to the mucosa. The myenteric (Auerbach) plexus lies inside the muscularis, between the inner circular and outer longitudinal layers. A sample that ends at the submucosa holds the submucosal plexus but not the myenteric plexus."},{"t":"mc","q":"A student forgets which layer holds the myenteric plexus. How does the name itself answer the question?\nA) Myo means mucus, so it sits in the mucosa\nB) Myo means muscle, so it sits in the muscularis\nC) Myo means outer, so it sits in the serosa\nD) Myo means under, so it sits in the submucosa\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Myo means muscle, so it sits in the muscularis","why":"Myenteric comes from myo, which means muscle. So the myenteric plexus sits in the muscularis, between the inner circular and outer longitudinal layers. The submucosal plexus is named the same way, since sub-mucosal sits in the submucosa."},{"t":"mc","q":"On a slide of the gut wall, you find a network of neurons in the connective tissue layer that lies just deep to the mucosa. Which structure is it?\nA) Myenteric (Auerbach) plexus  |  B) Submucosal (Meissner) plexus\nC) Celiac plexus  |  D) Vagus nerve\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Submucosal (Meissner) plexus","why":"The submucosa is the connective tissue layer just deep to the mucosa, and the submucosal (Meissner) plexus runs inside it. It controls the glands of the mucosa and the blood flow to it. The other enteric plexus, the myenteric plexus, sits deeper, between the two muscle layers of the muscularis."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which structure lies between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers of the gut wall and controls the movement of the gut?\nA) The muscularis mucosae  |  B) The submucosal plexus\nC) The lamina propria  |  D) The myenteric plexus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The myenteric plexus","why":"The myenteric plexus is one of the two nerve networks of the enteric nervous system, the nerve supply of the gut. It lies within the muscularis, between the inner circular and outer longitudinal layers of smooth muscle, which is exactly where it can control those muscles. It therefore controls the movement of the gut, such as the waves that push food along."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which statement correctly describes what the submucosal and myenteric plexuses each control?\nA) Both control movement, from inside the muscularis\nB) Submucosal controls movement; myenteric controls secretions\nC) Submucosal controls secretions; myenteric controls movement\nD) Both control secretions, from inside the mucosa\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Submucosal controls secretions; myenteric controls movement","why":"The enteric nervous system is the gut's own network of nerves, built from two plexuses in the wall. The submucosal plexus lies in the submucosa, close to the glands of the mucosa, and controls secretion. The myenteric plexus lies between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers of the muscularis and controls movement. Each plexus sits next to the tissue it controls."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Locate the submucosal and myenteric plexuses in the gut wall and match each to the wall layer it sits within. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to locate the submucosal and myenteric plexuses in the gut wall and match each to the wall layer it sits within."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to locate the submucosal and myenteric plexuses in the gut wall and match each to the wall layer it sits within. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: enteric nerve plexuses. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, enteric nerve plexuses, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w5-peritoneum-folds","name":"Peritoneum and folds","can":"Identify the parietal and visceral peritoneum, the peritoneal cavity, and the five folds greater omentum, lesser omentum, falciform ligament, mesentery, and mesocolon and state what each fold connects.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"In the pelvis you lift an S-shaped loop of large intestine and follow the fold that holds it back to the posterior abdominal wall. Which fold are you following?\nA) The sigmoid mesocolon  |  B) The transverse mesocolon\nC) The mesentery  |  D) The greater omentum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The sigmoid mesocolon.","why":"First identify the organ: the S-shaped part of the large intestine in the pelvis is the sigmoid colon. Then find its fold: the sigmoid colon hangs on the sigmoid mesocolon, which attaches it to the posterior abdominal wall and carries its vessels and nerves. That fold is why this part of the colon can be lifted."},{"t":"mc","q":"In the course notes, what is the fan-shaped fold of peritoneum that binds the jejunum and ileum to the posterior abdominal wall?\nA) The mesentery  |  B) The parietal peritoneum\nC) The lesser omentum  |  D) The greater omentum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The mesentery","why":"The mesentery is a fan-shaped double fold of peritoneum that binds the jejunum and ileum to the posterior abdominal wall. Its two layers carry blood vessels, lymphatic vessels, and nerves to the small intestine. It holds the long small intestine in place while still letting it move."},{"t":"mc","q":"The greater omentum hangs from which part of the stomach?\nA) The pylorus  |  B) The fundus\nC) The greater curvature  |  D) The lesser curvature\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The greater curvature","why":"The greater omentum is the largest fold of peritoneum, a fatty apron that hangs from the greater curvature of the stomach and drapes down over the intestines. The greater curvature is the long, convex left border of the stomach. Its partner, the lesser omentum, runs from the short lesser curvature up to the liver."},{"t":"mc","q":"In the course notes, which fold of peritoneum binds the transverse and sigmoid colon to the posterior abdominal wall?\nA) The mesocolon  |  B) The falciform ligament\nC) The greater omentum  |  D) The lesser omentum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The mesocolon","why":"The mesocolon is the fold of peritoneum that binds the transverse colon and the sigmoid colon to the posterior abdominal wall. Because these two parts of the colon are held by a fold rather than pressed against the wall, they are intraperitoneal and fairly mobile. The ascending and descending colon have no mesocolon and are retroperitoneal."},{"t":"mc","q":"A mesentery binds an organ to the posterior abdominal wall and carries its vessels. Which organs does the fan-shaped mesentery proper bind?\nA) Transverse and sigmoid colon  |  B) Jejunum and ileum\nC) Duodenum and pancreas  |  D) Liver and gallbladder\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Jejunum and ileum","why":"The mesentery proper is a fan-shaped fold of peritoneum. Its short root attaches to the posterior abdominal wall, and its long, ruffled outer edge holds the many coils of the jejunum and ileum. Blood vessels, lymphatic vessels and nerves run between its two layers to reach the intestine, which lets the small intestine move freely while staying supplied."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the parietal and visceral peritoneum, the peritoneal cavity, and the five folds greater omentum, lesser omentum, falciform ligament, mesentery, and mesocolon and state what each fold connects. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the parietal and visceral peritoneum, the peritoneal cavity, and the five folds greater omentum, lesser omentum, falciform ligament, mesentery, and mesocolon and state what each fold connects."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the parietal and visceral peritoneum, the peritoneal cavity, and the five folds greater omentum, lesser omentum, falciform ligament, mesentery, and mesocolon and state what each fold connects. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: peritoneum and folds. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, peritoneum and folds, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w5-retroperitoneal","name":"Retroperitoneal organs","can":"Distinguish retroperitoneal organs from intraperitoneal ones and name the kidneys, pancreas, duodenum, and parts of the colon as retroperitoneal.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A surgeon reaches the pancreas from behind without opening the peritoneal cavity. Which fact about the pancreas makes this possible?\nA) It is held to the liver by the lesser omentum\nB) It hangs on the mesentery proper\nC) It is retroperitoneal, behind the peritoneum\nD) It is wrapped inside the greater omentum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It is retroperitoneal, behind the peritoneum","why":"The pancreas lies posterior to the stomach and is retroperitoneal, meaning it sits against the posterior abdominal wall behind the parietal peritoneum. Because it is outside the peritoneal cavity, a surgeon can approach it from the back without passing through the peritoneum. Organs that hang inside the cavity could not be reached this way."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which organ is retroperitoneal, lying behind the peritoneum rather than within the peritoneal cavity?\nA) The pancreas  |  B) The transverse colon\nC) The stomach  |  D) The jejunum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The pancreas","why":"Retroperitoneal organs sit behind the parietal peritoneum, pressed against the posterior abdominal wall, rather than hanging inside the peritoneal cavity on folds. The pancreas is one of them, along with the kidneys, the duodenum, and parts of the colon (the ascending and descending colon). Organs that hang on folds, such as the stomach on the omenta or the jejunum on the mesentery, are inside the cavity instead."},{"t":"mc","q":"The pancreas lies behind the parietal peritoneum instead of hanging inside the peritoneal cavity. Which term describes this position?\nA) Retroperitoneal  |  B) Intraperitoneal\nC) Mediastinal  |  D) Subcutaneous\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Retroperitoneal","why":"Retro means behind, so a retroperitoneal organ sits behind the peritoneum, pressed against the posterior abdominal wall, rather than being wrapped in visceral peritoneum and suspended in the cavity. The pancreas is retroperitoneal, along with the kidneys, most of the duodenum, and parts of the colon. Its head sits in the curve of the duodenum and its body and tail stretch toward the spleen."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does it mean when an organ is described as retroperitoneal?\nA) It hangs inside the peritoneal cavity on a mesentery\nB) It lines the inner surface of the abdominal wall\nC) It lies behind the peritoneum, outside its cavity\nD) It hangs in front of the intestines like an apron\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It lies behind the peritoneum, outside its cavity","why":"The prefix retro means behind. A retroperitoneal organ sits against the posterior abdominal wall, behind the parietal peritoneum, so the peritoneum covers only its front surface and the organ is not suspended in the peritoneal cavity. The kidneys, pancreas, duodenum and parts of the colon are retroperitoneal."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which group of abdominal organs is retroperitoneal?\nA) Gallbladder, stomach, and ileum  |  B) Liver, ileum, and transverse colon\nC) Pancreas, duodenum, and kidneys  |  D) Stomach, jejunum, and spleen\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Pancreas, duodenum, and kidneys","why":"Retroperitoneal organs lie behind the peritoneum against the posterior abdominal wall. In this course the named retroperitoneal organs are the kidneys, the pancreas, the duodenum and parts of the colon. Each organ in the correct group sits flat against the back wall rather than hanging in the peritoneal cavity."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Distinguish retroperitoneal organs from intraperitoneal ones and name the kidneys, pancreas, duodenum, and parts of the colon as retroperitoneal. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish retroperitoneal organs from intraperitoneal ones and name the kidneys, pancreas, duodenum, and parts of the colon as retroperitoneal."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to distinguish retroperitoneal organs from intraperitoneal ones and name the kidneys, pancreas, duodenum, and parts of the colon as retroperitoneal. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: retroperitoneal organs. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, retroperitoneal organs, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w5-mouth-pharynx-esophagus","name":"Mouth, pharynx, esophagus","can":"Identify the oral cavity boundaries, hard and soft palate, uvula, fauces, tonsils, the three pharyngeal regions, and the esophagus with its hiatus.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient with a sore throat opens wide, and swollen, red masses of lymphatic tissue are visible on the side walls of the throat between the palatal arches. In which region of the pharynx are they located?\nA) Nasopharynx  |  B) Oropharynx\nC) Oral cavity proper  |  D) Laryngopharynx\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Oropharynx","why":"The palatine tonsils sit in the side walls of the throat between the two palatal arches, just behind the fauces. That places them in the oropharynx, the part of the pharynx directly behind the mouth. Because the oropharynx can be seen through an open mouth, swollen palatine tonsils are easy to spot in tonsillitis."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which two regions of the pharynx carry swallowed food toward the esophagus?\nA) The laryngopharynx and larynx  |  B) The nasopharynx and oropharynx\nC) The oropharynx and laryngopharynx  |  D) The nasopharynx and laryngopharynx\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The oropharynx and laryngopharynx","why":"The pharynx is a funnel-shaped passage with three regions stacked top to bottom: the nasopharynx, oropharynx, and laryngopharynx. Food passes from the mouth through the fauces into the oropharynx, then down into the laryngopharynx, which leads into the esophagus. The nasopharynx, behind the nasal cavity, carries only air and is closed off by the soft palate and uvula during swallowing."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which pairing correctly matches a wall of the oral cavity with the structure that forms it?\nA) Lateral walls: the cheeks  |  B) Roof: the tongue\nC) Anterior wall: the uvula of the soft palate  |  D) Floor: the hard palate\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Lateral walls: the cheeks","why":"The walls of the oral cavity are the lips in front, the cheeks on each side, the hard and soft palate above, and the mylohyoid muscle below, with the tongue resting on it. Only the pairing of the cheeks with the lateral walls matches this layout. The other choices put real mouth structures in the wrong position."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which sequence lists the regions of the pharynx from superior to inferior?\nA) Nasopharynx, oropharynx, laryngopharynx\nB) Nasopharynx, laryngopharynx, oropharynx\nC) Laryngopharynx, oropharynx, nasopharynx\nD) Oropharynx, nasopharynx, laryngopharynx\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Nasopharynx, oropharynx, laryngopharynx","why":"The pharynx is a funnel-shaped tube behind the nasal cavity, mouth, and larynx. Its regions are named for what lies in front of them. The nasopharynx is highest, behind the nasal cavity; the oropharynx is in the middle, behind the mouth; and the laryngopharynx is lowest, behind the larynx, where it leads into the esophagus."},{"t":"mc","q":"At its lower end, the laryngopharynx opens into which two tubes?\nA) Esophagus and larynx  |  B) Oral cavity and esophagus\nC) Larynx and nasal cavity  |  D) Trachea and nasopharynx\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Esophagus and larynx","why":"The laryngopharynx is where the paths for food and air separate. Behind, it continues downward as the esophagus, which carries food to the stomach. In front, it opens into the larynx, which leads to the trachea and lungs. During swallowing the larynx is covered so food goes into the esophagus."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the oral cavity boundaries, hard and soft palate, uvula, fauces, tonsils, the three pharyngeal regions, and the esophagus with its hiatus. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the oral cavity boundaries, hard and soft palate, uvula, fauces, tonsils, the three pharyngeal regions, and the esophagus with its hiatus."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the oral cavity boundaries, hard and soft palate, uvula, fauces, tonsils, the three pharyngeal regions, and the esophagus with its hiatus. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: mouth, pharynx, esophagus. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, mouth, pharynx, esophagus, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w5-canal-sphincters","name":"Major canal sphincters","can":"Locate the upper and lower esophageal, pyloric, ileocecal, and internal and external anal sphincters and state which regions each one separates.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A swallowed camera pill travels from the pylorus to the anus. Which is the first sphincter made of skeletal muscle that it passes?\nA) The external anal sphincter  |  B) The ileocecal sphincter\nC) The pyloric sphincter  |  D) The internal anal sphincter\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The external anal sphincter.","why":"Only the two ends of the canal have skeletal muscle sphincters: the upper esophageal sphincter at the top and the external anal sphincter at the bottom. Starting at the pylorus, the pill passes the pyloric sphincter, the ileocecal sphincter, and the internal anal sphincter, all smooth muscle. The external anal sphincter, outside the internal one, is the first skeletal muscle sphincter it meets, which is why it is the one under voluntary control."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which ring of muscle at the bottom of the esophagus closes to keep stomach contents from rising into the esophagus?\nA) The pyloric sphincter  |  B) The upper esophageal sphincter\nC) The lower esophageal sphincter  |  D) The ileocecal sphincter\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The lower esophageal sphincter","why":"The lower esophageal sphincter, also called the cardiac sphincter, is the ring of muscle at the bottom of the esophagus where it meets the stomach. It opens to let a bolus into the stomach and closes to keep acidic stomach contents out of the esophagus. When it is weak, reflux occurs, as in GERD."},{"t":"mc","q":"What opening does the lower esophageal (cardiac) sphincter guard?\nA) The opening from the esophagus into the stomach\nB) The opening from the pharynx into the esophagus\nC) The opening from the ileum into the cecum\nD) The opening from the stomach into the duodenum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The opening from the esophagus into the stomach","why":"The lower esophageal sphincter is a ring of muscle at the bottom of the esophagus. It guards the opening into the stomach, relaxing to let food pass and closing to keep stomach contents from flowing back up. It is also called the cardiac sphincter because it opens into the cardia, the region of the stomach around the esophageal opening."},{"t":"mc","q":"Where is the upper esophageal sphincter?\nA) Where the esophagus joins the cardia of the stomach\nB) Where the laryngopharynx meets the esophagus\nC) Where the mouth meets the oropharynx\nD) Where the pylorus of the stomach meets the duodenum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Where the laryngopharynx meets the esophagus","why":"The esophagus is guarded by a ring of muscle at each end. The upper esophageal sphincter is at the top, where the laryngopharynx joins the esophagus, and it stays closed between swallows so air does not enter the esophagus. The lower esophageal sphincter is at the bottom, where the esophagus joins the stomach."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which sphincter is also called the cardiac sphincter?\nA) The upper esophageal sphincter  |  B) The lower esophageal sphincter\nC) The ileocecal sphincter  |  D) The pyloric sphincter\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The lower esophageal sphincter","why":"The lower esophageal sphincter guards the bottom of the esophagus, where it opens into the cardia of the stomach. Because it sits at the cardia, it is also called the cardiac sphincter. The name has nothing to do with the heart's own muscle, even though the cardia lies just below the heart."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Locate the upper and lower esophageal, pyloric, ileocecal, and internal and external anal sphincters and state which regions each one separates. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to locate the upper and lower esophageal, pyloric, ileocecal, and internal and external anal sphincters and state which regions each one separates."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to locate the upper and lower esophageal, pyloric, ileocecal, and internal and external anal sphincters and state which regions each one separates. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: major canal sphincters. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, major canal sphincters, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w5-stomach","name":"Stomach regions and features","can":"Identify the cardia, fundus, body, pyloric part, curvatures, rugae, and the three-layered muscularis of the stomach.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The stomach must churn food into a liquid, not just push it along. Which structural feature best supports this churning?\nA) Villi that cover its mucosa\nB) Gastric pits that lead to gastric glands\nC) An inner oblique layer in its muscularis\nD) Rugae that flatten as it fills\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) An inner oblique layer in its muscularis","why":"Two layers of muscle can squeeze a tube narrower and shorter, which pushes contents forward. The stomach's third, inner oblique layer runs at an angle to the other two, so contracting all three together twists and kneads the contents. This churning mixes food with gastric juice until it becomes a liquid."},{"t":"mc","q":"The muscularis of the stomach has one more layer of smooth muscle than the rest of the canal. Which layer is the extra one?\nA) The outer longitudinal layer  |  B) The middle circular layer\nC) The muscularis mucosae  |  D) The inner oblique layer\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The inner oblique layer","why":"Most of the canal has a muscularis of two smooth muscle layers, an inner circular layer and an outer longitudinal layer. The stomach adds a third, an inner oblique layer whose fibers run at an angle, so its muscularis runs oblique, circular, then longitudinal from the inside out. The extra layer lets the stomach churn and mix its contents, not just squeeze them forward."},{"t":"mc","q":"The pyloric part of the stomach has three subdivisions. In what order does food meet them as it moves toward the duodenum?\nA) Pyloric antrum, pyloric canal, pylorus\nB) Pylorus, pyloric canal, pyloric antrum\nC) Pyloric canal, pyloric antrum, pylorus\nD) Pyloric antrum, pylorus, pyloric canal\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Pyloric antrum, pyloric canal, pylorus","why":"The pyloric part funnels food from the body of the stomach toward the small intestine. It begins with the wider pyloric antrum next to the body, narrows into the pyloric canal, and ends at the pylorus. The pylorus is guarded by the pyloric sphincter, which opens into the duodenum."},{"t":"mc","q":"A region of the stomach narrows like a funnel and connects the body of the stomach to the duodenum. Which region is it?\nA) The fundus  |  B) The pyloric part\nC) The cardia  |  D) The body\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The pyloric part","why":"The pyloric part is the exit region of the stomach. It narrows from the wide pyloric antrum through the pyloric canal to the pylorus, which is guarded by the pyloric sphincter where the stomach meets the duodenum. Its funnel shape guides the liquefied stomach contents toward the small intestine."},{"t":"mc","q":"How does the muscularis of the stomach differ from the muscularis of the rest of the canal?\nA) It is made of skeletal rather than smooth muscle\nB) It has only a single circular layer of smooth muscle\nC) It adds a third, inner oblique muscle layer\nD) It has a fibrous adventitia in place of a serosa\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It adds a third, inner oblique muscle layer","why":"Most of the canal has two layers of smooth muscle in its muscularis, an inner circular and an outer longitudinal layer. The stomach has three: an inner oblique layer, a middle circular layer, and an outer longitudinal layer. The extra oblique layer lets the stomach twist and churn its contents instead of only squeezing them forward."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the cardia, fundus, body, pyloric part, curvatures, rugae, and the three-layered muscularis of the stomach. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the cardia, fundus, body, pyloric part, curvatures, rugae, and the three-layered muscularis of the stomach."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the cardia, fundus, body, pyloric part, curvatures, rugae, and the three-layered muscularis of the stomach. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: stomach regions and features. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, stomach regions and features, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w5-small-intestine","name":"Small intestine and surface area","can":"Identify the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum and name the circular folds, villi, lacteal, and microvilli as features that expand absorptive surface area.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The lining of the small intestine is folded at three scales to expand its surface for absorption. Which sequence lists them from largest to smallest?\nA) Circular folds, villi, microvilli  |  B) Rugae, villi, microvilli\nC) Microvilli, villi, circular folds  |  D) Villi, circular folds, microvilli\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Circular folds, villi, microvilli","why":"The small intestine amplifies its surface three times over. Circular folds are ridges of the whole wall lining (mucosa and submucosa), villi are finger-like projections of the mucosa that cover those folds, and microvilli are microscopic projections on each absorptive cell that cover the villi. Each level sits on top of the one before it, which multiplies the area available for absorption."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which region of the small intestine begins at the pyloric sphincter and is mostly retroperitoneal?\nA) The jejunum  |  B) The stomach\nC) The duodenum  |  D) The ileum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The duodenum","why":"The small intestine runs duodenum, jejunum, then ileum. The duodenum is the first and shortest region, about 25 cm (10 inches), and it curves in a C shape from the pyloric sphincter around the head of the pancreas. It lies mostly behind the peritoneum, pressed against the posterior abdominal wall, which is what retroperitoneal means. The jejunum and ileum, by contrast, hang within the peritoneal cavity on the mesentery."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which surface feature of the small intestine is a permanent circular ridge of the mucosa and submucosa?\nA) Rugae  |  B) Microvilli\nC) Villi  |  D) Circular folds\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Circular folds","why":"The circular folds, or plicae circulares, are the largest of the small intestine's three surface features. They are permanent ridges that involve both the mucosa and the submucosa, so they do not flatten out when the intestine fills. Villi project from the folds, and microvilli cover the cells of each villus, so the surface is increased three times over."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which sequence names the three surface features that expand the absorptive surface of the small intestine, from largest to smallest?\nA) Microvilli, villi, circular folds  |  B) Circular folds, villi, microvilli\nC) Circular folds, rugae, microvilli  |  D) Villi, circular folds, microvilli\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Circular folds, villi, microvilli","why":"The small intestine increases its surface three times over, each feature smaller than the last. The circular folds (plicae circulares) are permanent ridges of the mucosa and submucosa. Villi are finger-like projections of the mucosa that stand on those folds. Microvilli are microscopic projections of the single absorptive cells covering each villus, forming the brush border."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is a lacteal?\nA) A nerve network in the submucosa that controls secretion\nB) A blood capillary in a villus that carries off sugars\nC) A pouch of the colon wall formed by the teniae coli\nD) A lymphatic capillary in a villus that absorbs fats\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) A lymphatic capillary in a villus that absorbs fats","why":"Each villus of the small intestine has a core of lamina propria that holds a network of blood capillaries and one central lymphatic capillary, the lacteal. Digested fats are packaged into particles too large to enter the blood capillaries, so they enter the lacteal instead and travel in the lymph. The milky look of this fat-rich lymph gives the lacteal its name."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum and name the circular folds, villi, lacteal, and microvilli as features that expand absorptive surface area. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum and name the circular folds, villi, lacteal, and microvilli as features that expand absorptive surface area."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum and name the circular folds, villi, lacteal, and microvilli as features that expand absorptive surface area. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: small intestine and surface area. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, small intestine and surface area, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w5-large-intestine","name":"Large intestine features","can":"Identify the cecum, appendix, four parts of the colon, rectum, and anal canal along with the teniae coli, haustra, and omental appendices.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which job belongs to the large intestine rather than the small intestine?\nA) Absorbing leftover water and forming the feces\nB) Churning food with acid into a liquid\nC) Taking up dietary fats into lacteals\nD) Absorbing most nutrients across the villi and microvilli\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Absorbing leftover water and forming the feces","why":"By the time material reaches the large intestine, most nutrients have already been absorbed in the small intestine. The large intestine absorbs much of the water that is left and compacts the remaining material into feces, which it stores until elimination. It has no villi, which fits a job that is about water and waste rather than nutrient absorption."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which three surface features are found on the large intestine and on no other part of the canal?\nA) Teniae coli, rugae, and circular folds\nB) Teniae coli, haustra, and omental appendices\nC) Rugae, haustra, and teniae coli\nD) Circular folds, villi, and microvilli\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Teniae coli, haustra, and omental appendices","why":"Three features identify the large intestine. The teniae coli are three flat bands of longitudinal smooth muscle running along the colon. They are shorter than the wall, so the wall bunches up between them into pouches called haustra. Small fat-filled pouches of visceral peritoneum, the omental appendices, hang from the surface of the colon, mostly along the teniae coli."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which region of the large intestine is the straight portion lying anterior to the sacrum and coccyx?\nA) The sigmoid colon  |  B) The rectum\nC) The anal canal  |  D) The cecum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The rectum","why":"The rectum is the straight portion of the large intestine, running down in the pelvis in front of the sacrum and coccyx. Its name comes from the Latin word for straight. It follows the sigmoid colon and stores feces before they pass into the anal canal."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which feature of the colon is a series of pouches formed by the pull of the teniae coli?\nA) Rugae  |  B) Circular folds\nC) Haustra  |  D) Villi\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Haustra","why":"Haustra are the pouches that give the colon its puckered appearance. The three teniae coli, bands of longitudinal muscle, are shorter than the colon wall and gather it into a row of sacs. Haustra, teniae coli, and omental appendices together identify the large intestine."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which bend of the colon lies near the liver, where the ascending colon turns to become the transverse colon?\nA) The sigmoid colon  |  B) The splenic flexure\nC) The hepatic flexure  |  D) The ileocecal valve\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The hepatic flexure","why":"As the ascending colon rises on the right side of the abdomen, it reaches the underside of the liver and turns to run across the abdomen as the transverse colon. This bend is the hepatic flexure, also called the right colic flexure, named for the liver (hepatic). The matching bend on the left, near the spleen, is the splenic flexure."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the cecum, appendix, four parts of the colon, rectum, and anal canal along with the teniae coli, haustra, and omental appendices. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the cecum, appendix, four parts of the colon, rectum, and anal canal along with the teniae coli, haustra, and omental appendices."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the cecum, appendix, four parts of the colon, rectum, and anal canal along with the teniae coli, haustra, and omental appendices. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: large intestine features. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, large intestine features, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w5-food-pathway","name":"Trace the pathway of food","can":"Trace a bite of food through every named segment of the alimentary canal in order from mouth to anus.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which sequence correctly orders these organs as food travels through the canal: esophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine?\nA) Esophagus, small intestine, stomach, large intestine\nB) Stomach, esophagus, small intestine, large intestine\nC) Esophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine\nD) Esophagus, stomach, large intestine, small intestine\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Esophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine","why":"Food moves through the canal in one direction. The esophagus carries it to the stomach, where it is churned into a liquid. It then passes into the small intestine, where most absorption happens, and finally into the large intestine, where water is absorbed and feces form."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which sequence correctly orders the regions of the small intestine as food moves through it, followed by the structure the ileum empties into?\nA) Duodenum, jejunum, ileum, then the rectum\nB) Duodenum, jejunum, ileum, then the cecum\nC) Jejunum, duodenum, ileum, then the cecum\nD) Duodenum, ileum, jejunum, then the cecum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Duodenum, jejunum, ileum, then the cecum","why":"The small intestine runs duodenum, jejunum, then ileum. The ileum empties through the ileocecal valve into the cecum, the pouch at the start of the large intestine. From the cecum, material moves through the colon and only later reaches the rectum."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which sequence correctly orders the four parts of the colon as material travels through it?\nA) Ascending, transverse, sigmoid, descending\nB) Ascending, transverse, descending, sigmoid\nC) Transverse, ascending, descending, sigmoid\nD) Ascending, descending, transverse, sigmoid\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Ascending, transverse, descending, sigmoid","why":"The colon frames the small intestine on three sides. Material rises from the cecum up the right side in the ascending colon, turns at the hepatic flexure to cross in the transverse colon, turns at the splenic flexure to drop down the left side in the descending colon, and then curves through the S-shaped sigmoid colon into the rectum."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which sequence correctly traces the end of the canal, from the straight segment that holds feces, to the short terminal segment, to the exit?\nA) Rectum, cecum, anus  |  B) Rectum, anal canal, anus\nC) Anal canal, rectum, anus  |  D) Sigmoid colon, rectum, anus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Rectum, anal canal, anus","why":"The final stretch of the canal begins with the rectum, the straight portion anterior to the sacrum and coccyx that stores feces. Next comes the anal canal, the last few centimeters of the tract, which passes through the pelvic floor. It ends at the anus, the exit, and the internal and external anal sphincters surround the anal canal."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which sequence lists the three regions of the small intestine in the order food passes through them?\nA) Duodenum, ileum, jejunum  |  B) Duodenum, jejunum, ileum\nC) Ileum, jejunum, duodenum  |  D) Jejunum, duodenum, ileum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Duodenum, jejunum, ileum","why":"Food leaves the stomach through the pyloric sphincter and enters the duodenum, the first and shortest region, about 25 cm (10 inches) long. It then moves through the jejunum, the middle region of about 1 m (3 feet), and finally the ileum, the longest region at about 2 m (6 feet). The ileum ends at the ileocecal sphincter, where it joins the large intestine."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Trace a bite of food through every named segment of the alimentary canal in order from mouth to anus. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace a bite of food through every named segment of the alimentary canal in order from mouth to anus."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to trace a bite of food through every named segment of the alimentary canal in order from mouth to anus. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: trace the pathway of food. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, trace the pathway of food, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w5-canal-disorders","name":"Clinical anatomy of canal disorders","can":"Match GERD, hiatal hernia, peptic ulcer, appendicitis, diverticulosis, IBD, hemorrhoids, and colorectal cancer to the canal structure each one affects.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A peptic ulcer in the stomach has eroded through the mucosa, submucosa, and muscularis. If it then perforates the serosa, where will the contents of the lumen spill?\nA) Into the mediastinum  |  B) Into the peritoneal cavity\nC) Into the pericardial cavity  |  D) Into the pleural cavity\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Into the peritoneal cavity","why":"The serosa of the stomach is the visceral peritoneum. Just outside it lies the peritoneal cavity, the slim, fluid-filled space between the visceral and parietal peritoneum. When an ulcer breaks through the serosa, stomach contents leak into that space, which can cause a dangerous infection of the peritoneum."},{"t":"mc","q":"An inflamed vermiform appendix ruptures. Which membrane is the spreading infection most likely to reach?\nA) The peritoneum  |  B) The pericardium\nC) The meninges  |  D) The pleura\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The peritoneum","why":"The appendix hangs from the cecum inside the abdominopelvic cavity, which is lined by the peritoneum, the serous membrane of the abdomen. When the appendix ruptures, its contents spill into the peritoneal cavity, so the infection spreads to the peritoneum. This is why appendicitis is treated quickly, before rupture."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has burning pain behind the breastbone after meals as stomach contents rise into the esophagus. Which sphincter has most likely weakened?\nA) Pyloric sphincter  |  B) Ileocecal sphincter\nC) Lower esophageal sphincter  |  D) Upper esophageal sphincter\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Lower esophageal sphincter","why":"The lower esophageal sphincter normally closes the bottom of the esophagus so acidic stomach contents stay in the stomach. When it weakens, stomach contents flow back up and irritate the esophageal lining, which is felt as heartburn behind the sternum because the esophagus runs through the thorax there. Long-term reflux of this kind is called GERD."},{"t":"mc","q":"Imaging shows that the top of a patient's stomach has slid upward through the opening in the diaphragm that normally passes the esophagus. Through which opening did it move?\nA) Fauces  |  B) Vena caval foramen\nC) Aortic hiatus  |  D) Esophageal hiatus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Esophageal hiatus","why":"The esophagus passes from the thorax into the abdomen through the esophageal hiatus, an opening in the diaphragm. In a hiatal hernia, part of the stomach pushes up through this same opening into the thorax. This can weaken the barrier at the lower esophageal sphincter and is linked with heartburn."},{"t":"mc","q":"A peptic ulcer begins in the mucosa of the stomach and erodes outward until it perforates the serosa. What is the main danger at that point?\nA) Bile backs up into the gallbladder and liver\nB) Stomach contents back up into the lower esophagus\nC) Lumen contents spill into the peritoneal cavity\nD) Lumen contents spill into the pleural cavity\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Lumen contents spill into the peritoneal cavity","why":"A peptic ulcer is an eroded sore in the wall of the stomach or duodenum. It starts in the mucosa and can work outward through the submucosa and muscularis. The serosa is the visceral peritoneum, so once the ulcer breaks through it, food, acid and bacteria leak into the peritoneal cavity, which can cause a serious infection of the peritoneum."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Match GERD, hiatal hernia, peptic ulcer, appendicitis, diverticulosis, IBD, hemorrhoids, and colorectal cancer to the canal structure each one affects. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to match GERD, hiatal hernia, peptic ulcer, appendicitis, diverticulosis, IBD, hemorrhoids, and colorectal cancer to the canal structure each one affects."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to match GERD, hiatal hernia, peptic ulcer, appendicitis, diverticulosis, IBD, hemorrhoids, and colorectal cancer to the canal structure each one affects. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: clinical anatomy of canal disorders. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, clinical anatomy of canal disorders, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]}]},{"week":13,"system":"Digestive","comps":[{"id":"w5-teeth-tongue","name":"Teeth and tongue","can":"Identify the teeth and tongue with the lingual frenulum, papillae, intrinsic and extrinsic muscles, and lingual glands.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A cavity has worn through a patient's enamel and dentin, and the tooth now aches constantly. Which part of the tooth has the decay most likely reached?\nA) Gingiva  |  B) Pulp\nC) Enamel  |  D) Cementum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Pulp","why":"A tooth has layers from the outside in: enamel over the crown, dentin beneath it, and the pulp cavity at the center. Enamel has no nerves, so decay there is often painless. Once decay passes through the dentin and reaches the pulp, it contacts the nerves and blood vessels there, which causes the constant ache."},{"t":"mc","q":"Below the gum line, a dentist begins cleaning the outer surface of a tooth's root. Which covering does the instrument touch first?\nA) Pulp  |  B) Enamel\nC) Dentin  |  D) Cementum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Cementum","why":"The root of a tooth is the part held in the bony socket below the gum. Its outer surface is covered by cementum, a calcified, bone-like layer that also gives attachment to the periodontal ligament. Beneath the cementum lies dentin, and at the center is the pulp."},{"t":"mc","q":"A newborn cannot push her tongue past her gums, and the fold of tissue tying the underside of her tongue to the floor of the mouth looks unusually short. Which structure is involved?\nA) Uvula  |  B) Lingual frenulum\nC) Palatine tonsil  |  D) Soft palate\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Lingual frenulum","why":"The lingual frenulum is a midline fold of mucous membrane that anchors the underside of the tongue to the floor of the mouth. When it is unusually short or attached too far forward, it restricts how far the tongue can move, a condition commonly called tongue-tie. This can interfere with feeding and, later, with speech."},{"t":"mc","q":"A dentist examines a 5-year-old and counts 20 teeth: incisors, canines, and molars, but no premolars. What does this tell the dentist?\nA) A complete permanent set  |  B) A permanent set without wisdom teeth\nC) A complete deciduous set  |  D) Missing premolars from injury\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A complete deciduous set","why":"The deciduous set has 20 teeth made up of incisors, canines, and molars only, with no premolars. The permanent premolars come in later, replacing the deciduous molars. A count of 20 with this mix of tooth types is exactly what a complete baby set looks like."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which statement correctly compares dentin and enamel?\nA) Dentin forms the bulk; enamel is harder and covers the crown\nB) Enamel forms the bulk; dentin is harder and covers the crown\nC) Both cover only the root and attach to the socket\nD) Both are soft tissues that hold the nerves of the tooth\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Dentin forms the bulk; enamel is harder and covers the crown","why":"Most of a tooth is made of dentin, a hard, bone-like tissue that surrounds the pulp cavity. Over the crown, the dentin is capped by enamel, the hardest substance in the body. Over the root, the dentin is covered by cementum instead."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the teeth and tongue with the lingual frenulum, papillae, intrinsic and extrinsic muscles, and lingual glands. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the teeth and tongue with the lingual frenulum, papillae, intrinsic and extrinsic muscles, and lingual glands."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the teeth and tongue with the lingual frenulum, papillae, intrinsic and extrinsic muscles, and lingual glands. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: teeth and tongue. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, teeth and tongue, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w5-salivary-glands","name":"Salivary glands","can":"Locate the parotid, submandibular, and sublingual glands and state where the duct of each opens.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A single large duct from each side opens on a small bump right beside the lingual frenulum, under the tongue. Which gland does it drain?\nA) The submandibular gland  |  B) A minor labial gland\nC) The sublingual gland  |  D) The parotid gland\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The submandibular gland","why":"The submandibular glands lie along the inner side of the mandible, and each has one main duct that runs forward under the tongue to open near the lingual frenulum, the midline fold that anchors the tongue. The sublingual glands, by contrast, drain through several small ducts spread along the floor of the mouth. One large duct beside the frenulum points to the submandibular gland."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which statement describes what saliva does?\nA) Carries most of the digestive enzymes into the duodenum\nB) Moistens the mouth and starts breaking down food\nC) Breaks fat into tiny droplets so it can be digested\nD) Absorbs water from waste and helps form the feces\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Moistens the mouth and starts breaking down food","why":"Saliva is the watery secretion of the salivary glands. It keeps the mouth moist and clean, lubricates food so it can be shaped into a ball and swallowed, and begins the chemical breakdown of food while it is still being chewed. The three large pairs of salivary glands make most of it."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which is the largest of the three large pairs of salivary glands?\nA) The parotid glands  |  B) The submandibular glands\nC) The sublingual glands  |  D) The buccal glands\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The parotid glands","why":"The parotid glands are the largest salivary glands. Each one sits anterior and inferior to the ear, between the skin and the masseter muscle, and its duct crosses the cheek to open beside the upper second molar. Their size and position are why swelling from mumps shows so clearly at the angle of the jaw."},{"t":"mc","q":"Where does the parotid duct open into the mouth?\nA) On the soft palate near the uvula  |  B) Beside the upper second molar\nC) Along the floor of the mouth  |  D) Beside the lingual frenulum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Beside the upper second molar","why":"The parotid gland sits in front of and below the ear, outside the mouth. Its duct runs forward across the masseter muscle, turns inward through the cheek, and opens on the inside of the cheek beside the upper second molar. You can feel the small bump of this opening with your tongue."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which salivary glands lie in the floor of the mouth along the inner side of the mandible?\nA) The parotid glands  |  B) The buccal glands\nC) The sublingual glands  |  D) The submandibular glands\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The submandibular glands","why":"Sub means below and mandibular refers to the mandible, so the submandibular glands sit just below and inside the body of the mandible, in the floor of the mouth. Their ducts run forward to open near the lingual frenulum. You can feel them as soft lumps under the jaw on each side."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Locate the parotid, submandibular, and sublingual glands and state where the duct of each opens. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to locate the parotid, submandibular, and sublingual glands and state where the duct of each opens."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to locate the parotid, submandibular, and sublingual glands and state where the duct of each opens. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: salivary glands. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, salivary glands, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w5-liver-gross-lobule","name":"Liver gross and lobule anatomy","can":"Identify the four liver lobes, falciform ligament, and ligamentum teres, and name the hepatocytes, sinusoids, canaliculi, and portal triad of the lobule.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which group of structures passes through the porta hepatis on the underside of the liver?\nA) The hepatic portal vein, inferior vena cava, and pancreatic duct\nB) The hepatic veins, hepatic portal vein, and common bile duct\nC) The hepatic portal vein, hepatic artery, and hepatic ducts\nD) The hepatic veins, hepatic artery, and cystic duct\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The hepatic portal vein, hepatic artery, and hepatic ducts","why":"The porta hepatis is the gateway on the inferior surface of the liver. Two vessels bring blood in through it, the hepatic portal vein (nutrient-rich blood from the digestive organs) and the hepatic artery (oxygen-rich blood), while the right and left hepatic ducts carry bile out. The common bile duct does not pass through it, because that duct forms below the liver where the common hepatic duct meets the cystic duct."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which accessory organ lies in the right upper quadrant, drains its secretion through the right and left hepatic ducts, and has a storage sac attached to its posterior surface?\nA) The liver  |  B) The gallbladder\nC) The duodenum  |  D) The pancreas\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The liver","why":"Every clue points to the liver. It sits under the diaphragm mostly in the right upper quadrant, its bile drains out through the right and left hepatic ducts, and the gallbladder, which stores that bile, is attached to its posterior surface. Putting several clues together like this is how you confirm a structure on a model."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which three structures make up a portal triad at the corner of a liver lobule?\nA) Cystic duct, hepatic artery branch, hepatic portal vein branch\nB) Bile duct, hepatic artery branch, hepatic portal vein branch\nC) Bile duct, hepatic artery branch, hepatic vein branch\nD) Bile duct, central vein, hepatic portal vein branch\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Bile duct, hepatic artery branch, hepatic portal vein branch","why":"A portal triad sits at each corner of a liver lobule and carries traffic in both directions. A branch of the hepatic artery brings oxygenated blood in, a branch of the hepatic portal vein brings nutrient-rich blood from the gut in, and a bile duct carries bile out. The two incoming blood supplies mix in the sinusoids as they flow past the hepatocytes."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which list names the four lobes of the liver?\nA) Head, body, tail, and caudate\nB) Right, left, superior, and inferior\nC) Superior, middle, inferior, and quadrate\nD) Right, left, quadrate, and caudate\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Right, left, quadrate, and caudate","why":"The liver has two large principal lobes, the right and left, separated on the anterior surface by the falciform ligament. On the posterior and inferior surface you can also see two small lobes, the quadrate lobe and the caudate lobe. All four are on the lab structure list, so practice finding the two small lobes when the liver is turned over."},{"t":"mc","q":"Hepatocytes are arranged in thin plates with blood channels running between them. What are these plates called?\nA) Acini  |  B) Portal triads\nC) Bile canaliculi  |  D) Hepatic laminae\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Hepatic laminae","why":"Hepatocytes, the functional cells of the liver, are lined up in plates called hepatic laminae (lamina means a thin sheet). Wide, leaky capillaries called hepatic sinusoids run between the plates, so nearly every hepatocyte has blood flowing right past it. This arrangement lets the cells pull nutrients from the blood and add products back to it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the four liver lobes, falciform ligament, and ligamentum teres, and name the hepatocytes, sinusoids, canaliculi, and portal triad of the lobule. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the four liver lobes, falciform ligament, and ligamentum teres, and name the hepatocytes, sinusoids, canaliculi, and portal triad of the lobule."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the four liver lobes, falciform ligament, and ligamentum teres, and name the hepatocytes, sinusoids, canaliculi, and portal triad of the lobule. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: liver gross and lobule anatomy. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, liver gross and lobule anatomy, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w5-bile-path","name":"Gallbladder and bile pathway","can":"Identify the gallbladder regions and trace bile from the canaliculi through the hepatic, cystic, and common bile ducts to the hepatopancreatic ampulla and duodenum.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A drop of bile is made in the liver, stored in the gallbladder overnight, and released into the duodenum at breakfast. Which duct does it pass through twice, once in each direction?\nA) The common hepatic duct  |  B) The cystic duct\nC) The main pancreatic duct  |  D) The common bile duct\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The cystic duct","why":"The full route is liver, hepatic ducts, common hepatic duct, then up the cystic duct into the gallbladder. At breakfast the gallbladder contracts, and the bile goes back down the same cystic duct, into the common bile duct, through the hepatopancreatic ampulla, and into the duodenum. The cystic duct is the gallbladder's only connection, so stored bile must use it both ways."},{"t":"mc","q":"Between meals, bile flows into the gallbladder for storage, and during a meal it flows back out the same way. Which duct carries bile both into and out of the gallbladder?\nA) The common hepatic duct  |  B) The cystic duct\nC) The common bile duct  |  D) The main pancreatic duct\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The cystic duct","why":"The gallbladder has only one duct, the cystic duct, which joins the common hepatic duct to form the common bile duct. When the sphincter of Oddi is closed between meals, bile backs up the cystic duct into the gallbladder for storage. When a fatty meal arrives, the gallbladder contracts and sends bile back down the same cystic duct into the common bile duct."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which sequence traces bile from the liver to the gallbladder for storage between meals?\nA) Liver, hepatic ducts, common hepatic duct, cystic duct, gallbladder\nB) Liver, hepatic ducts, common bile duct, cystic duct, gallbladder\nC) Liver, hepatic ducts, main pancreatic duct, cystic duct, gallbladder\nD) Liver, common bile duct, hepatic ducts, gallbladder\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Liver, hepatic ducts, common hepatic duct, cystic duct, gallbladder","why":"Bile leaves the liver through the right and left hepatic ducts, which merge into the common hepatic duct. When the sphincter of Oddi is closed between meals, bile cannot enter the duodenum, so it backs up and flows through the cystic duct into the gallbladder. The cystic duct branches off where the common hepatic duct becomes the common bile duct, so bile turns up into the gallbladder at that junction."},{"t":"mc","q":"Most accessory organs are glands that make a secretion. Which accessory organ is classified as a storage organ instead, because it holds a secretion made somewhere else?\nA) The gallbladder  |  B) The liver\nC) The pancreas  |  D) The parotid gland\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The gallbladder","why":"The glandular accessory organs, the salivary glands, liver, and pancreas, each produce their own secretion and deliver it through ducts. The gallbladder produces no secretion of its own. It stores bile made by the liver and concentrates it until a meal arrives, so the notes classify it as a storage organ."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which region of the gallbladder is the broad, rounded end at its inferior tip?\nA) The fundus  |  B) The cardia\nC) The body  |  D) The neck\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The fundus","why":"The gallbladder has three regions: the fundus, the body, and the neck. The fundus is the broad, rounded inferior end, the body is the middle, and the neck tapers at the superior end where the sac narrows into the cystic duct. The stomach also has a region called the fundus, so the word simply means the rounded end or base of a hollow organ."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the gallbladder regions and trace bile from the canaliculi through the hepatic, cystic, and common bile ducts to the hepatopancreatic ampulla and duodenum. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the gallbladder regions and trace bile from the canaliculi through the hepatic, cystic, and common bile ducts to the hepatopancreatic ampulla and duodenum."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the gallbladder regions and trace bile from the canaliculi through the hepatic, cystic, and common bile ducts to the hepatopancreatic ampulla and duodenum. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: gallbladder and bile pathway. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, gallbladder and bile pathway, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w5-pancreas","name":"Pancreas structure","can":"Identify the head, body, and tail of the pancreas with its main and accessory ducts, major duodenal papilla, sphincter of Oddi, acini, and islets.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which pancreatic duct empties separately into the duodenum instead of joining the common bile duct?\nA) The cystic duct  |  B) The common hepatic duct\nC) The accessory pancreatic duct  |  D) The main pancreatic duct\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The accessory pancreatic duct","why":"The pancreas usually drains through two ducts. The main pancreatic duct, also called the duct of Wirsung, joins the common bile duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla. The smaller accessory duct, also called the duct of Santorini, drains part of the head and empties into the duodenum on its own at the minor duodenal papilla, so only the main duct shares an opening with bile."},{"t":"mc","q":"Pancreatic juice made by the acini drains through the pancreatic ducts. Where does it finally empty?\nA) The stomach  |  B) The gallbladder\nC) The bloodstream  |  D) The duodenum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The duodenum","why":"The acini are the exocrine clusters of the pancreas. Exocrine glands release their products through ducts onto a surface, and for the pancreas that surface is the lining of the duodenum, the first part of the small intestine. The main pancreatic duct delivers juice through the hepatopancreatic ampulla, and the accessory duct empties separately, but both end in the duodenum."},{"t":"mc","q":"Where is the pancreas located?\nA) On the posterior surface of the liver  |  B) Posterior to the stomach\nC) In the lower right quadrant beside the cecum  |  D) Anterior to the stomach\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Posterior to the stomach","why":"The pancreas is a soft, tapering gland that lies posterior to the stomach, against the posterior abdominal wall in a retroperitoneal position. Its head sits in the C-shaped curve of the duodenum and its tail reaches toward the spleen. Because it hides behind the stomach, you usually have to lift the stomach to see it on a model."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which duct carries pancreatic juice from the pancreas and joins the common bile duct?\nA) The common bile duct  |  B) The main pancreatic duct\nC) The cystic duct  |  D) The common hepatic duct\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The main pancreatic duct","why":"The main pancreatic duct, also called the duct of Wirsung, runs the length of the pancreas collecting pancreatic juice from the acini. Near the duodenum it joins the common bile duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla, and the combined fluid enters the duodenum at the major duodenal papilla. This shared ending is why a stone at the ampulla can affect the pancreas."},{"t":"mc","q":"The pancreatic islets are also known by which name?\nA) The acini  |  B) The islets of Langerhans\nC) The hepatic laminae  |  D) The portal triads\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The islets of Langerhans","why":"The pancreatic islets, also called the islets of Langerhans, are small clusters of endocrine cells scattered among the much more numerous acini. They make up only about 1 percent of the pancreas and release hormones into the blood. The name islets fits because they look like little islands surrounded by a sea of acini."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the head, body, and tail of the pancreas with its main and accessory ducts, major duodenal papilla, sphincter of Oddi, acini, and islets. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the head, body, and tail of the pancreas with its main and accessory ducts, major duodenal papilla, sphincter of Oddi, acini, and islets."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the head, body, and tail of the pancreas with its main and accessory ducts, major duodenal papilla, sphincter of Oddi, acini, and islets. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: pancreas structure. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, pancreas structure, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w5-accessory-disorders","name":"Clinical anatomy of accessory disorders","can":"Match gallstones, cholecystitis, hepatitis, cirrhosis, jaundice, pancreatitis, and mumps to the accessory organ each one affects and explain how a stone at the ampulla can block bile and pancreatic flow at once.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A small gallstone lodges at the hepatopancreatic ampulla. Which two ducts can it block at the same time?\nA) The right and left hepatic ducts\nB) The common bile duct and the main pancreatic duct\nC) The cystic duct and the common hepatic duct\nD) The accessory pancreatic duct and the cystic duct\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The common bile duct and the main pancreatic duct","why":"The hepatopancreatic ampulla is where the common bile duct and the main pancreatic duct meet before opening into the duodenum. A stone stuck there blocks the shared passage, so neither bile nor pancreatic juice can drain. This shared anatomy is why a gallstone can cause pancreatitis as well as jaundice."},{"t":"mc","q":"A gallstone completely blocks the cystic duct. What happens to the flow of bile?\nA) Pancreatic juice can no longer reach the duodenum\nB) Bile can no longer leave the liver by any route\nC) Bile flows backward into the main pancreatic duct\nD) Bile is cut off from the gallbladder but still reaches the duodenum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Bile is cut off from the gallbladder but still reaches the duodenum","why":"The cystic duct is a side branch that connects only the gallbladder to the duct system. If it is blocked, the gallbladder is cut off, but the main route from the hepatic ducts to the common hepatic duct, common bile duct, and duodenum stays open. Trapped bile in the gallbladder is what often leads to cholecystitis."},{"t":"mc","q":"A child with mumps has painful swelling in front of and just below the ear, over the angle of the jaw. Which salivary gland is inflamed?\nA) Sublingual gland  |  B) Submandibular gland\nC) Parotid gland  |  D) Palatine tonsil\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Parotid gland","why":"Mumps is a viral infection that inflames and enlarges the parotid glands. The parotid gland lies anterior and inferior to the ear, between the skin and the masseter muscle, so when it swells the cheek in front of the ear puffs out. The location of the swelling points straight to this gland."},{"t":"mc","q":"A child with mumps has swelling of the glands the virus infects. Where would that swelling show?\nA) In the right upper quadrant of the abdomen\nB) At the back of the throat beside the uvula\nC) Under the tongue in the floor of the mouth\nD) In front of and below the ears\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) In front of and below the ears","why":"Mumps is a viral infection that inflames and enlarges the parotid glands. The parotids sit anterior and inferior to each ear, over the masseter muscle and the angle of the jaw. When they swell, the face looks puffy in front of and below the ears, which is the classic look of mumps."},{"t":"mc","q":"Cholecystitis, inflammation of the gallbladder, is most often caused by what?\nA) A gallstone blocking the cystic duct  |  B) Scarring of the liver tissue\nC) A viral infection of the liver  |  D) A buildup of bilirubin in the skin\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A gallstone blocking the cystic duct","why":"The gallbladder drains only through the cystic duct. When a gallstone blocks that duct, bile is trapped inside the gallbladder, and the trapped bile irritates and inflames its wall. That is why cholecystitis and gallstones so often appear together, with pain in the right upper quadrant."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Match gallstones, cholecystitis, hepatitis, cirrhosis, jaundice, pancreatitis, and mumps to the accessory organ each one affects and explain how a stone at the ampulla can block bile and pancreatic flow at once. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to match gallstones, cholecystitis, hepatitis, cirrhosis, jaundice, pancreatitis, and mumps to the accessory organ each one affects and explain how a stone at the ampulla can block bile and pancreatic flow at once."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to match gallstones, cholecystitis, hepatitis, cirrhosis, jaundice, pancreatitis, and mumps to the accessory organ each one affects and explain how a stone at the ampulla can block bile and pancreatic flow at once. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: clinical anatomy of accessory disorders. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, clinical anatomy of accessory disorders, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]}]},{"week":11,"system":"Muscular","comps":[{"id":"w5-lab-abdominal-wall","name":"Abdominal and pelvic wall muscles","can":"Identify the abdominal and pelvic wall muscles on the cadaver including the rectus abdominis, external and internal obliques, transversus abdominis, and the pelvic floor muscles.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"On the lateral chest wall, the lower slips of the serratus anterior interlock with the upper slips of which muscle?\nA) Internal intercostals  |  B) External oblique\nC) Pectoralis minor  |  D) Latissimus dorsi\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) External oblique","why":"The external oblique, the most superficial lateral abdominal muscle, arises by fleshy slips from the outer surfaces of the lower ribs. Its upper slips alternate with the lower slips of the serratus anterior along the lateral chest wall, like interlocking fingers. That zigzag line is a useful landmark for telling the two muscles apart on a model or cadaver."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which sequence lists the three flat abdominal muscles from superficial to deep?\nA) External oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis\nB) Transversus abdominis, internal oblique, external oblique\nC) External oblique, transversus abdominis, internal oblique\nD) Internal oblique, external oblique, transversus abdominis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) External oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis","why":"The external oblique is the outermost flat muscle, with fibers running down and toward the midline. Beneath it the internal oblique runs roughly at right angles to it, and deepest of all the transversus abdominis runs horizontally. Because the fibers of the three layers run in different directions, like the layers of plywood, together they make a strong wall from three thin sheets."},{"t":"mc","q":"A fit athlete has a visible six-pack, the rectus abdominis crossed by bands. Which vertical band separates the left half of the six-pack from the right?\nA) Tendinous intersections  |  B) Inguinal ligament\nC) Linea semilunaris  |  D) Linea alba\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Linea alba","why":"The two rectus abdominis muscles lie side by side, one on each side of the midline. Between them runs the linea alba, the white line formed where the aponeuroses of the left and right abdominal muscles meet. On a lean athlete it shows as the vertical groove down the center of the six-pack."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which abdominal wall muscle has fibers that run vertically from the pubis up to the lower ribs and sternum?\nA) Transversus abdominis  |  B) Rectus abdominis\nC) External oblique  |  D) Internal oblique\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Rectus abdominis","why":"Rectus means straight, and the rectus abdominis runs straight up the front of the abdomen on each side of the midline, from the pubis to the xiphoid process and the lower costal cartilages. Because its fibers run vertically, its main action is to flex the trunk, pulling the rib cage toward the pelvis as in a sit-up."},{"t":"mc","q":"The linea alba is a fibrous band running down the midline of the abdomen. What forms it?\nA) Costal cartilages of the lower ribs fused together\nB) The falciform ligament fused to the parietal peritoneum\nC) Tendinous intersections crossing the rectus abdominis\nD) Aponeuroses of both sides meeting at the midline\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Aponeuroses of both sides meeting at the midline","why":"An aponeurosis is a flat, sheet-like tendon. The three flat abdominal muscles on each side end in aponeuroses that wrap the rectus abdominis and then meet their partners from the other side in the midline. Where they interweave from the xiphoid process to the pubic symphysis, they form a tough white line, the linea alba (white line)."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the abdominal and pelvic wall muscles on the cadaver including the rectus abdominis, external and internal obliques, transversus abdominis, and the pelvic floor muscles. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the abdominal and pelvic wall muscles on the cadaver including the rectus abdominis, external and internal obliques, transversus abdominis, and the pelvic floor muscles."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the abdominal and pelvic wall muscles on the cadaver including the rectus abdominis, external and internal obliques, transversus abdominis, and the pelvic floor muscles. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: abdominal and pelvic wall muscles. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, abdominal and pelvic wall muscles, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w5-lab-thigh-gluteal","name":"Gluteal and thigh muscles","can":"Identify the gluteal and thigh muscles including the gluteal group, quadriceps, hamstrings, and adductor group on the cadaver.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"As you lower yourself slowly from standing into a chair, your knees bend under your body weight. Which muscle group works to control that bending and keep you from dropping?\nA) Adductor group  |  B) Gastrocnemius and soleus\nC) Hamstrings  |  D) Quadriceps femoris\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Quadriceps femoris","why":"When you sit down slowly, gravity is what bends your knees. To keep from dropping, the knee extensors, the quadriceps, stay active while lengthening, braking the knee flexion that gravity produces. That is why your thighs feel the effort in a slow squat even though your knees are bending."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient stands on her right leg only, and the left side of her pelvis drops instead of staying level. Weakness of which muscle best explains this?\nA) Left gluteus medius  |  B) Right gluteus medius\nC) Right adductor longus  |  D) Right gluteus maximus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Right gluteus medius","why":"When you stand on one leg, the gluteus medius on the standing side pulls on the pelvis from its attachment at the greater trochanter and keeps the opposite side from sagging. If that muscle is weak, the unsupported side of the pelvis drops. Here she is standing on the right leg, so the right gluteus medius is the muscle failing to hold the pelvis level. Clinicians call this a positive Trendelenburg sign."},{"t":"mc","q":"A tailor sits on the floor with legs crossed, each ankle resting on the opposite knee. The muscle named for this posture flexes, abducts, and laterally rotates the thigh while also flexing the knee. Which muscle is it?\nA) Tensor fasciae latae  |  B) Gracilis\nC) Semitendinosus  |  D) Sartorius\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Sartorius","why":"The name sartorius comes from the Latin word for tailor. Because it runs from the anterior superior iliac spine, across the front of the thigh, to the medial tibia, it crosses both the hip and the knee. Its pull flexes, abducts, and laterally rotates the thigh and also bends the knee, which together place the leg in the cross-legged position."},{"t":"mc","q":"A distance runner has pain along the outside of the thigh and knee where a tight band rubs over the lateral femoral epicondyle. Which structure is most likely irritated?\nA) Iliotibial (IT) band  |  B) Adductor longus tendon\nC) Patellar ligament  |  D) Semitendinosus tendon\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Iliotibial (IT) band","why":"The iliotibial (IT) band runs down the lateral thigh and crosses the outer side of the knee near the lateral femoral epicondyle. With repeated bending and straightening of the knee during running, a tight band can rub over this bony bump and become painful. Pain on the outer thigh and knee points to this band."},{"t":"mc","q":"A soccer player feels a sudden pull high on the inner thigh while sweeping the leg across the body to kick. Which muscle group was most likely strained?\nA) Gluteal group  |  B) Hamstring group\nC) Quadriceps group  |  D) Adductor group\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Adductor group","why":"Sweeping the leg across the front of the body pulls the thigh toward and past the midline, which is adduction. The adductors on the medial thigh produce that movement and attach high on the pubis, right at the groin. A sudden forceful contraction or stretch there is the classic groin pull, an adductor strain."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the gluteal and thigh muscles including the gluteal group, quadriceps, hamstrings, and adductor group on the cadaver. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the gluteal and thigh muscles including the gluteal group, quadriceps, hamstrings, and adductor group on the cadaver."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the gluteal and thigh muscles including the gluteal group, quadriceps, hamstrings, and adductor group on the cadaver. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: gluteal and thigh muscles. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, gluteal and thigh muscles, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w5-lab-leg-muscles","name":"Leg muscles","can":"Identify the muscles of the leg including the anterior, lateral, and posterior compartment muscles on the cadaver.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Standing on tiptoe lifts the heel off the ground. Which tendon carries the pull of the calf muscles to the heel bone?\nA) Calcaneal tendon  |  B) Patellar ligament\nC) Iliotibial (IT) band  |  D) Tibialis posterior tendon\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Calcaneal tendon","why":"Rising onto your toes is plantarflexion, done mainly by the gastrocnemius and soleus in the posterior compartment of the leg. Both muscles end in the calcaneal (Achilles) tendon, the thickest tendon in the body, which attaches to the calcaneus, the heel bone. When the calf contracts, this tendon pulls the heel up."},{"t":"mc","q":"A gym-goer does calf raises while seated with the knees bent at 90 degrees. Which muscle does most of the work of plantar flexion in this position?\nA) Fibularis tertius  |  B) Tibialis anterior\nC) Soleus  |  D) Gastrocnemius\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Soleus","why":"With the knee bent, the gastrocnemius is already shortened across the back of the knee because it arises from the femur. A shortened muscle cannot pull as hard, so it adds little force at the ankle. The soleus starts below the knee on the tibia and fibula, so knee position does not affect it, and it carries most of the load in a seated calf raise."},{"t":"mc","q":"During a basketball game a player feels a snap at the back of the heel and afterward cannot rise onto the toes on that side. Which structure was most likely torn?\nA) Patellar ligament  |  B) Extensor retinaculum\nC) Calcaneal tendon  |  D) Iliotibial (IT) band\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Calcaneal tendon","why":"Rising onto the toes is plantar flexion, and the strongest plantar flexors, the gastrocnemius and soleus, act through the calcaneal tendon at the back of the heel. When that tendon ruptures, the calf can no longer pull on the calcaneus, so the heel cannot be lifted against body weight. The location of the snap fits as well."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient's toes drag on the ground when he walks, so he lifts the knee high with each step. Weakness of which group of leg muscles best explains this?\nA) Anterior compartment  |  B) Deep posterior compartment\nC) Lateral compartment  |  D) Superficial posterior compartment\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Anterior compartment","why":"To clear the ground while the leg swings forward, the foot must be held in dorsiflexion, with the toes lifted. The anterior compartment muscles, led by the tibialis anterior, do this. When they are weak the foot hangs down, a problem called foot drop, and the person lifts the knee unusually high so the toes do not catch."},{"t":"mc","q":"A dancer has weak lateral compartment muscles and keeps rolling her ankle so the sole turns inward. Which movement are the weak muscles failing to produce?\nA) Inversion  |  B) Knee flexion\nC) Eversion  |  D) Dorsiflexion\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Eversion","why":"Rolling the ankle with the sole turning inward is excessive inversion. The muscles that resist it are the evertors, which turn the sole outward. The fibularis longus and brevis of the lateral compartment are the main evertors, so when they are weak, nothing pulls back against a sudden inward roll."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the muscles of the leg including the anterior, lateral, and posterior compartment muscles on the cadaver. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the muscles of the leg including the anterior, lateral, and posterior compartment muscles on the cadaver."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the muscles of the leg including the anterior, lateral, and posterior compartment muscles on the cadaver. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: leg muscles. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, leg muscles, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]}]},{"week":13,"system":"Regional Vessels & Nerves","comps":[{"id":"w5-lab-abdominal-vessels","name":"Abdominal aorta and portal system","can":"Identify the major branches of the abdominal aorta and the vessels of the hepatic portal system on the cadaver.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Old red blood cells broken down in the spleen release products into the splenic vein, which joins the hepatic portal vein. Which organ receives these products next?\nA) The kidneys  |  B) The pancreas\nC) The lungs  |  D) The liver\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The liver","why":"Blood leaving the spleen drains into the splenic vein, which joins the hepatic portal vein. The hepatic portal vein carries blood straight to the liver before it returns to the heart. As a result, the breakdown products of old red cells reach the liver first, where they can be processed."},{"t":"mc","q":"A clot blocks the superior mesenteric artery near its origin. Which part of the digestive tract is most at risk of losing its blood supply?\nA) The jejunum, ileum, and ascending colon\nB) The stomach and the first part of the duodenum\nC) The esophagus and the stomach\nD) The descending colon and the sigmoid colon\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The jejunum, ileum, and ascending colon","why":"The superior mesenteric artery supplies most of the small intestine, including the jejunum and ileum, along with the cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and most of the transverse colon. A clot near its origin cuts off flow to all of these branches at once. Without blood, the bowel wall can die, which is why a blocked superior mesenteric artery is a surgical emergency."},{"t":"mc","q":"A clot forms at the origin of the celiac trunk. Which of these organs would lose its main arterial supply?\nA) Ileum  |  B) Spleen\nC) Left kidney  |  D) Sigmoid colon\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Spleen","why":"The celiac trunk divides into the left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic arteries. The spleen gets its blood from the splenic artery, so a clot at the start of the celiac trunk cuts off its main supply. The stomach and liver would also lose arterial flow from this trunk, although the liver still receives blood through the hepatic portal vein."},{"t":"mc","q":"Scarring from cirrhosis makes it hard for blood to flow through the liver. Which vessel would back up first as blood struggles to enter the liver?\nA) The left renal vein  |  B) The hepatic portal vein\nC) The hepatic veins  |  D) The inferior vena cava\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The hepatic portal vein","why":"The hepatic portal vein delivers blood from the stomach, intestines, pancreas, and spleen into the liver. When scar tissue narrows the paths through the liver, blood has trouble getting through, so pressure rises upstream in the portal vein and its tributaries. This is called portal hypertension, and it can make the splenic vein and spleen swell as blood backs up."},{"t":"mc","q":"A mass presses on the left renal vein just before it reaches the inferior vena cava. Besides the left kidney, drainage from which organ could be affected?\nA) The descending colon  |  B) The right testis or ovary\nC) The spleen  |  D) The left testis or ovary\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The left testis or ovary","why":"The left gonadal vein does not reach the inferior vena cava on its own. It empties into the left renal vein, so anything that blocks the left renal vein near its end also slows drainage from the left testis or ovary. This is one reason swollen veins around the testis (a varicocele) are much more common on the left side."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the major branches of the abdominal aorta and the vessels of the hepatic portal system on the cadaver. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the major branches of the abdominal aorta and the vessels of the hepatic portal system on the cadaver."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the major branches of the abdominal aorta and the vessels of the hepatic portal system on the cadaver. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: abdominal aorta and portal system. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, abdominal aorta and portal system, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w5-lab-lower-limb-vessels-nerves","name":"Lower limb vessels and nerves","can":"Identify the femoral and lower-limb blood vessels and the major nerves of the abdomen and lower limb on the cadaver.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A car crash dislocates a person's knee, pushing the tibia forward on the femur. Which vessel behind the knee is most at risk of damage?\nA) The femoral artery  |  B) The dorsalis pedis artery\nC) The popliteal artery  |  D) The great saphenous vein\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The popliteal artery","why":"A dislocated knee is a displacement of the tibia relative to the femur, most often anteriorly. The popliteal artery runs through the popliteal fossa directly behind the knee joint, so it can be stretched or torn when the bones shift. Damage to it can cut off blood flow to the leg and foot, which makes a knee dislocation an emergency."},{"t":"mc","q":"A nurse gives an intramuscular injection into the buttock and places the needle in the upper outer quadrant. Which structure is the nurse mainly trying to avoid?\nA) Obturator nerve  |  B) Great saphenous vein\nC) Sciatic nerve  |  D) Femoral nerve\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Sciatic nerve","why":"The sciatic nerve leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen and runs deep to the gluteus maximus through the lower and medial part of the buttock before entering the back of the thigh. An injection in the upper outer quadrant stays well away from that path. A needle placed too low or too medial could injure the nerve and cause weakness and numbness down the back of the limb."},{"t":"mc","q":"A soccer player is kicked on the outside of the leg just below the knee and fractures the neck of the fibula. Which nerve lies against the bone at this spot and is most at risk?\nA) Sciatic nerve at the buttock  |  B) Tibial nerve\nC) Common fibular nerve  |  D) Femoral nerve\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Common fibular nerve","why":"After the sciatic nerve divides above the knee, the common fibular nerve follows the biceps femoris tendon to the lateral side of the knee and curves around the neck of the fibula, covered only by skin and thin tissue. A blow or fracture at that exact spot can stretch or crush it, so the location of the break points straight to this nerve."},{"t":"mc","q":"A surgeon needs a long length of vein to bypass blocked coronary arteries and harvests one from the medial side of the patient's leg and thigh. Which vein is most often used?\nA) Femoral vein  |  B) Small saphenous vein\nC) Great saphenous vein  |  D) Popliteal vein\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Great saphenous vein","why":"The great saphenous vein is long, runs near the surface along the medial leg and thigh, and is a superficial vein whose job can be taken over by the deep veins. That combination makes it the standard vein to remove and use as a graft in coronary artery bypass surgery. Its location on the medial side matches the description."},{"t":"mc","q":"A clot forms in a patient's popliteal vein and breaks loose. Traveling toward the heart, which vein does it enter next?\nA) External iliac vein  |  B) Femoral vein\nC) Small saphenous vein  |  D) Great saphenous vein\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Femoral vein","why":"Blood in the deep veins of the lower limb flows upward in the reverse order of the arteries: popliteal vein, femoral vein, external iliac vein, common iliac vein, inferior vena cava, and then the right atrium. A loose clot follows the same path. From the popliteal vein, the next vessel is the femoral vein, which it enters through the adductor hiatus."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the femoral and lower-limb blood vessels and the major nerves of the abdomen and lower limb on the cadaver. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the femoral and lower-limb blood vessels and the major nerves of the abdomen and lower limb on the cadaver."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the femoral and lower-limb blood vessels and the major nerves of the abdomen and lower limb on the cadaver. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: lower limb vessels and nerves. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, lower limb vessels and nerves, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]}]},{"week":14,"system":"Urinary","comps":[{"id":"w6-kidney-gross","name":"Kidney gross anatomy and layers","can":"Describe the location and tissue layers of the kidney and identify the hilum, capsule, adipose capsule, and renal fascia.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Three layers surround each kidney. Which one is the outer connective tissue layer that anchors the kidney to the abdominal wall?\nA) The adipose capsule  |  B) The renal capsule\nC) The parietal peritoneum  |  D) The renal fascia\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The renal fascia","why":"From deep to superficial, the three layers around each kidney are the renal capsule, the adipose capsule, and the renal fascia. The renal fascia is the outermost of the three, a layer of connective tissue that anchors the kidney and its fat to the abdominal wall. The capsule shapes the kidney, the fat cushions it, and the fascia ties it down."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which kidney usually sits slightly lower, and why?\nA) The right kidney, because the stomach sits above it\nB) The left kidney, because the spleen sits above it\nC) The right kidney, because the liver sits above it\nD) The left kidney, because the heart sits above it\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The right kidney, because the liver sits above it","why":"Both kidneys lie retroperitoneally between about T12 and L3, but they are not perfectly level. The liver, the largest organ in the abdomen, fills the upper right side, so the right kidney sits slightly lower than the left. This is one of the questions on the lab structure list, so be ready to say both the side and the reason."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which group of structures enters or leaves the kidney at the renal hilum?\nA) Renal artery, renal vein, ureter, and nerves\nB) Renal artery, renal vein, suprarenal gland, and ureter\nC) Collecting ducts, nephron loops, and arcuate arteries\nD) Renal artery, renal vein, urethra, and nerves\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Renal artery, renal vein, ureter, and nerves","why":"The renal hilum is the notch on the medial border of the kidney, and it is the kidney's only doorway. The renal artery enters there, while the renal vein and the ureter (continuing from the renal pelvis) leave there, along with the nerves and lymphatic vessels that serve the kidney. Everything that connects the kidney to the rest of the body passes through this one notch."},{"t":"mc","q":"The kidneys are described as retroperitoneal. What does that tell you about where they lie?\nA) They lie in the thoracic cavity, behind the pleura\nB) They hang inside the peritoneal cavity, suspended by a mesentery\nC) They lie behind the parietal peritoneum, against the posterior abdominal wall\nD) They sit low in the pelvic cavity, behind the urinary bladder\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) They lie behind the parietal peritoneum, against the posterior abdominal wall.","why":"Retro means behind, so retroperitoneal organs lie behind the peritoneum, the serous membrane that lines the abdominal cavity. The kidneys sit against the posterior abdominal wall, between about T12 and L3, with only the parietal peritoneum passing in front of them. Instead of hanging from a mesentery, each kidney is held in place by its own fat and fascia."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which pairing of a urinary organ with its job is correct?\nA) Kidney: filters the blood and forms urine\nB) Urethra: carries urine from the kidney to the bladder\nC) Urinary bladder: carries urine out of the body\nD) Ureter: stores urine until it is released\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Kidney: filters the blood and forms urine","why":"The kidneys are the working organs of the urinary system. They filter wastes out of the blood and form urine. The other three organs only move and hold that urine: the ureters carry it down to the bladder, the bladder stores it, and the urethra carries it to the outside of the body."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Describe the location and tissue layers of the kidney and identify the hilum, capsule, adipose capsule, and renal fascia. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to describe the location and tissue layers of the kidney and identify the hilum, capsule, adipose capsule, and renal fascia."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to describe the location and tissue layers of the kidney and identify the hilum, capsule, adipose capsule, and renal fascia. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: kidney gross anatomy and layers. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, kidney gross anatomy and layers, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w6-kidney-internal","name":"Internal kidney regions","can":"Identify the cortex, medulla, renal columns, pyramids, papillae, parenchyma, calyces, and renal pelvis on a sectioned kidney.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A kidney biopsy needs to include renal corpuscles for the pathologist to examine. Which region of the kidney must the needle sample?\nA) The renal medulla  |  B) The renal pelvis\nC) The renal sinus  |  D) The renal cortex\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The renal cortex","why":"All of the renal corpuscles, the glomeruli inside their capsules, sit in the renal cortex, the outer region of kidney tissue. The medulla holds the nephron loops and collecting ducts, but no corpuscles. So a sample that must show glomeruli has to come from the cortex."},{"t":"mc","q":"A small stone blocks one minor calyx and urine backs up behind it. Which part of the kidney is the backed-up urine coming from?\nA) The renal pelvis and the ureter below it\nB) The renal column beside that calyx\nC) The pyramid whose papilla opens into it\nD) Every renal pyramid in the kidney\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The pyramid whose papilla opens into it","why":"Urine drains out of tiny openings on the renal papilla, the tip of a renal pyramid, and each papilla empties into a cup called a minor calyx. A blockage makes urine back up into the parts above it. So the backup comes from the pyramid whose papilla opens into that calyx, while the rest of the kidney keeps draining through its own calyces."},{"t":"mc","q":"The renal pyramids are found in which region of the kidney?\nA) The renal pelvis  |  B) The renal medulla\nC) The renal capsule  |  D) The renal cortex\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The renal medulla","why":"The renal medulla is the inner region of kidney tissue, and it is made of cone-shaped renal pyramids. Each pyramid points its base toward the cortex and its tip, the renal papilla, toward the center of the kidney. The pyramids look striped because they are packed with nephron loops and collecting ducts running in parallel."},{"t":"mc","q":"The renal columns that dip down between the renal pyramids are extensions of which tissue?\nA) Adipose tissue  |  B) Fibrous capsule tissue\nC) Medullary tissue  |  D) Cortical tissue\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Cortical tissue","why":"The renal columns are extensions of the renal cortex that reach down between neighboring pyramids. Because the columns are cortex, the medulla is broken up into separate cone-shaped pyramids rather than forming one solid inner layer. The arteries that run between the pyramids travel in these columns."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which chambers collect urine directly from the renal papillae?\nA) The major calyces  |  B) The collecting ducts\nC) The minor calyces  |  D) The renal pelvis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The minor calyces","why":"Each minor calyx is a small cup that fits around the tip of a renal papilla and catches the urine draining out of it. Several minor calyces join to form a major calyx, and the major calyces empty into the renal pelvis. The sequence runs from small to large: minor calyx, major calyx, renal pelvis."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the cortex, medulla, renal columns, pyramids, papillae, parenchyma, calyces, and renal pelvis on a sectioned kidney. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the cortex, medulla, renal columns, pyramids, papillae, parenchyma, calyces, and renal pelvis on a sectioned kidney."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the cortex, medulla, renal columns, pyramids, papillae, parenchyma, calyces, and renal pelvis on a sectioned kidney. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: internal kidney regions. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, internal kidney regions, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w6-nephron-parts","name":"Nephron structure","can":"Identify the renal corpuscle, glomerulus, glomerular capsule, podocytes, PCT, nephron loop, DCT, and collecting duct of a nephron.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Filtrate has just left the nephron loop. Which segment of the nephron does it enter next?\nA) The distal convoluted tubule  |  B) The glomerular capsule\nC) The collecting duct  |  D) The proximal convoluted tubule\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The distal convoluted tubule","why":"The segments are named in the order the filtrate meets them: capsular space, proximal convoluted tubule, nephron loop, distal convoluted tubule, and then the collecting duct. After the filtrate climbs the ascending limb of the nephron loop, it enters the distal convoluted tubule, the second coiled segment. Proximal means near the renal corpuscle and distal means farther from it, which makes the order easy to rebuild."},{"t":"mc","q":"What forms the parietal (outer) layer of the glomerular capsule?\nA) Podocytes  |  B) Simple cuboidal epithelium\nC) Simple squamous epithelium  |  D) Transitional epithelium\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Simple squamous epithelium","why":"The glomerular capsule has two layers with the capsular space between them. The outer, parietal layer is simple squamous epithelium, a single layer of flat cells that forms the wall of the cup. The inner, visceral layer is made of podocytes, which wrap the glomerular capillaries. Filtrate passes into the capsular space between the two layers."},{"t":"mc","q":"Near the tip of a renal pyramid, collecting ducts join into larger ducts that open on the renal papilla. What are these larger ducts called?\nA) Papillary ducts  |  B) Minor calyces\nC) Distal convoluted tubules  |  D) Ureters\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Papillary ducts","why":"Collecting ducts run down through the renal pyramid and join into larger papillary ducts as they near the tip. The papillary ducts open on the renal papilla, where urine drips out into a minor calyx. The name tells you where they are: papillary ducts are the ducts of the papilla. They are on the lab structure list as the last part of the renal tubule system."},{"t":"mc","q":"The renal corpuscle is made up of which two structures together?\nA) A glomerulus enclosed in a glomerular capsule\nB) A renal pyramid and a renal column\nC) An afferent arteriole and an efferent arteriole\nD) A renal tubule and a collecting duct\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A glomerulus enclosed in a glomerular capsule","why":"The renal corpuscle is the filtering head of the nephron. It is a glomerulus, the tuft of capillaries where blood is filtered, sitting inside the glomerular (Bowman's) capsule, which catches the filtrate. The rest of the nephron, the renal tubule, carries and processes that filtrate."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which part of the nephron is its filtering head?\nA) The renal pelvis  |  B) The collecting duct\nC) The renal corpuscle  |  D) The renal tubule\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The renal corpuscle","why":"A nephron has two main parts. The renal corpuscle, a glomerulus inside a glomerular capsule, is the filtering head where fluid is pushed out of the blood. The renal tubule then carries that filtrate and changes it into urine. All the renal corpuscles sit in the cortex."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the renal corpuscle, glomerulus, glomerular capsule, podocytes, PCT, nephron loop, DCT, and collecting duct of a nephron. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the renal corpuscle, glomerulus, glomerular capsule, podocytes, PCT, nephron loop, DCT, and collecting duct of a nephron."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the renal corpuscle, glomerulus, glomerular capsule, podocytes, PCT, nephron loop, DCT, and collecting duct of a nephron. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: nephron structure. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, nephron structure, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w6-nephron-types","name":"Cortical vs juxtamedullary nephrons","can":"Compare cortical and juxtamedullary nephrons by loop depth and associated capillary beds.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which capillaries run deep into the medulla alongside the long loops of juxtamedullary nephrons?\nA) The arcuate arteries  |  B) The vasa recta\nC) The glomerular capillaries  |  D) The peritubular capillaries\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The vasa recta","why":"The vasa recta are long, straight capillaries that branch from the efferent arterioles of juxtamedullary nephrons and run deep into the medulla beside their long nephron loops. Vasa recta means straight vessels. Cortical nephrons, with their short loops, are wrapped by peritubular capillaries instead."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which vessels are long, straight capillaries that follow the loops of juxtamedullary nephrons deep into the medulla?\nA) The interlobar veins  |  B) The peritubular capillaries\nC) The vasa recta  |  D) The arcuate arteries\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The vasa recta","why":"Vasa recta means straight vessels. These long capillaries come off the efferent arterioles of juxtamedullary nephrons and run beside their long nephron loops deep into the medulla. Juxtamedullary nephrons sit near the medulla, make up about 15 percent of nephrons, and concentrate the urine."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which nephrons have long loops that reach deep into the medulla, and what share of nephrons are they?\nA) Cortical nephrons, about 85 percent of nephrons\nB) Juxtamedullary nephrons, about 85 percent of nephrons\nC) Juxtamedullary nephrons, about 15 percent of nephrons\nD) Cortical nephrons, about 15 percent of nephrons\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Juxtamedullary nephrons, about 15 percent of nephrons","why":"Juxtamedullary nephrons have their renal corpuscles in the cortex right next to the medulla, and their long loops reach deep into the medulla, where they are served by the vasa recta. They make up only about 15 percent of nephrons, but they are the ones that concentrate the urine. The other 85 percent are cortical nephrons with short loops."},{"t":"mc","q":"The word juxtamedullary describes where these nephrons begin. What does the name tell you?\nA) Their renal corpuscles sit next to the medulla\nB) Their renal corpuscles sit inside the medulla\nC) Their renal corpuscles sit in the outer cortex\nD) They make up about 85 percent of all nephrons\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Their renal corpuscles sit next to the medulla","why":"Juxta means next to, so a juxtamedullary nephron has its renal corpuscle in the deepest part of the cortex, right next to the medulla. From there, its long loop reaches deep into the medulla, close to the renal papilla."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which type of nephron makes up about 85 percent of all nephrons?\nA) Medullary nephrons  |  B) Papillary nephrons\nC) Cortical nephrons  |  D) Juxtamedullary nephrons\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Cortical nephrons","why":"Cortical nephrons make up about 85 percent of nephrons. They have short loops that barely enter the medulla and are wrapped by peritubular capillaries. The other roughly 15 percent are juxtamedullary nephrons, whose long loops reach deep into the medulla."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Compare cortical and juxtamedullary nephrons by loop depth and associated capillary beds. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to compare cortical and juxtamedullary nephrons by loop depth and associated capillary beds."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to compare cortical and juxtamedullary nephrons by loop depth and associated capillary beds. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: Cortical vs juxtamedullary nephrons. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, Cortical vs juxtamedullary nephrons, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w6-jga-filtration","name":"JGA and filtration membrane","can":"Identify the juxtaglomerular apparatus, macula densa, and JG cells, and name the three layers of the filtration membrane and what each holds back.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Where are the juxtaglomerular (granular) cells located?\nA) In the wall of the distal convoluted tubule\nB) In the wall of the collecting duct\nC) In the wall of the afferent arteriole\nD) In the visceral layer of the glomerular capsule\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) In the wall of the afferent arteriole","why":"Juxtaglomerular cells are modified smooth muscle cells, mostly in the wall of the afferent arteriole, at the spot where the tubule passes beside its own renal corpuscle. Together with the macula densa cells in the tubule wall, they form the juxtaglomerular apparatus, which monitors the tubule fluid and the arteriole and helps adjust filtration. The JG cells release renin when blood pressure is low. Juxtaglomerular means next to the glomerulus."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which layer of the filtration membrane lets plasma pass but blocks blood cells?\nA) The fenestrated endothelium  |  B) The slit membranes\nC) The parietal layer of the glomerular capsule  |  D) The basement membrane\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The fenestrated endothelium","why":"Blood is filtered across a three-layer membrane. The first layer is the fenestrated endothelium, the wall of the glomerular capillary, which is full of pores (fenestrations means windows). The pores are large enough for plasma to pass but too small for blood cells, so the cells stay in the blood. The basement membrane and slit membranes then hold back proteins."},{"t":"mc","q":"Healthy urine contains almost no protein, even though plasma is full of large proteins. Which layer of the filtration membrane blocks large plasma proteins?\nA) The renal capsule  |  B) The parietal layer of the glomerular capsule\nC) The fenestrated endothelium  |  D) The basement membrane\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The basement membrane","why":"The filtration membrane has three layers, and each one holds back something different. The fenestrated endothelium blocks blood cells, the basement membrane between the capillary and the podocytes blocks large plasma proteins, and the filtration slits of the podocytes hold back most of the smaller proteins that get past it. Because large proteins stop at the basement membrane, they stay in the blood instead of entering the filtrate."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which cells of the juxtaglomerular apparatus release renin when blood pressure is low?\nA) The proximal tubule cells  |  B) The juxtaglomerular cells\nC) The macula densa cells  |  D) The podocytes\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The juxtaglomerular cells","why":"The juxtaglomerular (granular) cells are modified smooth muscle cells in the wall of the afferent arteriole. Their granules hold the enzyme renin, which they release when blood pressure is low. They sit where the tubule passes beside its own renal corpuscle, close to the macula densa cells in the tubule wall."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the juxtaglomerular apparatus?\nA) Where the collecting duct opens at the tip of a renal papilla\nB) Where the distal tubule meets its own corpuscle's arterioles\nC) Where the proximal tubule leaves its own glomerular capsule\nD) Where a long nephron loop dips down beside the vasa recta\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Where the distal tubule meets its own corpuscle's arterioles","why":"Juxtaglomerular means next to the glomerulus. After the nephron loop rises back toward the cortex, the tubule passes right beside the afferent and efferent arterioles of its own renal corpuscle, where the ascending limb ends and the distal convoluted tubule begins. At that contact point, the macula densa cells in the tubule wall and the juxtaglomerular cells in the arteriole wall together form the juxtaglomerular apparatus, which monitors the tubule fluid and the arteriole and helps adjust filtration."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the juxtaglomerular apparatus, macula densa, and JG cells, and name the three layers of the filtration membrane and what each holds back. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the juxtaglomerular apparatus, macula densa, and JG cells, and name the three layers of the filtration membrane and what each holds back."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the juxtaglomerular apparatus, macula densa, and JG cells, and name the three layers of the filtration membrane and what each holds back. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: JGA and filtration membrane. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, JGA and filtration membrane, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w6-kidney-bloodsupply","name":"Kidney blood supply pathway","can":"Trace blood through the kidney from the renal artery to the renal vein, naming each vessel in order.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Unlike most capillary beds, the glomerulus drains into an arteriole instead of a venule. Where does blood in that efferent arteriole go next?\nA) Back into the afferent arteriole\nB) Directly into the renal vein\nC) Into the capsular space with the filtrate\nD) Into a second capillary bed around the renal tubules\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Into a second capillary bed around the renal tubules","why":"Blood in the kidney meets two capillary beds, one after the other. The first is the glomerulus, where filtration happens. The efferent arteriole carries the blood to the second bed, the peritubular capillaries and vasa recta that wrap the renal tubules, and only then does the blood drain into veins leading to the renal vein. The efferent arteriole is the vessel that links the two beds."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which vessel carries blood directly into a glomerulus?\nA) The efferent arteriole  |  B) The afferent arteriole\nC) The peritubular capillaries  |  D) The cortical radiate artery\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The afferent arteriole","why":"The afferent arteriole is the last vessel in the kidney's arterial chain, and it delivers blood straight into the glomerulus. Afferent means carrying toward, the same word used for sensory neurons that carry signals toward the brain. Blood then leaves the glomerulus through the efferent arteriole, meaning carrying away."},{"t":"mc","q":"After blood leaves the efferent arteriole, which capillaries wrap around the convoluted tubules in the cortex?\nA) The cortical radiate veins  |  B) The glomerular capillaries\nC) The vasa recta  |  D) The peritubular capillaries\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The peritubular capillaries","why":"Blood in the kidney passes through two capillary beds in a row. The first is the glomerulus, where filtration happens. The efferent arteriole then carries the blood to the second bed, the peritubular capillaries, which wrap the convoluted tubules in the cortex. Peritubular means around the tubules."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which vessel carries blood out of the glomerulus?\nA) The efferent arteriole  |  B) The cortical radiate artery\nC) The peritubular capillaries  |  D) The afferent arteriole\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The efferent arteriole","why":"Blood leaves the glomerulus through the efferent arteriole, meaning the arteriole that carries away. Unusually, blood leaving this capillary bed is still in an arteriole rather than a venule. The efferent arteriole then leads to a second capillary bed, the peritubular capillaries and vasa recta, around the renal tubules."},{"t":"mc","q":"Blood enters the kidney through the renal artery, which splits into segmental arteries in the renal sinus. Which arteries come next, running up through the renal columns between the pyramids?\nA) The afferent arterioles  |  B) The cortical radiate arteries\nC) The interlobar arteries  |  D) The arcuate arteries\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The interlobar arteries","why":"Inside the renal sinus, the renal artery divides into segmental arteries. These give off interlobar arteries, which run up through the renal columns between the renal pyramids. Interlobar means between the lobes, and each lobe is one pyramid with the cortex around it. At the bases of the pyramids the interlobar arteries turn into arcuate arteries."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Trace blood through the kidney from the renal artery to the renal vein, naming each vessel in order. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace blood through the kidney from the renal artery to the renal vein, naming each vessel in order."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to trace blood through the kidney from the renal artery to the renal vein, naming each vessel in order. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: kidney blood supply pathway. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, kidney blood supply pathway, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w6-urine-path","name":"Ureters, bladder, urethra and urine path","can":"Trace urine from the collecting duct to the outside and identify the ureter, bladder wall layers, trigone, detrusor, sphincters, and male and female urethra.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which list orders the urine-collecting spaces from the renal papilla to the ureter?\nA) Minor calyx, renal pelvis, major calyx, ureter\nB) Major calyx, minor calyx, renal pelvis, ureter\nC) Minor calyx, major calyx, renal pelvis, ureter\nD) Renal pelvis, minor calyx, major calyx, ureter\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Minor calyx, major calyx, renal pelvis, ureter","why":"The collecting spaces get larger as they merge. Each papilla drains into a small minor calyx, several minor calyces join to form a major calyx, and the major calyces empty into the single renal pelvis. The renal pelvis then narrows into the ureter as it leaves the kidney at the hilum."},{"t":"mc","q":"Fluid leaves the distal convoluted tubule. Which sequence correctly traces it to the minor calyx?\nA) Collecting duct, papillary duct, renal papilla, minor calyx\nB) Collecting duct, renal papilla, papillary duct, minor calyx\nC) Papillary duct, collecting duct, renal papilla, minor calyx\nD) Collecting duct, papillary duct, major calyx, minor calyx\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Collecting duct, papillary duct, renal papilla, minor calyx","why":"The distal convoluted tubules of many nephrons drain into a collecting duct, which runs down through a renal pyramid. Near the tip of the pyramid, collecting ducts join into larger papillary ducts that open on the renal papilla. From there the urine drips into a minor calyx, the cup that fits around each papilla. The ducts get larger and fewer as you move toward the calyx."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which statement correctly describes the detrusor muscle?\nA) Smooth muscle in the bladder wall that contracts to empty the bladder\nB) Smooth muscle in the ureter wall that pushes urine to the bladder\nC) Skeletal muscle around the urethra that gives voluntary control\nD) A ring of smooth muscle that keeps the bladder outlet closed\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Smooth muscle in the bladder wall that contracts to empty the bladder","why":"The detrusor muscle is the thick smooth muscle layer of the urinary bladder wall. When it contracts, it squeezes the bladder and pushes urine out into the urethra. Because it is smooth muscle, it works involuntarily, while conscious control of urination comes from the external urethral sphincter."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which sequence correctly names the regions of the male urethra, starting at the bladder?\nA) Spongy, membranous, prostatic  |  B) Prostatic, spongy, membranous\nC) Membranous, prostatic, spongy  |  D) Prostatic, membranous, spongy\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Prostatic, membranous, spongy","why":"Each region of the male urethra is named for what it passes through. Leaving the bladder, it runs through the prostate gland as the prostatic urethra, then through the perineum as the short membranous urethra, and finally through the penis as the spongy (penile) urethra, which lies in the corpus spongiosum. The male urethra is longer than the female urethra and carries both urine and semen."},{"t":"mc","q":"Where is the trigone found?\nA) On the floor of the urinary bladder  |  B) Within the wall of the ureter\nC) At the tip of a renal pyramid  |  D) Inside the renal pelvis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) On the floor of the urinary bladder","why":"The trigone is a smooth triangular area on the floor (base) of the urinary bladder. Its three corners are the two ureteral openings, where urine enters, and the internal urethral orifice, where urine leaves. Unlike the rest of the bladder lining, it stays smooth rather than folding into rugae."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Trace urine from the collecting duct to the outside and identify the ureter, bladder wall layers, trigone, detrusor, sphincters, and male and female urethra. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace urine from the collecting duct to the outside and identify the ureter, bladder wall layers, trigone, detrusor, sphincters, and male and female urethra."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to trace urine from the collecting duct to the outside and identify the ureter, bladder wall layers, trigone, detrusor, sphincters, and male and female urethra. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: ureters, bladder, urethra and urine path. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, ureters, bladder, urethra and urine path, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w6-urinary-disorders","name":"Clinical anatomy of urinary disorders","can":"Relate urinary disorders such as nephroptosis, kidney stones, and UTI to the anatomical structure or pathway each one affects.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"After rapid, severe weight loss, a patient's kidney drops out of its normal position and kinks the ureter. Loss of which layer most likely allowed this?\nA) Renal capsule  |  B) Renal fascia\nC) Adipose capsule  |  D) Parietal peritoneum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Adipose capsule","why":"The adipose capsule is the layer of fat around the kidney that cushions it and helps hold it in place. When a person loses a large amount of body fat quickly, this fat pad can shrink, and the kidney may drop lower in the abdomen. This condition is called nephroptosis, or a floating kidney, and the dropped kidney can kink the ureter and block the flow of urine."},{"t":"mc","q":"After rapid weight loss, a patient's kidney loses its fat support and drops from its normal position, kinking the ureter. Which disorder is this?\nA) Renal failure  |  B) Glomerulonephritis\nC) Nephroptosis  |  D) Polycystic kidney disease\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Nephroptosis","why":"Nephroptosis, also called a floating kidney, happens when the kidney drops from its normal position because the adipose capsule that holds it in place is lost. As the kidney sags, the ureter can kink and block the flow of urine. The name means a falling kidney: nephro (kidney) plus ptosis (drooping)."},{"t":"mc","q":"A kidney stone forms in the renal pelvis and causes no pain until it moves into the next tube along the path of urine, only millimeters wide. Which tube is that?\nA) The urethra  |  B) The papillary duct\nC) The ureter  |  D) The collecting duct\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The ureter","why":"Urine leaves the renal pelvis through the ureter, a narrow muscular tube only millimeters wide. A stone sitting in the roomy renal pelvis may cause no symptoms, but once it enters the ureter it scrapes the wall and blocks the flow of urine, causing sharp pain. Knowing the path of urine tells you where the pain will be."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient's kidney filters are inflamed, and the kidneys cannot filter the blood properly. Testing shows the problem is in the capillary tufts of the nephrons. Which disorder fits?\nA) Kidney stones  |  B) Glomerulonephritis\nC) Urinary incontinence  |  D) Nephroptosis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Glomerulonephritis","why":"The capillary tufts of the nephrons are the glomeruli, so inflammation there is glomerulonephritis. Because the glomeruli are where the blood is filtered, inflaming them impairs filtration. Reading the name in pieces gives the answer: glomerulo (glomeruli), nephr (kidney), itis (inflammation)."},{"t":"mc","q":"Urinary tract infections are more common in women. Which structural feature is one reason for this?\nA) The female bladder is larger  |  B) The female ureters are wider\nC) The female kidneys sit lower  |  D) The female urethra is shorter\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The female urethra is shorter","why":"Most urinary tract infections start when bacteria enter through the urethra and climb up to the bladder. The female urethra is much shorter than the male urethra, so bacteria have a shorter distance to travel before they reach the bladder. The anatomy of the urethra helps predict who is at higher risk."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Relate urinary disorders such as nephroptosis, kidney stones, and UTI to the anatomical structure or pathway each one affects. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to relate urinary disorders such as nephroptosis, kidney stones, and UTI to the anatomical structure or pathway each one affects."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to relate urinary disorders such as nephroptosis, kidney stones, and UTI to the anatomical structure or pathway each one affects. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: clinical anatomy of urinary disorders. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, clinical anatomy of urinary disorders, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]}]},{"week":15,"system":"Reproductive","comps":[{"id":"w6-scrotum-testes","name":"Scrotum and testis structure","can":"Identify the scrotum, dartos and cremaster muscles, tunica layers, seminiferous tubules, sustentacular and interstitial cells, rete testis, and efferent ductules.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The testes hang in the scrotum, outside the main body cavity. Why is this outside position important?\nA) The scrotum shields the testes better than the abdomen does\nB) Sperm production needs a temperature above core body temperature\nC) The testes are too large to fit inside the pelvic cavity\nD) Sperm production needs a temperature below core body temperature\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Sperm production needs a temperature below core body temperature","why":"Sperm form properly only at a temperature a few degrees below the body core of 37°C (98.6°F). Hanging the testes in the scrotum keeps them cooler than they would be in the abdomen. The cremaster and dartos muscles fine-tune that temperature by moving the testes and tightening the scrotal skin."},{"t":"mc","q":"The seminiferous tubules are very long and tightly coiled inside each testis. Why is this coiled design useful?\nA) Coiling keeps the tubules warmer than the rest of the body\nB) Coiling packs a long sperm-making tube into a small space\nC) Coiling lets the tubules secrete most of the fluid in semen\nD) Coiling stores sperm until they gain the ability to swim\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Coiling packs a long sperm-making tube into a small space","why":"The testes produce huge numbers of sperm, and that takes a great deal of sperm-forming tissue. Coiling very long tubules tightly inside the lobules fits an enormous total length of tubule, and the lining that makes sperm, into a small organ. This is the same idea as the folds and coils seen in other organs that need a lot of working surface in a small space."},{"t":"mc","q":"On a cold day the testes are pulled up closer to the body, and on a warm day they hang lower. Why is this adjustment useful?\nA) It keeps the testes at full core body temperature\nB) It protects the testes from injury during movement\nC) It keeps the testes near the cooler temperature sperm need\nD) It moves sperm from the testes into the epididymis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It keeps the testes near the cooler temperature sperm need","why":"Sperm form best a few degrees below the body core, so the testes must stay slightly cool but not too cold. In the cold, the cremaster muscle pulls them toward the warm body, and in warm weather it relaxes so they hang lower and lose heat. The dartos helps by tightening or loosening the scrotal skin."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why do the testes sit in the scrotum, outside the abdominopelvic cavity?\nA) Testosterone can only enter the blood from outside the body cavity\nB) The scrotum shields the testes better than the bony pelvis could\nC) Sperm production needs a temperature a few degrees below the body core\nD) Sperm production needs a temperature a few degrees above the body core\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Sperm production needs a temperature a few degrees below the body core","why":"Sperm develop best at a temperature a few degrees below the body core temperature of 37°C (98.6°F). Hanging the testes in the scrotum keeps them cooler, and two muscles fine-tune that temperature: the cremaster raises or lowers the testes, and the dartos wrinkles the scrotal skin. When a testis fails to descend (cryptorchidism), the extra warmth can damage its sperm-forming cells if it is not corrected."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which pairing correctly matches the seam on the outside of the scrotum with the wall that divides it on the inside?\nA) Scrotal septum outside, raphe inside\nB) Dartos outside, cremaster inside\nC) Tunica vaginalis outside, albuginea inside\nD) Raphe outside, scrotal septum inside\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Raphe outside, scrotal septum inside","why":"The scrotum is a pouch of skin and subcutaneous tissue that holds the testes. A raphe, a ridge that looks like a seam, runs down the middle of its outer surface. Inside, the scrotal septum divides the pouch into two compartments, one for each testis, and the raphe on the surface marks where that septum lies underneath."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the scrotum, dartos and cremaster muscles, tunica layers, seminiferous tubules, sustentacular and interstitial cells, rete testis, and efferent ductules. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the scrotum, dartos and cremaster muscles, tunica layers, seminiferous tubules, sustentacular and interstitial cells, rete testis, and efferent ductules."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the scrotum, dartos and cremaster muscles, tunica layers, seminiferous tubules, sustentacular and interstitial cells, rete testis, and efferent ductules. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: scrotum and testis structure. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, scrotum and testis structure, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w6-sperm-path","name":"Sperm duct system and pathway","can":"Trace the path of sperm from the seminiferous tubules to the outside, naming the epididymis, ductus deferens, ejaculatory duct, urethra, and spermatic cord contents.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Starting at the testis, in what order do sperm reach these three places: the ejaculatory duct, the epididymis, and the inguinal canal?\nA) Inguinal canal, epididymis, ejaculatory duct\nB) Ejaculatory duct, epididymis, inguinal canal\nC) Epididymis, inguinal canal, ejaculatory duct\nD) Epididymis, ejaculatory duct, inguinal canal\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Epididymis, inguinal canal, ejaculatory duct","why":"Sperm first enter the epididymis on the back of the testis, inside the scrotum. The ductus deferens then carries them up in the spermatic cord, through the inguinal canal in the abdominal wall, and into the pelvis. Deep in the pelvis, behind the bladder, the ductus deferens joins the seminal vesicle duct to form the ejaculatory duct."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a vasectomy, a surgeon cuts and ties the duct that carries sperm upward from the scrotum in the spermatic cord. Which duct is cut?\nA) The epididymis  |  B) The ejaculatory duct\nC) The ductus deferens  |  D) The urethra\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The ductus deferens","why":"A vasectomy cuts the ductus deferens, also called the vas deferens, which is where the procedure gets its name. The duct is easy to reach because it runs in the spermatic cord just under the scrotal skin. Once it is cut, sperm cannot travel past the scrotum, but the accessory glands still add their fluid, so semen is still released without sperm."},{"t":"mc","q":"Sperm collected directly from the seminiferous tubules are fully formed but cannot swim. Which step have they not yet gone through?\nA) Maturing in the epididymis\nB) Passing through the ejaculatory duct\nC) Receiving fructose from the seminal vesicles\nD) Mixing with prostate fluid\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Maturing in the epididymis","why":"Sperm leave the seminiferous tubules with all their parts, but they gain the ability to move only after time in the epididymis. The epididymis stores them while they mature, so a reserve of motile sperm is ready at ejaculation. Sperm taken straight from the tubules have skipped this step."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which statement correctly describes the epididymis?\nA) A short duct through the prostate that empties into the urethra\nB) A pair of glands behind the bladder that make fructose-rich fluid\nC) A comma-shaped duct on the back of the testis that stores sperm\nD) A muscular duct in the spermatic cord that carries sperm upward\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A comma-shaped duct on the back of the testis that stores sperm","why":"The epididymis is a comma-shaped, tightly coiled duct that sits along the posterior side of each testis. Sperm that leave the testis are stored here and gain the ability to move, a process called maturation. It is the first duct of the male duct system, linking sperm production in the testis to the ductus deferens that carries sperm away."},{"t":"mc","q":"The ductus deferens widens just before it joins the duct of the seminal vesicle. What is this widened end called?\nA) The rete testis  |  B) The ampulla\nC) The bulb  |  D) The crus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The ampulla","why":"After passing through the inguinal canal, the ductus deferens runs into the pelvis, crosses over the ureter, and travels down the back of the urinary bladder. Its last stretch widens into the ampulla, a word that means flask. The ampulla then joins the duct of the seminal vesicle to form the ejaculatory duct."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Trace the path of sperm from the seminiferous tubules to the outside, naming the epididymis, ductus deferens, ejaculatory duct, urethra, and spermatic cord contents. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace the path of sperm from the seminiferous tubules to the outside, naming the epididymis, ductus deferens, ejaculatory duct, urethra, and spermatic cord contents."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to trace the path of sperm from the seminiferous tubules to the outside, naming the epididymis, ductus deferens, ejaculatory duct, urethra, and spermatic cord contents. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: sperm duct system and pathway. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, sperm duct system and pathway, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w6-sperm-cell","name":"Sperm cell structure","can":"Identify the head, acrosome, neck, middle piece, principal piece, and end piece of a sperm cell.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A sperm cell has a normal head and a normal middle piece, but it cannot drive itself forward. Which part is most likely defective?\nA) The principal piece  |  B) The acrosome\nC) The nucleus  |  D) The end piece\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The principal piece","why":"The principal piece is the longest part of the tail and does most of the whipping that moves the cell forward. The middle piece makes the ATP that powers the tail, and here it is normal. So a sperm that cannot swim forward points to a problem in the principal piece."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which list gives the parts of the sperm tail in order, from the head to the tip?\nA) Middle piece, neck, principal piece, end piece\nB) Neck, middle piece, principal piece, end piece\nC) Neck, principal piece, middle piece, end piece\nD) Neck, middle piece, end piece, principal piece\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Neck, middle piece, principal piece, end piece","why":"The tail is a long flagellum with four parts. The short neck sits just behind the head, then comes the middle piece packed with mitochondria, then the long principal piece, and finally the thin end piece at the tip."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which pairing of a sperm cell part with what it holds is correct?\nA) Middle piece: the enzymes  |  B) End piece: the mitochondria\nC) Acrosome: the chromosomes  |  D) Neck: the centrioles\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Neck: the centrioles","why":"Each part of a sperm cell holds something specific. The head holds the nucleus, capped by the enzyme-filled acrosome. The neck holds the centrioles, the middle piece is packed with mitochondria, and the principal and end pieces make up the rest of the tail."},{"t":"mc","q":"On a sperm smear, which part is the dark, densely stained oval at the front, and what makes up most of it?\nA) The middle piece, which is mostly mitochondria\nB) The head, which is mostly the nucleus\nC) The neck, which is mostly centrioles\nD) The acrosome, which is mostly mitochondria\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The head, which is mostly the nucleus","why":"On a sperm smear, the head stains as a dark, dense oval and the tail is the long thin line behind it. The head is a flattened oval made mostly of the nucleus, which holds 23 chromosomes, with almost no cytoplasm. The acrosome forms a cap over the front of that nucleus."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which part of a sperm cell is packed with mitochondria that power its movement?\nA) The middle piece  |  B) The acrosome\nC) The end piece  |  D) The head\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The middle piece","why":"The middle piece is the part of the tail just behind the neck, and it is packed with mitochondria, the organelles that make ATP, the energy molecule cells run on. That energy drives the whipping motion of the rest of the tail. In order, the tail runs from the neck to the middle piece, then the principal piece, and finally the end piece."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the head, acrosome, neck, middle piece, principal piece, and end piece of a sperm cell. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the head, acrosome, neck, middle piece, principal piece, and end piece of a sperm cell."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the head, acrosome, neck, middle piece, principal piece, and end piece of a sperm cell. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: sperm cell structure. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, sperm cell structure, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w6-male-glands-penis","name":"Accessory glands and penis","can":"Name the seminal vesicles, prostate, and bulbourethral glands and identify the root, body, glans, prepuce, and three erectile columns of the penis.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which statement correctly describes the prostate?\nA) A pair of glands behind the bladder that make fructose-rich fluid\nB) A pair of pea-sized glands that release mucus into the urethra\nC) A single coiled duct on the testis where sperm are stored\nD) A single gland below the bladder that surrounds the urethra\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) A single gland below the bladder that surrounds the urethra","why":"The prostate is a single gland, about the size of a walnut, that sits just below the urinary bladder and wraps around the first part of the urethra, the prostatic urethra. It adds a milky fluid that helps sperm stay active. Because the urethra passes straight through it, an enlarged prostate can squeeze the urethra and slow urine flow."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does the mucus from the bulbourethral glands do?\nA) Keeps sperm active with a milky fluid\nB) Lubricates the urethra and neutralizes leftover urine\nC) Supplies the fructose that sperm use for energy\nD) Shields developing sperm from the blood supply\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Lubricates the urethra and neutralizes leftover urine","why":"The bulbourethral glands, also called Cowper's glands, are pea-sized glands just below the prostate. Before ejaculation they release a clear, alkaline mucus into the spongy urethra, where it lubricates the passage and neutralizes traces of acidic urine. Alkaline means slightly basic, the opposite of acidic."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which paired glands lie just below the prostate in the male?\nA) The greater vestibular glands  |  B) The testes\nC) The seminal vesicles  |  D) The bulbourethral glands\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The bulbourethral glands","why":"The bulbourethral glands, also called Cowper's glands, are a pair of pea-sized glands just inferior to the prostate, on either side of the membranous urethra. Their ducts open into the spongy urethra, where they release a clear alkaline mucus. Going from top to bottom, the order is bladder, prostate, then the bulbourethral glands."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which structure expands at the tip of the penis to form the glans?\nA) The spongy urethra  |  B) The corpora cavernosa\nC) The corpus spongiosum  |  D) The prepuce\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The corpus spongiosum","why":"The corpus spongiosum is the single ventral column of erectile tissue that surrounds the spongy urethra. At the distal end of the penis it expands into the glans penis, which is why the urethra opens at the tip of the glans. At the other end, in the root, it expands into the bulb."},{"t":"mc","q":"Into which region of the urethra do the bulbourethral glands release their mucus?\nA) The prostatic urethra  |  B) The ejaculatory duct\nC) The membranous urethra  |  D) The spongy urethra\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The spongy urethra","why":"The bulbourethral glands sit on either side of the membranous urethra, just below the prostate, but their small ducts run forward and open into the spongy urethra. Their clear, alkaline mucus lubricates this part of the urethra and neutralizes traces of acidic urine. So the glands lie beside one region and empty into the next one downstream."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Name the seminal vesicles, prostate, and bulbourethral glands and identify the root, body, glans, prepuce, and three erectile columns of the penis. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to name the seminal vesicles, prostate, and bulbourethral glands and identify the root, body, glans, prepuce, and three erectile columns of the penis."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to name the seminal vesicles, prostate, and bulbourethral glands and identify the root, body, glans, prepuce, and three erectile columns of the penis. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: accessory glands and penis. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, accessory glands and penis, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w6-male-disorders","name":"Clinical anatomy of male disorders","can":"Relate male reproductive disorders such as cryptorchidism and benign prostatic hyperplasia to the affected structure and its position.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A 68-year-old man has a weak urine stream because a non-cancerous growth of a gland is squeezing his urethra just below the bladder. Which gland is enlarged?\nA) The prostate  |  B) The bulbourethral gland\nC) The epididymis  |  D) The seminal vesicle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The prostate","why":"The prostate sits just below the bladder and wraps around the prostatic urethra. In benign prostatic hyperplasia, a non-cancerous enlargement common in older men, the growing gland presses inward on the urethra and narrows it. That is how a reproductive gland causes a urinary symptom like a weak stream."},{"t":"mc","q":"During a self-exam, a 25-year-old man finds a firm lump in one testis. If the lump is malignant, which disorder is it?\nA) Hydrocele  |  B) Prostate cancer\nC) Testicular cancer  |  D) Varicocele\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Testicular cancer","why":"Testicular cancer is a malignant tumor of the testis, usually starting in the sperm-forming cells of the seminiferous tubules. Because the testes hang in the scrotum outside the body, a man can feel them directly, which is why self-exams are taught. A firm lump in the testis itself is the warning sign."},{"t":"mc","q":"In cryptorchidism, an undescended testis stays at core body temperature. Which cells are at risk if it is not corrected?\nA) The erectile tissue of the penis\nB) The mucus glands beside the urethra\nC) The sperm-forming cells of the testis\nD) The fluid-secreting cells of the prostate\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The sperm-forming cells of the testis","why":"Sperm production needs a temperature a few degrees below the body core, which is why the testes normally hang in the scrotum. An undescended testis stays inside at core body temperature, and if the problem is not corrected, the warmth can damage the cells that form sperm. The problem is local to the testis that did not descend."},{"t":"mc","q":"An older man's enlarged prostate makes urination difficult. Which tube is the prostate squeezing?\nA) The ureter  |  B) The ductus deferens\nC) The urethra  |  D) The ejaculatory duct\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The urethra","why":"The male urethra passes straight through the prostate just below the bladder. When the prostate enlarges, it presses inward on this part of the urethra and narrows it, so urine has trouble flowing out. That is how a reproductive gland causes a urinary symptom."},{"t":"mc","q":"A man's prostate is enlarged and squeezing his urethra, but a biopsy shows no cancer. What is this condition called?\nA) Varicocele  |  B) Benign prostatic hyperplasia\nC) Prostate cancer  |  D) Hydrocele\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Benign prostatic hyperplasia","why":"Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) is a non-cancerous enlargement of the prostate. Benign means not cancer, and hyperplasia means growth from an increase in the number of cells. Because the prostate surrounds the urethra, the enlarged gland squeezes it and makes urination difficult."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Relate male reproductive disorders such as cryptorchidism and benign prostatic hyperplasia to the affected structure and its position. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to relate male reproductive disorders such as cryptorchidism and benign prostatic hyperplasia to the affected structure and its position."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to relate male reproductive disorders such as cryptorchidism and benign prostatic hyperplasia to the affected structure and its position. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: clinical anatomy of male disorders. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, clinical anatomy of male disorders, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w6-ovary","name":"Ovary structure and follicle stages","can":"Identify the ovary layers, ligaments, and follicle stages including the corpus luteum and corpus albicans.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which list contains only organs of the female reproductive system?\nA) Ovaries, uterine tubes, uterus, vagina\nB) Ovaries, uterine tubes, uterus, urinary bladder\nC) Ovaries, ductus deferens, uterus, vagina\nD) Ovaries, uterine tubes, rectum, vagina\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Ovaries, uterine tubes, uterus, vagina","why":"The female reproductive system is built from the ovaries (the gonads), the genital tract that carries the oocyte and receives sperm (uterine tubes, uterus, and vagina), the external genitalia (the vulva), and the mammary glands. Every organ in the correct list belongs to that system. Each of the other lists slips in one neighbor from the pelvis or from the male system."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which order correctly lists the layers of the ovary from the surface inward?\nA) Germinal epithelium, tunica albuginea, cortex, medulla\nB) Germinal epithelium, cortex, tunica albuginea, medulla\nC) Germinal epithelium, tunica albuginea, medulla, cortex\nD) Tunica albuginea, germinal epithelium, cortex, medulla\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Germinal epithelium, tunica albuginea, cortex, medulla","why":"The ovary is read from the outside in. Its surface is covered by the germinal epithelium, a single layer of low cuboidal cells. Just beneath it is the tunica albuginea, a whitish capsule of dense connective tissue. Deep to the capsule is the cortex, which holds the follicles, and at the center is the medulla, the looser connective tissue that carries blood vessels and nerves."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which ligament attaches the ovary to the lateral pelvic wall and carries the ovarian artery and vein?\nA) The round ligament  |  B) The uterosacral ligament\nC) The suspensory ligament  |  D) The ovarian ligament\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The suspensory ligament","why":"The suspensory ligament is the fold that holds the ovary to the lateral wall of the pelvis. The ovarian artery and vein travel in this fold to reach the ovary, which is why it is the ligament to look for when you trace the ovarian vessels on a model."},{"t":"mc","q":"The broad ligament attaches to the ovary by which portion?\nA) The suspensory ligament  |  B) The ovarian ligament\nC) The mesovarium  |  D) The round ligament\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The mesovarium","why":"The broad ligament is the fold of peritoneum draped over the uterus and tubes. The part of it that attaches to the ovary is called the mesovarium. The prefix meso- is used for peritoneal folds that suspend an organ, just as the mesentery suspends the small intestine."},{"t":"mc","q":"After ovulation, which glandular body forms in the ovary from the granulosa and theca cells left behind?\nA) The corpus albicans  |  B) The corpus luteum\nC) The ovarian medulla  |  D) The germinal epithelium\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The corpus luteum","why":"At ovulation the mature follicle ruptures and releases the secondary oocyte. The granulosa and theca cells left behind become the corpus luteum, the yellow body, a large glandular body. If no pregnancy occurs, it later breaks down into the corpus albicans, a pale scar."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the ovary layers, ligaments, and follicle stages including the corpus luteum and corpus albicans. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the ovary layers, ligaments, and follicle stages including the corpus luteum and corpus albicans."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the ovary layers, ligaments, and follicle stages including the corpus luteum and corpus albicans. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: ovary structure and follicle stages. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, ovary structure and follicle stages, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w6-ovum-path","name":"Uterine tubes and ovum pathway","can":"Trace the oocyte from the ovary to the uterus, naming the fimbriae, infundibulum, ampulla, and isthmus.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"In pelvic inflammatory disease, infection can climb from the vagina through the uterus and tubes and reach the pelvic cavity. Which structure provides that open route into the cavity?\nA) The isthmus of the uterine tube  |  B) The external os of the cervix\nC) The infundibulum of the tube  |  D) The fornix of the vagina\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The infundibulum of the tube","why":"The infundibulum opens into the pelvic cavity near the ovary instead of sealing onto it. That opening is how the fimbriae catch the oocyte, but it also leaves an open path from the outside into the pelvic cavity: vagina, cervix, uterus, uterine tube, and out through the infundibulum. An infection can climb that same route."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which list traces an oocyte from the ovary to the uterus?\nA) Ovary, infundibulum, isthmus, ampulla, uterus\nB) Ovary, ampulla, infundibulum, isthmus, uterus\nC) Ovary, isthmus, ampulla, infundibulum, uterus\nD) Ovary, infundibulum, ampulla, isthmus, uterus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Ovary, infundibulum, ampulla, isthmus, uterus","why":"At ovulation the oocyte leaves the ovary, and the fimbriae sweep it into the infundibulum, the funnel at the open end of the tube. It moves into the ampulla, the wide part where fertilization usually happens, then through the narrow isthmus. The isthmus joins the uterus, so the oocyte or early embryo enters the uterine cavity last."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which order lists the regions of the uterine tube from the end near the ovary to the end at the uterus?\nA) Infundibulum, ampulla, isthmus  |  B) Ampulla, infundibulum, isthmus\nC) Infundibulum, isthmus, ampulla  |  D) Isthmus, ampulla, infundibulum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Infundibulum, ampulla, isthmus","why":"The uterine tube begins at the infundibulum, the funnel-shaped end that opens into the pelvic cavity beside the ovary and is fringed by the fimbriae. Next comes the ampulla, the widest and longest part, where fertilization usually happens. The tube ends at the isthmus, the narrow, thick-walled segment that joins the uterus."},{"t":"mc","q":"An infection of the upper reproductive tract spreads through the uterus and tubes and can scar the uterine tubes. What is this disorder called?\nA) Endometriosis  |  B) Cervical cancer\nC) Pelvic inflammatory disease  |  D) Ovarian cysts\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Pelvic inflammatory disease","why":"Pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) is an infection that climbs from the vagina through the cervix, uterus, and uterine tubes into the pelvic cavity. It can reach the pelvic cavity because the infundibulum is open and does not seal onto the ovary. The infection can leave scarring that narrows a uterine tube. The key word in the question is infection."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which sequence correctly traces the oocyte from ovulation until it enters the uterine tube?\nA) Ovary, then infundibulum, then fimbriae\nB) Fimbriae, then ovary, then infundibulum\nC) Infundibulum, then ovary, then fimbriae\nD) Ovary, then fimbriae, then infundibulum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Ovary, then fimbriae, then infundibulum","why":"At ovulation the mature follicle releases the oocyte from the surface of the ovary. The tube is not attached to the ovary, so the fimbriae sweep over the ovary and draw the oocyte toward the tube. The oocyte then enters the funnel-shaped infundibulum, the opening of the uterine tube."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Trace the oocyte from the ovary to the uterus, naming the fimbriae, infundibulum, ampulla, and isthmus. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace the oocyte from the ovary to the uterus, naming the fimbriae, infundibulum, ampulla, and isthmus."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to trace the oocyte from the ovary to the uterus, naming the fimbriae, infundibulum, ampulla, and isthmus. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: uterine tubes and ovum pathway. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, uterine tubes and ovum pathway, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w6-uterus","name":"Uterus regions, ligaments, and wall","can":"Name the regions and supporting ligaments of the uterus and identify the perimetrium, myometrium, and endometrium.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A non-cancerous tumor of smooth muscle is found in the rounded top of the uterus, above where the uterine tubes enter. Which layer and region of the uterus is it in?\nA) The myometrium of the fundus  |  B) The perimetrium of the cervix\nC) The myometrium of the cervix  |  D) The endometrium of the body\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The myometrium of the fundus","why":"Uterine fibroids are non-cancerous tumors of the smooth muscle of the myometrium. The fundus is the rounded top of the uterus, above the points where the uterine tubes enter, and it is also where the myometrium is thickest. So a smooth muscle tumor at the top lies in the myometrium of the fundus."},{"t":"mc","q":"The cervical canal runs through the cervix. At which opening does the canal open upward into the uterine cavity?\nA) The vaginal orifice  |  B) The external os\nC) The vaginal fornix  |  D) The internal os\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The internal os","why":"The cervical canal is the passage through the cervix, the narrow lower part of the uterus. Its upper opening, the internal os, leads into the uterine cavity inside the body of the uterus. Its lower opening, the external os, leads into the vagina. Os means mouth or opening."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which paired ligaments connect the uterus posteriorly to the sacrum?\nA) The round ligaments  |  B) The broad ligaments\nC) The cardinal ligaments  |  D) The uterosacral ligaments\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The uterosacral ligaments","why":"The uterosacral ligaments run from the cervix, the lower part of the uterus, back to the sacrum. They anchor the uterus from behind. Each uterine ligament is named for what it connects: the round ligaments reach the labia majora, the cardinal ligaments reach the lateral pelvic walls, and the broad ligaments are folds of peritoneum out to the lateral pelvic walls."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which pairing of a uterine wall layer with its tissue is correct?\nA) Endometrium and serous membrane  |  B) Perimetrium and smooth muscle\nC) Myometrium and serous membrane  |  D) Myometrium and smooth muscle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Myometrium and smooth muscle","why":"The uterine wall has three layers. The perimetrium on the outside is a serous membrane, part of the visceral peritoneum. The myometrium in the middle is the thick layer of smooth muscle, and myo means muscle. The endometrium on the inside is the mucous lining that sheds and rebuilds each cycle."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which peritoneal pouch lies between the uterus and the rectum and forms the lowest point of the peritoneal cavity in females?\nA) The rectouterine pouch  |  B) The uterine cavity\nC) The vaginal fornix  |  D) The vesicouterine pouch\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The rectouterine pouch","why":"The peritoneum dips down between the pelvic organs to form pouches. Behind the uterus it drops between the uterus and the rectum to form the rectouterine pouch, also called the pouch of Douglas. This is the lowest part of the peritoneal cavity in females, so fluid, blood, or pus in the abdomen tends to collect there."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Name the regions and supporting ligaments of the uterus and identify the perimetrium, myometrium, and endometrium. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to name the regions and supporting ligaments of the uterus and identify the perimetrium, myometrium, and endometrium."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to name the regions and supporting ligaments of the uterus and identify the perimetrium, myometrium, and endometrium. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: uterus regions, ligaments, and wall. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, uterus regions, ligaments, and wall, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w6-vagina-vulva","name":"Vagina and external genitalia","can":"Identify the vagina, fornix, hymen, and the vulvar structures including the clitoris, vestibule, labia, and greater vestibular glands.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The ovary is the female gonad. Which male organ is its homolog, the structure that develops from the same embryonic tissue?\nA) The prostate  |  B) The scrotum\nC) The epididymis  |  D) The testis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The testis","why":"Homologous structures develop from the same embryonic tissue in males and females, even though they look different in the adult. The ovaries and the testes are both gonads, the primary reproductive organs that make the gametes (oocytes or sperm) and secrete sex hormones. That shared role and origin is why they are counted as homologs."},{"t":"mc","q":"The greater vestibular glands secrete lubricating mucus beside the vaginal opening. Which male glands are their homologs?\nA) The seminal glands  |  B) The sebaceous glands\nC) The bulbourethral glands  |  D) The prostate gland\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The bulbourethral glands","why":"Homologs are male and female structures that develop from the same embryonic tissue. The greater vestibular (Bartholin's) glands and the bulbourethral (Cowper's) glands are both small, paired glands near the lower end of the tract that release mucus during arousal. Their matching position and mucus secretion reflect their shared origin."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which two openings are found in the vestibule of the vulva?\nA) The urethra and the vagina  |  B) The urethra and the anus\nC) The cervix and the urethra  |  D) The vagina and the anus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The urethra and the vagina","why":"The vestibule is the area enclosed by the labia minora. Two openings sit in it: the urethral orifice in front and the vaginal orifice behind it. The anus is close by but lies outside the vestibule, in the anal triangle of the perineum."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which female external structure is homologous to the penis?\nA) The vestibule  |  B) The clitoris\nC) The mons pubis  |  D) The labia majora\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The clitoris","why":"Homologous structures develop from the same embryonic tissue. The clitoris and the penis both form from the same embryonic swelling and both contain erectile tissue that fills with blood during arousal. That is why the clitoris is the female homolog of the penis."},{"t":"mc","q":"Within the vestibule, how is the urethral orifice positioned relative to the vaginal orifice?\nA) Lateral to the vaginal orifice  |  B) Posterior to the vaginal orifice\nC) Anterior to the vaginal orifice  |  D) Inside the vaginal orifice\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Anterior to the vaginal orifice","why":"Both the urethra and the vagina open into the vestibule, the area between the labia minora. The urethral orifice lies in front, between the clitoris and the vaginal orifice, and the vaginal orifice lies behind it. The anus is farther back still, outside the vestibule."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the vagina, fornix, hymen, and the vulvar structures including the clitoris, vestibule, labia, and greater vestibular glands. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the vagina, fornix, hymen, and the vulvar structures including the clitoris, vestibule, labia, and greater vestibular glands."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the vagina, fornix, hymen, and the vulvar structures including the clitoris, vestibule, labia, and greater vestibular glands. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: vagina and external genitalia. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, vagina and external genitalia, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w6-mammary","name":"Mammary gland structure","can":"Identify the lobes, lobules, alveoli, lactiferous ducts, nipple, areola, and suspensory ligaments of the mammary gland.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which order lists the parts of the mammary gland from the largest division down to the milk-secreting sac?\nA) Lobes, alveoli, lobules  |  B) Lobes, lobules, alveoli\nC) Lobules, lobes, alveoli  |  D) Alveoli, lobules, lobes\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Lobes, lobules, alveoli","why":"The mammary gland is nested. Each breast has 15 to 20 lobes, each lobe is divided into smaller lobules, and each lobule holds clusters of alveoli, the small sacs that secrete milk. Going from largest to smallest runs lobes, lobules, alveoli."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which sequence traces milk from where it is made to where it leaves the breast?\nA) Alveoli, lactiferous sinus, lobule ducts, lactiferous duct, nipple\nB) Lactiferous duct, alveoli, lobule ducts, lactiferous sinus, nipple\nC) Alveoli, lobule ducts, lactiferous duct, lactiferous sinus, nipple\nD) Areola, lactiferous sinus, lactiferous duct, alveoli, nipple\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Alveoli, lobule ducts, lactiferous duct, lactiferous sinus, nipple","why":"Milk is secreted by the gland cells of the alveoli inside each lobule. Small ducts carry it out of the lobule into the one lactiferous duct that drains that lobe. Just before the nipple the duct widens into a lactiferous sinus where milk collects, and then it leaves through openings on the nipple."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which ligaments support the breast by running through the mammary tissue to the skin?\nA) The round ligaments  |  B) The uterosacral ligaments\nC) The broad ligaments  |  D) The suspensory ligaments\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The suspensory ligaments","why":"The suspensory ligaments (Cooper ligaments) are bands of connective tissue that run from the skin through the breast, between the lobes, to the fascia over the pectoralis major. They support the breast from within. A tumor that pulls on one of these ligaments can dimple the skin above it."},{"t":"mc","q":"What are the milk-producing glands of the breast called?\nA) The seminal glands  |  B) The greater vestibular glands\nC) The bulbourethral glands  |  D) The mammary glands\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The mammary glands","why":"The mammary glands are the milk-producing glands of the breasts. They lie over the chest muscles and are built from lobes, lobules, and alveoli, with lactiferous ducts carrying milk to the nipple. They are studied with the female reproductive system because they feed the newborn."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the circular pigmented area of skin around the nipple called?\nA) The nipple  |  B) The lobule\nC) The alveolus  |  D) The areola\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The areola","why":"The areola is the circle of pigmented skin that surrounds the nipple. The nipple itself is the raised projection in the center, where the lactiferous ducts open. Both are on the surface of the breast, while lobules and alveoli are deep inside the gland."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the lobes, lobules, alveoli, lactiferous ducts, nipple, areola, and suspensory ligaments of the mammary gland. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the lobes, lobules, alveoli, lactiferous ducts, nipple, areola, and suspensory ligaments of the mammary gland."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the lobes, lobules, alveoli, lactiferous ducts, nipple, areola, and suspensory ligaments of the mammary gland. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: mammary gland structure. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, mammary gland structure, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w6-fertilization-implant","name":"Fertilization and implantation","can":"Order the events from zygote through cleavage, morula, and blastocyst to implantation, naming where each occurs.","items":[{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Order the events from zygote through cleavage, morula, and blastocyst to implantation, naming where each occurs. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to order the events from zygote through cleavage, morula, and blastocyst to implantation, naming where each occurs."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to order the events from zygote through cleavage, morula, and blastocyst to implantation, naming where each occurs. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: fertilization and implantation. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, fertilization and implantation, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w6-placenta-membranes","name":"Placenta and fetal membranes","can":"Name the placenta, umbilical cord vessels, amnion, chorion, and yolk sac and describe what each contributes.","items":[{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Name the placenta, umbilical cord vessels, amnion, chorion, and yolk sac and describe what each contributes. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to name the placenta, umbilical cord vessels, amnion, chorion, and yolk sac and describe what each contributes."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to name the placenta, umbilical cord vessels, amnion, chorion, and yolk sac and describe what each contributes. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: placenta and fetal membranes. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, placenta and fetal membranes, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w6-gravid-labor","name":"Gravid uterus and stages of labor","can":"Describe the gravid uterus, fetal vertex and breech positions, and order the three stages of labor.","items":[{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Describe the gravid uterus, fetal vertex and breech positions, and order the three stages of labor. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to describe the gravid uterus, fetal vertex and breech positions, and order the three stages of labor."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to describe the gravid uterus, fetal vertex and breech positions, and order the three stages of labor. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: gravid uterus and stages of labor. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, gravid uterus and stages of labor, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]}]},{"week":11,"system":"Endocrine","comps":[{"id":"w6-endocrine-locate","name":"Major endocrine glands and locations","can":"Name the major endocrine glands and locate each one in the body from the hypothalamus to the gonads.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A pituitary tumor enlarges and presses upward on the optic chiasm, causing loss of peripheral vision. What anatomical feature forces the tumor to expand upward?\nA) The gland lies in the third ventricle\nB) The gland has two lobes of different tissues\nC) The gland hangs from the infundibulum\nD) The gland is boxed inside the bony sella turcica\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The gland is boxed inside the bony sella turcica","why":"The pituitary gland sits in the sella turcica, a pocket of the sphenoid bone with a bony floor and bony front and back walls. When a pituitary tumor grows, that bone holds it in below, in front, and behind, so it usually expands upward. Just above the pituitary lies the optic chiasm, and pressure there can cause a loss of peripheral vision."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient develops a goiter, a swelling visible at the front of the neck. Which gland has enlarged?\nA) The thyroid gland  |  B) The thymus\nC) The adrenal gland  |  D) The pituitary gland\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The thyroid gland","why":"The thyroid gland lies in the anterior neck just under the skin, below the larynx and wrapped around the trachea. Because it is so superficial, an enlarged thyroid, called a goiter, shows as a visible and palpable swelling at the front of the neck."},{"t":"mc","q":"An imaging study ordered to view a kidney also shows a mass sitting on top of it. Which endocrine gland was imaged by chance?\nA) The thyroid gland  |  B) The pineal gland\nC) The adrenal gland  |  D) The thymus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The adrenal gland","why":"Each adrenal gland rests on the superior pole of a kidney. Because the two organs are so close, imaging ordered for the kidney usually captures the adrenal too, and an adrenal mass is sometimes discovered by accident this way."},{"t":"mc","q":"During thyroid surgery, the surgeon carefully spares several tiny glands on the back surface of the thyroid. Which glands are they?\nA) The pancreatic islets  |  B) The adrenal glands\nC) The thyroid follicles  |  D) The parathyroid glands\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The parathyroid glands","why":"The parathyroid glands, usually four, are tiny glands on the posterior surface of the thyroid lobes, each in its own thin capsule. The thyroid's position just under the skin of the anterior neck makes surgery on it accessible, but because the parathyroids sit on its back, the surgeon must find and protect them."},{"t":"mc","q":"A gland wraps around the trachea just below the larynx and has two lateral lobes joined across the front. Which gland is described?\nA) The adrenal glands  |  B) The parathyroid glands\nC) The thymus  |  D) The thyroid gland\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The thyroid gland","why":"The thyroid gland sits in the anterior neck, just below the larynx. Its two lateral lobes lie against the trachea, and the isthmus joins them across the front. The parathyroid glands are on its posterior surface, and the thymus is lower down, in the chest."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Name the major endocrine glands and locate each one in the body from the hypothalamus to the gonads. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to name the major endocrine glands and locate each one in the body from the hypothalamus to the gonads."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to name the major endocrine glands and locate each one in the body from the hypothalamus to the gonads. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: major endocrine glands and locations. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, major endocrine glands and locations, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w6-pituitary","name":"Pituitary gross anatomy and lobes","can":"Describe the pituitary in the sella turcica, its infundibulum link to the hypothalamus, and compare the anterior and posterior lobes by tissue type.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A pituitary tumor grows upward out of the sella turcica. Which structure, lying just above and in front of the gland, is it most likely to press on?\nA) The hypophyseal portal veins  |  B) The optic chiasm\nC) The body of the sphenoid bone  |  D) The nasal cavity\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The optic chiasm.","why":"The pituitary sits in the sella turcica, a saddle-shaped pocket of the sphenoid bone. The bone forms the floor and the front and back walls of the pocket, so the open side faces up. The optic chiasm lies just above and in front of the gland. A tumor that grows up out of the pocket meets the optic chiasm first."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which statement correctly compares how the two pituitary lobes connect to the hypothalamus?\nA) Both lobes by a nerve fiber tract\nB) Anterior by a nerve fiber tract; posterior by portal blood vessels\nC) Both lobes by the hypophyseal portal system\nD) Anterior by portal blood vessels; posterior by a nerve fiber tract\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Anterior by portal blood vessels; posterior by a nerve fiber tract","why":"The type of tissue in each lobe tells you how it connects to the hypothalamus. The anterior lobe is glandular epithelium, so the hypothalamus reaches it through blood, using a set of vessels called the hypophyseal portal system. The posterior lobe is nervous tissue, an extension of the hypothalamus, so it is connected by a tract of nerve fibers running down through the infundibulum."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which structure is nervous tissue that is a direct extension of the hypothalamus itself?\nA) The posterior lobe of the pituitary  |  B) The adrenal medulla\nC) The pineal gland  |  D) The anterior lobe of the pituitary\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The posterior lobe of the pituitary","why":"The posterior lobe of the pituitary, the neurohypophysis, is nervous tissue that grows down from the hypothalamus itself. Axons from hypothalamic neurons run through the infundibulum and end in the posterior lobe, where their hormones are stored. That is why its link to the hypothalamus is a nerve tract rather than blood vessels."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which structure carries hypothalamic signals to the anterior lobe of the pituitary?\nA) A tract of nerve fibers through the infundibulum\nB) The cerebrospinal fluid of the third ventricle\nC) The sella turcica\nD) The hypophyseal portal system\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The hypophyseal portal system","why":"The anterior lobe is glandular tissue, so the hypothalamus reaches it through the blood. The hypophyseal portal system is a set of vessels in which blood passes through capillaries in the hypothalamus, then travels down to a second capillary bed in the anterior lobe. This carries hypothalamic signals straight to the anterior pituitary without first passing through the rest of the body."},{"t":"mc","q":"A gland lies in the sella turcica of the sphenoid bone, hanging from a stalk below the hypothalamus. Which gland is it?\nA) The pineal gland  |  B) The pituitary gland\nC) The hypothalamus  |  D) The thyroid gland\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The pituitary gland","why":"The pituitary gland is identified by its position. It sits in the sella turcica of the sphenoid bone and hangs from the hypothalamus by a stalk called the infundibulum. No other endocrine gland lies in the skull base, so the sella turcica alone points to the pituitary."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Describe the pituitary in the sella turcica, its infundibulum link to the hypothalamus, and compare the anterior and posterior lobes by tissue type. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to describe the pituitary in the sella turcica, its infundibulum link to the hypothalamus, and compare the anterior and posterior lobes by tissue type."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to describe the pituitary in the sella turcica, its infundibulum link to the hypothalamus, and compare the anterior and posterior lobes by tissue type. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: pituitary gross anatomy and lobes. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, pituitary gross anatomy and lobes, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w6-thyroid-parathyroid","name":"Thyroid and parathyroid anatomy","can":"Identify the thyroid lobes, isthmus, follicles, colloid, follicular and parafollicular cells, and the parathyroid chief and oxyphil cells.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Unlike most endocrine glands, the thyroid stores its product in bulk outside its cells. Where is it stored?\nA) In the parathyroid glands on its back surface\nB) In the isthmus that joins the two lobes\nC) Inside the parafollicular cells\nD) In the colloid at the center of each follicle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) In the colloid at the center of each follicle","why":"Each thyroid follicle is a hollow sphere of follicular cells surrounding a central space filled with colloid. The thyroid stores the material it uses to make its hormones in this colloid, outside the cells, which is unusual among endocrine glands. This store lets the gland hold a large reserve."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which two cell types are found in the thyroid gland?\nA) Follicular cells and parafollicular cells  |  B) Alpha cells and beta cells\nC) Chief cells and follicular cells  |  D) Chromaffin cells and chief cells\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Follicular cells and parafollicular cells","why":"The thyroid gland is made of microscopic hollow spheres called follicles. Follicular cells form the wall of each follicle and surround the stored colloid. Parafollicular cells, also called C cells, are scattered in the spaces between the follicles."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a thyroid follicle, which cells form the wall around the colloid?\nA) Chief cells  |  B) Parafollicular cells\nC) Chromaffin cells  |  D) Follicular cells\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Follicular cells","why":"The thyroid is built of microscopic hollow spheres called follicles. A single layer of follicular cells, a simple cuboidal epithelium, forms the wall of each follicle, and the center is filled with colloid, a protein-rich fluid that stains pink. Parafollicular (C) cells sit between follicles or at the outer edge of a follicle wall, and they never touch the colloid."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the isthmus of the thyroid gland?\nA) The bridge of tissue connecting the two thyroid lobes\nB) The microscopic hollow spheres that make up the gland\nC) The protein-rich fluid stored inside each follicle\nD) A small gland on the back of the thyroid\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The bridge of tissue connecting the two thyroid lobes","why":"The thyroid gland has two lateral lobes, one on each side of the trachea. The isthmus is the narrow bridge of thyroid tissue that crosses the front of the trachea and joins the two lobes, giving the gland its butterfly or H shape."},{"t":"mc","q":"What are the thyroid follicles?\nA) The connective tissue capsule around the gland\nB) The four small glands behind the thyroid\nC) The microscopic hollow spheres that make up the thyroid\nD) The endocrine cell clusters of the pancreas\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The microscopic hollow spheres that make up the thyroid","why":"The thyroid gland is built of thousands of microscopic hollow spheres called follicles. Each follicle has a wall of follicular cells around a center filled with colloid, the protein-rich fluid where the thyroid stores its product. Parafollicular cells sit in the spaces between follicles."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the thyroid lobes, isthmus, follicles, colloid, follicular and parafollicular cells, and the parathyroid chief and oxyphil cells. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the thyroid lobes, isthmus, follicles, colloid, follicular and parafollicular cells, and the parathyroid chief and oxyphil cells."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the thyroid lobes, isthmus, follicles, colloid, follicular and parafollicular cells, and the parathyroid chief and oxyphil cells. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: thyroid and parathyroid anatomy. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, thyroid and parathyroid anatomy, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w6-adrenal","name":"Adrenal gland zones","can":"Identify the adrenal cortex zones in order and the adrenal medulla with its chromaffin cells.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"An adrenal cortex zone is the thick middle zone, with its cells arranged in straight columns. Which zone is it?\nA) Adrenal medulla  |  B) Zona reticularis\nC) Zona fasciculata  |  D) Zona glomerulosa\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Zona fasciculata","why":"The adrenal cortex has three zones. The zona fasciculata is the middle zone and the widest of the three, and its cells line up in long straight columns, which is what fasciculata (bundled) describes. It sits between the thin outer zona glomerulosa and the inner zona reticularis."},{"t":"mc","q":"A part of the adrenal gland is modified nervous tissue forming the inner core. Which part is it?\nA) The adrenal cortex  |  B) The adrenal medulla\nC) The adrenal capsule  |  D) The zona glomerulosa\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The adrenal medulla","why":"Each adrenal gland is two glands in one. The outer adrenal cortex is glandular epithelial tissue arranged in three zones, and the inner adrenal medulla is modified nervous tissue belonging to the sympathetic nervous system. The medulla is made of chromaffin cells, large cells in clusters around blood vessels that are modified sympathetic neurons."},{"t":"mc","q":"An adrenal cortex zone is made of cells arranged in a branching network. Which zone is it?\nA) Zona glomerulosa  |  B) Zona fasciculata\nC) Adrenal medulla  |  D) Zona reticularis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Zona reticularis","why":"The names of the three cortical zones describe how their cells are arranged. The zona glomerulosa has rounded clusters, the zona fasciculata has straight columns, and the zona reticularis has a branching network (reticulum means little net). The zona reticularis is also the deepest zone, lying against the medulla."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which sequence orders the three zones of the adrenal cortex from the most superficial, just under the capsule, to the deepest, against the medulla?\nA) Glomerulosa, reticularis, fasciculata\nB) Fasciculata, glomerulosa, reticularis\nC) Glomerulosa, fasciculata, reticularis\nD) Reticularis, fasciculata, glomerulosa\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Glomerulosa, fasciculata, reticularis","why":"The adrenal cortex has three zones in order from the surface inward. The zona glomerulosa is a thin outer layer just under the capsule. The zona fasciculata is the thick middle zone, the widest of the three. The zona reticularis is the inner zone, a branching network of cells against the medulla."},{"t":"mc","q":"Viewing a slide of the adrenal cortex from the capsule inward, which zone do you reach immediately after the zona glomerulosa?\nA) Zona fasciculata  |  B) Adrenal medulla\nC) Adrenal capsule  |  D) Zona reticularis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Zona fasciculata","why":"The adrenal cortex is layered in a fixed order from outside in: capsule, zona glomerulosa, zona fasciculata, zona reticularis, then the medulla. The zona fasciculata comes right after the glomerulosa and is the thickest of the three zones, with its cells lined up in straight columns."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the adrenal cortex zones in order and the adrenal medulla with its chromaffin cells. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the adrenal cortex zones in order and the adrenal medulla with its chromaffin cells."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the adrenal cortex zones in order and the adrenal medulla with its chromaffin cells. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: adrenal gland zones. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, adrenal gland zones, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w6-endocrine-tissue-organs","name":"Pancreas, gonads, and organs with endocrine tissue","can":"Locate the pancreatic islets and gonads and name other organs such as the heart, kidneys, and skin that contain endocrine tissue.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which statement correctly compares the pancreatic islets and the acini?\nA) Both release hormones directly into the blood\nB) Islets release enzymes into ducts; acini release hormones into blood\nC) Both release digestive enzymes through ducts\nD) Islets release hormones into blood; acini release enzymes into ducts\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Islets release hormones into blood; acini release enzymes into ducts","why":"The pancreas is a mixed organ that sits behind the stomach. Most of its tissue is exocrine acini, clusters of cells that make digestive enzymes and send them through ducts to the small intestine. Scattered among them are the pancreatic islets, or islets of Langerhans, small clusters of endocrine cells that release hormones directly into the blood."},{"t":"mc","q":"A slide of the pancreas shows pale, rounded clusters of cells surrounded by many darker-staining glandular clusters. What are the pale clusters?\nA) The pancreatic islets  |  B) The acini\nC) The thyroid follicles  |  D) The chief cells\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The pancreatic islets","why":"On a pancreas slide, most of the tissue is acini, exocrine clusters whose cells stain darkly. Scattered among them are the pancreatic islets, also called the islets of Langerhans, which stain more lightly and look like pale islands in a dark sea. The islets are the endocrine portion of the pancreas."},{"t":"mc","q":"A soft gland lies behind the stomach and does both an exocrine and an endocrine job. Which organ is it?\nA) The pancreas  |  B) The thymus\nC) The liver  |  D) The adrenal gland\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The pancreas","why":"The pancreas is a soft, elongated gland that lies behind the stomach. It is a mixed organ: most of it is exocrine acini, which make digestive enzymes and send them through ducts, and scattered among them are the endocrine pancreatic islets, which release hormones into the blood."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which paired endocrine organs lie in the female pelvis, one on each side of the uterus?\nA) The ovaries  |  B) The testes\nC) The placenta  |  D) The adrenal glands\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The ovaries","why":"The ovaries are the paired female gonads. They lie in the female pelvis, one on each side of the uterus. Like the testes, they are mixed organs, producing sex cells and also acting as endocrine glands that secrete estrogen and progesterone."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which temporary organ of pregnancy releases hormones that support the pregnancy?\nA) The placenta  |  B) Adipose tissue\nC) The uterus  |  D) The ovaries\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The placenta","why":"The placenta is a temporary organ that forms during pregnancy, attached to the uterine wall. Besides exchanging materials between mother and fetus, it contains endocrine tissue that releases hormones supporting the pregnancy. It is delivered after the baby, which is why it is called temporary."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Locate the pancreatic islets and gonads and name other organs such as the heart, kidneys, and skin that contain endocrine tissue. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to locate the pancreatic islets and gonads and name other organs such as the heart, kidneys, and skin that contain endocrine tissue."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to locate the pancreatic islets and gonads and name other organs such as the heart, kidneys, and skin that contain endocrine tissue. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: pancreas, gonads, and organs with endocrine tissue. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, pancreas, gonads, and organs with endocrine tissue, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]}]},{"week":16,"system":"Nervous System","comps":[{"id":"w7-brain-organization","name":"Brain gross organization","can":"Identify the four major brain divisions cerebrum, diencephalon, brainstem, and cerebellum, and distinguish the cortical gray matter from the deep white matter on a specimen.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A slice of CNS tissue shows pale white matter around the outside and gray matter in the center. Which part of the CNS could it come from?\nA) The cerebrum  |  B) The cerebral cortex\nC) The brainstem  |  D) The cerebellum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The brainstem","why":"The cerebrum and cerebellum share one plan, with gray cortex on the outside and white matter inside. The brainstem and spinal cord are arranged the other way around, with most of their white matter on the outside. A slice with white matter outside and gray matter in the center fits the brainstem."},{"t":"mc","q":"Moving down the central axis of the brain from the cerebrum to the spinal cord, which sequence of regions is correct?\nA) Cerebrum, brainstem, diencephalon, spinal cord\nB) Cerebrum, diencephalon, brainstem, spinal cord\nC) Cerebrum, cerebellum, brainstem, spinal cord\nD) Cerebellum, diencephalon, brainstem, spinal cord\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Cerebrum, diencephalon, brainstem, spinal cord","why":"The cerebrum sits on top, and the diencephalon lies deep beneath it in the center of the brain. Below the diencephalon, the brainstem runs downward as the midbrain, pons, and medulla, and the medulla continues as the spinal cord. The cerebellum is off this central axis, attached to the back of the brainstem."},{"t":"mc","q":"Moving inward from the surface of a cerebral hemisphere, which sequence of tissues is correct?\nA) Cerebral cortex, cerebral white matter, basal nuclei\nB) Basal nuclei, cerebral white matter, cerebral cortex\nC) Cerebral cortex, basal nuclei, cerebral white matter\nD) Cerebral white matter, cerebral cortex, basal nuclei\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Cerebral cortex, cerebral white matter, basal nuclei","why":"The cerebral cortex is the thin layer of gray matter on the surface of the cerebrum. Beneath it lies the cerebral white matter, the myelinated axons that connect cortical areas with each other and with lower regions. The basal nuclei are masses of gray matter buried deep within that white matter, so they are the deepest of the three."},{"t":"mc","q":"What are the four major regions of the brain?\nA) The midbrain, pons, medulla oblongata, and hypothalamus\nB) The frontal, parietal, temporal, and occipital lobes\nC) The cortex, ventricles, gyri, and sulci\nD) The cerebrum, diencephalon, brainstem, and cerebellum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The cerebrum, diencephalon, brainstem, and cerebellum","why":"The brain is divided into four major regions. The cerebrum is the large upper region for conscious thought and voluntary movement, the diencephalon sits deep beneath it, the brainstem is the stalk that connects to the spinal cord, and the cerebellum sits behind the brainstem and coordinates movement. Every other named brain part, such as a lobe, the thalamus, or the pons, is a subdivision of one of these four."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which is the largest part of the brain?\nA) The cerebellum  |  B) The diencephalon\nC) The cerebrum  |  D) The brainstem\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The cerebrum","why":"The cerebrum is the largest part of the brain and is the seat of conscious thought, sensation, and voluntary movement. It is made of the right and left cerebral hemispheres, joined by the corpus callosum. Its surface is folded into gyri and sulci so that more cortex fits inside the skull."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the four major brain divisions cerebrum, diencephalon, brainstem, and cerebellum, and distinguish the cortical gray matter from the deep white matter on a specimen. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the four major brain divisions cerebrum, diencephalon, brainstem, and cerebellum, and distinguish the cortical gray matter from the deep white matter on a specimen."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the four major brain divisions cerebrum, diencephalon, brainstem, and cerebellum, and distinguish the cortical gray matter from the deep white matter on a specimen. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: brain gross organization. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, brain gross organization, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w7-brain-development","name":"Neural tube brain development","can":"Trace brain development from the neural tube through the primary and secondary vesicles and match each secondary vesicle to its adult structures and ventricle.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A lesion affects the cerebellum and the upper part of the fourth ventricle. Which secondary vesicle gave rise to both?\nA) Myelencephalon  |  B) Mesencephalon\nC) Diencephalon  |  D) Metencephalon\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Metencephalon","why":"The metencephalon becomes the pons and cerebellum, and its part of the tube cavity becomes the upper part of the fourth ventricle. The myelencephalon below it becomes the medulla oblongata and the lower part of the fourth ventricle. Only the metencephalon matches both the structure and the cavity in this lesion."},{"t":"mc","q":"An imaging study finds a lesion in the adult structure that develops from the mesencephalon. Which region is this?\nA) The midbrain  |  B) The cerebrum\nC) The pons  |  D) The medulla oblongata\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The midbrain","why":"The mesencephalon is the one primary brain vesicle that does not divide again, and it matures into the midbrain and the cerebral aqueduct running through it. The midbrain is the most superior region of the brainstem. Its name and the vesicle's name match, since mesencephalon means middle brain."},{"t":"mc","q":"The secondary brain vesicle called the telencephalon matures into which adult structure?\nA) The midbrain  |  B) The thalamus\nC) The medulla oblongata  |  D) The cerebrum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The cerebrum","why":"The five secondary brain vesicles line up from front to back in the same order as the adult brain. The telencephalon, the most anterior vesicle, becomes the cerebrum and the lateral ventricles inside it. The diencephalon, mesencephalon, metencephalon, and myelencephalon form the regions behind it."},{"t":"mc","q":"An imaging study shows a blockage in the cavity that runs through the adult midbrain. Which cavity is it, and which vesicle did it come from?\nA) Third ventricle, from the diencephalon\nB) Fourth ventricle, from the metencephalon\nC) Cerebral aqueduct, from the rhombencephalon\nD) Cerebral aqueduct, from the mesencephalon\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Cerebral aqueduct, from the mesencephalon","why":"The midbrain develops from the mesencephalon, the one primary vesicle that does not divide. Its part of the neural tube cavity becomes the cerebral aqueduct, which runs through the adult midbrain and opens into the fourth ventricle below."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which secondary brain vesicle develops into the medulla oblongata, and which cavity does it keep in the adult?\nA) Mesencephalon, the cerebral aqueduct\nB) Myelencephalon, the third ventricle\nC) Myelencephalon, the lower part of the fourth ventricle\nD) Metencephalon, the upper part of the fourth ventricle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Myelencephalon, the lower part of the fourth ventricle","why":"The rhombencephalon splits into the metencephalon and the myelencephalon. The myelencephalon becomes the medulla oblongata, the part next to the spinal cord, and myel means marrow. Its cavity becomes the lower part of the fourth ventricle, which continues into the central canal of the spinal cord."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Trace brain development from the neural tube through the primary and secondary vesicles and match each secondary vesicle to its adult structures and ventricle. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace brain development from the neural tube through the primary and secondary vesicles and match each secondary vesicle to its adult structures and ventricle."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to trace brain development from the neural tube through the primary and secondary vesicles and match each secondary vesicle to its adult structures and ventricle. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: neural tube brain development. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, neural tube brain development, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w7-cerebrum-surface","name":"Cerebral surface and lobes","can":"Identify the cerebral hemispheres, five lobes, gyri, sulci, and the named central, lateral, and parieto-occipital sulci along with the longitudinal and transverse fissures on a specimen.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"To see the insula on a brain specimen, which lobe do you gently pull away, and from which lobes?\nA) The frontal lobe, away from the parietal lobe\nB) The temporal lobe, away from frontal and parietal\nC) One hemisphere, away from the other\nD) The occipital lobe, away from the cerebellum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The temporal lobe, away from frontal and parietal","why":"The insula is the fifth lobe, hidden deep inside the lateral sulcus. The lateral sulcus separates the temporal lobe below from the frontal and parietal lobes above, and those three lobes cover the insula. Gently pulling the temporal lobe away from the frontal and parietal lobes opens the sulcus so you can look in."},{"t":"mc","q":"The precentral gyrus lies at the rear of which lobe of the cerebrum?\nA) The parietal lobe  |  B) The occipital lobe\nC) The temporal lobe  |  D) The frontal lobe\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The frontal lobe","why":"The precentral gyrus is the ridge just in front of the central sulcus, which makes it the most posterior gyrus of the frontal lobe. It holds the primary motor cortex, which initiates voluntary movement. The name tells you its position: pre means in front of, and central refers to the central sulcus."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which lobe of the cerebrum sits at the very back and is dedicated to processing vision?\nA) The parietal lobe  |  B) The temporal lobe\nC) The frontal lobe  |  D) The occipital lobe\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The occipital lobe","why":"The occipital lobe forms the back of each cerebral hemisphere, beneath the occipital bone, and handles visual processing. The primary visual cortex lies at the calcarine sulcus on its medial surface. Damage to this lobe causes loss of vision even when the eyes themselves are healthy."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which cerebral lobe lies on the lower side of the cerebrum and is associated with hearing and memory?\nA) The insula  |  B) The parietal lobe\nC) The temporal lobe  |  D) The occipital lobe\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The temporal lobe","why":"The temporal lobe sits on the lower side of each hemisphere, below the lateral sulcus and beneath the temporal bone. It holds the primary auditory cortex, much of the language comprehension area (Wernicke area), and structures used for memory. Its position near the ear helps students remember its role in hearing."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which cerebral lobe is buried deep within the lateral sulcus rather than lying on the outer surface?\nA) The temporal lobe  |  B) The insula\nC) The parietal lobe  |  D) The frontal lobe\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The insula","why":"Each cerebral hemisphere has five lobes. Four of them lie on the surface, but the fifth, the insula, is hidden deep within the lateral sulcus and covered by the frontal, parietal, and temporal lobes. The insula is involved in taste, visceral sensation, and emotion."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the cerebral hemispheres, five lobes, gyri, sulci, and the named central, lateral, and parieto-occipital sulci along with the longitudinal and transverse fissures on a specimen. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the cerebral hemispheres, five lobes, gyri, sulci, and the named central, lateral, and parieto-occipital sulci along with the longitudinal and transverse fissures on a specimen."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the cerebral hemispheres, five lobes, gyri, sulci, and the named central, lateral, and parieto-occipital sulci along with the longitudinal and transverse fissures on a specimen. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: cerebral surface and lobes. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, cerebral surface and lobes, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w7-cortex-functional-areas","name":"Functional cortical areas","can":"Map the motor, sensory, and association areas of the cortex to their gyri and lobes, including primary motor, primary somatosensory, primary visual, auditory, gustatory, and olfactory cortices, Broca and Wernicke areas, and the prefrontal cortex.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient suddenly cannot understand spoken or written language but still speaks fluently, although the words make little sense. Damage to which area best explains this?\nA) Wernicke's area  |  B) Broca's area\nC) The primary auditory cortex  |  D) The primary motor cortex\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Wernicke's area","why":"Wernicke's area, at the junction of the temporal and parietal lobes and usually on the left, interprets the meaning of both spoken and written words. When it is damaged, a person can still produce fluent speech but cannot understand language, so what they say often makes little sense. Because written words are affected too, the problem is with interpreting language rather than with hearing."},{"t":"mc","q":"After a stroke, a patient's eyes and optic nerves are healthy, but the patient can no longer see. Which lobe was most likely damaged?\nA) Frontal lobe  |  B) Temporal lobe\nC) Occipital lobe  |  D) Parietal lobe\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Occipital lobe","why":"The occipital lobe at the back of the cerebrum holds the primary visual cortex, which receives raw visual input, and the visual association area around it, which recognizes faces and objects. If the eyes and optic nerves are intact but the occipital cortex is destroyed, the signals arrive with nowhere to be processed, so vision is lost. Because each lobe has its own job, the deficit points to the location of the injury."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient understands everything said to her but speaks slowly in broken, effortful phrases. Which area was most likely damaged?\nA) Prefrontal cortex  |  B) Primary auditory cortex\nC) Wernicke's area  |  D) Broca's area\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Broca's area","why":"Broca's area, in the inferior frontal gyrus usually on the left, controls the motor production of speech. When it is damaged, comprehension stays intact because Wernicke's area is untouched, but the person struggles to form words and speaks in slow, effortful fragments. This pattern is often called expressive aphasia (aphasia means a language disorder)."},{"t":"mc","q":"A stroke destroys the postcentral gyrus on one side, and the patient can no longer feel touch on the opposite side of the body. Which lobe was struck?\nA) The temporal lobe  |  B) The frontal lobe\nC) The parietal lobe  |  D) The occipital lobe\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The parietal lobe","why":"The postcentral gyrus is the first ridge behind the central sulcus, and it belongs to the parietal lobe. It holds the primary somatosensory cortex, which receives conscious touch, pressure, and body position. Because the sensory pathways cross to the opposite side, damage on one side causes loss of feeling on the other side of the body."},{"t":"mc","q":"Damage to Broca's area leaves comprehension intact but blocks fluent speech. In which lobe is Broca's area?\nA) The temporal lobe  |  B) The occipital lobe\nC) The frontal lobe  |  D) The parietal lobe\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The frontal lobe","why":"Broca's area sits in the inferior frontal gyrus, usually on the left side. It controls the motor production of speech, planning the movements of the lips, tongue, and larynx needed to form words. That is why a person with damage here understands language but struggles to speak fluently."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Map the motor, sensory, and association areas of the cortex to their gyri and lobes, including primary motor, primary somatosensory, primary visual, auditory, gustatory, and olfactory cortices, Broca and Wernicke areas, and the prefrontal cortex. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to map the motor, sensory, and association areas of the cortex to their gyri and lobes, including primary motor, primary somatosensory, primary visual, auditory, gustatory, and olfactory cortices, Broca and Wernicke areas, and the prefrontal cortex."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to map the motor, sensory, and association areas of the cortex to their gyri and lobes, including primary motor, primary somatosensory, primary visual, auditory, gustatory, and olfactory cortices, Broca and Wernicke areas, and the prefrontal cortex. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: functional cortical areas. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, functional cortical areas, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w7-white-matter-tracts","name":"Cerebral white matter tracts","can":"Compare association, commissural, and projection fibers by what each connects and identify the corpus callosum and internal capsule on a specimen.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A small stroke in one narrow band of white matter weakens an entire side of the body. Which structure funnels nearly all traffic between the cortex and the body into that band?\nA) The internal capsule  |  B) The anterior commissure\nC) The corpus callosum  |  D) The arcuate fasciculus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The internal capsule","why":"The internal capsule is the major projection pathway, a compact band of white matter beside the basal ganglia. Nearly all the fibers running between the cortex and the brainstem and spinal cord squeeze through it. Because so much wiring is packed into so little space, a tiny lesion there can weaken a whole side of the body."},{"t":"mc","q":"In rare surgeries, the corpus callosum is cut to stop severe seizures from spreading. What connection does this surgery remove?\nA) The main route for motor commands from cortex to spinal cord\nB) The main link between the cerebrum and the cerebellum\nC) The main route for signals between the two hemispheres\nD) The links between gyri within the same hemisphere\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The main route for signals between the two hemispheres","why":"The corpus callosum is the largest commissural tract, a thick arch of white matter that joins matching areas of the two hemispheres. Cutting it means the two halves can no longer readily share information, which is why it can stop seizure activity from spreading from one side to the other. The rest of the wiring inside each hemisphere and down to the body stays intact."},{"t":"mc","q":"The corpus callosum is the largest example of which class of cerebral white matter fibers?\nA) Projection fibers  |  B) Ascending tract fibers\nC) Commissural fibers  |  D) Association fibers\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Commissural fibers","why":"Cerebral white matter is sorted into three classes of fibers by where they begin and end. Commissural fibers connect matching areas of the two hemispheres, and the corpus callosum is the largest commissure. The word commissure means a joining across the midline."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which class of cerebral white matter fibers connects matching areas of the two cerebral hemispheres?\nA) Commissural fibers  |  B) Association fibers\nC) Reticular fibers  |  D) Projection fibers\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Commissural fibers","why":"Cerebral white matter is sorted into three classes by where each tract starts and ends. Commissural fibers cross the midline and join matching gyri of the two hemispheres; the corpus callosum is the largest example. Association fibers stay within one hemisphere, and projection fibers run between the cortex and lower regions."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the internal capsule?\nA) A band of association fibers linking the two language areas\nB) A band of commissural fibers joining the two hemispheres\nC) A band of projection fibers between the cortex and lower regions\nD) A fold of dura mater separating the cerebrum from the cerebellum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A band of projection fibers between the cortex and lower regions","why":"The internal capsule is the major band of projection fibers, carrying signals up from the thalamus to the cortex and down from the cortex to the brainstem and spinal cord. It passes beside the basal nuclei and the thalamus, funneling nearly all traffic between the cortex and the body into a narrow strip. That is why a small stroke there can weaken a whole side of the body."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Compare association, commissural, and projection fibers by what each connects and identify the corpus callosum and internal capsule on a specimen. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to compare association, commissural, and projection fibers by what each connects and identify the corpus callosum and internal capsule on a specimen."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to compare association, commissural, and projection fibers by what each connects and identify the corpus callosum and internal capsule on a specimen. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: cerebral white matter tracts. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, cerebral white matter tracts, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w7-basal-ganglia","name":"Basal ganglia","can":"Identify the caudate nucleus, putamen, and globus pallidus and locate the functionally grouped substantia nigra and subthalamic nucleus.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The basal nuclei (basal ganglia) are deep masses of which type of matter, and within which brain region?\nA) Gray matter, deep within the cerebrum\nB) White matter, deep within the cerebrum\nC) White matter, within the brainstem\nD) Gray matter, within the cerebellum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Gray matter, deep within the cerebrum","why":"The basal nuclei, also called the basal ganglia or cerebral nuclei, are clusters of neuron cell bodies buried within the white matter of the cerebrum, which makes them gray matter. They include the caudate nucleus, putamen, and globus pallidus. They help start and regulate movement and suppress unwanted movement."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which midbrain nucleus works with the basal nuclei, and its loss causes which disease?\nA) Superior colliculus, Parkinson disease\nB) Inferior colliculus, Alzheimer disease\nC) Substantia nigra, multiple sclerosis\nD) Substantia nigra, Parkinson disease\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Substantia nigra, Parkinson disease","why":"The substantia nigra is a darkly pigmented nucleus in the midbrain that helps regulate movement by working with the basal nuclei, so it is grouped with them by function. When its neurons are lost, the basal nuclei lose an important input, and movement becomes slow and stiff, often with a resting tremor. This loss is the basis of Parkinson disease."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which core component of the basal ganglia is the long, curved nucleus?\nA) The putamen  |  B) The caudate nucleus\nC) The subthalamic nucleus  |  D) The globus pallidus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The caudate nucleus","why":"The caudate nucleus is a long, C-shaped nucleus that curves along the wall of the lateral ventricle, with a large head in front and a thin tail trailing behind. Its name comes from the Latin for tail. It is one of the core basal ganglia that help start and regulate movement."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which deep masses of gray matter, including the putamen and globus pallidus, help start and regulate voluntary movement?\nA) The basal nuclei  |  B) The cranial nerve nuclei\nC) The thalamic nuclei  |  D) The deep cerebellar nuclei\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The basal nuclei","why":"The basal nuclei, also called the basal ganglia or cerebral nuclei, are gray matter masses buried deep in the cerebral white matter. Their core parts are the caudate nucleus, putamen, and globus pallidus. They help start movement, suppress unwanted movement, and adjust muscle tone, as in the automatic arm swing of walking."},{"t":"mc","q":"The putamen and the globus pallidus together form which lens-shaped structure of the basal nuclei?\nA) The caudate nucleus  |  B) The subthalamic nucleus\nC) The lentiform nucleus  |  D) The red nucleus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The lentiform nucleus","why":"Lentiform means lens-shaped. The putamen lies on the lateral side and the globus pallidus on the medial side, and together they form this lens-shaped mass lateral to the internal capsule. The caudate nucleus is the separate, long curved nucleus of the basal nuclei."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the caudate nucleus, putamen, and globus pallidus and locate the functionally grouped substantia nigra and subthalamic nucleus. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the caudate nucleus, putamen, and globus pallidus and locate the functionally grouped substantia nigra and subthalamic nucleus."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the caudate nucleus, putamen, and globus pallidus and locate the functionally grouped substantia nigra and subthalamic nucleus. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: basal ganglia. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, basal ganglia, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w7-diencephalon","name":"Diencephalon","can":"Identify the thalamus with the massa intermedia, the hypothalamus, the epithalamus with the pineal gland, and the third ventricle they enclose.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The thalamus and hypothalamus are both structures of which major brain region?\nA) The diencephalon  |  B) The cerebrum\nC) The cerebellum  |  D) The brainstem\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The diencephalon","why":"The diencephalon is the deep central region between the cerebrum and the brainstem. It has three parts: the thalamus, the hypothalamus, and the epithalamus, and it surrounds the third ventricle. The thalamus is its largest part, and the hypothalamus lies just below the thalamus."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which brain region lies deep between the cerebrum and the brainstem and contains the main sensory relay station and the homeostasis control center?\nA) The cerebellum  |  B) The corpus callosum\nC) The diencephalon  |  D) The medulla oblongata\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The diencephalon","why":"The diencephalon is the deep central region that links the cerebral hemispheres to the brainstem. It contains the thalamus, which relays sensory signals to the cortex, the hypothalamus, which controls homeostasis, and the epithalamus with the pineal gland. It surrounds the third ventricle."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which small endocrine gland is housed within the epithalamus?\nA) The hypothalamus  |  B) The pineal gland\nC) The thalamus  |  D) The pituitary gland\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The pineal gland","why":"The epithalamus is the small posterior part of the diencephalon, and its best-known structure is the pineal gland. The pineal gland is an endocrine gland that secretes melatonin, which helps set sleep-wake rhythms. It sits in the midline at the back of the third ventricle."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which part of the diencephalon links the nervous system with the endocrine system and is the main control center for homeostasis?\nA) The thalamus  |  B) The epithalamus\nC) The pineal gland  |  D) The hypothalamus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The hypothalamus","why":"The hypothalamus lies below the thalamus and is the master control center for homeostasis. It controls the autonomic nervous system, regulates body temperature, hunger, thirst, and sleep-wake rhythms, and directs endocrine function through the pituitary gland. That pituitary connection is why it is called the link between the nervous and endocrine systems."},{"t":"mc","q":"The third ventricle is the midline cavity enclosed by which brain region?\nA) The cerebellum  |  B) The midbrain\nC) The cerebrum  |  D) The diencephalon\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The diencephalon","why":"The diencephalon surrounds the third ventricle. The right and left thalami form its upper side walls, and the hypothalamus forms its floor and lower walls, with the massa intermedia crossing the ventricle in most brains. In front the third ventricle opens into each lateral ventricle through an interventricular foramen, and behind it drains into the cerebral aqueduct."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the thalamus with the massa intermedia, the hypothalamus, the epithalamus with the pineal gland, and the third ventricle they enclose. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the thalamus with the massa intermedia, the hypothalamus, the epithalamus with the pineal gland, and the third ventricle they enclose."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the thalamus with the massa intermedia, the hypothalamus, the epithalamus with the pineal gland, and the third ventricle they enclose. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: diencephalon. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, diencephalon, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w7-cerebellum","name":"Cerebellum","can":"Identify the cerebellar hemispheres, vermis, folia, arbor vitae, deep nuclei, and the three cerebellar peduncles with their brainstem connections.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient can move every limb but cannot coordinate movement, keep posture, or balance well. Which brain region is most likely affected?\nA) The thalamus  |  B) The cerebellum\nC) The hypothalamus  |  D) The cerebrum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The cerebellum","why":"The cerebellum sits behind the brainstem in the posterior cranial fossa and coordinates movement, posture, and balance. It compares the movement that was commanded with what the body is actually doing and smooths out the difference. When it is damaged, a person can still move, but movements become clumsy and balance is poor."},{"t":"mc","q":"How many pairs of cerebellar peduncles connect the cerebellum to the brainstem, and what are they called?\nA) Two pairs: anterior and posterior\nB) Three pairs: superior, middle, and inferior\nC) Four pairs: one per brainstem region plus the cord\nD) Three pairs: anterior, lateral, and posterior\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Three pairs: superior, middle, and inferior","why":"Three pairs of cerebellar peduncles, thick stalks of white matter, attach the cerebellum to each level of the brainstem. The superior peduncles connect to the midbrain, the middle peduncles to the pons, and the inferior peduncles to the medulla. Their names follow the brainstem regions from top to bottom."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which cerebellar peduncle carries mostly output from the cerebellum, and where does it connect?\nA) Superior peduncle, connecting to the midbrain\nB) Inferior peduncle, connecting to the spinal cord\nC) Inferior peduncle, connecting to the medulla\nD) Middle peduncle, connecting to the pons\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Superior peduncle, connecting to the midbrain","why":"The superior cerebellar peduncles connect the cerebellum to the midbrain and carry mostly output, the signals the cerebellum sends out to adjust movement. The middle and inferior peduncles mainly bring information in. Remembering that most output leaves through the top pair makes the three easy to sort."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which brain structure sits behind the brainstem and coordinates movement, posture, and balance?\nA) The cerebellum  |  B) The thalamus\nC) The pons  |  D) The cerebrum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The cerebellum","why":"The cerebellum is the second largest part of the brain. It sits in the posterior cranial fossa, behind the brainstem and below the occipital lobes, and is attached to the brainstem by three pairs of cerebellar peduncles. It coordinates movement, posture, and balance so that motion is smooth and accurate."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the branching, tree-like pattern of white matter inside the cerebellum called?\nA) The internal capsule  |  B) The folia\nC) The corpus callosum  |  D) The arbor vitae\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The arbor vitae","why":"When the cerebellum is cut in a sagittal section, its inner white matter branches out like the limbs of a tree under the outer gray cortex. This pattern is called the arbor vitae, which means tree of life. It carries signals into and out of the cerebellar cortex."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the cerebellar hemispheres, vermis, folia, arbor vitae, deep nuclei, and the three cerebellar peduncles with their brainstem connections. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the cerebellar hemispheres, vermis, folia, arbor vitae, deep nuclei, and the three cerebellar peduncles with their brainstem connections."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the cerebellar hemispheres, vermis, folia, arbor vitae, deep nuclei, and the three cerebellar peduncles with their brainstem connections. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: cerebellum. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, cerebellum, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w7-limbic-system","name":"Limbic system","can":"Locate the cingulate gyrus, amygdala, and hippocampus as the limbic structures ringing the diencephalon.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"After a brain injury, a patient remembers childhood events clearly but cannot form any new long-term memories. Which limbic structure was most likely damaged?\nA) The cingulate gyrus  |  B) The hippocampus\nC) The pineal gland  |  D) The amygdala\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The hippocampus","why":"Old memories are stored across the cerebral cortex, but forming new long-term memories depends on the hippocampus in the medial temporal lobe. Damage to the hippocampus therefore spares the past while blocking new learning. This pattern of keeping old memories but losing new ones points straight to the hippocampus."},{"t":"mc","q":"In lab, a brain is dissected to open the inferior horn of the lateral ventricle. Which limbic structure is the curved ridge in its floor?\nA) Amygdala  |  B) Hippocampus\nC) Cingulate gyrus  |  D) Fornix\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Hippocampus","why":"The hippocampus is a curved ridge of cortex rolled into the floor of the inferior horn of the lateral ventricle, also called the temporal horn. It lies deep to the parahippocampal gyrus in the medial temporal lobe, and its name means seahorse, after its shape."},{"t":"mc","q":"A small lesion lies deep to the uncus, just in front of the hippocampus. Which limbic structure is it most likely in?\nA) Amygdala  |  B) Hippocampus\nC) Cingulate gyrus  |  D) Mammillary body\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Amygdala","why":"The amygdala is an almond-shaped nucleus deep in the front of the medial temporal lobe. It lies deep to the uncus, the hooked front end of the parahippocampal gyrus, and just in front of the hippocampus. It is involved in fear and links emotion to memory."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which sequence traces the limbic ring from above the corpus callosum down into the temporal lobe?\nA) Cingulate gyrus, uncus, parahippocampal gyrus\nB) Parahippocampal gyrus, cingulate gyrus, uncus\nC) Uncus, cingulate gyrus, parahippocampal gyrus\nD) Cingulate gyrus, parahippocampal gyrus, uncus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Cingulate gyrus, parahippocampal gyrus, uncus","why":"The cingulate gyrus arches just above the corpus callosum on the medial surface. It curves around the back of the corpus callosum and continues down into the temporal lobe as the parahippocampal gyrus. The front end of the parahippocampal gyrus hooks back on itself to form the uncus, completing the C-shaped ring."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which limbic structure is essential for forming new long-term memories?\nA) The cingulate gyrus  |  B) The amygdala\nC) The pineal gland  |  D) The hippocampus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The hippocampus","why":"The hippocampus is a curved limbic structure in the medial temporal lobe. It is needed to turn new experiences into long-term memories. Memories already stored elsewhere in the cortex can still be recalled after hippocampal damage, but new ones are not laid down properly."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Locate the cingulate gyrus, amygdala, and hippocampus as the limbic structures ringing the diencephalon. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to locate the cingulate gyrus, amygdala, and hippocampus as the limbic structures ringing the diencephalon."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to locate the cingulate gyrus, amygdala, and hippocampus as the limbic structures ringing the diencephalon. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: limbic system. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, limbic system, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w7-circle-of-willis","name":"Cerebral arterial supply","can":"Trace the anterior and posterior circulations through the Circle of Willis and identify the anterior, middle, and posterior cerebral arteries, the communicating arteries, the internal carotid, vertebral, and basilar arteries, and the cerebellar branches.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A slowly narrowing internal carotid artery causes few symptoms because blood reaches its territory through other vessels. Which structure makes this rerouting possible?\nA) The middle cerebral artery  |  B) The Circle of Willis\nC) The anterior spinal artery  |  D) The superior sagittal sinus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The Circle of Willis","why":"The Circle of Willis is an anastomosis, a ring of connected arteries at the base of the brain that joins the anterior and posterior circulations through the communicating arteries. If one internal carotid artery narrows, blood can cross the ring from the other internal carotid or from the vertebral and basilar system. That alternate route keeps blood flowing to the territory of the narrowed artery."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which cerebral artery supplies the lateral surface of the cerebrum, including much of the motor and sensory cortex for the body?\nA) The anterior cerebral artery  |  B) The middle cerebral artery\nC) The posterior cerebral artery  |  D) The basilar artery\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The middle cerebral artery","why":"The internal carotid artery divides into the anterior and middle cerebral arteries. The middle cerebral artery is the larger branch, and it runs out through the lateral sulcus to supply the lateral surface of the cerebrum. That territory includes most of the primary motor and somatosensory cortex for the body, so a blockage here often causes weakness and numbness on the opposite side."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which two arteries unite to form the basilar artery?\nA) The posterior communicating arteries  |  B) The vertebral arteries\nC) The posterior cerebral arteries  |  D) The internal carotid arteries\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The vertebral arteries","why":"The two vertebral arteries travel up through the transverse foramina of the cervical vertebrae, enter the skull through the foramen magnum, and join on the front of the brainstem to form the single basilar artery. The basilar artery then runs up the pons and supplies the brainstem and cerebellum. This vertebral and basilar system is the posterior circulation of the brain."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which artery supplies most of the lateral surface of the cerebrum?\nA) Middle cerebral artery  |  B) Basilar artery\nC) Posterior cerebral artery  |  D) Anterior cerebral artery\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Middle cerebral artery","why":"The middle cerebral artery is the largest branch of the internal carotid artery. It runs out through the lateral sulcus and fans across the lateral surface of the cerebrum, supplying large parts of the frontal, parietal, and temporal lobes, including much of the motor and sensory cortex. This is why a stroke in this artery often weakens the opposite side of the body."},{"t":"mc","q":"The ring of arteries at the base of the brain that joins the anterior and posterior circulations is commonly called what?\nA) The basilar artery  |  B) The confluence of sinuses\nC) The Circle of Willis  |  D) The choroid plexus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The Circle of Willis","why":"The cerebral arterial circle, usually called the Circle of Willis, is a ring of connected arteries under the brain. It links the internal carotid supply in front with the vertebral and basilar supply behind through the communicating arteries. Because it is a ring, blood can sometimes take another route if one vessel narrows slowly."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Trace the anterior and posterior circulations through the Circle of Willis and identify the anterior, middle, and posterior cerebral arteries, the communicating arteries, the internal carotid, vertebral, and basilar arteries, and the cerebellar branches. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace the anterior and posterior circulations through the Circle of Willis and identify the anterior, middle, and posterior cerebral arteries, the communicating arteries, the internal carotid, vertebral, and basilar arteries, and the cerebellar branches."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to trace the anterior and posterior circulations through the Circle of Willis and identify the anterior, middle, and posterior cerebral arteries, the communicating arteries, the internal carotid, vertebral, and basilar arteries, and the cerebellar branches. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: cerebral arterial supply. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, cerebral arterial supply, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w7-brainstem-regions","name":"Brainstem regions","can":"Locate the midbrain, pons, and medulla oblongata in order and describe how the brainstem connects the higher brain to the spinal cord.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Where is the midbrain located within the brainstem, relative to the pons and medulla?\nA) It is the most superior part  |  B) It is the lowest part, joining the cord\nC) It lies behind the pons and medulla  |  D) It is the middle, rounded part\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) It is the most superior part","why":"The midbrain, also called the mesencephalon, is the short, most superior region of the brainstem, just below the diencephalon. It surrounds the cerebral aqueduct and contains the colliculi, the cerebral peduncles, and the substantia nigra. Below it come the pons and then the medulla oblongata."},{"t":"mc","q":"The pons is the middle region of the brainstem. Which two regions sit immediately above and below it?\nA) The medulla above and the midbrain below\nB) The cerebrum above and the spinal cord below\nC) The midbrain above and the medulla below\nD) The diencephalon above and the cerebellum below\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The midbrain above and the medulla below","why":"From top to bottom, the brainstem runs midbrain, pons, medulla oblongata. The pons sits between the midbrain above and the medulla below, and it also forms a bridge to the cerebellum behind it. Its pontine nuclei relay motor information from the cortex to the cerebellum."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which list orders the three regions of the brainstem from superior to inferior?\nA) Midbrain, medulla oblongata, pons  |  B) Medulla oblongata, pons, midbrain\nC) Midbrain, pons, medulla oblongata  |  D) Pons, midbrain, medulla oblongata\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Midbrain, pons, medulla oblongata","why":"The brainstem runs from the midbrain at the top, just below the diencephalon, through the rounded pons in the middle, to the medulla oblongata at the bottom, which merges with the spinal cord at the foramen magnum. The cranial nerve nuclei follow the same order, with III and IV in the midbrain, V through VIII in the pons, and IX, X, and XII in the medulla."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which brainstem region is continuous with the spinal cord at the foramen magnum?\nA) The midbrain  |  B) The pons\nC) The cerebellum  |  D) The medulla oblongata\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The medulla oblongata","why":"The medulla oblongata is the lowest region of the brainstem. At the level of the foramen magnum, the large opening in the occipital bone, it merges smoothly into the spinal cord. The fourth ventricle behind it continues downward as the central canal of the cord."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which statement correctly describes the tracts that pass through the brainstem?\nA) No long tracts pass through; it holds only cranial nerve nuclei\nB) Only ascending sensory tracts pass through; motor tracts bypass it\nC) Only descending motor tracts pass through; sensory tracts bypass it\nD) All tracts between the brain and spinal cord pass through it\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) All tracts between the brain and spinal cord pass through it","why":"The brainstem is the stalk that connects the cerebrum, diencephalon, and cerebellum to the spinal cord. Every ascending sensory tract and every descending motor tract traveling between the brain and the body passes through it. That is why even a small brainstem lesion can interrupt many functions at once."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Locate the midbrain, pons, and medulla oblongata in order and describe how the brainstem connects the higher brain to the spinal cord. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to locate the midbrain, pons, and medulla oblongata in order and describe how the brainstem connects the higher brain to the spinal cord."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to locate the midbrain, pons, and medulla oblongata in order and describe how the brainstem connects the higher brain to the spinal cord. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: brainstem regions. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, brainstem regions, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w7-midbrain","name":"Midbrain structures","can":"Identify the tectum with superior and inferior colliculi, the cerebral peduncles, the substantia nigra, and the cerebral aqueduct on a midbrain specimen.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient develops tremor, rigid muscles, and slow movements. The cause is loss of neurons in a darkly pigmented nucleus of the midbrain. Which structure is this?\nA) The superior colliculi  |  B) The substantia nigra\nC) The respiratory center  |  D) The pontine nuclei\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The substantia nigra","why":"Tremor, rigidity, and slow movement are the classic signs of Parkinson disease. Its basis is the loss of neurons in the substantia nigra, a dark-pigmented midbrain nucleus that works with the basal ganglia to start and smooth movement. Without it, the basal ganglia circuit cannot regulate movement normally."},{"t":"mc","q":"A sudden bright flash makes a person turn the head and eyes toward it before they even think about it. Which midbrain structure handles this reflex?\nA) The superior colliculi  |  B) The inferior colliculi\nC) The substantia nigra  |  D) The cerebral peduncles\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The superior colliculi","why":"The superior colliculi, the upper pair of bumps on the roof of the midbrain, are visual reflex centers. They turn the eyes and head toward something seen without any conscious decision. Becoming aware of what you are looking at happens later, in the visual cortex of the occipital lobe."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which paired stalks on the front of the midbrain carry descending motor tracts?\nA) The cerebral peduncles  |  B) The colliculi\nC) The middle cerebellar peduncles  |  D) The pyramids\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The cerebral peduncles","why":"The cerebral peduncles are two thick stalks on the anterior side of the midbrain. They carry the descending motor tracts from the cerebral cortex down toward the pons, medulla, and spinal cord. Lower down, many of these same fibers form the pyramids of the medulla."},{"t":"mc","q":"On a cross section of the midbrain, which structures lie on the tectum, behind the cerebral aqueduct?\nA) The substantia nigra and crus cerebri\nB) The cerebral peduncles\nC) The tegmentum and its cranial nerve nuclei\nD) The superior and inferior colliculi\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The superior and inferior colliculi","why":"The cerebral aqueduct is the small hole near the back of a midbrain cross section, and it orients the specimen. Everything behind it is the roof, the tectum, which carries the four rounded bumps called the corpora quadrigemina. These are the superior and inferior colliculi."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which region of the brainstem surrounds the cerebral aqueduct?\nA) The pons  |  B) The midbrain\nC) The medulla oblongata  |  D) The diencephalon\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The midbrain","why":"The midbrain, or mesencephalon, is the short upper region of the brainstem, and the cerebral aqueduct runs through its center. The aqueduct carries CSF from the third ventricle down to the fourth. The roof of the midbrain behind the aqueduct is the tectum, with its four colliculi."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the tectum with superior and inferior colliculi, the cerebral peduncles, the substantia nigra, and the cerebral aqueduct on a midbrain specimen. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the tectum with superior and inferior colliculi, the cerebral peduncles, the substantia nigra, and the cerebral aqueduct on a midbrain specimen."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the tectum with superior and inferior colliculi, the cerebral peduncles, the substantia nigra, and the cerebral aqueduct on a midbrain specimen. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: midbrain structures. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, midbrain structures, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w7-pons","name":"Pons structures","can":"Identify the pons, its pontine nuclei, the middle cerebellar peduncles, and the fourth ventricle behind it.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A small lesion damages the back part of the pons, just in front of the fourth ventricle. Which part of the pons is this, and what does it hold?\nA) The middle cerebellar peduncle, which holds the dentate nucleus\nB) The tectum, which holds the colliculi\nC) The basilar pons, which holds the pontine nuclei\nD) The tegmentum, which holds cranial nerve nuclei\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The tegmentum, which holds cranial nerve nuclei","why":"The pons has a front part and a back part. The basilar pons in front is a thick band of crosswise fibers with the pontine nuclei scattered through it. The back part is the tegmentum, which carries cranial nerve nuclei and part of the reticular formation, and it forms part of the floor of the fourth ventricle."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which structures relay motor information from the cerebral cortex to the cerebellum?\nA) The substantia nigra  |  B) The colliculi\nC) The pontine nuclei  |  D) The pyramids\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The pontine nuclei","why":"The pontine nuclei are clusters of gray matter in the pons. They receive motor information from the cerebral cortex and pass it to the cerebellum through the thick middle cerebellar peduncles. This lets the cerebellum compare what the cortex intends with what the body is doing."},{"t":"mc","q":"On a midsagittal model, you find the fourth ventricle, with the cerebellum over its roof. What forms its floor?\nA) The front of the pons, the basilar pons\nB) The back of the pons and upper medulla\nC) The corpus callosum and the fornix\nD) The thalamus and the hypothalamus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The back of the pons and upper medulla","why":"The fourth ventricle is a diamond-shaped space filled with cerebrospinal fluid, behind the pons and upper medulla. Its floor is the back of the pons and the upper medulla, and the cerebellum lies over its roof. The cerebral aqueduct opens into it from above."},{"t":"mc","q":"On a brainstem model, you follow the crosswise fibers of the basilar pons around its side. Where do they lead?\nA) Up into the thalamus, through the cerebral aqueduct\nB) Down into the medulla, through the fourth ventricle\nC) Forward to the cerebral cortex, through the fornix\nD) Into the cerebellum, as the middle cerebellar peduncles\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Into the cerebellum, as the middle cerebellar peduncles","why":"The crosswise fibers of the basilar pons carry the axons of the pontine nuclei. They curve back on each side and leave the pons as the middle cerebellar peduncles, the thick stalks that enter each cerebellar hemisphere. These are the largest of the three pairs of cerebellar peduncles."},{"t":"mc","q":"What do the pontine nuclei relay?\nA) Motor information from the cortex to the cerebellum\nB) Hormone signals from the hypothalamus to the pituitary\nC) Visual reflex signals from the eyes to the neck\nD) Sensory signals from the body to the cortex\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Motor information from the cortex to the cerebellum","why":"The pontine nuclei are clusters of gray matter in the pons. They receive motor information from the cerebral cortex about planned movements and relay it through the middle cerebellar peduncles into the cerebellum. This lets the cerebellum compare what the cortex intends with what the body is actually doing."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the pons, its pontine nuclei, the middle cerebellar peduncles, and the fourth ventricle behind it. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the pons, its pontine nuclei, the middle cerebellar peduncles, and the fourth ventricle behind it."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the pons, its pontine nuclei, the middle cerebellar peduncles, and the fourth ventricle behind it. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: pons structures. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, pons structures, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w7-medulla","name":"Medulla oblongata structures","can":"Identify the pyramids, olives, and the decussation of the pyramids on the medulla and locate the vital cardiac, respiratory, and vasomotor centers.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"An injury to one small brainstem region is immediately life-threatening because that region holds the centers for the heartbeat and breathing. Which region is it?\nA) The pons  |  B) The tectum\nC) The medulla oblongata  |  D) The midbrain\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The medulla oblongata","why":"The cardiac, respiratory, and vasomotor centers all sit in the medulla oblongata. They adjust the rate and force of the heartbeat, set the basic rhythm of breathing, and adjust blood vessel diameter, all without conscious effort. Damage here can stop breathing and disrupt circulation, which is why a medullary injury is so often fatal."},{"t":"mc","q":"Breathing continues during deep sleep and under general anesthesia even though the cerebral cortex is quiet. Which region keeps the basic breathing rhythm going?\nA) The thalamus  |  B) The medulla oblongata\nC) The primary motor cortex  |  D) The cerebellum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The medulla oblongata","why":"The respiratory center that sets the basic rhythm of breathing lies in the medulla oblongata, below the level of conscious control. Because it works automatically, breathing goes on whether or not the cortex is active. You can override it for a short time, as when holding your breath, but the medulla takes back control."},{"t":"mc","q":"Most corticospinal fibers cross in the medulla before they enter the spinal cord. An injury damages the descending motor tracts in the left half of the cervical spinal cord. Which side of the body below the injury becomes weak?\nA) The left side  |  B) Both sides equally\nC) The right side  |  D) Neither side\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The left side","why":"The crossing happens at the decussation of the pyramids in the lower medulla, above the spinal cord. By the time the motor fibers run down the left half of the cord, they have already crossed and serve the left side of the body. Damage to the left half of the cord therefore weakens the left side below the injury, while a lesion above the crossing, such as in the left cerebrum, weakens the right side."},{"t":"mc","q":"An injury to the medulla oblongata leaves a patient unable to regulate heart rate and breathing. Which centers were damaged?\nA) The pontine nuclei  |  B) The cardiac and respiratory centers\nC) The cerebral peduncles  |  D) The superior and inferior colliculi\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The cardiac and respiratory centers","why":"The medulla oblongata holds three vital centers. The cardiac center adjusts the rate and force of the heartbeat, and the respiratory center sets the basic rhythm of breathing. The vasomotor center beside them controls blood vessel diameter, which is why injury to the medulla is so dangerous."},{"t":"mc","q":"A stroke in the right cerebral hemisphere weakens the left side of the body. Which brainstem feature explains this crossover?\nA) The reticular activating system  |  B) The internal capsule\nC) The corpus callosum  |  D) The decussation of the pyramids\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The decussation of the pyramids","why":"The corticospinal motor fibers run down from the cortex and form the pyramids on the front of the medulla. At the lower medulla, most of them cross to the opposite side at the decussation of the pyramids. By the time they reach the spinal cord they serve the opposite side of the body, so right-sided brain damage weakens the left side."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the pyramids, olives, and the decussation of the pyramids on the medulla and locate the vital cardiac, respiratory, and vasomotor centers. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the pyramids, olives, and the decussation of the pyramids on the medulla and locate the vital cardiac, respiratory, and vasomotor centers."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the pyramids, olives, and the decussation of the pyramids on the medulla and locate the vital cardiac, respiratory, and vasomotor centers. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: medulla oblongata structures. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, medulla oblongata structures, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w7-cranial-nerve-nuclei","name":"Cranial nerve nuclei distribution","can":"Match cranial nerve nuclei III through XII to the brainstem region that houses them.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A clinician finds the abducens nerve (CN VI) weak and looks for the lesion in the brainstem region that holds its nucleus. Which region is that?\nA) The medulla oblongata  |  B) The pons\nC) The midbrain  |  D) The cerebellum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The pons","why":"Each cranial nerve has a fixed nucleus, so testing the nerves one at a time helps localize a brainstem problem. The abducens nucleus sits in the pons, along with the nuclei for CN V, VII, and VIII. A weak CN VI therefore points the clinician to the pons."},{"t":"mc","q":"A clinician finds that a patient's tongue is weak on one side and looks for the lesion at the hypoglossal (CN XII) nucleus. Which brain region holds that nucleus?\nA) The upper cervical cord  |  B) The midbrain\nC) The medulla oblongata  |  D) The pons\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The medulla oblongata","why":"The hypoglossal nucleus lies in the medulla oblongata, near the midline. Testing each cranial nerve helps localize brainstem lesions because each nucleus has a fixed home. A weak tongue from a CN XII problem points the clinician to the medulla."},{"t":"mc","q":"The brainstem holds the nuclei for which cranial nerves?\nA) Cranial nerves III through X and XII  |  B) All twelve cranial nerves\nC) Cranial nerves I through VI  |  D) Cranial nerves IX, X, and XII only\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Cranial nerves III through X and XII","why":"Cranial nerves I and II connect to the forebrain, so they have no brainstem nucleus. The midbrain holds the nuclei of III and IV, the pons holds V through VIII, and the medulla holds IX, X, and XII, with the VIII nuclei sitting at the junction of the pons and medulla. The nucleus of XI sits just below the brainstem, in the upper cervical spinal cord."},{"t":"mc","q":"The nuclei for the oculomotor nerve (CN III) and the trochlear nerve (CN IV) lie in which brainstem region?\nA) The midbrain  |  B) The pons\nC) The cerebellum  |  D) The medulla oblongata\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The midbrain","why":"Cranial nerve nuclei are stacked in the brainstem in roughly the same order as the nerve numbers, from top to bottom. The midbrain, the highest region, holds the nuclei for CN III and CN IV. Below it, the pons holds V through VIII, and the medulla holds IX, X, and XII."},{"t":"mc","q":"The nuclei for cranial nerves V, VI, VII, and VIII are found mainly in which brainstem region?\nA) The pons  |  B) The medulla oblongata\nC) The midbrain  |  D) The cerebellum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The pons","why":"The pons holds the nuclei for the trigeminal (V), abducens (VI), facial (VII), and vestibulocochlear (VIII) nerves. The CN VIII nuclei sit at the junction of the pons and medulla, so VIII is listed with both regions. The order follows the numbers: III and IV above in the midbrain, V through VIII in the pons, and IX, X, and XII below in the medulla."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Match cranial nerve nuclei III through XII to the brainstem region that houses them. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to match cranial nerve nuclei III through XII to the brainstem region that houses them."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to match cranial nerve nuclei III through XII to the brainstem region that houses them. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: cranial nerve nuclei distribution. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, cranial nerve nuclei distribution, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w7-reticular-formation","name":"Reticular formation","can":"Locate the reticular formation as a net of gray matter through the brainstem core and distinguish its ascending reticular activating system from its descending component.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient loses a large area of cerebral cortex but stays awake. Later, a small brainstem injury drops the patient into a coma. Which system did the brainstem injury most likely damage?\nA) The basal ganglia  |  B) The vasomotor center\nC) The reticular activating system  |  D) The cerebral peduncles\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The reticular activating system","why":"Staying awake depends on the reticular activating system, the ascending part of the reticular formation in the brainstem. It sends a steady stream of arousing signals up to the cortex. A person can lose much of the cortex and remain awake, but damage to this small ascending network can withdraw that arousal and cause coma."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which function is the reticular activating system responsible for?\nA) Maintaining consciousness and arousal  |  B) Coordinating balance and posture\nC) Producing cerebrospinal fluid  |  D) Relaying sensory signals to the cortex\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Maintaining consciousness and arousal","why":"The reticular activating system, or RAS, is the ascending part of the reticular formation in the brainstem. It keeps the cortex awake, holds attention, and filters out unimportant stimuli so the brain is not overloaded. When its activity drops, a person falls asleep, and severe damage to it can cause coma."},{"t":"mc","q":"On a cross section of the brainstem, where do you find the reticular formation?\nA) In the central core, behind the large descending motor tracts\nB) On the front surface, among the crosswise fibers of the pons\nC) Over the roof of the fourth ventricle, under the cerebellum\nD) On the outer surface, as a raised ridge of gray matter\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) In the central core, behind the large descending motor tracts","why":"The reticular formation fills the central core of the brainstem, the tegmentum. It lies behind the large descending motor tracts and in front of the ventricle or aqueduct. It runs this way through the medulla, pons, and midbrain."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the network of gray matter neurons spread through the core of the entire brainstem called?\nA) The reticular formation  |  B) The tectum\nC) The substantia nigra  |  D) The pontine nuclei\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The reticular formation","why":"The reticular formation is not a single nucleus. It is a net of gray matter cell bodies woven through the white matter of the midbrain, pons, and medulla. Its ascending part, the reticular activating system, keeps the cortex awake, and its descending part helps set resting muscle tone."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the ascending part of the reticular formation that keeps the cerebral cortex awake and alert called?\nA) The superior colliculi  |  B) The reticular activating system\nC) The substantia nigra  |  D) The vasomotor center\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The reticular activating system","why":"The reticular activating system, or RAS, is the ascending part of the reticular formation. It sends a constant stream of signals up to the cortex that keeps it awake, alert, and attentive, and it filters out unimportant stimuli. When the RAS is turned down, the result is sleep, and severe damage to it can cause coma."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Locate the reticular formation as a net of gray matter through the brainstem core and distinguish its ascending reticular activating system from its descending component. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to locate the reticular formation as a net of gray matter through the brainstem core and distinguish its ascending reticular activating system from its descending component."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to locate the reticular formation as a net of gray matter through the brainstem core and distinguish its ascending reticular activating system from its descending component. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: reticular formation. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, reticular formation, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w7-cranial-meninges","name":"Cranial meninges and dural reflections","can":"Identify the dura, arachnoid, and pia mater and the dural reflections falx cerebri, falx cerebelli, and tentorium cerebelli, and trace dural venous sinus drainage from the superior sagittal sinus to the internal jugular vein.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The cranial dura mater has two layers. Where they separate, they form channels that collect venous blood from the brain. What are these channels called?\nA) The interventricular foramina  |  B) The dural venous sinuses\nC) The arachnoid granulations  |  D) The subdural spaces\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The dural venous sinuses","why":"The two layers of the cranial dura, the periosteal and meningeal layers, are fused except where they part to form the dural venous sinuses. These channels collect venous blood from the brain and CSF returned by the arachnoid granulations. The main route runs from the superior sagittal sinus to the confluence of sinuses, through the transverse and sigmoid sinuses, and out the internal jugular veins."},{"t":"mc","q":"From its Latin name, which meninx is the tough mother on the outside?\nA) The falx cerebri  |  B) The dura mater\nC) The pia mater  |  D) The arachnoid mater\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The dura mater","why":"Mater means mother, and the meninges are named from tough mother on the outside to tender mother on the inside. Dura means tough, so the dura mater is the tough outer layer. Pia means tender, so the pia mater is the delicate innermost layer, and the arachnoid, named for its spider-web look, lies between them."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which list orders the three meninges from the skull inward toward the brain surface?\nA) Pia mater, arachnoid mater, dura mater\nB) Dura mater, pia mater, arachnoid mater\nC) Arachnoid mater, dura mater, pia mater\nD) Dura mater, arachnoid mater, pia mater\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Dura mater, arachnoid mater, pia mater","why":"From the skull inward, the dura mater is the tough outer layer, the arachnoid mater is the delicate middle layer, and the pia mater is the thin inner layer clinging to the brain. The Latin names run from tough mother on the outside to tender mother within. The CSF-filled subarachnoid space lies between the arachnoid and the pia."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which structure separates the right and left cerebellar hemispheres?\nA) The tentorium cerebelli  |  B) The falx cerebelli\nC) The vermis  |  D) The falx cerebri\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The falx cerebelli","why":"The falx cerebelli is a small vertical fold of meningeal dura that projects between the right and left cerebellar hemispheres. It is the cerebellar counterpart of the falx cerebri, which separates the cerebral hemispheres. Falx means sickle, describing the curved shape of both folds."},{"t":"mc","q":"The dura mater is the toughest of the meninges. Which tissue type is it built from?\nA) Simple squamous epithelium  |  B) Hyaline cartilage\nC) Areolar connective tissue  |  D) Dense connective tissue\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Dense connective tissue","why":"The dura mater is a tough membrane of dense connective tissue, packed with collagen fibers that resist stretching and tearing. Its strength suits its position as the outermost protective layer against the skull and vertebral canal. The thinner arachnoid and pia are more delicate connective tissue layers."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the dura, arachnoid, and pia mater and the dural reflections falx cerebri, falx cerebelli, and tentorium cerebelli, and trace dural venous sinus drainage from the superior sagittal sinus to the internal jugular vein. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the dura, arachnoid, and pia mater and the dural reflections falx cerebri, falx cerebelli, and tentorium cerebelli, and trace dural venous sinus drainage from the superior sagittal sinus to the internal jugular vein."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the dura, arachnoid, and pia mater and the dural reflections falx cerebri, falx cerebelli, and tentorium cerebelli, and trace dural venous sinus drainage from the superior sagittal sinus to the internal jugular vein. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: cranial meninges and dural reflections. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, cranial meninges and dural reflections, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w7-spinal-meninges-spaces","name":"Spinal meninges and meningeal spaces","can":"Identify the spinal meninges, the denticulate ligaments and filum terminale, and distinguish the epidural, subdural, and subarachnoid spaces by location and contents.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"After a head injury, blood collects in the potential space just beneath the dura, between the dura and the arachnoid. Which condition is this?\nA) A subdural hematoma  |  B) An epidural hematoma\nC) Hydrocephalus  |  D) A subarachnoid hemorrhage\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) A subdural hematoma","why":"Sub means beneath, so a subdural hematoma is a collection of blood beneath the dura, in the subdural space between the dura and the arachnoid. That space is normally only a potential space, so the blood forces the two layers apart. Naming each bleed by its layers tells a clinician exactly where it is."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has fever, headache, and a stiff neck, and tests show inflammation of the membranes that wrap the brain and spinal cord, caused by an infection. Which disorder is this?\nA) Hydrocephalus  |  B) Meningitis\nC) Encephalitis  |  D) Epidural hematoma\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Meningitis","why":"Meningitis is inflammation of the meninges, the three connective tissue membranes around the brain and cord, usually caused by infection. The suffix itis means inflammation, and mening refers to the meninges. It is diagnosed by testing CSF drawn at a lumbar puncture, since the CSF flows between these membranes."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which meningeal space is normally only a potential space, with a thin film of fluid between its two layers?\nA) The epidural space  |  B) The subarachnoid space\nC) The subdural space  |  D) The lumbar cistern\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The subdural space","why":"The subdural space lies between the dura mater and the arachnoid mater. Normally these two layers lie against each other with only a thin film of fluid between them, which is why it is called a potential space. It becomes a real space only when something, such as blood from a torn vein, pushes the layers apart."},{"t":"mc","q":"An epidural hematoma is bleeding located between which two structures?\nA) The dura mater and the arachnoid mater  |  B) The dura mater and the skull\nC) The pia mater and the brain  |  D) The arachnoid mater and the pia mater\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The dura mater and the skull","why":"Epi means upon, so an epidural hematoma is a collection of blood on the outside of the dura, between the dura mater and the skull. In the cranium the dura is normally attached to the bone, so the bleeding strips the dura away from the skull as it collects. Each named bleed is defined by which layers it lies between."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which space lies between the spinal dura mater and the surrounding bone of the vertebral canal?\nA) The subdural space  |  B) The epidural space\nC) The subarachnoid space  |  D) The central canal\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The epidural space","why":"The spinal dura is a single layer that is not attached to bone, so a true epidural space lies between it and the vertebrae. This space is filled with fat and veins. Inside the skull, by contrast, the periosteal dura is attached to the bone, so the cranial epidural space is only a potential space."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the spinal meninges, the denticulate ligaments and filum terminale, and distinguish the epidural, subdural, and subarachnoid spaces by location and contents. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the spinal meninges, the denticulate ligaments and filum terminale, and distinguish the epidural, subdural, and subarachnoid spaces by location and contents."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the spinal meninges, the denticulate ligaments and filum terminale, and distinguish the epidural, subdural, and subarachnoid spaces by location and contents. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: spinal meninges and meningeal spaces. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, spinal meninges and meningeal spaces, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w7-cord-termination-lp","name":"Cord termination and lumbar puncture","can":"Identify the conus medullaris, cauda equina, and lumbar cistern and list in order the layers a lumbar puncture needle crosses from skin to subarachnoid space.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient needs CSF drawn for testing. The needle is placed in the midline between the L3 and L4 spinous processes. Which CSF-filled space is the target?\nA) The subdural space  |  B) The epidural space\nC) The lumbar cistern  |  D) The central canal\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The lumbar cistern","why":"The spinal cord ends at the conus medullaris near the L1 to L2 disc, but the subarachnoid space continues lower as the lumbar cistern, a pool of CSF holding only the floating cauda equina. A needle between L3 and L4 reaches this pool safely below the end of the cord. This mismatch between cord and column is the whole basis of the lumbar puncture."},{"t":"mc","q":"During a lumbar puncture, after crossing the epidural space, the needle pierces the tough outer meningeal membrane. Which membrane is this?\nA) The ligamentum flavum  |  B) The pia mater\nC) The arachnoid mater  |  D) The dura mater\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The dura mater","why":"A lumbar puncture needle passes through skin, fat, three ligaments, and the epidural space before meeting the meninges. The first membrane it pierces is the dura mater, the tough outer layer. It then passes the delicate arachnoid mater and enters the subarachnoid space, where CSF is withdrawn."},{"t":"mc","q":"A clinician performs a lumbar puncture between the L3 and L4 spines. Why is this site safe for the spinal cord?\nA) The dura ends at L2, so no membranes are pierced\nB) The denticulate ligaments pull the cord aside there\nC) The central canal widens into a large reservoir there\nD) The cord ends near L1 to L2, above the needle site\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The cord ends near L1 to L2, above the needle site","why":"The spinal cord ends at the conus medullaris near the L1 to L2 disc, but the subarachnoid space continues lower as the lumbar cistern. A needle placed between L3 and L4 enters this pool of CSF where only the loose roots of the cauda equina float, and they tend to drift aside. The cord itself is safely above the needle."},{"t":"mc","q":"A spinal tap, or lumbar puncture, draws cerebrospinal fluid from which space?\nA) The subdural space  |  B) The subarachnoid space\nC) A dural venous sinus  |  D) The epidural space\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The subarachnoid space","why":"A lumbar puncture withdraws CSF from the subarachnoid space in the lower back, where it widens into the lumbar cistern below the end of the cord. The needle passes the epidural space, then the dura and arachnoid, before CSF appears. Because the cord ends near the L1 to L2 disc, a needle placed lower meets only the floating cauda equina."},{"t":"mc","q":"During a lumbar puncture, which tough elastic ligament joining the laminae of adjacent vertebrae does the needle pass just before entering the epidural space?\nA) The interspinous ligament  |  B) The ligamentum flavum\nC) The denticulate ligament  |  D) The supraspinous ligament\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The ligamentum flavum","why":"From superficial to deep, a lumbar puncture needle crosses the skin, subcutaneous fat, the supraspinous ligament, the interspinous ligament, and then the ligamentum flavum before entering the epidural space. The ligamentum flavum is tough and elastic, and clinicians feel it give way as a landmark. Flavum means yellow, from the color of its elastic fibers."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the conus medullaris, cauda equina, and lumbar cistern and list in order the layers a lumbar puncture needle crosses from skin to subarachnoid space. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the conus medullaris, cauda equina, and lumbar cistern and list in order the layers a lumbar puncture needle crosses from skin to subarachnoid space."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the conus medullaris, cauda equina, and lumbar cistern and list in order the layers a lumbar puncture needle crosses from skin to subarachnoid space. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: cord termination and lumbar puncture. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, cord termination and lumbar puncture, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w7-ventricles","name":"Ventricular system","can":"Identify the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles and the central canal and describe how the interventricular foramina, cerebral aqueduct, and fourth ventricle apertures connect them.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A ventricle receives CSF from the cerebral aqueduct and passes it on to both the central canal and the subarachnoid space. Which ventricle is it?\nA) The central canal  |  B) The fourth ventricle\nC) A lateral ventricle  |  D) The third ventricle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The fourth ventricle","why":"The fourth ventricle sits between the brainstem and cerebellum, at the end of the ventricular route. CSF arrives from the cerebral aqueduct and leaves through three apertures (two lateral and one median) into the subarachnoid space, with some passing down into the central canal of the cord. It is the only ventricle that opens to the subarachnoid space."},{"t":"mc","q":"A tumor blocks the cerebral aqueduct. Which ventricles would fill with trapped cerebrospinal fluid and enlarge?\nA) Only the fourth ventricle below it\nB) Both lateral ventricles and the third\nC) Only the two lateral ventricles\nD) All four ventricles of the brain\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Both lateral ventricles and the third","why":"CSF moves from each lateral ventricle through an interventricular foramen into the third ventricle. The third ventricle drains down through the cerebral aqueduct into the fourth ventricle. A block in the aqueduct traps the fluid in every space above it, the third and both lateral ventricles, while the fourth ventricle lies below the block."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which ventricle is the fluid-filled cavity located within the diencephalon?\nA) The fourth ventricle  |  B) The third ventricle\nC) A lateral ventricle  |  D) The central canal\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The third ventricle","why":"The third ventricle is the narrow, midline cavity enclosed by the diencephalon, with the thalamus forming its side walls. It receives CSF from the lateral ventricles through the interventricular foramina and sends it on through the cerebral aqueduct to the fourth ventricle. Like the other ventricles, it contains choroid plexus."},{"t":"mc","q":"The lateral ventricles connect to the third ventricle through which openings?\nA) The cerebral aqueduct  |  B) The interventricular foramina\nC) The apertures of the fourth ventricle  |  D) The central canal\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The interventricular foramina","why":"Each lateral ventricle opens into the third ventricle through an interventricular foramen, also called a foramen of Monro. This is the first step in CSF flow after the fluid is made in the lateral ventricles. The name describes the location: inter means between, so these foramina lie between ventricles."},{"t":"mc","q":"The third and fourth ventricles are connected by which channel?\nA) The central canal  |  B) The cerebral aqueduct\nC) An arachnoid granulation  |  D) The interventricular foramina\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The cerebral aqueduct","why":"The cerebral aqueduct, also called the aqueduct of Sylvius, is a narrow channel running through the midbrain. It carries CSF from the third ventricle in the diencephalon down to the fourth ventricle behind the pons. Because it is so narrow, a blockage here backs CSF up into the third and lateral ventricles and causes hydrocephalus."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles and the central canal and describe how the interventricular foramina, cerebral aqueduct, and fourth ventricle apertures connect them. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles and the central canal and describe how the interventricular foramina, cerebral aqueduct, and fourth ventricle apertures connect them."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles and the central canal and describe how the interventricular foramina, cerebral aqueduct, and fourth ventricle apertures connect them. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: ventricular system. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, ventricular system, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w7-csf-circulation","name":"CSF production and circulation","can":"Trace CSF from production at the choroid plexus through the ventricles and subarachnoid space to reabsorption at the arachnoid granulations.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A child's scan shows enlarged lateral ventricles and an enlarged third ventricle, but a normal-sized fourth ventricle. Where is the CSF most likely blocked?\nA) At one interventricular foramen  |  B) At the arachnoid granulations\nC) At the cerebral aqueduct  |  D) At the apertures of the fourth ventricle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) At the cerebral aqueduct","why":"CSF backs up behind a blockage, so every chamber upstream enlarges and every chamber downstream stays normal. The lateral and third ventricles are upstream of the cerebral aqueduct, while the fourth ventricle lies downstream of it. The aqueduct is a narrow channel through the midbrain, which makes it a common site of blockage."},{"t":"mc","q":"If CSF production continued normally but reabsorption at the arachnoid granulations were blocked, which condition would result?\nA) Subarachnoid hemorrhage  |  B) Hydrocephalus\nC) Subdural hematoma  |  D) Meningitis\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Hydrocephalus","why":"CSF volume stays steady only when reabsorption keeps pace with production. If the arachnoid granulations cannot return fluid to the venous blood, CSF accumulates, the ventricles enlarge, and pressure inside the rigid skull rises. That buildup of CSF is hydrocephalus."},{"t":"mc","q":"The brain floats in cerebrospinal fluid inside the skull. How does this fluid protect the brain?\nA) It insulates the axons so that signals travel faster\nB) It filters harmful substances out of the blood\nC) It buoys the brain and cushions it against blows\nD) It anchors the brain firmly against the skull\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) It buoys the brain and cushions it against blows","why":"Cerebrospinal fluid fills the subarachnoid space around the brain and spinal cord. Floating in it greatly reduces the brain's effective weight, so the soft tissue does not crush its own base. The fluid also acts as a liquid cushion that spreads out the force of a jolt or blow before the brain strikes bone."},{"t":"mc","q":"Cerebrospinal fluid is returned to the venous blood through projections of which meningeal layer?\nA) The dura mater  |  B) The pia mater\nC) The ependymal lining  |  D) The arachnoid mater\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The arachnoid mater","why":"The arachnoid mater sends small projections called arachnoid granulations up through the dura into the dural venous sinuses, especially the superior sagittal sinus. CSF in the subarachnoid space passes through these granulations into the venous blood. This completes the one-way loop of CSF, from production in the ventricles to reabsorption into the blood."},{"t":"mc","q":"Hydrocephalus is a buildup of cerebrospinal fluid that enlarges which structures?\nA) The subdural spaces  |  B) The arachnoid granulations\nC) The dural sinuses  |  D) The ventricles\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The ventricles","why":"In hydrocephalus, CSF builds up because its flow or reabsorption is blocked, so the ventricles fill and enlarge. Because the skull cannot expand in an adult, intracranial pressure rises and the enlarging ventricles press on the brain. Narrow points such as the cerebral aqueduct are common places for a blockage."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Trace CSF from production at the choroid plexus through the ventricles and subarachnoid space to reabsorption at the arachnoid granulations. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to trace CSF from production at the choroid plexus through the ventricles and subarachnoid space to reabsorption at the arachnoid granulations."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to trace CSF from production at the choroid plexus through the ventricles and subarachnoid space to reabsorption at the arachnoid granulations. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: CSF production and circulation. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, CSF production and circulation, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w7-bbb","name":"Blood-brain barrier structure","can":"Name the three structural components of the blood-brain barrier the tight-junctioned capillary endothelium, the basement membrane, and the astrocyte end-feet.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Which three components build the blood-brain barrier?\nA) Endothelium, basement membrane, astrocyte end-feet\nB) The dura, the arachnoid, and the pia mater\nC) Ependymal cells, the choroid plexus, and the pia mater\nD) Microglia, oligodendrocytes, and Schwann cell sheaths\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Endothelium, basement membrane, astrocyte end-feet","why":"The blood-brain barrier is the seal that controls what passes from the blood into brain tissue. Its first layer is the endothelial cells lining brain capillaries, joined by tight junctions and lacking the pores of ordinary capillaries. These cells sit on a basement membrane and are wrapped by the end-feet of astrocytes, which help control the barrier."},{"t":"mc","q":"How do the endothelial cells of brain capillaries differ from those of ordinary capillaries, helping form the blood-brain barrier?\nA) They are full of pores that let solutes pass freely\nB) They are lined on the inside by ependymal cells\nC) They are joined by tight junctions and lack pores\nD) They are wrapped in myelin from oligodendrocytes\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) They are joined by tight junctions and lack pores","why":"In most capillaries, small gaps or pores let substances leak from the blood into the tissue. The endothelial cells of brain capillaries are sealed together by tight junctions and lack those pores, so substances must pass through the cells themselves. A basement membrane and astrocyte end-feet surround these capillaries to complete the barrier."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which statement best describes the main job of the blood-brain barrier?\nA) It returns cerebrospinal fluid to the venous blood\nB) It limits what passes from blood into brain tissue\nC) It cushions the brain against sudden blows to the head\nD) It produces cerebrospinal fluid from blood plasma\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) It limits what passes from blood into brain tissue","why":"Neurons need a stable chemical environment, but the chemistry of the blood changes constantly. The blood-brain barrier is built from capillary endothelial cells sealed by tight junctions, a basement membrane, and the end-feet of astrocytes wrapped around the capillary. Together they let needed substances through while keeping out many toxins, drugs, and pathogens."},{"t":"mc","q":"Brain capillaries have tight junctions and no pores. How must most substances cross from the blood into brain tissue?\nA) By slipping through the gaps between the endothelial cells\nB) By passing through the endothelial cells themselves\nC) Through pores in the capillary wall\nD) They cannot cross at all, so the brain takes nothing from the blood\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) By passing through the endothelial cells themselves","why":"The endothelial cells lining brain capillaries are joined by tight junctions that seal the gaps between them, and these capillaries lack the pores and wide clefts found in many capillaries elsewhere. With no way around the cells, most substances can cross only by passing through the endothelial cells themselves. That is why the capillary endothelium is the most important part of the barrier."},{"t":"mc","q":"Moving from the blood outward into brain tissue, in which order do you cross the parts of the blood-brain barrier?\nA) Basement membrane, endothelium, astrocyte end-feet\nB) Astrocyte end-feet, basement membrane, endothelium\nC) Endothelium, astrocyte end-feet, basement membrane\nD) Endothelium, basement membrane, astrocyte end-feet\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Endothelium, basement membrane, astrocyte end-feet","why":"The capillary endothelium lines the vessel, so it is the first layer the blood touches. The basement membrane surrounds the endothelium, and the astrocyte end-feet wrap the outside of the capillary, covering the basement membrane."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Name the three structural components of the blood-brain barrier the tight-junctioned capillary endothelium, the basement membrane, and the astrocyte end-feet. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to name the three structural components of the blood-brain barrier the tight-junctioned capillary endothelium, the basement membrane, and the astrocyte end-feet."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to name the three structural components of the blood-brain barrier the tight-junctioned capillary endothelium, the basement membrane, and the astrocyte end-feet. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: blood-brain barrier structure. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, blood-brain barrier structure, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w8-ns-organization","name":"Nervous system divisions","can":"Outline the organization of the nervous system into CNS and PNS and its somatic, autonomic, and enteric motor divisions.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The sensory division of the peripheral nervous system is also known by which name?\nA) The afferent division  |  B) The autonomic division\nC) The somatic division  |  D) The efferent division\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The afferent division","why":"Afferent means carrying toward, so the afferent (sensory) division carries signals from sensory receptors toward the CNS. Its partner, the efferent (motor) division, carries commands away from the CNS to the muscles and glands. Afferent signals arrive at the CNS, and efferent signals exit it."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which subdivision of the motor division carries voluntary commands to skeletal muscle?\nA) The somatic nervous system  |  B) The enteric nervous system\nC) The sensory (afferent) division  |  D) The autonomic nervous system\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The somatic nervous system","why":"The motor (efferent) division of the PNS is split by the kind of effector it controls. The somatic nervous system is the voluntary branch, and its motor neurons carry commands from the CNS directly to skeletal muscle. The autonomic nervous system, by contrast, controls effectors that work without conscious control."},{"t":"mc","q":"The motor division of the peripheral nervous system is also known by which name?\nA) The efferent division  |  B) The autonomic division\nC) The somatic division  |  D) The afferent division\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The efferent division","why":"Efferent means carrying away, so the efferent (motor) division carries commands from the CNS out to the effectors, the muscles and glands. It includes the somatic nervous system for skeletal muscle, the autonomic nervous system for smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands, and the enteric nervous system in the wall of the digestive tract. Its counterpart is the afferent (sensory) division, which carries input toward the CNS."},{"t":"mc","q":"You touch a hot pan and pull your hand away. Which sequence lists the three functions of the nervous system in the order they happen?\nA) Sensory, integrative, motor  |  B) Integrative, sensory, motor\nC) Motor, sensory, integrative  |  D) Sensory, motor, integrative\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Sensory, integrative, motor","why":"The nervous system handles every task in three steps. In the sensory function, receptors detect a change, here the heat, and send input toward the CNS. In the integrative function, the CNS analyzes the input and decides on a response, and in the motor function, commands go out to the effectors, here the arm muscles that pull the hand away."},{"t":"mc","q":"The sensory receptors in the skin belong to which division of the nervous system?\nA) The central nervous system  |  B) The peripheral nervous system\nC) The motor (efferent) division  |  D) The autonomic nervous system\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The peripheral nervous system","why":"The peripheral nervous system includes every neural structure outside the brain and spinal cord: the nerves, the ganglia, and the sensory receptors. Receptors in the skin detect changes such as touch and temperature and start signals that sensory neurons carry toward the CNS. They feed the sensory (afferent) side of the PNS."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Outline the organization of the nervous system into CNS and PNS and its somatic, autonomic, and enteric motor divisions. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to outline the organization of the nervous system into CNS and PNS and its somatic, autonomic, and enteric motor divisions."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to outline the organization of the nervous system into CNS and PNS and its somatic, autonomic, and enteric motor divisions. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: nervous system divisions. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, nervous system divisions, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w8-neuron-parts","name":"Neuron structure","can":"Identify the cell body, dendrites, axon, axon hillock, Nissl bodies, and axon terminals of a neuron and state the direction a signal travels through them.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Within a neuron, which structures receive signals and carry them toward the cell body?\nA) The axon terminals  |  B) The axon hillock\nC) The axon  |  D) The dendrites\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The dendrites","why":"Dendrites are the short, branched, tree-like processes of a neuron, and they are its main receiving surface. Signals from other neurons arrive at synapses on the dendrites and travel toward the cell body. From there the signal can continue to the axon hillock and out along the axon."},{"t":"mc","q":"The cell body of a neuron is also known by which single-word term?\nA) The axolemma  |  B) The axoplasm\nC) The soma  |  D) The neurolemma\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The soma","why":"Soma means body, so soma is the one-word name for the cell body of a neuron; perikaryon is another name for the same part. The soma holds the nucleus and the Nissl bodies and is the metabolic center of the neuron. The look-alike terms axoplasm, axolemma, and neurolemma all describe the axon or its wrapping instead."},{"t":"mc","q":"On a neuron model, you point to the fine branches at the far end of the axon that pass the signal to the next cell. Which part are you pointing to?\nA) The dendrites  |  B) The cell body\nC) The axon hillock  |  D) The axon terminals\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The axon terminals","why":"The axon ends by splitting into fine branches called axon terminals, and each terminal ends in a synaptic end bulb that contacts the next cell. These terminals are where the neuron hands its signal off to another neuron, a muscle cell, or a gland. Dendrites look branched too, but they are at the receiving end, clustered around the cell body."},{"t":"mc","q":"The axon hillock forms the junction between which two parts of a neuron?\nA) Two neighboring myelin segments  |  B) The cell body and the axon\nC) The dendrites and the cell body  |  D) The axon and its terminals\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The cell body and the axon","why":"The axon hillock is the cone-shaped region of the cell body that tapers into the beginning of the axon. It is the boundary between the receiving and processing part of the neuron, the cell body, and the conducting part, the axon. Each neuron has one axon, so it has one axon hillock."},{"t":"mc","q":"What is the axon hillock?\nA) The gaps in the myelin sheath spaced along the axon\nB) The swelling on a spinal root that holds sensory cell bodies\nC) The fine branching ends of the axon that contact the target cell\nD) The cone-shaped region joining the cell body to the axon\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The cone-shaped region joining the cell body to the axon","why":"The axon hillock is the cone-shaped part of the cell body that narrows down into the axon. It marks where the cell body ends and the axon begins, so it is the landmark for finding the start of the axon on a neuron model or slide. A hillock is a small hill, which describes its tapered, mound-like shape."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the cell body, dendrites, axon, axon hillock, Nissl bodies, and axon terminals of a neuron and state the direction a signal travels through them. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the cell body, dendrites, axon, axon hillock, Nissl bodies, and axon terminals of a neuron and state the direction a signal travels through them."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the cell body, dendrites, axon, axon hillock, Nissl bodies, and axon terminals of a neuron and state the direction a signal travels through them. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: neuron structure. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, neuron structure, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w8-synapse-parts","name":"Synapse structures","can":"Identify the components of a chemical synapse including synaptic end bulbs, synaptic vesicles, and synaptic cleft, and name the three synapse types by the site they contact.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"At a chemical synapse, which sequence lists the structures in order from the sending neuron to the receiving cell?\nA) Synaptic cleft, synaptic vesicles, postsynaptic cell\nB) Synaptic vesicles, synaptic cleft, postsynaptic cell\nC) Postsynaptic cell, synaptic cleft, synaptic vesicles\nD) Synaptic vesicles, postsynaptic cell, synaptic cleft\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Synaptic vesicles, synaptic cleft, postsynaptic cell","why":"The sending neuron stores neurotransmitter in synaptic vesicles inside its synaptic end bulbs. When a signal arrives, the vesicles release the neurotransmitter into the synaptic cleft, the narrow gap between the cells. The neurotransmitter diffuses across the cleft and reaches the postsynaptic cell on the other side."},{"t":"mc","q":"The neuromuscular junction is the synapse between a motor neuron and which structure?\nA) Another neuron's dendrite  |  B) A skeletal muscle fiber\nC) A secretory gland cell  |  D) A supporting glial cell\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A skeletal muscle fiber","why":"A synapse is any site where a neuron communicates with another neuron, a muscle cell, or a gland. The neuromuscular junction is the specific synapse where the axon terminal of a somatic motor neuron meets a skeletal muscle fiber. The neurotransmitter released there crosses the cleft and triggers the muscle fiber to contract."},{"t":"mc","q":"At a synapse, which term names the neuron that sends the signal across the cleft?\nA) The afferent neuron  |  B) The presynaptic neuron\nC) The postsynaptic neuron  |  D) The efferent neuron\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The presynaptic neuron","why":"At a synapse, pre means before and post means after, measured from the synaptic cleft. The presynaptic neuron is the sender, and its synaptic end bulbs release neurotransmitter into the cleft. The postsynaptic cell, which may be a neuron, muscle cell, or gland cell, receives that neurotransmitter on the far side."},{"t":"mc","q":"An axon ends on a dendrite of another neuron. What type of synapse is this?\nA) An axosomatic synapse  |  B) An axodendritic synapse\nC) An axoaxonic synapse  |  D) A neuromuscular junction\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) An axodendritic synapse","why":"Synapses between neurons are named for the two parts that meet, always starting with the axon of the sending neuron. An axon ending on a dendrite is axodendritic, on a cell body is axosomatic, and on another axon is axoaxonic. Dendrites are the most common receiving site because they are the receiving processes of the neuron."},{"t":"mc","q":"An axon ends on the cell body of another neuron. What type of synapse is this?\nA) An axoaxonic synapse  |  B) A neuromuscular junction\nC) An axosomatic synapse  |  D) An axodendritic synapse\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) An axosomatic synapse","why":"Synapses between neurons are named for the parts that meet, starting with the axon of the sending neuron. The cell body is also called the soma, so an axon ending on a cell body forms an axosomatic synapse. The other two types are axodendritic, ending on a dendrite, and axoaxonic, ending on another axon."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the components of a chemical synapse including synaptic end bulbs, synaptic vesicles, and synaptic cleft, and name the three synapse types by the site they contact. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the components of a chemical synapse including synaptic end bulbs, synaptic vesicles, and synaptic cleft, and name the three synapse types by the site they contact."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the components of a chemical synapse including synaptic end bulbs, synaptic vesicles, and synaptic cleft, and name the three synapse types by the site they contact. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: synapse structures. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, synapse structures, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w8-neuron-classification","name":"Neuron classification","can":"Classify neurons structurally as multipolar, bipolar, or unipolar and functionally as sensory, motor, or interneuron with an example of each.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"In a withdrawal reflex, a neuron inside the gray matter of the spinal cord links the incoming sensory neuron to the outgoing motor neuron. By function, which type of neuron is it?\nA) An interneuron  |  B) A motor neuron\nC) A bipolar neuron  |  D) A sensory neuron\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) An interneuron","why":"Interneurons, also called association neurons, lie entirely within the brain and spinal cord and connect sensory neurons to motor neurons. In a withdrawal reflex the sensory neuron enters the posterior horn of the cord, where it synapses with interneurons that then pass the signal to motor neurons. Because the pathway has interneurons, the withdrawal reflex is a polysynaptic reflex."},{"t":"mc","q":"An interneuron lies entirely within which division of the nervous system?\nA) The autonomic nervous system  |  B) The somatic nervous system\nC) The central nervous system  |  D) The peripheral nervous system\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The central nervous system","why":"Interneurons, also called association neurons, lie entirely within the brain and spinal cord. They connect sensory neurons to motor neurons and link neurons to each other, so they carry out much of the processing the CNS performs. Sensory and motor neurons, by contrast, have long processes that extend out into the PNS."},{"t":"mc","q":"By function, which type of neuron carries signals from receptors toward the central nervous system?\nA) The sensory neuron  |  B) The interneuron\nC) The motor neuron  |  D) The multipolar neuron\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The sensory neuron","why":"Neurons are classified by function according to the direction they carry signals relative to the CNS. Sensory (afferent) neurons carry input from receptors toward the CNS, and most of them are unipolar in shape. Motor neurons carry commands away from the CNS, and interneurons connect the two inside the CNS."},{"t":"mc","q":"Neurons are sorted into multipolar, bipolar, and unipolar types according to which feature?\nA) The direction in which they carry signals\nB) The type of glial cell that supports them\nC) Whether their axon has a myelin sheath\nD) How many processes leave the cell body\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) How many processes leave the cell body","why":"The structural classification of neurons is based on shape, specifically how many processes leave the cell body. Multipolar neurons have many (several dendrites and one axon), bipolar neurons have two (one dendrite and one axon), and unipolar neurons have a single process that then splits. The other way to classify neurons is functional, by the direction they carry signals."},{"t":"mc","q":"Pyramidal cells of the cerebral cortex and Purkinje cells of the cerebellum share which structural feature?\nA) Both are glial support cells  |  B) Both are multipolar neurons\nC) Both are unipolar neurons  |  D) Both are bipolar neurons\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Both are multipolar neurons","why":"Pyramidal cells in the cerebral cortex and Purkinje cells in the cerebellum are two named examples of multipolar neurons, each with many dendrites and one axon. They have distinctive shapes, which is why they are given their own names, but structurally they still fall in the multipolar class. Multipolar neurons are the most common type in the brain and spinal cord."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Classify neurons structurally as multipolar, bipolar, or unipolar and functionally as sensory, motor, or interneuron with an example of each. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to classify neurons structurally as multipolar, bipolar, or unipolar and functionally as sensory, motor, or interneuron with an example of each."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to classify neurons structurally as multipolar, bipolar, or unipolar and functionally as sensory, motor, or interneuron with an example of each. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: neuron classification. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, neuron classification, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w8-neuroglia","name":"Neuroglia identification","can":"Name the four CNS glia and two PNS glia and describe the support role each one performs.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"After a small stroke, glial cells in the damaged brain tissue engulf the dead cells and debris. Which glial cells are doing this?\nA) Oligodendrocytes  |  B) Astrocytes\nC) Microglia  |  D) Ependymal cells\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Microglia","why":"Microglia are small phagocytic glial cells of the CNS, meaning they engulf and digest material. When brain tissue is damaged, as in a stroke, they move in and remove dead cells, debris, and any microbes. This makes them the defense and cleanup crew of the brain and spinal cord."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which glial cell helps form the blood-brain barrier?\nA) The astrocyte  |  B) The oligodendrocyte\nC) The ependymal cell  |  D) The microglia\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The astrocyte","why":"Astrocytes are star-shaped glial cells of the CNS. Their processes wrap around the capillaries of the brain and help form the blood-brain barrier, which limits what can pass from the blood into brain tissue. They also give structural support, guide the growth of neurons, and keep the chemical environment around neurons stable."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which glial cell lines the cavities of the brain and spinal cord and helps circulate cerebrospinal fluid?\nA) The ependymal cell  |  B) The astrocyte\nC) The oligodendrocyte  |  D) The microglia\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The ependymal cell","why":"Ependymal cells are CNS glia that line the ventricles of the brain and the central canal of the spinal cord. They help produce cerebrospinal fluid and help move it through those spaces. Their position as the lining of the fluid-filled cavities is what sets them apart from the other three CNS glia."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which glial cell surrounds and supports the neuron cell bodies in peripheral ganglia?\nA) The oligodendrocyte  |  B) The satellite cell\nC) The astrocyte  |  D) The Schwann cell\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The satellite cell","why":"Satellite cells are PNS glia that form a layer around each neuron cell body inside a ganglion, such as a posterior root ganglion. They support those cell bodies in the same way that astrocytes support neurons in the CNS. The PNS has only two kinds of glia: satellite cells around cell bodies and Schwann cells around axons."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which pair names the two types of glial cells found in the peripheral nervous system?\nA) Astrocytes and microglia  |  B) Satellite cells and ependymal cells\nC) Schwann cells and satellite cells  |  D) Schwann cells and oligodendrocytes\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Schwann cells and satellite cells","why":"The PNS has two kinds of glia. Schwann cells wrap peripheral axons in myelin and form the neurolemma, and satellite cells surround and support the neuron cell bodies inside ganglia. The other four glial types, astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, microglia, and ependymal cells, are found in the CNS."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Name the four CNS glia and two PNS glia and describe the support role each one performs. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to name the four CNS glia and two PNS glia and describe the support role each one performs."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to name the four CNS glia and two PNS glia and describe the support role each one performs. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: neuroglia identification. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, neuroglia identification, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w8-myelination","name":"Myelination and regeneration","can":"Describe the myelin sheath, nodes of Ranvier, and neurolemma and contrast PNS and CNS myelination by the glial cell involved and by regeneration potential.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has a slow-growing mass of dividing cells in the brain. Which cell type most likely gave rise to it?\nA) A multipolar neuron  |  B) A glial cell\nC) An interneuron  |  D) A sensory neuron\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A glial cell","why":"A tumor is a mass of dividing cells, so it must start from a cell type that can still divide. Mature neurons generally do not divide, but glial cells can. A tumor of glial cells is a glioma, the most common primary malignant brain tumor."},{"t":"mc","q":"An infant's nerve impulses travel more slowly than an adult's. Which structural difference best explains this?\nA) The infant has less myelination  |  B) The infant's neurons lack dendrites\nC) The infant's neurons lack nuclei  |  D) The infant has fewer neurons\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The infant has less myelination","why":"Myelin insulates axons and lets the impulse jump from node to node, which makes conduction much faster. Myelination is not complete at birth; it increases from birth to maturity. With less myelin, an infant's nerve conduction is slower than an adult's."},{"t":"mc","q":"A surgeon repairing a severed nerve in the forearm lines up the cut ends so the axons can regrow. Which feature of the PNS makes this regrowth possible?\nA) The neurolemma of Schwann cells  |  B) The end-feet of astrocytes\nC) The phagocytic action of microglia  |  D) The myelin of the oligodendrocytes\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The neurolemma of Schwann cells","why":"In the PNS, each Schwann cell leaves its outer layer, the neurolemma, wrapped around the axon. After a cut, the neurolemma forms a tube that guides the regrowing axon back toward its target. In the CNS, oligodendrocytes leave no neurolemma, which is why a cut nerve in the arm can regrow but a cut tract in the cord usually cannot."},{"t":"mc","q":"In multiple sclerosis, the immune system strips a covering from CNS axons and signals slow or fail. Which structure is being lost?\nA) The dendrites  |  B) The cell body\nC) The myelin sheath  |  D) The synaptic vesicles\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The myelin sheath","why":"The myelin sheath is a lipid-rich covering that insulates axons and lets the impulse jump from node to node. In multiple sclerosis, the immune system destroys the myelin around CNS axons, the myelin made by oligodendrocytes. Without it, signals along those axons slow down or fail."},{"t":"mc","q":"A nerve in the forearm is cut cleanly. Which part of each cut axon dies?\nA) The part beyond the cut, away from the cell body\nB) The part still attached to the cell body\nC) Only the cell body, while the whole axon survives\nD) Neither part, because an axon can live on its own\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The part beyond the cut, away from the cell body","why":"The cell body is the life support of the neuron because its Nissl bodies make the proteins that the axon needs. When an axon is cut, the part beyond the cut is separated from that supply and dies. The part still attached to the cell body survives, and in the PNS it can regrow along the tube formed by the neurolemma."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Describe the myelin sheath, nodes of Ranvier, and neurolemma and contrast PNS and CNS myelination by the glial cell involved and by regeneration potential. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to describe the myelin sheath, nodes of Ranvier, and neurolemma and contrast PNS and CNS myelination by the glial cell involved and by regeneration potential."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to describe the myelin sheath, nodes of Ranvier, and neurolemma and contrast PNS and CNS myelination by the glial cell involved and by regeneration potential. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: myelination and regeneration. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, myelination and regeneration, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w8-cranial-nerves","name":"Cranial nerves","can":"Name the twelve cranial nerves in order, classify each as sensory, motor, or mixed, and match each to its primary function and skull foramen.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A tumor at the jugular foramen causes trouble swallowing, a hoarse voice, and weakness shrugging one shoulder. Which set of cranial nerves is compressed?\nA) CN VII, CN VIII, and CN XII  |  B) CN IX, CN X, and CN XI\nC) CN III, CN IV, and CN VI  |  D) CN V, CN VII, and CN VIII\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) CN IX, CN X, and CN XI","why":"The jugular foramen carries the glossopharyngeal (IX), vagus (X), and accessory (XI) nerves side by side. A mass there can press on all three at once. The glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves serve swallowing and the voice, and the accessory nerve serves the shoulder, producing this recognizable cluster of signs."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient wakes up with one side of the face drooping and cannot close that eye or smile on that side. Which cranial nerve is involved in this Bell's palsy?\nA) The accessory nerve (CN XI)  |  B) The facial nerve (CN VII)\nC) The trigeminal nerve (CN V)  |  D) The oculomotor nerve (CN III)\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The facial nerve (CN VII)","why":"Bell's palsy is a sudden weakness of the facial nerve, CN VII, which controls facial expression. Each facial nerve serves only its own side, so the affected side droops while the other side works normally. Taste from the front of the tongue can also be affected because CN VII carries it."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has brief, stabbing pain along the cheek and jaw on one side of the face. Which cranial nerve is the source of this trigeminal neuralgia?\nA) The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)\nB) The vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII)\nC) The trigeminal nerve (CN V)\nD) The facial nerve (CN VII)\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The trigeminal nerve (CN V)","why":"Trigeminal neuralgia is a stabbing facial pain that follows the branches of CN V, the trigeminal nerve, which carries touch and pain from the face. The cheek is served by V2 and the jaw by V3, so pain along these areas matches the nerve's branches. The anatomy of the nerve predicts exactly where the pain appears."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient's eye movement problem is traced to a nucleus in the midbrain. The nerve involved is purely motor, but the eyelid and pupil are normal. Which nerve is most likely affected?\nA) The oculomotor nerve (CN III)  |  B) The trochlear nerve (CN IV)\nC) The optic nerve (CN II)  |  D) The abducens nerve (CN VI)\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The trochlear nerve (CN IV)","why":"Two cranial nerves have their motor nuclei in the midbrain, the oculomotor (III) and the trochlear (IV), and both move the eye. The oculomotor nerve also raises the eyelid and constricts the pupil, so a normal eyelid and pupil point away from it. That leaves the trochlear nerve, the smallest cranial nerve, which moves the eye through a single muscle."},{"t":"mc","q":"A fracture runs through the superior orbital fissure. Which group of cranial nerves is most at risk?\nA) CN IX, X, and XI  |  B) CN III, IV, V1, and VI\nC) CN II, III, IV, and VI  |  D) CN VII and VIII only\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) CN III, IV, V1, and VI","why":"The superior orbital fissure is the slit between the greater and lesser wings of the sphenoid at the back of the orbit. The nerves that move the eye, CN III, IV, and VI, pass through it along with V1, the ophthalmic branch of the trigeminal nerve. Damage here can cause eye movement problems and numbness of the forehead together."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Name the twelve cranial nerves in order, classify each as sensory, motor, or mixed, and match each to its primary function and skull foramen. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to name the twelve cranial nerves in order, classify each as sensory, motor, or mixed, and match each to its primary function and skull foramen."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to name the twelve cranial nerves in order, classify each as sensory, motor, or mixed, and match each to its primary function and skull foramen. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: cranial nerves. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, cranial nerves, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]}]},{"week":17,"system":"Nervous System","comps":[{"id":"w8-tissue-collections","name":"CNS and PNS tissue terms","can":"Define nucleus, ganglion, tract, and nerve and state whether each names a cluster of cell bodies or a bundle of axons in the CNS or PNS.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"Nerves and ganglia have one feature in common. Which statement describes it?\nA) Both are bundles of axons  |  B) Both are clusters of cell bodies\nC) Both lie inside the CNS  |  D) Both lie outside the CNS\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Both lie outside the CNS","why":"Nerves and ganglia are the two main structures of the peripheral nervous system, which includes every neural structure outside the brain and spinal cord. A nerve is a bundle of axons and a ganglion is a cluster of neuron cell bodies, so they differ in what they contain but share their location outside the CNS. Inside the CNS, the matching structures are called tracts and nuclei."},{"t":"mc","q":"A cluster of neuron cell bodies sits deep inside the brain. What is this cluster called?\nA) A ganglion  |  B) A nerve\nC) A nucleus  |  D) A tract\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A nucleus","why":"In nervous tissue, a cluster of neuron cell bodies inside the CNS is called a nucleus, and the matching cluster outside the CNS is called a ganglion. Because this cluster sits inside the brain, it is a nucleus. This use of nucleus is different from the nucleus of a single cell, which is the organelle that holds its DNA."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which pair of terms both name bundles of axons?\nA) Nucleus and ganglion  |  B) Nerve and ganglion\nC) Tract and nucleus  |  D) Tract and nerve\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Tract and nerve","why":"The nervous system uses four terms that sort into two pairs. A bundle of axons is a tract in the CNS and a nerve in the PNS, while a cluster of cell bodies is a nucleus in the CNS and a ganglion in the PNS. Deciding first whether the structure is axons or cell bodies, and then whether it is inside or outside the CNS, leads to the right term."},{"t":"mc","q":"Inside the CNS, which pair of terms names a bundle of axons and a cluster of cell bodies?\nA) Tract for the axon bundle, nucleus for the cell-body cluster\nB) Tract for the axon bundle, ganglion for the cell-body cluster\nC) Nerve for the axon bundle, nucleus for the cell-body cluster\nD) Nerve for the axon bundle, ganglion for the cell-body cluster\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Tract for the axon bundle, nucleus for the cell-body cluster","why":"The same structures take different names inside and outside the CNS. Inside the brain and spinal cord, a bundle of axons is a tract and a cluster of cell bodies is a nucleus. Outside the CNS, they are a nerve and a ganglion."},{"t":"mc","q":"In the central nervous system, a cluster of neuron cell bodies is called what?\nA) A tract  |  B) A nucleus\nC) A nerve  |  D) A ganglion\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A nucleus","why":"The nervous system uses different names for the same kind of structure depending on whether it is in the CNS or the PNS. A cluster of cell bodies is a nucleus in the CNS and a ganglion in the PNS. A bundle of axons is a tract in the CNS and a nerve in the PNS."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Define nucleus, ganglion, tract, and nerve and state whether each names a cluster of cell bodies or a bundle of axons in the CNS or PNS. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to define nucleus, ganglion, tract, and nerve and state whether each names a cluster of cell bodies or a bundle of axons in the CNS or PNS."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to define nucleus, ganglion, tract, and nerve and state whether each names a cluster of cell bodies or a bundle of axons in the CNS or PNS. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: CNS and PNS tissue terms. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, CNS and PNS tissue terms, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w8-gray-white-matter","name":"Gray and white matter","can":"Distinguish gray matter from white matter by composition and locate each in the brain and the spinal cord.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A brain MRI shows damage in the band of myelinated axons just beneath the cerebral cortex. Which tissue is damaged?\nA) Gray matter  |  B) A ganglion\nC) White matter  |  D) The pia mater\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) White matter","why":"In the brain, gray matter forms the outer cortex and white matter lies beneath it. White matter is made of myelinated axons running in bundles, and the myelin gives it its color. Damage to the band of myelinated axons under the cortex is therefore damage to white matter, the kind of change a radiologist looks for on a scan."},{"t":"mc","q":"The corpus callosum is a broad band of myelinated axons linking the two cerebral hemispheres. Which tissue type is it?\nA) A nucleus  |  B) A ganglion\nC) Gray matter  |  D) White matter\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) White matter","why":"White matter is made of myelinated axons running together in bundles, and the lipid-rich myelin gives it its white color. The corpus callosum is a large band of white matter that carries axons between the right and left cerebral hemispheres. In the cerebrum, white matter lies beneath the gray cortex."},{"t":"mc","q":"The cortex of the cerebellum is made mostly of neuron cell bodies and dendrites. Which tissue type is it?\nA) Gray matter  |  B) White matter\nC) A peripheral nerve  |  D) A ganglion\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Gray matter","why":"Gray matter is made of neuron cell bodies, dendrites, and unmyelinated axons. In the brain, including the cerebellum, gray matter forms the outer cortex and white matter lies beneath it. The spinal cord is arranged the other way around, with gray matter inside."},{"t":"mc","q":"Why does white matter look white?\nA) It is packed with neuron cell bodies\nB) Its axons are wrapped in fatty myelin\nC) It is filled with cerebrospinal fluid\nD) It contains no blood vessels at all\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Its axons are wrapped in fatty myelin","why":"White matter is made of myelinated axons, and myelin is a multilayered covering rich in lipid. That fatty material is what makes the tissue look white. Gray matter has few myelinated axons and many cell bodies, so it looks gray instead."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which description fits gray matter?\nA) Cell bodies, dendrites, and unmyelinated axons\nB) Bundles of myelinated axons wrapped by Schwann cells\nC) Connective tissue that wraps fascicles of nerve axons\nD) Myelinated axons running in long bundles called tracts\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Cell bodies, dendrites, and unmyelinated axons","why":"Gray matter is made of neuron cell bodies, dendrites, unmyelinated axons, and neuroglia. Because it lacks the fatty myelin that makes white matter pale, it looks gray. Gray matter is where synapses happen and information is processed, such as the cerebral cortex and the inner core of the spinal cord."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Distinguish gray matter from white matter by composition and locate each in the brain and the spinal cord. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to distinguish gray matter from white matter by composition and locate each in the brain and the spinal cord."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to distinguish gray matter from white matter by composition and locate each in the brain and the spinal cord. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: gray and white matter. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, gray and white matter, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w8-reflex-arc","name":"Reflex arc components","can":"Name the five components of a reflex arc in order from receptor to effector and trace a signal through them.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"In a withdrawal reflex, which sequence traces the signal from the structure that detects the stimulus to the synapses where it is processed?\nA) Receptor, sensory neuron, integration center\nB) Effector, motor neuron, integration center\nC) Receptor, integration center, sensory neuron\nD) Sensory neuron, receptor, integration center\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Receptor, sensory neuron, integration center","why":"The first half of a reflex arc brings information in. The receptor in the skin detects the painful stimulus, the sensory neuron carries the signal into the spinal cord, and the integration center, here a set of synapses with interneurons in the cord, processes it. The second half then sends the command out through the motor neuron to the effector."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a withdrawal reflex, the signal has just been processed in the spinal cord. Which sequence completes the arc from that point?\nA) Integration center, sensory neuron, effector\nB) Integration center, motor neuron, receptor\nC) Integration center, motor neuron, effector\nD) Integration center, effector, motor neuron\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Integration center, motor neuron, effector","why":"The second half of a reflex arc sends the response out. From the integration center in the spinal cord, a motor neuron carries the command out through the anterior root to the effector. In a withdrawal reflex, the effector is the set of muscles that pull the limb away from the painful stimulus."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient has no patellar reflex on one side. Along which structure could the break lie?\nA) The cerebral cortex  |  B) The corpus callosum\nC) The reflex arc  |  D) The cerebellum\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The reflex arc","why":"The patellar reflex depends on every link of its reflex arc: the receptor in the thigh muscle, the sensory neuron, the integration center in the spinal cord, the motor neuron, and the thigh muscle as the effector. Tapping the tendon tests all of these at once. If the reflex is missing, the break could be at any point along that arc."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a reflex arc, which component lies directly between the sensory neuron and the motor neuron?\nA) The receptor  |  B) The effector\nC) The integration center  |  D) The posterior root ganglion\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The integration center","why":"The five components of a reflex arc are, in order, receptor, sensory neuron, integration center, motor neuron, and effector. The integration center is the one or more synapses in the CNS where the incoming signal is processed and passed to the motor neuron. In a spinal reflex it lies in the gray matter of the spinal cord."},{"t":"mc","q":"In the patellar reflex, where is the integration center located?\nA) In the posterior root ganglion  |  B) In the thigh muscle\nC) In the cerebral cortex  |  D) In the spinal cord\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) In the spinal cord","why":"The integration center of a reflex arc is the synapse or synapses in the CNS where the incoming signal is processed. The patellar reflex is a spinal reflex, so its integration center is in the gray matter of the spinal cord, where the sensory neuron synapses with the motor neuron. Because the signal does not have to travel to the brain, the response is very fast."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Name the five components of a reflex arc in order from receptor to effector and trace a signal through them. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to name the five components of a reflex arc in order from receptor to effector and trace a signal through them."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to name the five components of a reflex arc in order from receptor to effector and trace a signal through them. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: reflex arc components. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, reflex arc components, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w8-reflex-types","name":"Types of reflexes","can":"Compare somatic versus autonomic, monosynaptic versus polysynaptic, and innate versus learned reflexes and give an example of each.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A withdrawal reflex pulls the hand off a hot surface using skeletal muscle, with interneurons in the spinal cord. Which description fits this reflex?\nA) Monosynaptic and autonomic  |  B) Polysynaptic and autonomic\nC) Monosynaptic and somatic  |  D) Polysynaptic and somatic\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Polysynaptic and somatic","why":"Reflexes can be described by synapse count and by effector at the same time. The withdrawal reflex has interneurons between the sensory and motor neurons, so it has two or more synapses and is polysynaptic. Its effector is skeletal muscle in the arm, so it is also a somatic reflex."},{"t":"mc","q":"A goalkeeper who has trained for years blocks a sudden shot before having time to think about it. Based on whether it is inborn or practiced, which class of reflex fits this response?\nA) An autonomic reflex  |  B) A learned reflex\nC) A monosynaptic reflex  |  D) An innate reflex\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A learned reflex","why":"When reflexes are classified by origin, innate reflexes are present from birth and learned reflexes are built through practice and repetition. The goalkeeper's save is fast and automatic only because of years of training, so it is a learned reflex. Catching a suddenly dropped object is another common example."},{"t":"mc","q":"The patellar reflex acts on skeletal muscle. By the effector-based classification, which type of reflex is it?\nA) An autonomic reflex  |  B) A somatic reflex\nC) A learned reflex  |  D) A polysynaptic reflex\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A somatic reflex","why":"Reflexes can be named for their effector. A somatic reflex acts on skeletal muscle, and an autonomic reflex acts on smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, or a gland. In the patellar (knee-jerk) reflex, the effector is the skeletal muscle on the front of the thigh, so it is a somatic reflex."},{"t":"mc","q":"The pupillary light reflex changes the size of the pupil using smooth muscle in the iris. By the effector-based classification, which type of reflex is it?\nA) A learned reflex  |  B) A somatic reflex\nC) A monosynaptic reflex  |  D) An autonomic reflex\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) An autonomic reflex","why":"When reflexes are classified by effector, a somatic reflex acts on skeletal muscle and an autonomic reflex acts on smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, or a gland. The muscle of the iris that changes the size of the pupil is smooth muscle. That makes the pupillary light reflex an autonomic reflex."},{"t":"mc","q":"A reflex pathway includes interneurons and has three synapses in the integration center. By synapse count, which type of reflex is it?\nA) An innate reflex  |  B) A polysynaptic reflex\nC) A somatic reflex  |  D) A monosynaptic reflex\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A polysynaptic reflex","why":"When reflexes are classified by synapse count, a monosynaptic reflex has one synapse and a polysynaptic reflex has two or more. Extra synapses mean there are interneurons between the sensory and motor neurons. The withdrawal reflex, which pulls a hand from a hot surface, is the standard polysynaptic example."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Compare somatic versus autonomic, monosynaptic versus polysynaptic, and innate versus learned reflexes and give an example of each. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to compare somatic versus autonomic, monosynaptic versus polysynaptic, and innate versus learned reflexes and give an example of each."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to compare somatic versus autonomic, monosynaptic versus polysynaptic, and innate versus learned reflexes and give an example of each. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: types of reflexes. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, types of reflexes, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w8-neuronal-pools","name":"Neuronal pools and pathways","can":"Describe convergence and divergence circuits and distinguish ascending pathways, descending pathways, and decussation.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A single sensory neuron entering the spinal cord branches and passes its signal to many interneurons at several levels of the cord. Which circuit pattern is this?\nA) Decussation  |  B) Serial processing\nC) Convergence  |  D) Divergence\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Divergence","why":"In a diverging circuit, one presynaptic neuron branches and synapses on many postsynaptic neurons. Here a single sensory neuron branches inside the cord and reaches many interneurons at different levels, so one input reaches many targets. Convergence is the reverse, with many neurons feeding onto one."},{"t":"mc","q":"A signal is sent along several separate pathways at the same time instead of down one chain. Which pattern is this?\nA) Serial processing  |  B) Decussation\nC) Parallel processing  |  D) Convergence\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Parallel processing","why":"In parallel processing, a signal travels along several pathways at the same time, so different parts of the CNS can handle the same information at once. Serial processing, by contrast, uses a single chain, as in a reflex arc."},{"t":"mc","q":"A signal needs to reach several brain regions at the same time so they can work on it at once. Which processing pattern does this?\nA) Convergence  |  B) Serial processing\nC) Parallel processing  |  D) Decussation\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Parallel processing","why":"In parallel processing, a signal travels along several pathways at the same time. That lets several brain regions receive and work on the same information at once instead of one after another. Serial processing, by contrast, passes the signal down a single chain, as in a reflex arc."},{"t":"mc","q":"In a neuronal pool, many sensory neurons all feed their signals onto a single output neuron. Which circuit pattern is this?\nA) Serial processing  |  B) Decussation\nC) Divergence  |  D) Convergence\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Convergence","why":"In a converging circuit, signals from many neurons come together onto one target neuron. That single neuron then combines, or integrates, all the input into one response. Convergence lets the CNS combine information from many sources before acting."},{"t":"mc","q":"Descending pathways of the spinal cord carry what kind of information, and in which direction?\nA) Motor commands down from the brain\nB) Motor commands up to the brain\nC) Sensory input down from the brain\nD) Sensory input carried up to the brain\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Motor commands down from the brain","why":"The white matter of the spinal cord holds two sets of long tracts. Descending pathways carry motor commands from the brain down to the body, and ascending pathways carry sensory information up to the brain. A spinal cord injury interrupts both, which is why both sensation and movement are lost below the level of the injury."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Describe convergence and divergence circuits and distinguish ascending pathways, descending pathways, and decussation. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to describe convergence and divergence circuits and distinguish ascending pathways, descending pathways, and decussation."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to describe convergence and divergence circuits and distinguish ascending pathways, descending pathways, and decussation. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: neuronal pools and pathways. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, neuronal pools and pathways, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w8-cord-external","name":"Spinal cord external anatomy","can":"Identify the cervical and lumbar enlargements, conus medullaris, cauda equina, filum terminale, anterior median fissure, and posterior median sulcus.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"After a fall, a patient has damage to the spinal cord that interrupts its ascending and descending tracts, and function is lost below the level of the damage. Which condition is this?\nA) Spinal cord injury  |  B) Meningitis\nC) Hydrocephalus  |  D) Stroke\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Spinal cord injury","why":"The ascending and descending tracts in the cord's white matter carry every signal between the brain and the body below. A spinal cord injury interrupts these tracts, so sensation and movement are lost below the level of the injury, while everything above it is spared. The higher the injury, the more of the body is affected."},{"t":"mc","q":"Where is the cauda equina located relative to the conus medullaris?\nA) Above it, in the cervical region of the cord\nB) Lateral to it, outside the vertebral column\nC) Inside it, as the central canal\nD) Below it, within the vertebral canal\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Below it, within the vertebral canal","why":"The spinal cord ends at the conus medullaris near the L1 to L2 disc, but the nerve roots for the lumbar, sacral, and coccygeal spinal nerves must still travel down to their exit points. These roots form the cauda equina, a hair-like bundle that hangs in the vertebral canal below the conus. Its name means horse's tail."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which structure is the bundle of nerve roots that hangs in the vertebral canal below the point where the spinal cord ends?\nA) The lateral horn  |  B) The filum terminale\nC) The conus medullaris  |  D) The cauda equina\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The cauda equina","why":"The spinal cord stops growing in length in early childhood while the vertebral column keeps growing, so the adult cord ends near the L1 to L2 disc. The roots of the lower spinal nerves continue down the canal to reach their exit points, forming a hair-like bundle called the cauda equina. These roots float in the CSF of the lumbar cistern."},{"t":"mc","q":"At which vertebral level does the adult spinal cord typically end?\nA) At the L1 to L2 level  |  B) At the T6 level\nC) At the L4 to L5 level  |  D) At the S2 level\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) At the L1 to L2 level","why":"The adult spinal cord ends at the conus medullaris near the disc between L1 and L2. The vertebral canal continues much lower, so below this point the canal holds only the cauda equina and CSF in the lumbar cistern. This is why a lumbar puncture is done lower, at L3 to L4 or L4 to L5."},{"t":"mc","q":"The cervical enlargement of the spinal cord gives rise to the nerves of which body region?\nA) The lower limbs  |  B) The upper limbs\nC) The pelvic organs  |  D) The thoracic wall\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The upper limbs","why":"The cervical enlargement runs from about C4 to T1. The arms need many motor and sensory neurons for their detailed movement and sensation, so this part of the cord holds extra neurons and is wider. Its nerves form the brachial plexus, which supplies the upper limbs."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the cervical and lumbar enlargements, conus medullaris, cauda equina, filum terminale, anterior median fissure, and posterior median sulcus. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the cervical and lumbar enlargements, conus medullaris, cauda equina, filum terminale, anterior median fissure, and posterior median sulcus."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the cervical and lumbar enlargements, conus medullaris, cauda equina, filum terminale, anterior median fissure, and posterior median sulcus. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: spinal cord external anatomy. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, spinal cord external anatomy, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w8-cord-roots","name":"Roots, rootlets, and root ganglion","can":"Identify the rootlets, posterior and anterior roots, posterior root ganglion, and intervertebral foramen and state which root carries sensory versus motor axons.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"In shingles, a virus reactivates and causes a rash along the exact strip of skin served by one spinal segment. Where did the virus lie dormant?\nA) In the posterior root ganglion  |  B) In the anterior gray horn\nC) In the lateral gray horn  |  D) In the posterior gray horn of the cord\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) In the posterior root ganglion","why":"The varicella zoster virus, which causes chickenpox, hides for decades in sensory neuron cell bodies. For a spinal segment, those cell bodies sit in the posterior root ganglion. When the virus reactivates, it travels down that ganglion's sensory axons, so the rash follows the one strip of skin, or dermatome, that the ganglion serves."},{"t":"mc","q":"A surgeon cuts only the posterior roots of one spinal segment. What is the most likely result in the area served by that segment?\nA) Loss of sensation, with movement preserved\nB) Loss of movement, with sensation preserved\nC) No change, because the anterior root takes over\nD) Loss of both sensation and movement\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Loss of sensation, with movement preserved","why":"The posterior root is the sensory root, carrying sensory axons into the cord. Cutting it stops sensation from that segment from reaching the cord and brain. The anterior, motor root is untouched, so motor commands still reach the muscles and voluntary movement remains."},{"t":"mc","q":"A virus that has been dormant in one dorsal root ganglion reactivates and causes a painful rash in a band of skin along one dermatome. Which disorder is it?\nA) Carpal tunnel syndrome  |  B) Peripheral neuropathy\nC) Bell palsy  |  D) Shingles\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Shingles","why":"Shingles is caused by the varicella zoster virus, the chickenpox virus, which can hide in a dorsal root ganglion for decades. When it reactivates, it travels along the sensory axons of that one spinal nerve, so the rash follows the dermatome that ganglion serves. This is why a shingles rash usually forms a band on one side of the body."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does the posterior (dorsal) root of a spinal nerve carry?\nA) Motor axons from the cord out to muscles\nB) Ascending tracts running up the cord to the brain\nC) Both sensory and motor axons mixed together\nD) Sensory axons from the periphery into the cord\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Sensory axons from the periphery into the cord","why":"Each spinal nerve connects to the cord by two roots. The posterior (dorsal) root is the sensory root, carrying sensory axons from receptors in the body into the back of the cord, and it bears a swelling, the dorsal root ganglion, that holds the sensory neuron cell bodies. Posterior is sensory in, anterior is motor out."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does the anterior (ventral) root of a spinal nerve carry?\nA) Both sensory and motor axons mixed together\nB) Sensory axons from the periphery into the cord\nC) Motor axons from the cord out to muscles\nD) The cell bodies of sensory neurons\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Motor axons from the cord out to muscles","why":"The anterior (ventral) root is the motor root. It carries axons from motor neurons whose cell bodies sit in the anterior and lateral horns of the gray matter, out of the front of the cord toward skeletal muscles, smooth muscle, and glands. It joins the posterior root to form the mixed spinal nerve."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the rootlets, posterior and anterior roots, posterior root ganglion, and intervertebral foramen and state which root carries sensory versus motor axons. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the rootlets, posterior and anterior roots, posterior root ganglion, and intervertebral foramen and state which root carries sensory versus motor axons."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the rootlets, posterior and anterior roots, posterior root ganglion, and intervertebral foramen and state which root carries sensory versus motor axons. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: roots, rootlets, and root ganglion. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, roots, rootlets, and root ganglion, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w8-cord-internal","name":"Spinal cord internal anatomy","can":"Identify the gray horns, central canal, commissures, and white columns on a cord cross-section and state the contents of the posterior, lateral, and anterior horns.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"On a cross-section of the spinal cord, a student points to the gray matter that holds the cell bodies of somatic motor neurons driving skeletal muscle. Which part of the gray matter is this?\nA) The posterior (dorsal) horn  |  B) The lateral horn\nC) The anterior (ventral) horn  |  D) The gray commissure\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The anterior (ventral) horn","why":"The gray matter of the cord is shaped into horns. The anterior (ventral) horn holds somatic motor nuclei, the cell bodies of motor neurons whose axons leave through the anterior root and make skeletal muscle contract. Damage to these cell bodies causes weakness or paralysis of the muscles served by that segment."},{"t":"mc","q":"A spinal cord cross-section is relatively small and is mostly gray matter, with only a thin rim of white matter. Which segment is it most likely from?\nA) A thoracic segment  |  B) A lumbar segment\nC) A cervical segment  |  D) A sacral segment\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) A sacral segment","why":"White matter thins as you go down the cord because fewer tracts are still running at each lower level. By the sacral segments, little white matter remains, so a sacral section is relatively small and has more gray matter than white matter."},{"t":"mc","q":"A spinal cord cross-section is almost circular and fairly large, with less white matter than a cervical section. Which segment is it most likely from?\nA) A thoracic segment  |  B) A lumbar segment\nC) A cervical segment  |  D) A coccygeal segment\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) A lumbar segment","why":"Lumbar segments sit in the lumbar enlargement, so they are fairly large with big anterior horns for the lower limbs. They are almost circular in outline and carry less white matter than cervical segments, because fewer tracts are still running that low. Shape and the amount of white matter are the main clues for identifying a segment."},{"t":"mc","q":"The white matter of the spinal cord is divided into anterior, posterior, and lateral columns. What do these columns contain?\nA) Nuclei, clusters of cell bodies in the CNS\nB) Ganglia, clusters of cell bodies in the PNS\nC) Tracts, bundles of axons within the CNS\nD) Nerves, bundles of axons in the PNS\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Tracts, bundles of axons within the CNS","why":"The white matter of the spinal cord is sorted into anterior, posterior, and lateral columns, and each column holds tracts. A tract is a bundle of axons within the CNS that share a common origin and destination. Ascending tracts carry sensory information up to the brain, and descending tracts carry motor commands down."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which order runs from the smallest structure to the largest: a single axon, a tract, and a white column of the spinal cord?\nA) White column, tract, axon  |  B) Axon, white column, tract\nC) Tract, axon, white column  |  D) Axon, tract, white column\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Axon, tract, white column","why":"The white matter of the spinal cord is organized in levels. A single axon is the smallest unit, many axons with a common origin and destination form a tract, and several tracts together make up a white column. The cord has anterior, posterior, and lateral white columns."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the gray horns, central canal, commissures, and white columns on a cord cross-section and state the contents of the posterior, lateral, and anterior horns. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the gray horns, central canal, commissures, and white columns on a cord cross-section and state the contents of the posterior, lateral, and anterior horns."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the gray horns, central canal, commissures, and white columns on a cord cross-section and state the contents of the posterior, lateral, and anterior horns. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: spinal cord internal anatomy. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, spinal cord internal anatomy, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w8-nerve-structure","name":"Nerve connective tissue wrappings","can":"Identify the axon, fascicle, endoneurium, perineurium, and epineurium of a peripheral nerve and state the level each layer wraps.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A surgeon repairs a cut nerve by lining up its fascicles. Which connective tissue wrappings hold the nerve's shape so the fascicles can be matched?\nA) The myelin sheath and neurilemma  |  B) The dura mater and arachnoid\nC) The epineurium and perineurium  |  D) The endomysium and perimysium\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The epineurium and perineurium","why":"The epineurium wraps the whole nerve and the perineurium wraps each fascicle, and these two layers hold a cut nerve's shape. Because each fascicle stays outlined by its perineurium, a surgeon can line up the cut ends and stitch these layers together. This connective tissue framework is part of why peripheral nerves can be repaired."},{"t":"mc","q":"What does the epineurium surround?\nA) A single axon  |  B) A single fascicle\nC) A muscle fascicle  |  D) The entire nerve\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) The entire nerve","why":"The epineurium surrounds the entire nerve, enclosing all of its fascicles along with the small blood vessels that supply the nerve. It is the outermost and toughest of the three nerve wrappings. Together with the perineurium, it holds a cut nerve's shape so a surgeon can line up the fascicles during repair."},{"t":"mc","q":"The endoneurium of a nerve matches which wrapping in a skeletal muscle?\nA) The epimysium  |  B) The endomysium\nC) The perimysium  |  D) The sarcolemma\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The endomysium","why":"The endoneurium wraps each single axon in a nerve, and the endomysium wraps each single muscle fiber in a skeletal muscle. Nerves and muscles are organized the same way, with one wrapping around each fiber, another around each fascicle, and an outer one around the whole organ. The shared prefixes line them up: endo- for one fiber, peri- for a fascicle, and epi- for the whole organ."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which choice lists the three connective tissue wrappings of a nerve from the smallest scale to the largest?\nA) Endoneurium, perineurium, epineurium\nB) Perineurium, endoneurium, epineurium\nC) Epineurium, perineurium, endoneurium\nD) Endoneurium, epineurium, perineurium\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Endoneurium, perineurium, epineurium","why":"From the smallest scale to the largest, the wrappings are the endoneurium around each axon, the perineurium around each fascicle, and the epineurium around the whole nerve. The prefixes give the order: endo- means within, peri- means around, and epi- means upon or over. Each wrapping is named for the level it surrounds."},{"t":"mc","q":"A cross section of a nerve shows several round bundles inside the thick outer layer, each outlined by its own thin ring of connective tissue. What are the bundles and their ring called?\nA) Fascicles wrapped by endoneurium  |  B) Fascicles wrapped by perineurium\nC) Axons wrapped by epineurium  |  D) Ganglia wrapped by perineurium\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Fascicles wrapped by perineurium","why":"On a nerve cross section, the thick outer ring is the epineurium. The round bundles inside it are fascicles, and each one is outlined by a thin ring of perineurium. The tiny circles packed inside a fascicle are single axons, each surrounded by endoneurium."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Identify the axon, fascicle, endoneurium, perineurium, and epineurium of a peripheral nerve and state the level each layer wraps. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to identify the axon, fascicle, endoneurium, perineurium, and epineurium of a peripheral nerve and state the level each layer wraps."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to identify the axon, fascicle, endoneurium, perineurium, and epineurium of a peripheral nerve and state the level each layer wraps. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: nerve connective tissue wrappings. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, nerve connective tissue wrappings, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w8-spinal-nerves-rami","name":"Spinal nerves and rami","can":"State the count of the 31 spinal nerve pairs by region and identify the posterior ramus, anterior ramus, meningeal branch, and rami communicantes by the territory each supplies.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"A nerve injury weakens the limbs but spares the muscles and skin of the back. Which branches of the spinal nerves were most likely involved?\nA) The rami communicantes  |  B) The posterior rami\nC) The anterior rami  |  D) The meningeal branches\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The anterior rami","why":"The anterior rami supply the limbs and the front and sides of the trunk, and they form the plexuses that send nerves into the limbs. An injury to the anterior rami or their plexuses affects the limbs, while the posterior rami, which serve the back, keep working. That is why the back is spared."},{"t":"mc","q":"Just after a spinal nerve leaves the vertebral column through the intervertebral foramen, it splits into branches. What are these branches called?\nA) Rami  |  B) Roots\nC) Fascicles  |  D) Ganglia\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Rami","why":"Just past the intervertebral foramen, each spinal nerve divides into branches called rami (singular ramus). The posterior ramus supplies the deep back muscles and the skin of the back, and the anterior ramus supplies the limbs and the front and sides of the trunk. Smaller branches include the meningeal branch and the rami communicantes, which link to the sympathetic trunk."},{"t":"mc","q":"Spinal nerves regroup into networks called plexuses. Which part of the spinal nerves forms these plexuses?\nA) The ventral roots  |  B) The dorsal roots\nC) The ventral rami  |  D) The dorsal rami\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The ventral rami","why":"The ventral (anterior) rami of spinal nerves join and regroup into plexuses before their axons travel on to the body. This regrouping lets each region receive axons from several spinal nerves, so damage to one spinal nerve rarely erases all function in an area. The ventral rami of T2 to T12 are the exception; they run straight to the thoracic wall as the intercostal nerves."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which choice lists the spinal nerve regions in order from the neck down toward the tailbone?\nA) Cervical, thoracic, sacral, lumbar, coccygeal\nB) Cervical, lumbar, thoracic, sacral, coccygeal\nC) Thoracic, cervical, lumbar, sacral, coccygeal\nD) Cervical, thoracic, lumbar, sacral, coccygeal\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Cervical, thoracic, lumbar, sacral, coccygeal","why":"The spinal nerves are named for the region of the vertebral column where they leave, so they follow the same order as the column: cervical (8 pairs), thoracic (12), lumbar (5), sacral (5), and coccygeal (1). The neck comes first, then the chest, the lower back, the sacrum, and finally the tailbone. Knowing the order of the vertebral regions gives you the order of the nerve regions."},{"t":"mc","q":"The anterior rami of which spinal nerves do not join a plexus but run straight to the thoracic wall?\nA) T2 to T12  |  B) C1 to C4\nC) L1 to L4  |  D) C5 to T1\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) T2 to T12","why":"The anterior rami of T2 to T12 do not form a plexus. Instead, they run between the ribs as the intercostal nerves (T12, below the last rib, is called the subcostal nerve). They supply the intercostal muscles, the muscles of the abdominal wall, and the skin of the chest and abdomen. T1 joins the brachial plexus, and every other region also sends its anterior rami into a plexus."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: State the count of the 31 spinal nerve pairs by region and identify the posterior ramus, anterior ramus, meningeal branch, and rami communicantes by the territory each supplies. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to state the count of the 31 spinal nerve pairs by region and identify the posterior ramus, anterior ramus, meningeal branch, and rami communicantes by the territory each supplies."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to state the count of the 31 spinal nerve pairs by region and identify the posterior ramus, anterior ramus, meningeal branch, and rami communicantes by the territory each supplies. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: spinal nerves and rami. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, spinal nerves and rami, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w8-sensory-receptors","name":"Sensory receptor classification","can":"Classify sensory receptors by stimulus, location, and structure and match named receptors such as tactile corpuscles, lamellar corpuscles, and muscle spindles to their location and stimulus.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"The fingertips sense fine detail far better than the skin of the back. Which difference best explains this?\nA) Larger dorsal root ganglia for the hand\nB) Thicker myelin on their sensory axons\nC) More tactile corpuscles and Merkel discs\nD) No free nerve endings to compete for signals\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) More tactile corpuscles and Merkel discs","why":"The fingertips are crowded with tactile (Meissner) corpuscles and tactile (Merkel) discs, the receptors for fine, discriminative touch. The more of these receptors packed into an area, the smaller the details that skin can tell apart. The skin of the back has far fewer of them, so it senses touch only crudely."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patch of skin can still feel pain and temperature but cannot sense fine touch. Which receptors are still working there?\nA) Tactile (Meissner) corpuscles  |  B) Free nerve endings\nC) Lamellar corpuscles  |  D) Muscle spindles\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) Free nerve endings","why":"Free nerve endings sense pain, temperature, and some touch, so if pain and temperature remain, the free nerve endings are intact. Fine, discriminative touch depends on the tactile (Meissner) corpuscles and the Merkel discs, so those must be the receptors not working. Matching each remaining sensation to its receptor shows which receptors survived."},{"t":"mc","q":"After bumping an elbow, a person rubs the spot. Which receptors detect the touch and pressure of the rubbing?\nA) Mechanoreceptors  |  B) Thermoreceptors\nC) Chemoreceptors  |  D) Nociceptors\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Mechanoreceptors","why":"Mechanoreceptors respond to mechanical force such as touch, pressure, vibration, and stretch. Rubbing the skin presses on and moves it, so mechanoreceptors in the skin detect the rubbing. The pain from the bump itself came from nociceptors, which respond to potentially damaging stimuli."},{"t":"mc","q":"A receptor is a bare dendrite tip with no connective tissue covering, and it detects pain and temperature. What is it?\nA) A lamellar (Pacinian) corpuscle  |  B) A tactile (Meissner) corpuscle\nC) A free nerve ending  |  D) A muscle spindle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A free nerve ending","why":"A free nerve ending is the simplest sensory receptor, a bare dendrite tip with no capsule around it. Free nerve endings are found in the epithelium and connective tissue throughout the body. They detect pain, temperature, and some touch."},{"t":"mc","q":"A sensory nerve ending is wrapped in a connective tissue capsule and detects touch and pressure. What is its structural class?\nA) A nociceptor  |  B) A free nerve ending\nC) An interoceptor  |  D) An encapsulated receptor\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) An encapsulated receptor","why":"Encapsulated receptors are nerve endings wrapped in a connective tissue capsule. Most of them are mechanoreceptors for touch, pressure, vibration, or stretch, such as the tactile (Meissner) corpuscles and lamellar (Pacinian) corpuscles. Structure is one of three ways receptors are classified, along with stimulus type and location."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Classify sensory receptors by stimulus, location, and structure and match named receptors such as tactile corpuscles, lamellar corpuscles, and muscle spindles to their location and stimulus. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to classify sensory receptors by stimulus, location, and structure and match named receptors such as tactile corpuscles, lamellar corpuscles, and muscle spindles to their location and stimulus."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to classify sensory receptors by stimulus, location, and structure and match named receptors such as tactile corpuscles, lamellar corpuscles, and muscle spindles to their location and stimulus. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: sensory receptor classification. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, sensory receptor classification, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w8-nerve-plexuses","name":"Nerve plexuses","can":"Name the cervical, brachial, lumbar, sacral, and coccygeal plexuses with the spinal nerves that form each and predict the body region affected if a given plexus is damaged.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"An injury to the cervical plexus on one side also damages the phrenic nerve. Which structure is most directly weakened?\nA) The muscles of the upper limb  |  B) The muscles of the lower abdominal wall\nC) The diaphragm on that side  |  D) The tongue on that side\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The diaphragm on that side","why":"The phrenic nerve leaves the cervical plexus, which forms mainly from C1 to C4, and it is the sole motor supply to the diaphragm. If it is damaged on one side, the diaphragm on that side weakens and breathing is affected. Other cervical plexus damage shows up as weakness or numbness of the neck and shoulder."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which choice correctly names the plexus formed from C5 to T1, one nerve that comes from it, and the region that nerve serves?\nA) Sacral plexus, median nerve, upper limb\nB) Cervical plexus, phrenic nerve, upper limb\nC) Brachial plexus, median nerve, upper limb\nD) Brachial plexus, sciatic nerve, lower limb\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Brachial plexus, median nerve, upper limb","why":"The anterior rami of C5 to T1 form the brachial plexus, which serves the shoulder and the entire upper limb. One of its major nerves is the median nerve, which runs down the front of the arm and forearm and passes through the carpal tunnel into the hand. Every part of the correct answer belongs to the upper limb, which is how the pieces fit together."},{"t":"mc","q":"A patient with a spinal cord injury high in the neck cannot breathe without a ventilator. Which nerve and plexus best explain this?\nA) The vagus nerve from the brainstem\nB) The axillary nerve from the brachial plexus\nC) The phrenic nerve from the cervical plexus\nD) The intercostal nerves from the thoracic rami\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The phrenic nerve from the cervical plexus","why":"The diaphragm is the main muscle of breathing, and its only motor supply is the phrenic nerve, which arises from the cervical plexus at C3 to C5. A cord injury above these levels cuts the diaphragm off from the brain, so the patient cannot breathe without support. That is the meaning behind C3, 4, 5 keep the diaphragm alive."},{"t":"mc","q":"After a motorcycle crash that forced the head away from the shoulder, a rider loses movement and sensation across the whole upper limb. Which structure was most likely stretched or torn?\nA) The lumbar plexus  |  B) The brachial plexus\nC) The sacral plexus  |  D) The cervical plexus\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The brachial plexus","why":"The brachial plexus runs from the lower neck (C5 to T1) across the shoulder region into the arm. Forcing the head and shoulder apart stretches this network, and because all the upper limb nerves pass through it, the whole limb can lose movement and sensation. A deficit covering the entire upper limb points to the plexus rather than a single named nerve."},{"t":"mc","q":"A pelvic fracture damages the sacral plexus. Which pattern of deficits would you expect?\nA) Weakness of the front and inner thigh and lower abdominal wall\nB) Numbness limited to a small patch of skin over the coccyx\nC) Weakness and numbness across the shoulder and whole upper limb\nD) Weakness of the buttock, back of the thigh, and the leg and foot\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) Weakness of the buttock, back of the thigh, and the leg and foot","why":"The sacral plexus lies on the posterior wall of the pelvis, so a pelvic fracture can injure it directly. It serves the buttock, the perineum, the back of the thigh, and most of the leg and foot. Damage therefore causes weakness and sensory loss in that territory."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Name the cervical, brachial, lumbar, sacral, and coccygeal plexuses with the spinal nerves that form each and predict the body region affected if a given plexus is damaged. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to name the cervical, brachial, lumbar, sacral, and coccygeal plexuses with the spinal nerves that form each and predict the body region affected if a given plexus is damaged."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to name the cervical, brachial, lumbar, sacral, and coccygeal plexuses with the spinal nerves that form each and predict the body region affected if a given plexus is damaged. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: nerve plexuses. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, nerve plexuses, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w8-ans-pathway","name":"Autonomic two-neuron pathway","can":"Contrast the somatic and autonomic motor systems and trace the two-neuron pathway through a preganglionic neuron, autonomic ganglion, and postganglionic neuron to an effector.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"An autonomic preganglionic axon runs from the CNS to which structure?\nA) To the dorsal gray horn  |  B) Directly to the effector organ\nC) To a skeletal muscle  |  D) To an autonomic ganglion\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"D) To an autonomic ganglion","why":"The preganglionic axon leaves the CNS and runs to an autonomic ganglion, where it synapses with a postganglionic neuron. The postganglionic axon then carries the signal the rest of the way to the effector. This synapse in a ganglion is the one feature the somatic motor pathway does not have."},{"t":"mc","q":"Where does the cell body of an autonomic postganglionic neuron sit?\nA) In the dorsal root ganglion  |  B) In the brainstem\nC) In the autonomic ganglion  |  D) In the lateral horn of the spinal cord\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) In the autonomic ganglion","why":"The postganglionic neuron is the second neuron of the autonomic pathway. Its cell body sits in the autonomic ganglion, where it receives the synapse from the preganglionic axon, and its unmyelinated axon runs on to the effector. The word postganglionic means after the ganglion, describing the axon that leaves it."},{"t":"mc","q":"A motor pathway reaches a skeletal muscle through a single neuron with no ganglion along the way. Which system is this?\nA) The sympathetic division  |  B) The parasympathetic division\nC) The somatic motor system  |  D) The autonomic nervous system\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The somatic motor system","why":"The somatic motor system uses one motor neuron that runs from the CNS straight to a skeletal muscle, with no ganglion in between. Its cell body sits in the ventral gray horn or a brainstem motor nucleus. The autonomic system, by contrast, always uses two neurons with a synapse in a ganglion."},{"t":"mc","q":"An autonomic neuron has its cell body inside an autonomic ganglion and sends its axon out to a gland. Which neuron is this?\nA) A somatic motor neuron  |  B) A preganglionic neuron\nC) A postganglionic neuron  |  D) A sensory neuron\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) A postganglionic neuron","why":"A postganglionic neuron is the second neuron of the autonomic pathway. Its cell body sits in the autonomic ganglion, and its unmyelinated axon runs from the ganglion to the effector, here a gland. The name means after the ganglion, which describes the path of its axon."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which of these axons is typically unmyelinated?\nA) The preganglionic axon  |  B) A somatic motor axon to skeletal muscle\nC) The postganglionic axon  |  D) A sensory axon from a lamellar corpuscle\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) The postganglionic axon","why":"In the autonomic pathway, the preganglionic axon is lightly myelinated and the postganglionic axon is unmyelinated. Myelin is the fatty wrapping around an axon, and postganglionic axons lack it. The synapse in the ganglion marks the switch from the lightly myelinated first axon to the unmyelinated second one."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Contrast the somatic and autonomic motor systems and trace the two-neuron pathway through a preganglionic neuron, autonomic ganglion, and postganglionic neuron to an effector. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to contrast the somatic and autonomic motor systems and trace the two-neuron pathway through a preganglionic neuron, autonomic ganglion, and postganglionic neuron to an effector."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to contrast the somatic and autonomic motor systems and trace the two-neuron pathway through a preganglionic neuron, autonomic ganglion, and postganglionic neuron to an effector. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: autonomic two-neuron pathway. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, autonomic two-neuron pathway, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]},{"id":"w8-ans-ganglia-divisions","name":"Autonomic ganglia and divisions","can":"Compare the sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions by CNS origin and ganglion location and identify the sympathetic trunk, prevertebral ganglia, terminal ganglia, and autonomic and enteric plexuses.","items":[{"t":"mc","q":"During surgery high in the chest, a structure is cut that runs as a chain of ganglia beside the vertebral column. Which structure was cut?\nA) The vagus nerve  |  B) The sympathetic trunk\nC) The celiac plexus  |  D) The pelvic splanchnic nerves\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"B) The sympathetic trunk","why":"The sympathetic trunk is a chain of ganglia that runs on each side of the vertebral column from the base of the skull to the coccyx. In the thorax it lies along the heads of the ribs, beside the vertebral bodies. Cutting it interrupts the sympathetic fibers traveling through that part of the chain."},{"t":"mc","q":"In an autonomic pathway to the stomach, the second neuron sits in the stomach wall and its axon travels only a few millimeters. Which division does this describe?\nA) The parasympathetic division  |  B) The somatic motor system\nC) The sympathetic division  |  D) The visceral sensory system\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The parasympathetic division","why":"A second neuron sitting in the wall of the organ means the ganglion is an intramural (terminal) ganglion, and terminal ganglia belong to the parasympathetic division. Because the ganglion is at the organ, the postganglionic axon is very short, while the preganglionic axon, here traveling in the vagus nerve, is long. Sympathetic pathways show the opposite pattern."},{"t":"mc","q":"A preganglionic cell body sits in the lateral gray horn of a thoracic spinal segment. To which division does this neuron belong?\nA) The sympathetic division  |  B) The parasympathetic division\nC) The enteric nervous system  |  D) The somatic motor system\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) The sympathetic division","why":"The lateral gray horn of segments T1 to L2 holds the preganglionic cell bodies of the sympathetic division. A thoracic segment falls within that range, so this neuron is sympathetic. Its axon will leave through the ventral root and head for the sympathetic trunk."},{"t":"mc","q":"Which set of structures all belong to the parasympathetic division?\nA) Cranial nerve X, the pelvic splanchnic nerves, and terminal ganglia\nB) The sympathetic trunk, the thoracic splanchnic nerves, and prevertebral ganglia\nC) Cranial nerve X, the sympathetic trunk, and paravertebral ganglia\nD) The pelvic splanchnic nerves, the lateral horn, and chain ganglia\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"A) Cranial nerve X, the pelvic splanchnic nerves, and terminal ganglia","why":"The vagus nerve (cranial nerve X) carries the cranial parasympathetic outflow to the chest and abdomen, the pelvic splanchnic nerves carry the sacral outflow from S2 to S4, and terminal ganglia are where parasympathetic fibers synapse near or in the organ. All three belong to the craniosacral, or parasympathetic, division. The sympathetic trunk, the lateral horn, and the chain and prevertebral ganglia all belong to the sympathetic division."},{"t":"mc","q":"A person's heart rate rises at the start of exercise, even before the sympathetic division speeds it up. Which change best explains the early rise?\nA) The enteric system stimulates the heart\nB) The sympathetic trunk is cut on both sides\nC) Parasympathetic input is withdrawn\nD) The somatic system contracts the heart\nChoose one answer, then draw and explain why it is correct.","ans":"C) Parasympathetic input is withdrawn","why":"At rest, parasympathetic input from the vagus nerve holds the heart at a slower rate. Because the two divisions pull in opposite directions, easing off this parasympathetic brake lets the heart rate rise even before the sympathetic division begins to speed it up. This shows how the body can adjust an organ by withdrawing one division, not only by pushing with the other."},{"t":"draw","q":"Draw to know it. Build a labeled drawing that shows you can do this: Compare the sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions by CNS origin and ganglion location and identify the sympathetic trunk, prevertebral ganglia, terminal ganglia, and autonomic and enteric plexuses. Explain each part out loud as you draw."},{"t":"draw","q":"Teach it. A classmate missed this topic. Use a drawing to teach them how to compare the sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions by CNS origin and ganglion location and identify the sympathetic trunk, prevertebral ganglia, terminal ganglia, and autonomic and enteric plexuses."},{"t":"draw","q":"Propose a model. Design your own diagram, chart, or map that organizes everything you need to compare the sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions by CNS origin and ganglion location and identify the sympathetic trunk, prevertebral ganglia, terminal ganglia, and autonomic and enteric plexuses. Explain why you arranged it that way."},{"t":"draw","q":"From memory. Without notes, draw everything you know about this topic: autonomic ganglia and divisions. Then talk through what each part of your drawing shows."},{"t":"draw","q":"Connect it. Draw a model of this topic, autonomic ganglia and divisions, and link it to one other topic from this unit. Explain the connection out loud."}]}]}]};
